{"qid": "tq:qb_5712", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Coffee was historically called \u201cThe Wine of Islam\u201d because it provided a stimulating effect similar to wine while allowing Muslims to avoid alcohol during religious observances.\n\nAnswer: Coffee<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "Coffee"}
{"qid": "tq:qb_5712", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The drink known as \"The Wine of Islam\" is **coffee**.\n\nCoffee earned this nickname because it became the socially and religiously acceptable alternative to alcoholic wine in Islamic cultures. Originating in Ethiopia and Yemen, coffee spread throughout the Muslim world beginning in the 15th century, providing stimulation without intoxication (which is prohibited in Islam). In Arabic, the word \"qahwa\" (coffee) was originally used for wine, but shifted to describe the new beverage that replaced wine in social and intellectual gatherings across the Islamic world.\n\nAnswer: Coffee", "correct": true, "gold": "Coffee"}
{"qid": "tq:qw_984", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Hallmark\n\nAnswer: Hallmark", "correct": true, "gold": "Hall Mark"}
{"qid": "tq:qw_984", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The set of marks is called \"hallmarks.\" These are official stamps applied to gold, silver, and other precious metal items to certify their purity, origin, and often the year of testing.\n\nAnswer: Hallmarks", "correct": true, "gold": "Hall Mark"}
{"qid": "tq:qb_5712", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "Coffee originated in the Islamic world (Ethiopia/Yemen) and was adopted by Sufis to stay awake during night prayers and dhikr. Because its Arabic name *qahwa* was the same word once used for wine, and because it served a socially and ritually analogous function once wine was forbidden, it acquired the nickname \u201cthe wine of Islam.\u201d\nAnswer: coffee", "correct": true, "gold": "Coffee"}
