{"qid": "tq:dpql_1443", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "Fleming's left-hand rule (also called the motor rule) relates the directions of magnetic field, current, and resulting force/thrust on a conductor. It is the standard mnemonic taught for predicting rotor motion in DC motors and other electromechanical devices. (The matching right-hand rule applies to generators.)\nThe rule is named for its originator, the British electrical engineer Sir John Ambrose Fleming.\nAnswer: Fleming (J. A. Fleming)", "correct": true, "gold": "John Ambrose Fleming"}
{"qid": "tq:qw_10603", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The term for the deliberate killing of one's own mother is derived from Latin roots (\"mater\" for mother + \"-cide\" for killing). This is distinct from related terms like patricide (father) or parricide (a parent or close relative in general).  \n\nAnswer: matricide", "correct": true, "gold": "Matricide"}
{"qid": "tq:bb_3448", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "In hairdressing, the perming process generally ends with **neutralising** \u2014 applying a neutraliser (fixing lotion) after rinsing out the perm solution. This step stops the chemical action, re-forms the hair's disulphide bonds in their new curled shape, and locks in the perm result.\n\nAnswer: Neutralising (applying the neutraliser/fixing lotion to set the curl)", "correct": false, "gold": "Neutralizing"}
{"qid": "tq:bb_3448", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The final stage of a hair perming process is the neutralization step, where a neutralizer is applied to stop the chemical reaction, lock in the curl pattern, and prepare the hair for rinsing and conditioning.  \n\nAnswer: Neutralization (application of neutralizer).<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Neutralizing"}
{"qid": "tq:qg_2775", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "Julia Child was the pioneering American TV chef whose long-running PBS series *The French Chef* (and later programs) brought French cooking techniques to mainstream U.S. audiences beginning in the 1960s.  \n\nAfter her death, her family donated the entire contents of the modest Cambridge, Massachusetts, kitchen in which she had filmed many of her shows. The Smithsonian\u2019s National Museum of American History painstakingly reconstructed and installed that kitchen as a permanent exhibition that opened in 2002; it remains on view today, complete with her pegboard wall of copper pots, her Garland commercial range, and thousands of well-used utensils.  \n\nNo other television chef\u2019s workspace has received comparable institutional recognition at the Smithsonian.  \n\nAnswer: Julia Child", "correct": true, "gold": "Julia Child"}
{"qid": "tq:bb_3448", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "The final stage of a hair perming process is **neutralisation**. \r\n\r\nThis involves applying a neutraliser to the hair, which reforms the disulfide bonds in the new curled shape, locking the perm in place. After neutralisation, the hair is rinsed, blotted dry, and styled as desired. \r\n\r\nAnswer: Neutralisation", "correct": false, "gold": "Neutralizing"}
