{"qid": "tq:bb_3448", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The perming process (also called permanent waving) relies on two sequential chemical actions to reshape the hair. First, a reducing agent (perm lotion) is applied to break the disulfide bonds in the cortex while the hair is held in curlers. Once that reaction is complete, the lotion is rinsed away and a neutraliser (an oxidising agent) is applied. The neutraliser re-forms the disulfide bonds in their new, curved positions, locking the curl or wave permanently in place.\nBecause this bond-reformation step completes the chemical transformation, neutralising is regarded as the final stage of the actual perming process. Any subsequent rinsing, rod removal or blow-drying is simply finishing work, not part of the perm itself.\nAnswer: neutralising (applying neutraliser)", "correct": false, "gold": "Neutralizing"}
