{"qid": "tq:sfq_8652", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The English war artist who painted both 'Totes Meer' (1940-41) and 'Battle of Britain' (1941) is **Paul Nash** (1889-1946). Nash was an official war artist in both World War I and World War II, and these two paintings are among his most famous works from the latter conflict. 'Totes Meer' depicts a scrapyard of wrecked German aircraft, while 'Battle of Britain' shows aerial combat contrails in the sky.\n\nAnswer: Paul Nash", "correct": true, "gold": "PAUL NASH"}
{"qid": "tq:tc_2901", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The pancreas contains clusters of endocrine cells (islets of Langerhans) that release two primary hormones in response to blood-glucose fluctuations: insulin (from beta cells) lowers glucose by promoting cellular uptake and storage, while glucagon (from alpha cells) raises glucose by stimulating hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis. Together they maintain homeostasis; other islet secretions (e.g., somatostatin) play only minor modulatory roles.\n\nAnswer: insulin and glucagon", "correct": true, "gold": "Insulin"}
{"qid": "tq:sfq_8652", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Answer: Paul Nash", "correct": true, "gold": "PAUL NASH"}
{"qid": "tq:sfq_8652", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "Paul Nash was a leading English surrealist painter and official war artist during both world wars. His WWII works include the well-known canvases *Totes Meer* (a surreal seascape of wrecked German aircraft) and *Battle of Britain* (an aerial view of the 1940 air fighting). These titles are not associated with any other British war artist of the period.\n\nAnswer: Paul Nash", "correct": true, "gold": "PAUL NASH"}
