{"qid": "tq:bb_4900", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The trademarked bass-frequency cinematic effect developed by Cerwin-Vega and Universal Studios for the 1974 film *Earthquake* is **Sensurround**.\n\nAnswer: Sensurround", "correct": true, "gold": "Sensurround"}
{"qid": "tq:bb_4900", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The trademarked effect is **Sensurround**.\n\nThis system used Cerwin-Vega high-powered bass speakers installed in theaters to produce low-frequency rumbling (around 10-20 Hz) that physically shook the audience and the theater itself, simulating the tactile sensation of an earthquake. It was later used for other films like *Midway* (1976) and *Rollercoaster* (1977).\n\nAnswer: Sensurround", "correct": true, "gold": "Sensurround"}
{"qid": "tq:odql_557", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "In the imperial system:\n\n- 1 gallon = 4 quarts\n- 1 quart = 2 pints\n- 1 pint = 4 gills\n\nSo:\n\n4 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 4 = 32 gills\n\nAnswer: 32", "correct": true, "gold": "32"}
{"qid": "tq:bb_4900", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "Sensurround was a trademarked low-frequency audio process (using large subwoofers and a special control tone to drive them) created jointly by Cerwin-Vega and Universal Studios. It was premiered with the 1974 disaster film *Earthquake* to produce physically felt bass vibrations that simulated tremors in the theater. The name itself was registered as a trademark and later applied to a handful of other Universal releases before being superseded by modern digital surround formats.\n\nAnswer: Sensurround", "correct": true, "gold": "Sensurround"}
