{"qid": "tq:dpql_3475", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Arthur Miller wrote *The Crucible* as an allegory for the anti-communist hysteria of his own time, but its historical foundation is the real events of the Salem witch trials that took place in colonial Massachusetts between 1692 and 1693, when dozens of people were accused, tried, and executed for witchcraft.  \n\nAnswer: The Salem witch trials of 1692\u20131693.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "Salem Witch Trials"}
{"qid": "tq:dpql_3475", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The Crucible is a fictionalized dramatization of real events from colonial Massachusetts. It draws directly on the 1692\u20131693 Salem witch trials, in which more than 200 people were accused of witchcraft and 20 were executed. Miller used the historical record (court documents, contemporary accounts, and the social dynamics of Puritan theocracy) as the play\u2019s literal subject matter while also employing it as an allegory for 1950s McCarthyism.\nAnswer: 1692\u20131693 Salem witch trials (Massachusetts)", "correct": true, "gold": "Salem Witch Trials"}
