{"qid": "tq:dpql_3475", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "The Crucible (1953) dramatizes the Salem witch trials of 1692 in colonial Massachusetts, in which a wave of hysteria led to accusations of witchcraft, unjust trials, and executions of innocent people. Miller wrote the play as an allegory for McCarthyism and the anti-communist \"Red Scare\" of the early 1950s, drawing parallels between the paranoia and false accusations of Salem and the House Un-American Activities Committee's persecution of suspected communists in his own time.\n\nAnswer: The Crucible dramatizes the Salem witch trials of 1692, using this historical episode of religious hysteria and false accusations as an allegory for the McCarthy-era Red Scare and anti-communist witch hunts of the 1950s.", "correct": true, "gold": "Salem Witch Trials"}
{"qid": "tq:sfq_1192", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "Keane is a British alternative rock band whose debut studio album, *Hopes and Fears* (2004), reached No. 1 in the UK and spawned hit singles such as \u201cSomewhere Only We Know.\u201d Their follow-up album, *Under the Iron Sea* (2006), also topped the UK charts and produced further hits including \u201cIs It Any Wonder?\u201d These two releases are the only studio albums that match the titles given in the query, confirming the group in question.\n\nAnswer: Keane", "correct": true, "gold": "Keane"}
