{"qid": "tq:bb_2843", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The wasabi condiment is made from the thick, underground stem (rhizome) of the wasabi plant, which contains the characteristic pungent compounds.\n\nAnswer: rhizome<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Roots"}
{"qid": "tq:odql_15045", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The Twelve Labours of Heracles are a fixed sequence of tasks in Greek mythology. Ladon, the multi-headed dragon, is the guardian of the golden apples in the Garden of the Hesperides.  \n\nThat garden and its apples are the object of the eleventh labour (stealing the apples). In the relevant versions of the myth Heracles slays Ladon with an arrow before (or while) obtaining the fruit, either directly or with Atlas\u2019s help.  \n\nThus Ladon is associated exclusively with the Hesperides labour.\n\nAnswer: Apples of the Hesperides (11th labour)", "correct": true, "gold": "The apples of the Hesperides"}
{"qid": "tq:bb_2843", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The Japanese condiment wasabi is produced from the **rhizome** (the underground stem) of the wasabi plant (*Eutrema japonicum*). The rhizome is grated into a paste, which releases the volatile isothiocyanates that give wasabi its characteristic pungent heat and aroma. While often referred to colloquially as the \"root,\" it is botanically a rhizome.\n\nAnswer: The rhizome (underground stem) of the wasabi plant.", "correct": false, "gold": "Roots"}
