{"qid": "tq:qw_669", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "Nirvana (or nibbana) is the soteriological goal of Buddhism: the complete cessation of craving, aversion, and delusion that binds beings to sams\u0101ra, the cycle of rebirth and suffering. Once these causes are extinguished, no further rebirth is generated; the liberated person (an arhat or a buddha) is therefore described as \u201crelieved of the necessity of rebirth.\u201d Classical sources characterize this unconditioned state as a profound peace or \u201cblissful repose\u201d beyond ordinary existence, sometimes rendered in English as \u201cabsolute existence\u201d because it is not subject to arising, decay, or death. The term parinirv\u0101\u1e47a is occasionally used for the final passing of such a being, but the state itself\u2014whether realized in life or after death\u2014is simply nirvana.\nAnswer: nirvana", "correct": true, "gold": "Nirvana"}
