{"qid": "tq:sfq_14641", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The unit found by multiplying volts by amperes is the watt, which measures power.\n\nAnswer: watt", "correct": false, "gold": "WATTS"}
{"qid": "tq:sfq_14641", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Power in an electrical circuit is calculated as the product of voltage (volts) and current (amperes). Since power is measured in watts, multiplying volts by amperes gives watts. \n\nDimensionally: 1 volt = 1 joule/coulomb and 1 ampere = 1 coulomb/second, so 1 volt \u00d7 1 ampere = 1 joule/second = 1 watt.\n\nAnswer: watt (or watts)", "correct": true, "gold": "WATTS"}
{"qid": "tq:sfq_14641", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "Volts measure electrical potential difference (or voltage), while amperes measure electrical current. Their product therefore has the dimensions of electrical power, which is expressed by the SI derived unit known as the watt (W). This relation is expressed by the basic equation \\(P = VI\\), where \\(P\\) is power in watts. (In AC circuits the same product is sometimes labeled \u201cvolt-amperes\u201d to distinguish apparent power from real power, but the underlying SI unit remains the watt.)\nAnswer: watt (W)", "correct": false, "gold": "WATTS"}
