{"qid": "tq:qw_3161", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The question describes a well-known group of North African-based sea raiders active from the late Middle Ages through the early 19th century. These raiders operated chiefly out of the port cities of the Maghreb (Algiers, Tunis, Tripoli, and Sal\u00e9) and preyed on Mediterranean shipping as well as coastal towns in southern Europe. Although popularly labeled \u201cBarbary pirates,\u201d the contemporary and historiographic term for them is \u201ccorsairs.\u201d The word \u201ccorsair\u201d (from the Latin *cursus*, \u201craid\u201d) distinguishes licensed privateers sailing under the sanction of North African rulers or the Ottoman sultan from unlicensed freebooters. Hence the group is conventionally known in historical sources as the Barbary corsairs (or Ottoman corsairs).\nAnswer: corsairs (Barbary corsairs)", "correct": true, "gold": "Corsairs"}
