{"qid": "tq:sfq_17938", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The oboe is the instrument that typically plays the reference A note for an orchestra to tune to before a performance. The principal oboist (first oboist) usually sounds this A, providing a clear, stable pitch that all other musicians can match.\n\nAnswer: the oboe<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "OBOE"}
{"qid": "tq:sfq_17938", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The **oboe** plays the A note to which the orchestra tunes. \n\nThe oboe is traditionally used for this purpose because it has a penetrating, stable sound that can be heard clearly across the entire orchestra, and its pitch is relatively fixed (unlike string instruments). The principal oboist sounds a concert A (440 Hz or 442 Hz, depending on the standard), and the woodwinds tune first, followed by the brass and then the strings.\n\nAnswer: The oboe.", "correct": true, "gold": "OBOE"}
