{"qid": "tq:qw_16174", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "A common feature of komodo dragons, aphids, and whiptail lizards is that they can all reproduce through parthenogenesis, a form of asexual reproduction where females produce offspring without fertilization by a male. While komodo dragons usually reproduce sexually, they have been known to produce viable offspring via parthenogenesis in captivity. Aphids commonly reproduce parthenogenetically, especially in spring and summer. Many species of whiptail lizards, particularly in the genus *Aspidoscelis*, are entirely female and reproduce exclusively through parthenogenesis.\n\nAnswer: parthenogenesis", "correct": false, "gold": "The ability to reproduce asexually"}
