{"qid": "tq:qw_16174", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "These three species share the ability to reproduce through **parthenogenesis** \u2014 a form of asexual reproduction in which females can produce offspring without fertilization by a male.\n\n- **Komodo dragons** can undergo facultative parthenogenesis when isolated from males, producing viable (typically male) offspring.\n- **Aphids** commonly reproduce parthenogenetically during favorable seasons, allowing rapid population growth from a single female.\n- **Whiptail lizards** include several species that are entirely female and reproduce exclusively through obligate parthenogenesis, with no males in the population at all.\n\nAnswer: They can all reproduce asexually through parthenogenesis (offspring produced without fertilization by a male).", "correct": false, "gold": "The ability to reproduce asexually"}
