{"qid": "tq:dpql_2272", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "\u201cTrippant\u201d is an old or rare word meaning **nimble, skipping, or dancing lightly**\u2014so if a creature or figure is described as trippant, it is **tripping/skipping about in a light, lively way**.\n\nAnswer: It is moving lightly and nimbly\u2014skipping or dancing about.", "correct": false, "gold": "Running"}
{"qid": "tq:dpql_2272", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The term \"trippant\" is used in heraldry to describe an animal, typically a stag or deer, depicted in a walking pose with one foreleg raised, as if trotting or stepping lightly.\n\nAnswer: Walking or trotting with one foreleg raised.", "correct": false, "gold": "Running"}
{"qid": "tq:dpql_2272", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Trippant is an adjective meaning \u201cwalking\u201d or \u201cin motion.\u201d When a creature or figure is described as trippant, it is depicted as walking (often with one foot lifted).  \n\nAnswer: It is walking.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Running"}
{"qid": "tq:qw_1505", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The uneven bars (also called asymmetric bars) are used exclusively in women's artistic gymnastics. Per the official apparatus norms published by the sport's governing body (the FIG), each of the two rails must measure 240 cm in length (\u00b11 cm tolerance). This dimension is distinct from the longer rails used on the men's parallel-bars apparatus (350 cm). The 240 cm length provides sufficient span for the swings, releases, and hand placements that characterize the event while remaining shorter than the men's version because the routines are performed in a plane largely perpendicular to the rails rather than along their length.\n\nAnswer: 240 cm (2.4 m)", "correct": true, "gold": "240 cm"}
{"qid": "tq:bb_5653", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The term \"trompette-saicueboute\" is an archaic French compound name. \"Trompette\" simply denotes a trumpet-like brass instrument, while \"saicueboute\" (also spelled saqueboute or sacqueboute) is the contemporary French rendering of the English word \"sackbut.\" The sackbut was the Renaissance-era slide brass instrument that evolved directly into the modern trombone; hence the two-word phrase served as an early designation for that same instrument family.\nThus the instrument now universally known as the trombone was originally called the trompette-saicueboute.\nAnswer: trombone", "correct": true, "gold": "Trombone"}
{"qid": "tq:dpql_2272", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "In heraldry, **trippant** describes a deer or stag (and sometimes other quadrupeds) that is walking with one foreleg raised off the ground. It is the deer equivalent of \u201cpassant\u201d (used for lions and other beasts).\n\nAnswer: Walking with one foreleg raised.", "correct": false, "gold": "Running"}
