{"qid": "tq:sfq_610", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "Posset is a traditional hot drink dating back to at least the 14th century in Britain and parts of Europe. It is made by gently heating milk (sometimes with cream or sugar) until it is near boiling, then adding an acidic or alcoholic liquid such as wine, ale, or sack. The acidity and alcohol cause the milk to curdle, separating into soft curds and a spiced whey. The mixture is then flavored with spices such as nutmeg, cinnamon, ginger, or mace and often strained or served in a special two-handled posset pot. Contemporary recipe collections (e.g., the Forme of Cury and later Tudor household manuals) describe exactly this process, confirming that posset was both a festive beverage and a common medicinal remedy for colds. Other period drinks (syllabub, caudle) share some ingredients but differ in preparation or era; only posset matches the described curdling technique and medieval popularity.\nAnswer: posset", "correct": true, "gold": "A POSSET"}
{"qid": "tq:sfq_610", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The drink is **posset** (also known as sack posset when made with sherry/sack). This medieval beverage was prepared by pouring hot milk into wine or ale, causing the milk to curdle and separate. Spices such as nutmeg, cinnamon, and ginger were then added. It was consumed as a remedy for colds, a digestive aid, and a festive treat, with the liquid whey drunk through the curdled layer on top.\n\nAnswer: Posset", "correct": true, "gold": "A POSSET"}
