{"qid": "tq:qw_14413", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The mill referenced in the query is the well-documented Woodbridge Tide Mill (also called Woodbridge Tidal Mill) on the River Deben in Suffolk. Historical records place a mill at the same site as early as 1170; the present timber-framed structure was erected in 1792\u20131793 on the medieval foundations. After falling out of regular use in the mid-20th century it was restored in 2010 and is now maintained in full working order, producing flour by means of its original machinery.\nBecause the mill is driven by impounded tidal water rather than by a river or by wind, it constitutes one of the earliest securely dated English examples of a tide mill. Its power source is therefore the rise and fall of the tide.\nAnswer: the tide (tidal water)", "correct": true, "gold": "Tide"}
