{"qid": "tq:odql_11414", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**1907: The Year of *The Playboy of the Western World*** \r\n\r\n1907 was the most infamous and pivotal year in John Millington Synge's life, defined almost entirely by the controversy surrounding his masterpiece. \r\n\r\n- **26 January 1907**: Synge's play *The Playboy of the Western World* premiered at the Abbey Theatre in Dublin. Set in County Mayo, it follows Christy Mahon, a young man who gains admiration after claiming to have killed his father. \r\n- **Opening night riots**: The play's frank depictions of violence, sexuality, and its satirical take on rural Irish idealism sparked immediate outrage. Protesters, including nationalist groups, booed, hissed, and clashed with supporters, leading to riots that continued for several nights.\r\n- **Defiant response**: Despite intense backlash, Synge refused to censor his work, defending it as a truthful portrayal of Irish life. He famously dismissed critics, stating he wrote for those who understood the reality of the West of Ireland, not for those clinging to romantic stereotypes.\r\n- **Lasting legacy**: Though controversial at the time, *The Playboy of the Western World* would go on to be recognised as one of the greatest works of Irish drama, solidifying Synge's place alongside W.B. Yeats and Lady Gregory in the Abbey Theatre's legacy. \r\n\r\nAnswer: 1907, the year Synge's *The Playboy of the Western World* premiered and sparked the infamous Abbey Theatre riots.", "correct": false, "gold": "1871-1909"}
