{"qid": "tq:jp_4324", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The furthest square from Go in Monopoly is **Illinois Avenue**.  \n\nIn a standard Monopoly board, there are 40 spaces. Go is at position 0. The maximum distance around the board is 20 spaces in either direction (since the board is a loop). Illinois Avenue is at position 24, which is 24 spaces forward or 16 spaces backward from Go. The farthest possible distance is 20 spaces, and Illinois Avenue is the only property exactly 20 spaces from Go when measured the shorter way (from Go to Illinois Avenue going backwards is 16 spaces, but going forward is 24; the shorter distance is min(24, 16) = 16, not 20).  \n\nActually, the correct answer is **B&O Railroad** (position 25) or **Illinois Avenue** (position 24)? Let's check: The farthest square in terms of shortest path distance is 20 spaces away. The squares exactly opposite Go (20 spaces away in either direction) are **Go to Jail** (space 30? No, that's 10 from Go). Wait\u2014the board has 40 spaces, so the square directly opposite Go is 20 spaces away. That square is **Free Parking** (position 20). But Free Parking is a corner, not a property. If we consider all squares, Free Parking is furthest. However, the question likely means a property square (colored property or railroad/utility). Among properties, the ones 20 spaces away are **Illinois Avenue** (position 24? No, 24 is 4 past 20) and **B&O Railroad** (position 25). Actually, let's list: Go=0, then 1-39. The opposite point is 20. So squares at 20 (Free Parking) and also 20 spaces the other way is 20 as well. So the maximum shortest distance is 20. Which properties are exactly 20 away? None, because 20 is Free Parking. The next closest are at 19 and 21 (19: Kentucky Avenue? 21: Indiana Avenue? Actually, positions: 19 is Kentucky Avenue, 21 is Indiana Avenue). Those are 19 and 21 steps away, so shortest distance is min(19,21)=19? No, from Go to 21: forward 21, backward 19, so shortest is 19. So the maximum shortest distance among properties is 19? Let's check: For position 1 (Mediterranean), shortest distance is 1. For position 39 (Boardwalk), shortest is 1 (backward). So the maximum shortest distance is 20 for Free Parking, but for properties, the maximum is 19 (Kentucky at 19 and Indiana at 21 both have shortest distance 19). Also positions 18 (Illinois? No, Illinois is 24). Let's systematically find the property with the largest minimum of (position, 40-position). That maximum is 20, but only at position 20 (Free Parking, not a property). So the property with the largest minimum is at positions 19 and 21 (min=19). So the furthest property squares are Kentucky Avenue (19) and Indiana Avenue (21). But the question says \"Monopoly square\", which includes all squares. So the furthest square from Go is **Free Parking** (20 spaces away). However, many people consider \"furthest\" as the longest path around the board, which would be 39 spaces away (Boardwalk or Mediterranean), but that's not the usual interpretation\u2014\"furthest\" typically means the greatest distance along the board in either direction, taking the shorter route. In Monopoly, the square farthest from Go (by shortest path) is Free Parking.  \n\nGiven common trivia, the answer often given is **Illinois Avenue** because it is 24 spaces forward and 16 backward, so the shorter distance is 16, not the farthest. Actually, I recall a common question: \"What is the farthest property from Go?\" and the answer is Illinois Avenue because it is the only property that is exactly 24 spaces from Go (the maximum possible distance in a straight line if you don't allow going backwards? No). Let's double-check: The board is circular. The distance between two spaces is the minimum number of steps clockwise or counterclockwise. The maximum possible minimum distance is 20. So no property is 20 away; the maximum for properties is 19. But Illinois Avenue is at 24, so min(24,16)=16, which is less than 19. So Illinois is not the farthest.  \n\nWait\u2014I think I've seen this before: The square furthest from Go is actually **Go to Jail**? No. Let's calculate all:  \n- Go: 0  \n- Mediterranean: 1 (min=1)  \n- Community Chest: 2 (min=2)  \n- Baltic: 3 (min=3)  \n- Income Tax: 4 (min=4)  \n- Reading Railroad: 5 (min=5)  \n- Oriental: 6 (min=6)  \n- Chance: 7 (min=7)  \n- Vermont: 8 (min=8)  \n- Connecticut: 9 (min=9)  \n- Jail/Just Visiting: 10 (min=10)  \n- St. Charles: 11 (min=11)  \n- Electric Company: 12 (min=12)  \n- States: 13 (min=13)  \n- Virginia: 14 (min=14)  \n- Pennsylvania Railroad: 15 (min=15)  \n- St. James: 16 (min=16)  \n- Community Chest: 17 (min=17)  \n- Tennessee: 18 (min=18)  \n- New York: 19 (min=19)  \n- Free Parking: 20 (min=20)  \n- Kentucky: 21 (min=19)  \n- Chance: 22 (min=18)  \n- Indiana: 23 (min=17)  \n- Illinois: 24 (min=16)  \n- B&O Railroad: 25 (min=15)  \n- Atlantic: 26 (min=14)  \n- Ventnor: 27 (min=13)  \n- Water Works: 28 (min=12)  \n- Marvin Gardens: 29 (min=11)  \n- Go to Jail: 30 (min=10)  \n- Pacific: 31 (min=9)  \n- North Carolina: 32 (min=8)  \n- Community Chest: 33 (min=7)  \n- Pennsylvania: 34 (min=6)  \n- Short Line: 35 (min=5)  \n- Chance: 36 (min=4)  \n- Park Place: 37 (min=3)  \n- Luxury Tax: 38 (min=2)  \n- Boardwalk: 39 (min=1)  \n\nSo the maximum min distance is 20 at Free Parking. Thus, the furthest square from Go is **Free Parking**.  \n\nAnswer: Free Parking", "correct": true, "gold": "Free Parking"}
