{"qid": "tq:dpql_5647", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The RMS Queen Elizabeth was a famous Cunard liner that was retired from service in 1968. After an unsuccessful stint as a floating hotel attraction in Port Everglades, Florida, the vessel was sold to Hong Kong businessman C. Y. Tung. It was towed to Hong Kong in 1971 to be refitted as the floating university \u201cSeawise University.\u201d On 9 January 1972 a series of arson fires broke out aboard the ship while it lay at anchor in Victoria Harbour; the blaze gutted the vessel and it later capsized. Thus the city whose harbour witnessed the destruction was Hong Kong.\nAnswer: Hong Kong", "correct": true, "gold": "Hong Kong"}
{"qid": "tq:sfq_17649", "domain": "triviaqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The five docks were the Pomona Docks (numbered 1\u20135), named after **Pomona**, the Roman goddess of fruit and abundance. The Pomona Metrolink stop serves the area today.\n\nAnswer: Pomona", "correct": true, "gold": "Pomona"}
