{"qid": "gsm:4", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The problem involves a constant buildup rate of 20 snowballs per hour combined with a constant melting loss of 2 snowballs every 15 minutes (equivalent to a loss of 8 per hour). This produces a net accumulation rate of 12 snowballs per hour.\nDividing the target quantity by the net rate immediately gives the required time:\n\\[\n\\frac{60}{12} = 5\n\\]\nhours.\nTo confirm the constant-loss assumption never produces a negative stock, the same process can be examined in 15-minute increments. In each increment Jay produces 5 snowballs and loses 2, for a net gain of 3. After exactly 20 such increments the stock reaches 60, which is again 5 hours. Because the stock is always greater than 2 after the first increment, the fixed melting quantity remains valid throughout.\nAnswer: 5 hours", "correct": true, "gold": "5"}
