{"qid": "gsm:7", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "Let's break it down step by step.\n\n- Each horse eats 5 pounds per meal.  \n- There are 4 horses, so per meal: \\(4 \\times 5 = 20\\) pounds.  \n- He feeds them twice a day, so per day: \\(20 \\times 2 = 40\\) pounds.  \n- For five days: \\(40 \\times 5 = 200\\) pounds.  \n- Each bag contains 50 pounds, so number of bags: \\(200 \\div 50 = 4\\).\n\nAnswer: 4", "correct": true, "gold": "4"}
{"qid": "gsm:5", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Let $r$ be the number of rats in each cage.\n\n**Hamsters:**  \nThere are 10 cages with 1 hamster each (kept alone), so 10 hamsters.  \nEach gets 5 pieces of straw: $10 \\times 5 = 50$ pieces.\n\n**Rabbits:**  \nThe pen of rabbits receives 20 pieces of straw.\n\n**Rats:**  \nThere are 3 cages with $r$ rats each, so $3r$ rats total.  \nEach rat gets 6 pieces of straw: $3r \\times 6 = 18r$ pieces.\n\n**Total:**  \nThe problem states 160 pieces were distributed. Assuming this total includes the rabbits (or that rabbits are counted among the \"small rodents\" for this problem), we set up the equation:\n\n$$18r + 50 + 20 = 160$$\n\n$$18r + 70 = 160$$\n\n$$18r = 90$$\n\n$$r = 5$$\n\nThus, there are 5 rats in each cage.\n\nAnswer: 5", "correct": true, "gold": "5"}
{"qid": "gsm:7", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "First, calculate the daily consumption per horse: 5 pounds per meal \u00d7 2 meals per day = 10 pounds per horse per day.\n\nNext, calculate the total daily consumption for all four horses: 10 pounds \u00d7 4 horses = 40 pounds per day.\n\nThen, calculate the total consumption for five days: 40 pounds per day \u00d7 5 days = 200 pounds.\n\nFinally, determine how many 50-pound bags are needed: 200 pounds \u00f7 50 pounds per bag = 4 bags.\n\nAnswer: 4", "correct": true, "gold": "4"}
{"qid": "gsm:8", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "If Digimon\u2019s 20th anniversary means it came out 20 years ago, then:\n\n- John is 28 now, so when Digimon came out he was **28 - 20 = 8**.\n- At that time, John was twice as old as Jim, so Jim was **8 \u00f7 2 = 4**.\n- Jim is now **4 + 20 = 24**.\n\nAnswer: 24", "correct": true, "gold": "24"}
{"qid": "gsm:6", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:** \r\n\r\n1. **Monday:** Meredith wrote 5 articles.\r\n2. **Tuesday:** She wrote 2/5 times more articles than on Monday. This means she wrote an additional 2/5 of Monday's total: (2/5) \u00d7 5 = 2, so 5 + 2 = 7 articles.\r\n3. **Wednesday:** She wrote twice the number of articles as Tuesday: 2 \u00d7 7 = 14 articles.\r\n4. **Total articles:** 5 (Mon) + 7 (Tue) + 14 (Wed) = 26 articles.\r\n5. **Total hours:** Each article takes 4 hours, so 26 \u00d7 4 = 104 hours. \r\n\r\nAnswer: 104 hours", "correct": true, "gold": "104"}
