{"qid": "gsm:13", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "For a fair six-sided die:\n\n- Probability of rolling a number greater than 3: outcomes {4, 5, 6}  \n  \\[\n  \\frac{3}{6}=\\frac{1}{2}=50\\%\n  \\]\n\n- Probability of rolling two even numbers in a row: even outcomes are {2, 4, 6}, so each roll has probability  \n  \\[\n  \\frac{3}{6}=\\frac{1}{2}\n  \\]\n  Two in a row:\n  \\[\n  \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{4}=25\\%\n  \\]\n\nDifference:\n\\[\n50\\%-25\\%=25\\%\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 25%", "correct": true, "gold": "25"}
{"qid": "gsm:12", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:** \r\n\r\n1. George has 18 pens.\r\n2. Timothy has three times as many as George: `3 \u00d7 18 = 54` pens.\r\n3. Sarah has half as many as Timothy: `54 \u00f7 2 = 27` pens.\r\n\r\nAnswer: 27", "correct": true, "gold": "27"}
{"qid": "gsm:13", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Answer: 25%**\n\n- P(rolling greater than 3) = P(4, 5, or 6) = 3/6 = 1/2 = 50%\n- P(rolling two even numbers in a row) = (1/2) \u00d7 (1/2) = 1/4 = 25%\n\nDifference = 50% \u2212 25% = **25%**\n\nAnswer: 25%", "correct": true, "gold": "25"}
{"qid": "gsm:13", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "First, find the probability of rolling a number greater than 3 on a six-sided die.  \nNumbers greater than 3: 4, 5, 6 \u2192 3 outcomes.  \nProbability = 3/6 = 1/2.\n\nNext, probability of rolling two even numbers in a row.  \nEven numbers: 2, 4, 6 \u2192 3 out of 6 = 1/2 each roll.  \nProbability of two in a row = (1/2) \u00d7 (1/2) = 1/4.\n\nNow, compare how much more likely the first event is.  \nDifference in probability = 1/2 \u2212 1/4 = 1/4.  \nTo express as a percentage relative to the second probability:  \n(1/4) / (1/4) \u00d7 100% = 100% more likely.  \nAlternatively, if the question means absolute difference as a percentage: 1/4 = 25%.  \nBut \"how much more likely\" usually means relative increase:  \n(0.5 \u2212 0.25) / 0.25 = 1 = 100%.\n\nAnswer: 100%", "correct": false, "gold": "25"}
{"qid": "gsm:10", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "First, convert all measurements to the same unit. The flower width is 12 inches = 1 foot, and the spacing between plants is 1.5 feet. \n\nNext, determine how many plants can fit in the 111-foot flower bed. Since Pat is placing plants in a row with 1.5 feet between each one, the total length is: \n\n`Total length = (width of one plant \u00d7 number of plants) + (spacing \u00d7 (number of plants - 1))`\n\nPlug in the values: \n\n`111 = (1 \u00d7 n) + (1.5 \u00d7 (n - 1))`\n\nSimplify: \n\n`111 = n + 1.5n - 1.5`\n`111 = 2.5n - 1.5`\n`112.5 = 2.5n`\n`n = 45`\n\nSo, the flower bed can hold a total of 45 plants. \n\nPat already owns 17 plants, so she needs: `45 - 17 = 28` more plants. \n\nEach plant costs $6, so the total cost is: `28 \u00d7 $6 = $168`. \n\nAnswer: $168", "correct": false, "gold": "342"}
{"qid": "gsm:14", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Ophelia has 20 sofas, and since she has 2 fewer sofas than chairs, she has:\n\n- Chairs = 20 + 2 = 22\n\nJenna has 3 times as many chairs as Ophelia:\n\n- Jenna\u2019s chairs = 3 \u00d7 22 = 66\n\nJenna also has 2 fewer sofas than chairs:\n\n- Jenna\u2019s sofas = 66 - 2 = 64\n\nNow add all sofas and chairs:\n\n- Ophelia: 20 sofas + 22 chairs = 42\n- Jenna: 64 sofas + 66 chairs = 130\n\nTotal = 42 + 130 = 172\n\nAnswer: 172", "correct": true, "gold": "172"}
{"qid": "gsm:10", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "First, convert the flower width to feet: 12 inches = 1 foot.\n\nTo find how many plants fit in 111 feet:\n- Each plant occupies 1 foot of width.\n- There must be 1.5 feet of space between each pair of plants.\n- For $n$ plants, there are $(n-1)$ gaps.\n\nThe total length equation is:\n$$n(1) + (n-1)(1.5) = 111$$\n$$n + 1.5n - 1.5 = 111$$\n$$2.5n = 112.5$$\n$$n = 45$$\n\nPat needs 45 plants total. She already owns 17, so she must buy:\n$$45 - 17 = 28\\text{ plants}$$\n\nAt \\$6 per plant:\n$$28 \\times \\$6 = \\$168$$\n\nAnswer: $168", "correct": false, "gold": "342"}
{"qid": "gsm:14", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "Let's define variables.\n\nLet \\( O_c \\) = number of chairs Ophelia has.  \nOphelia has 2 fewer sofas than chairs, so her sofas = \\( O_c - 2 \\).  \nWe are told Ophelia has 20 sofas:  \n\\[\nO_c - 2 = 20 \\implies O_c = 22\n\\]\n\nJenna has 3 times as many chairs as Ophelia:  \nJenna's chairs = \\( 3 \\times 22 = 66 \\).  \n\nJenna also has 2 fewer sofas than chairs:  \nJenna's sofas = \\( 66 - 2 = 64 \\).\n\nNow total sofas: Ophelia 20 + Jenna 64 = 84.  \nTotal chairs: Ophelia 22 + Jenna 66 = 88.  \n\nTotal sofas and chairs = \\( 84 + 88 = 172 \\).\n\nAnswer: 172", "correct": true, "gold": "172"}
{"qid": "gsm:14", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Ophelia:**\n- Sofas = 20\n- Chairs = Sofas + 2 = 20 + 2 = 22\n\n**Jenna:**\n- Chairs = 3 \u00d7 Ophelia's chairs = 3 \u00d7 22 = 66\n- Sofas = Chairs \u2212 2 = 66 \u2212 2 = 64\n\n**Total:**\n20 + 22 + 64 + 66 = 172\n\nAnswer: 172", "correct": true, "gold": "172"}
{"qid": "gsm:13", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The probability of rolling a number greater than 3 is \\( \\frac{3}{6}=0.5 \\) (50%).  \nThe probability of rolling two even numbers in a row is \\( \\left(\\frac{3}{6}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{4}=0.25 \\) (25%).  \n\nEvent A is twice as likely as event B, which means it is \\( \\frac{0.5-0.25}{0.25}=1 \\) or 100% more likely.\n\nAnswer: 100%<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "25"}
{"qid": "gsm:14", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "Ophelia has 20 sofas, and each person has 2 fewer sofas than chairs. This gives Ophelia 22 chairs.  \nJenna has 3 times as many chairs as Ophelia, which is 66 chairs, and therefore 64 sofas.  \nAdding all items yields a combined total of 172.  \n\nAnswer: 172", "correct": true, "gold": "172"}
{"qid": "gsm:15", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Let last year\u2019s number of cookies be \\(x\\).\n\nThis year, he wanted to make twice as many, so he meant to bake:\n\\[\n2x\n\\]\n\nBut he actually baked 15 more than that:\n\\[\n2x + 15\n\\]\n\nThen he dropped 5 cookies, leaving him with 110:\n\\[\n2x + 15 - 5 = 110\n\\]\n\nSimplify:\n\\[\n2x + 10 = 110\n\\]\n\\[\n2x = 100\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 50\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 50", "correct": true, "gold": "50"}
{"qid": "gsm:14", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>First, determine the number of chairs Ophelia has. Since she has 2 fewer sofas than chairs, her chairs equal her sofas plus 2:\n\n- Ophelia\u2019s sofas = 20  \n- Ophelia\u2019s chairs = 20\u202f+\u202f2 = 22  \n\nJenna has three times as many chairs as Ophelia:\n\n- Jenna\u2019s chairs = 3\u202f\u00d7\u202f22 = 66  \n\nJenna also has 2 fewer sofas than chairs, so her sofas equal her chairs minus 2:\n\n- Jenna\u2019s sofas = 66\u202f\u2212\u202f2 = 64  \n\nNow sum the sofas and chairs for both:\n\n- Total sofas = 20\u202f+\u202f64 = 84  \n- Total chairs = 22\u202f+\u202f66 = 88  \n\nCombined total furniture = 84\u202f+\u202f88 = 172.\n\nAnswer: 172<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "172"}
{"qid": "gsm:15", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "He baked **50 cookies** last year.\n\nCheck: 2\u00d750=100, +15=115, -5=110 \u2713\n\nAnswer: 50", "correct": true, "gold": "50"}
{"qid": "gsm:14", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Ophelia has 20 sofas. Since she has 2 fewer sofas than chairs, she has $20 + 2 = 22$ chairs.\n\nJenna has 3 times as many chairs as Ophelia, so Jenna has $3 \\times 22 = 66$ chairs.\n\nSince Jenna also has 2 fewer sofas than chairs, she has $66 - 2 = 64$ sofas.\n\nTotal sofas: $20 + 64 = 84$\nTotal chairs: $22 + 66 = 88$\n\nCombined total: $84 + 88 = 172$\n\nAnswer: 172", "correct": true, "gold": "172"}
{"qid": "gsm:15", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "Let\u2019s define \\( x \\) as the number of cookies Henry baked last year.  \n\nHe wants to make twice as many: \\( 2x \\).  \nHe actually baked 15 more than that: \\( 2x + 15 \\).  \nHe drops 5, so he has: \\( 2x + 15 - 5 = 2x + 10 \\).  \nWe know this equals 110:  \n\n\\[\n2x + 10 = 110\n\\]  \n\\[\n2x = 100\n\\]  \n\\[\nx = 50\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 50", "correct": true, "gold": "50"}
{"qid": "gsm:15", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "Let \\( x \\) be the number of cookies Henry baked last year.  \nHe intends to bake twice as many, or \\( 2x \\).  \nHe actually bakes 15 more than intended, for a total of \\( 2x + 15 \\).  \nAfter dropping 5 cookies he has \\( (2x + 15) - 5 = 110 \\).  \nSimplifying gives \\( 2x + 10 = 110 \\), so \\( 2x = 100 \\) and \\( x = 50 \\).  \n\n(Equivalently: 110 cookies remain after the drop, so 115 were baked; subtracting the extra 15 yields the intended 100, which is twice last year\u2019s total.)  \n\nAnswer: 50", "correct": true, "gold": "50"}
{"qid": "gsm:15", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Let \\(L\\) be the number of cookies Henry baked last year.  \nHe intended to bake twice that amount: \\(2L\\).  \nHe actually baked 15 more than intended, so he baked \\(2L + 15\\) cookies.  \nAfter dropping 5 cookies, he had \\(2L + 15 - 5 = 110\\) cookies left.  \n\nSolve for \\(L\\):\n\\[\n2L + 10 = 110 \\\\\n2L = 100 \\\\\nL = 50\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 50<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "50"}
{"qid": "gsm:15", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Let $x$ be the number of cookies Henry baked last year.\n\nHe wanted to bake twice as many as last year, so he intended to bake $2x$ cookies.\n\nHe actually baked 15 more than intended: $2x + 15$ cookies.\n\nAfter dropping 5 cookies, he has: $(2x + 15) - 5 = 2x + 10$ cookies.\n\nThis equals 110:\n$$2x + 10 = 110$$\n$$2x = 100$$\n$$x = 50$$\n\nVerification: Last year he baked 50. This year he wanted 100, baked 115, dropped 5, leaving 110. \u2713\n\nAnswer: 50", "correct": true, "gold": "50"}
