{"qid": "gsm:13", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "A fair six-sided die has faces {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}.\nThe event \u201crolls a number greater than 3\u201d occurs for the three faces {4, 5, 6}, so its probability on a single roll is exactly 3/6 = 1/2.\nThe event \u201crolls two even numbers in a row\u201d requires two independent rolls. There are three even faces {2, 4, 6}, therefore the probability of an even outcome on any given roll is also 3/6 = 1/2. The joint probability for two successive evens is therefore (1/2) \u00d7 (1/2) = 1/4.\nThe first probability is twice the second:\n(1/2) / (1/4) = 2.\nBeing twice as large means the first probability is larger by a factor of one, which is an increase of 100 percent.\nThus the probability of rolling a number greater than 3 is 100 percent greater than the probability of rolling two even numbers in succession.\nAnswer: 100%", "correct": false, "gold": "25"}
