{"qid": "gsm:55", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>First, convert all amounts to cents to avoid decimals.  \n- $5.00 = 500 cents.  \n- 1% of $5.00 is $0.05 = 5 cents, so George spent 99% of his money: 0.99\u202f\u00d7\u202f500\u202f=\u202f495 cents.  \n\nHe bought 3 bags of chips at 40 cents each: 3\u202f\u00d7\u202f40\u202f=\u202f120 cents.  \nLet \\(c\\) be the number of candy bars he purchased. Each candy bar costs 75 cents, so the total cost is  \n\n\\(120 + 75c = 495\\).\n\nSolving for \\(c\\):  \n\\(75c = 495 - 120 = 375\\) \u2192 \\(c = 375 \u00f7 75 = 5\\).\n\nThus, George bought 5 candy bars.\n\nAnswer: 5<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "5"}
{"qid": "gsm:55", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "Let\u2019s break it down step by step.\n\n- George spent $5.00 total.\n- He had 1% of his money left after spending, meaning $5.00 is 99% of his original money.\n- So original money = \\( \\frac{5.00}{0.99} \\approx 5.0505 \\) dollars, or 505.05 cents.\n- 1% left = \\( 0.01 \\times 505.05 \\approx 5.05 \\) cents left. That seems odd in real terms, but mathematically it works.\n\nNow, chips cost 40 cents each, candy bars 75 cents each.\n- He bought 3 bags of chips: \\( 3 \\times 40 = 120 \\) cents.\n- Let \\( c \\) = number of candy bars.\n- Total spent: \\( 120 + 75c \\) cents = 500 cents (since $5 = 500 cents).\n\nSo:\n\\[\n120 + 75c = 500\n\\]\n\\[\n75c = 380\n\\]\n\\[\nc = \\frac{380}{75} = \\frac{76}{15} \\approx 5.0667\n\\]\n\nThis is not an integer, so something is off. The \"1% of his money left\" likely means he had 1% of his original money left *after* buying chips and candy bars, and the $5 spent is not the total spent but the amount spent on chips and candy bars combined.\n\nLet original money = \\( M \\).\n- He spent $5 = 500 cents.\n- Left with 1% of M, so spent = 99% of M.\n- \\( 0.99M = 500 \\) \u2192 \\( M = \\frac{500}{0.99} \\approx 505.0505 \\) cents.\n- Left = \\( 0.01M \\approx 5.0505 \\) cents.\n\nNow, 3 chips = 120 cents.\nSpent on candy bars = \\( 500 - 120 = 380 \\) cents.\nNumber of candy bars = \\( 380 / 75 = 5.066... \\) \u2014 still not integer.\n\nThus, maybe the $5 is what he had initially, not what he spent. Let\u2019s try that:\n- Initial = $5 = 500 cents.\n- Left 1% = 5 cents.\n- Spent = 495 cents.\n- 3 chips = 120 cents.\n- Spent on candy bars = 495 - 120 = 375 cents.\n- Candy bars = \\( 375 / 75 = 5 \\).\n\nThat works perfectly.\n\nAnswer: 5", "correct": true, "gold": "5"}
{"qid": "gsm:56", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "Let's break it down:\n\n- Martha starts collecting at age 5.\n- She collects 1 shell per month.\n- From her 5th birthday to her 10th birthday is 5 years.\n- 5 years \u00d7 12 months per year = 60 months.\n- 60 months \u00d7 1 shell per month = 60 shells.\n\nAnswer: 60", "correct": true, "gold": "60"}
{"qid": "gsm:56", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Martha collects shells for the period between her 5th and 10th birthdays, which is 5 years. Since she collects one shell per month and there are 12 months in a year:\n\n5 years \u00d7 12 months/year = 60 months\n\nTherefore, she collects 60 shells.\n\nAnswer: 60", "correct": true, "gold": "60"}
