{"qid": "gsm:59", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We start with the daytime temperature: 45 degrees.  \nAt night, it is 33 degrees colder, so the temperature drops to  \n45 - 33 = 12 degrees.  \n\nThe pipes freeze if the temperature falls below 32 degrees.  \nThe current temperature is 12 degrees, so we need to raise it to at least 32 degrees.  \nThat requires an increase of 32 - 12 = 20 degrees.  \n\nEach log raises the temperature by 5 degrees, so the number of logs needed is  \n20 \u00f7 5 = 4 logs.  \n\nAnswer: 4", "correct": true, "gold": "4"}
{"qid": "gsm:58", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Assuming the mixture reaches a weighted average temperature with no heat loss: \r\n\r\nTotal volume = 4 + 1 = 5 ounces. \r\nMixture temperature = (4 \u00d7 33 + 1 \u00d7 T) / 5 \u2265 40, where T is the unknown temperature. \r\n\r\nSolve: \r\n(132 + T) / 5 \u2265 40 \u2192 132 + T \u2265 200 \u2192 T \u2265 68. \r\n\r\nAnswer: 68 degrees", "correct": true, "gold": "68"}
{"qid": "gsm:59", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>To keep the pipes from freezing, the nighttime temperature must be at least 32\u00b0F.  \n\n- Daytime temperature: 45\u00b0F  \n- Nighttime temperature: 45\u00b0F \u2212 33\u00b0F = 12\u00b0F  \n\nRequired temperature increase: 32\u00b0F \u2212 12\u00b0F = 20\u00b0F  \n\nEach log raises the temperature by 5\u00b0F, so the number of logs needed is:  \n20\u00b0F \u00f7 5\u00b0F per log = 4 logs  \n\nAnswer: 4<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "4"}
{"qid": "gsm:55", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The problem is solved by working in cents for precision. George inserted 500 cents (equivalent to $5). After his purchases, he had 1% of that amount left as change, meaning he spent exactly 495 cents total.\nHe bought 3 bags of chips at 40 cents each, for a subtotal of 120 cents. Let \\(x\\) be the number of candy bars purchased at 75 cents each. This produces the equation\n\\[\n120 + 75x = 495.\n\\]\nSubtracting the chip cost yields\n\\[\n75x = 375 \\implies x = 5.\n\\]\nVerification: \\(5 \\times 75 = 375\\), and \\(120 + 375 = 495\\), leaving exactly 5 cents (1% of the original 500 cents).\nAnswer: 5", "correct": true, "gold": "5"}
