{"qid": "gsm:90", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "Let\u2019s break it down step by step.\n\n- At 8:00, there were 5000 people.\n- By midday, \\(\\frac{2}{5}\\) had voted.  \n  Number who voted by midday:  \n  \\[\n  \\frac{2}{5} \\times 5000 = 2000\n  \\]  \n  Remaining after midday:  \n  \\[\n  5000 - 2000 = 3000\n  \\]\n\n- By 16:00, \\(\\frac{2}{3}\\) of the remaining 3000 had voted.  \n  Number who voted between midday and 16:00:  \n  \\[\n  \\frac{2}{3} \\times 3000 = 2000\n  \\]  \n  Remaining after 16:00:  \n  \\[\n  3000 - 2000 = 1000\n  \\]\n\nSo, the number who had not voted by 16:00 is 1000.\n\nAnswer: 1000", "correct": true, "gold": "1000"}
{"qid": "gsm:89", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The total amount of apples is 12 kg.\n\nPeter (Pierre) takes: $\\frac{1}{4} \\times 12 = 3$ kg  \nPaul takes: $\\frac{1}{3} \\times 12 = 4$ kg  \n\nTogether they take: $3 + 4 = 7$ kg  \n\nJames (Jacques) will have left: $12 - 7 = 5$ kg  \n\nAlternatively, calculating the remaining fraction: $1 - \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{12}{12} - \\frac{3}{12} - \\frac{4}{12} = \\frac{5}{12}$, and $\\frac{5}{12} \\times 12 = 5$ kg.\n\nAnswer: 5 kg (or 5 kilograms)", "correct": true, "gold": "5"}
{"qid": "gsm:90", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "At 8:00 there are 5000 people.\n\nBy midday, $\\frac{2}{5}$ had voted:\n$$\\frac{2}{5} \\times 5000 = 2000 \\text{ voted}$$\n$$5000 - 2000 = 3000 \\text{ remaining}$$\n\nBy 16:00, $\\frac{2}{3}$ of the remaining 3000 had voted:\n$$\\frac{2}{3} \\times 3000 = 2000 \\text{ voted}$$\n\nThis leaves:\n$$3000 - 2000 = 1000 \\text{ people who had not voted}$$\n\nAlternatively, if $\\frac{2}{3}$ of the remaining voted, then $\\frac{1}{3}$ had not voted:\n$$\\frac{1}{3} \\times 3000 = 1000$$\n\nAnswer: 1000", "correct": true, "gold": "1000"}
{"qid": "gsm:89", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>To find the amount left for James:\n\n- Total apples: 12\u202fkg  \n- Peter takes a quarter: \\( \\frac{1}{4} \\times 12 = 3 \\)\u202fkg  \n- Paul takes a third: \\( \\frac{1}{3} \\times 12 = 4 \\)\u202fkg  \n\nTotal taken by Peter and Paul: \\(3 + 4 = 7\\)\u202fkg  \n\nRemaining apples: \\(12 - 7 = 5\\)\u202fkg  \n\nAnswer: 5 kilograms.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "5"}
