{"qid": "gsm:114", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>First, determine Dana\u2019s walking speed.  \n- Skip speed = 3\u202fmph.  \n- Run speed is twice the skip speed (since skipping is half as fast as running):  \n  \\( \\text{Run speed} = 2 \\times 3 = 6\\)\u202fmph.  \n- She runs four times faster than she walks, so her walking speed is:  \n  \\( \\text{Walk speed} = \\frac{\\text{Run speed}}{4} = \\frac{6}{4} = 1.5\\)\u202fmph.\n\nNext, calculate the time spent running and walking over six hours.  \n- Running time = \\(\\frac{1}{3} \\times 6 = 2\\)\u202fhours.  \n- Walking time = \\(\\frac{2}{3} \\times 6 = 4\\)\u202fhours.\n\nNow compute the distances covered during each activity.  \n- Distance running = \\(6\\ \\text{mph} \\times 2\\ \\text{h} = 12\\)\u202fmiles.  \n- Distance walking = \\(1.5\\ \\text{mph} \\times 4\\ \\text{h} = 6\\)\u202fmiles.\n\nTotal distance traveled = \\(12 + 6 = 18\\)\u202fmiles.\n\nAnswer: 18<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "18"}
