{"qid": "gsm:171", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The sandcastle\u2019s levels double in area from top to bottom.  \n- Top level: \\(16\\) sq ft  \n- 2nd level: \\(16 \\times 2 = 32\\) sq ft  \n- 3rd level: \\(32 \\times 2 = 64\\) sq ft  \n- Bottom level: \\(64 \\times 2 = 128\\) sq ft  \n\nTotal area \\(= 16 + 32 + 64 + 128 = 240\\) sq ft.  \nAverage area per level \\(= \\frac{240}{4} = 60\\) sq ft.\n\nAnswer: 60<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "60"}
{"qid": "gsm:172", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Marcus avoids turning in the homework if any of these happen:\n\n- Substitute teacher: 50%\n- Normal teacher comes **and** whole class gets extension: 50% \u00d7 40% = 20%\n- Normal teacher comes, no class extension, **and** Marcus gets personal extension: 50% \u00d7 60% \u00d7 20% = 6%\n\nTotal chance he does **not** have to turn it in:\n\n50% + 20% + 6% = 76%\n\nSo the chance he **does** have to turn it in is:\n\n100% \u2212 76% = 24%\n\nAnswer: 24%", "correct": true, "gold": "24"}
{"qid": "gsm:170", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "He runs:\n\n- \\(12\\) miles/day \u00d7 \\(5\\) days/week = \\(60\\) miles/week\n\nAt \\(10\\) miles per hour, time is:\n\n- \\(60 \\div 10 = 6\\) hours/week\n\nAnswer: 6 hours", "correct": true, "gold": "6"}
{"qid": "gsm:171", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The sandcastle has 4 levels whose areas form a geometric sequence, with each level having exactly half the area of the one below it.  \n\nLabel the levels from the bottom up:  \n- Bottom level area = \\(A\\)  \n- Next level up = \\(A/2\\)  \n- Next level up = \\(A/4\\)  \n- Top level = \\(A/8\\)  \n\nThe top level is given as 16, so  \n\\[\nA/8 = 16 \\implies A = 128.\n\\]  \nThe four areas are therefore 128, 64, 32 and 16.  \n\nTheir average is  \n\\[\n\\frac{128 + 64 + 32 + 16}{4} = 60.\n\\]  \n\n(Equivalently, working downward from the top yields the same four values and the same average.)  \n\nAnswer: 60", "correct": true, "gold": "60"}
{"qid": "gsm:172", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**24%**\n\nCalculation: 0.5 (no substitute) \u00d7 0.6 (no class extension) \u00d7 0.8 (no personal extension) = 0.24 = **24%**\n\nAnswer: 24%", "correct": true, "gold": "24"}
{"qid": "gsm:172", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We want the probability that Marcus has to turn in his homework tomorrow.  \nThat happens only if:  \n- No substitute (50% chance of substitute \u2192 50% chance normal teacher)  \n- AND no extension for the whole class (60% chance no extension given normal teacher)  \n- AND no personal extension (80% chance no personal extension given no class extension)\n\nSo:  \nProbability = 0.50 \u00d7 0.60 \u00d7 0.80 = 0.24 = 24%\n\nAnswer: 24%", "correct": true, "gold": "24"}
{"qid": "gsm:171", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The top level has 16 square feet. Since each level below is twice as large as the one above it:\n\n- Level 4 (top): 16\n- Level 3: 16 \u00d7 2 = 32\n- Level 2: 32 \u00d7 2 = 64\n- Level 1 (bottom): 64 \u00d7 2 = 128\n\nTotal square footage: 16 + 32 + 64 + 128 = 240\n\nAverage: 240 \u00f7 4 = 60\n\nAnswer: 60", "correct": true, "gold": "60"}
{"qid": "gsm:173", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "First, find the number of semi-automatic cars:  \n20 total - 12 automatic - 4 manual = 4 semi-automatic.\n\nThen, calculate the percentage:  \n(4 / 20) \u00d7 100% = 20%.\n\nAnswer: 20%", "correct": true, "gold": "20"}
{"qid": "gsm:173", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "There are 20 cars total.\n\nAutomatic + manual = 12 + 4 = 16\n\nSo semi-automatic = 20 - 16 = 4\n\nPercentage semi-automatic = \\( \\frac{4}{20} \\times 100 = 20\\% \\)\n\nAnswer: 20%", "correct": true, "gold": "20"}
{"qid": "gsm:172", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: 24%<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "24"}
{"qid": "gsm:173", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "Total cars = 20  \nAutomatic = 12  \nManual = 4  \nSemi-automatic = 20 - 12 - 4 = 4  \nPercentage semi-automatic = (4 / 20) \u00d7 100 = 20  \n\nAnswer: 20%", "correct": true, "gold": "20"}
{"qid": "gsm:173", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Semi-automatic cars = 20 - 12 - 4 = 4\n\nPercentage = (4/20) \u00d7 100 = 20%\n\nAnswer: 20%", "correct": true, "gold": "20"}
{"qid": "gsm:173", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The number of semi\u2011automatic cars is \\(20 - (12 + 4) = 4\\).  \nThe percentage is \\(\\frac{4}{20} \\times 100 = 20\\%\\).\n\nAnswer: 20%<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "20"}
{"qid": "gsm:172", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The only scenario where Marcus must turn in the homework is when all three of the following occur: the regular teacher shows up (probability \\(1 - 0.5 = 0.5\\)), the class does not receive an extension (probability \\(1 - 0.4 = 0.6\\)), and Marcus fails to obtain a personal extension (probability \\(1 - 0.2 = 0.8\\)).\n\nThese events are sequential and conditional, so their joint probability is obtained by multiplying:\n\\[\n0.5 \\times 0.6 \\times 0.8 = 0.24\n\\]\nExpressed as a percentage, this is exactly 24.\n\nNo other combination of outcomes requires Marcus to submit the assignment.\n\nAnswer: 24", "correct": true, "gold": "24"}
{"qid": "gsm:171", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "The levels from top to bottom are: \r\n\r\n- Level 1 (top): 16 sq. ft.\r\n- Level 2: 16 \u00d7 2 = 32 sq. ft.\r\n- Level 3: 32 \u00d7 2 = 64 sq. ft.\r\n- Level 4 (bottom): 64 \u00d7 2 = 128 sq. ft.\r\n\r\nTotal square footage = 16 + 32 + 64 + 128 = 240 sq. ft.\r\n\r\nAverage = 240 \u00f7 4 = 60 sq. ft.\r\n\r\nAnswer: 60", "correct": true, "gold": "60"}
{"qid": "gsm:173", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "First, find the number of semi-automatic cars:  \n$20 - 12 - 4 = 4$ semi-automatic cars.\n\nThen calculate the percentage:  \n$\\frac{4}{20} \\times 100\\% = 20\\%$\n\nAnswer: 20%", "correct": true, "gold": "20"}
{"qid": "gsm:174", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Initial workforce: 200 employees\n\nThe company hires 20 new employees each month:\n- After 1 month: 220 employees\n- After 2 months: 240 employees\n- After 3 months: 260 employees\n\nEach employee earns $4000 per month, so after three months the monthly payroll is:\n260 \u00d7 4000 = 1,040,000\n\nAnswer: $1,040,000", "correct": false, "gold": "2880000"}
{"qid": "gsm:172", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "To find the probability that Marcus has to turn in his homework, I'll calculate the probability of each path where he avoids turning it in, then subtract from 100%.\n\n**Paths where Marcus does NOT turn in homework:**\n1. **Substitute teacher:** 50% chance \u2192 no homework\n2. **Normal teacher (50%) + class extension (40%):** 0.50 \u00d7 0.40 = 20% \u2192 no homework  \n3. **Normal teacher (50%) + no class extension (60%) + personal extension (20%):** 0.50 \u00d7 0.60 \u00d7 0.20 = 6% \u2192 no homework\n\n**Total probability of no homework:** 50% + 20% + 6% = 76%\n\n**Probability Marcus MUST turn in homework:** 100% - 76% = 24%\n\nAlternatively, calculating directly: Normal teacher (50%) \u00d7 no class extension (60%) \u00d7 no personal extension (80%) = 0.5 \u00d7 0.6 \u00d7 0.8 = 0.24 = 24%\n\nAnswer: 24%", "correct": true, "gold": "24"}
{"qid": "gsm:173", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:** \r\n\r\n1. **Find the number of semi-automatic cars:** Total cars = 20. Automatic = 12, Manual = 4. So, Semi-automatic = 20 - 12 - 4 = 4. \r\n\r\n2. **Calculate the percentage:** (Number of semi-automatic / Total cars) \u00d7 100 = (4 / 20) \u00d7 100 = 20%. \r\n\r\nAnswer: 20%", "correct": true, "gold": "20"}
{"qid": "gsm:174", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Setting up the employee count each month:**\n\n- **Month 1:** 200 employees (initial workforce)\n- **Month 2:** 200 + 20 = 220 employees\n- **Month 3:** 220 + 20 = 240 employees\n\n**Calculating monthly payroll (employees \u00d7 $4000):**\n\n- Month 1: 200 \u00d7 $4000 = $800,000\n- Month 2: 220 \u00d7 $4000 = $880,000\n- Month 3: 240 \u00d7 $4000 = $960,000\n\n**Total payroll over three months:**\n\n$800,000 + $880,000 + $960,000 = **$2,640,000**\n\nAnswer: $2,640,000", "correct": false, "gold": "2880000"}
{"qid": "gsm:172", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "The chance that Marcus will actually have to turn in his homework is determined by the scenarios where *none* of his escapes happen: \r\n\r\n1. **Normal teacher comes in**: 100% - 50% (substitute) = 50%.\r\n2. **No class-wide extension**: Given a normal teacher, 100% - 40% = 60%.\r\n3. **No personal extension**: Given no class extension, 100% - 20% = 80%.\r\n\r\nMultiply these together: `0.50 * 0.60 * 0.80 = 0.24`, or 24%. \r\n\r\nAnswer: 24%", "correct": true, "gold": "24"}
{"qid": "gsm:174", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We start with 200 employees. Each month, 20 new employees are hired, and all employees are paid $4000 per month.\n\n**Month 1:**  \nEmployees = 200  \nPayment = 200 \u00d7 4000 = $800,000\n\n**Month 2:**  \nEmployees = 200 + 20 = 220  \nPayment = 220 \u00d7 4000 = $880,000\n\n**Month 3:**  \nEmployees = 220 + 20 = 240  \nPayment = 240 \u00d7 4000 = $960,000\n\n**Total after three months:**  \n800,000 + 880,000 + 960,000 = $2,640,000\n\nAnswer: $2,640,000", "correct": false, "gold": "2880000"}
{"qid": "gsm:175", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Let Anne have \\(A\\) books.\n\nThen:\n- Sofie has \\(A + 25\\)\n- Anne has 12 fewer than Fawn, so Fawn has \\(A + 12\\)\n\nTogether they have 85 books:\n\\[\nA + (A+25) + (A+12) = 85\n\\]\n\\[\n3A + 37 = 85\n\\]\n\\[\n3A = 48\n\\]\n\\[\nA = 16\n\\]\n\nSo Fawn has:\n\\[\n16 + 12 = 28\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 28", "correct": true, "gold": "28"}
{"qid": "gsm:174", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The company starts with 200 employees.  \nEach month it adds 20 new hires, and every employee receives a $4,000 monthly salary.\n\n**Month\u202f1:**  \nEmployees = 200\u202f+\u202f20\u202f=\u202f220  \nPayroll = 220\u202f\u00d7\u202f$4,000 = $880,000  \n\n**Month\u202f2:**  \nEmployees = 220\u202f+\u202f20\u202f=\u202f240  \nPayroll = 240\u202f\u00d7\u202f$4,000 = $960,000  \n\n**Month\u202f3:**  \nEmployees = 240\u202f+\u202f20\u202f=\u202f260  \nPayroll = 260\u202f\u00d7\u202f$4,000 = $1,040,000  \n\n**Total after three months:**  \n$880,000\u202f+\u202f$960,000\u202f+\u202f$1,040,000 = $2,880,000  \n\nAnswer: $2,880,000.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "2880000"}
{"qid": "gsm:175", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Anne = Fawn - 12, Sofie = Anne + 25 = Fawn + 13\n\nSum: Fawn + (Fawn - 12) + (Fawn + 13) = 85\n3\u00b7Fawn + 1 = 85\n3\u00b7Fawn = 84\nFawn = 28\n\nAnswer: 28", "correct": true, "gold": "28"}
{"qid": "gsm:175", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Let $F$, $A$, and $S$ represent the number of books Fawn, Anne, and Sofie have, respectively.\n\nFrom the problem:\n- $S + A + F = 85$\n- $S = A + 25$\n- $A = F - 12$\n\nSubstituting the third equation into the second:\n$S = (F - 12) + 25 = F + 13$\n\nSubstituting both into the first equation:\n$(F + 13) + (F - 12) + F = 85$\n\nSimplifying:\n$3F + 1 = 85$\n$3F = 84$\n$F = 28$\n\nVerification: Fawn has 28, Anne has $28-12=16$, Sofie has $16+25=41$, and $28+16+41=85$.\n\nAnswer: 28", "correct": true, "gold": "28"}
{"qid": "gsm:175", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>First, set up the equations based on the given relationships:\n\n- Sofie\u2019s books: \\(S = A + 25\\)\n- Anne\u2019s books: \\(A = F - 12\\) \u2192 \\(F = A + 12\\)\n- Total books: \\(S + A + F = 85\\)\n\nSubstitute \\(S\\) and \\(F\\) in terms of \\(A\\) into the total:\n\n\\[\n(A + 25) + A + (A + 12) = 85 \\\\\n3A + 37 = 85 \\\\\n3A = 48 \\\\\nA = 16\n\\]\n\nNow find \\(F\\):\n\n\\[\nF = A + 12 = 16 + 12 = 28\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 28.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "28"}
{"qid": "gsm:175", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "Let \\(A\\) = Anne's books, \\(F\\) = Fawn's books, and \\(S\\) = Sofie's books.\n\nThe given conditions translate to the system:\n- \\(S + A + F = 85\\)\n- \\(S = A + 25\\)\n- \\(A = F - 12\\)\n\nSubstitute the third equation into the second to obtain \\(S = F + 13\\).\n\nPlug both expressions into the first equation:\n\\[\n(F + 13) + (F - 12) + F = 85\n\\]\n\\[\n3F + 1 = 85\n\\]\n\\[\n3F = 84\n\\]\n\\[\nF = 28\n\\]\n\nVerification: Anne has \\(28 - 12 = 16\\) books and Sofie has \\(16 + 25 = 41\\) books, which sum to \\(41 + 16 + 28 = 85\\).\n\nAnswer: 28", "correct": true, "gold": "28"}
{"qid": "gsm:176", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "People invited:\n- 2 families of 6 = 2 \u00d7 6 = 12\n- 3 families of 4 = 3 \u00d7 4 = 12\n\nTotal invited = 12 + 12 = 24\n\nPeople who couldn\u2019t come:\n- Due to illness = 8\n- Previous commitments = 1/4 of 8 = 2\n\nTotal not coming = 8 + 2 = 10\n\nPeople who show up = 24 - 10 = 14\n\nAnswer: 14", "correct": true, "gold": "14"}
{"qid": "gsm:176", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**14 people** show up for Martha's party.\n\nReasoning:\n- Total invited: (2 families \u00d7 6 people) + (3 families \u00d7 4 people) = 12 + 12 = 24 people\n- Couldn't come due to illness: 8 people\n- Previous commitments: 1/4 \u00d7 8 = 2 people\n- Total not attending: 8 + 2 = 10 people\n- People who show up: 24 \u2212 10 = 14 people\n\nAnswer: 14", "correct": true, "gold": "14"}
{"qid": "gsm:176", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "Let's break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, calculate the total number of people invited:  \n- 2 families \u00d7 6 people = 12 people  \n- 3 families \u00d7 4 people = 12 people  \nTotal invited = 12 + 12 = 24 people.\n\nNext, 8 people couldn't come due to illness.  \nThen, 1/4 of that number (1/4 of 8) had previous commitments:  \n1/4 \u00d7 8 = 2 people.\n\nTotal who cannot come = 8 + 2 = 10 people.\n\nNumber who show up = 24 \u2212 10 = 14 people.\n\nAnswer: 14", "correct": true, "gold": "14"}
{"qid": "gsm:176", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "Total invited: (2 families \u00d7 6) + (3 families \u00d7 4) = 24.  \nOf those, 8 could not attend due to illness and \u00bc of 8 (= 2) had prior commitments, for 10 total absences.  \nThus 24 \u2212 10 = 14 attended.\n\nAnswer: 14", "correct": true, "gold": "14"}
{"qid": "gsm:175", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Let\u2019s solve the problem step by step.\n\n### Problem statement recap\nTogether, Sofie, Anne, and Fawn have 85 books. If Sofie has 25 more books than Anne, and Anne has 12 fewer books than Fawn does, how many books does Fawn have?\n\n### Solution\nLet\u2019s define variables:\n- Let $F$ be the number of books Fawn has.\n- Anne has 12 fewer than Fawn: $A = F - 12$.\n- Sofie has 25 more than Anne: $S = A + 25 = (F - 12) + 25 = F + 13$.\n\nTotal: $S + A + F = 85$.\nSubstitute:\n$(F + 13) + (F - 12) + F = 85$.\n\nCombine like terms:\n$3F + 1 = 85$.\n\nSolve:\n$3F = 84 \\implies F = 28$.\n\nCheck: Fawn 28, Anne 16, Sofie 41. Sum 28+16+41=85. OK.\n\nAnswer: 28", "correct": true, "gold": "28"}
{"qid": "gsm:176", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "First, calculate the total number of people invited:\n- 2 families \u00d7 6 people = 12 people\n- 3 families \u00d7 4 people = 12 people\n- Total invited: 12 + 12 = 24 people\n\nNext, calculate how many couldn't attend:\n- Illness: 8 people\n- Previous commitments: 1/4 \u00d7 8 = 2 people\n- Total absent: 8 + 2 = 10 people\n\nFinally, subtract the absent people from the total invited:\n- 24 - 10 = 14 people\n\nAnswer: 14", "correct": true, "gold": "14"}
{"qid": "gsm:177", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "German Shepherds: 3 \u00d7 5 = 15 kg per day  \nBulldogs: 2 \u00d7 3 = 6 kg per day  \n\nTotal per day: 15 + 6 = 21 kg  \n\nFor a week: 21 \u00d7 7 = 147 kg  \n\nAnswer: 147 kilograms", "correct": true, "gold": "147"}
{"qid": "gsm:177", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Daily consumption:**\n- German Shepherds: 3 \u00d7 5 kg = 15 kg\n- Bulldogs: 2 \u00d7 3 kg = 6 kg\n- Total per day: 15 + 6 = 21 kg\n\n**Weekly consumption:**\n21 kg \u00d7 7 days = 147 kg\n\nAnswer: 147 kilograms", "correct": true, "gold": "147"}
{"qid": "gsm:176", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:** \r\n\r\n1. **Total invited:** (2 families \u00d7 6 people) + (3 families \u00d7 4 people) = 12 + 12 = 24 people.\r\n2. **Illness:** 8 people couldn't come.\r\n3. **Previous commitments:** 1/4 of 8 = 2 people couldn't come.\r\n4. **Total unable to attend:** 8 + 2 = 10 people.\r\n5. **Total attending:** 24 - 10 = 14 people. \r\n\r\nAnswer: 14", "correct": true, "gold": "14"}
{"qid": "gsm:174", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The total salary expenditure must account for the fact that the workforce grows over time, so each month's payroll is different. The initial workforce of 200 employees is used for the first month's payroll. After that, 20 employees are added at the end of each month, producing the following head-count for each payroll:\n\n- Month 1: 200 employees  \n- Month 2: 220 employees  \n- Month 3: 240 employees  \n\nThe three monthly payrolls therefore form the arithmetic series 200 + 220 + 240. Their sum is 660 employee-months. Multiplying by the monthly salary per employee gives the cumulative outlay:\n\n\\[\n660 \\times 4000 = 2{,}640{,}000\n\\]\n\nThus the company has paid a total of $2,640,000 after three months.\n\nAnswer: 2640000", "correct": false, "gold": "2880000"}
{"qid": "gsm:177", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Daily consumption for German Shepherds: $3 \\times 5 = 15$ kg  \nDaily consumption for Bulldogs: $2 \\times 3 = 6$ kg  \nTotal daily consumption: $15 + 6 = 21$ kg  \nWeekly consumption: $21 \\times 7 = 147$ kg  \n\nAnswer: 147 kilograms", "correct": true, "gold": "147"}
{"qid": "gsm:177", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "There are 3 German Shepherds each consuming 5 kg/day, for a subtotal of 15 kg/day.  \nThere are 2 Bulldogs each consuming 3 kg/day, for a subtotal of 6 kg/day.  \nCombined daily total is therefore 21 kg.  \nOver 7 days this equals 147 kg.\n\nAnswer: 147 kg", "correct": true, "gold": "147"}
{"qid": "gsm:177", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The three German Shepherds eat \\(3 \\times 5 = 15\\)\u202fkg per day, and the two Bulldogs eat \\(2 \\times 3 = 6\\)\u202fkg per day. Together they consume \\(15 + 6 = 21\\)\u202fkg each day. Over a week (7 days), they need \\(21 \\times 7 = 147\\)\u202fkg of dog food.\n\nAnswer: 147 kilograms.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "147"}
{"qid": "gsm:177", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We need to find the total weekly food consumption.\n\nFirst, find the daily consumption:\n- German Shepherds: 3 dogs \u00d7 5 kg = 15 kg per day\n- Bulldogs: 2 dogs \u00d7 3 kg = 6 kg per day\n- Total per day: 15 + 6 = 21 kg\n\nThen for a week (7 days):\n21 kg/day \u00d7 7 days = 147 kg\n\nAnswer: 147", "correct": true, "gold": "147"}
