{"qid": "gsm:197", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "First, find how many pencils are in one box: \r\n\r\n- Jam has 3 boxes + 2 loose = 26 pencils.\r\n- Pencils in boxes = 26 - 2 = 24.\r\n- Pencils per box = 24 \u00f7 3 = 8.\r\n\r\nNext, find the total number of pencils: \r\n\r\n- Jam = 26, Meg = 46, so total = 26 + 46 = 72 pencils.\r\n\r\nFinally, determine how many boxes are needed: \r\n\r\n- Boxes needed = 72 \u00f7 8 = 9.\r\n\r\nAnswer: 9 boxes", "correct": true, "gold": "9"}
{"qid": "gsm:197", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>First, determine the capacity of one box.  \nJam has 3 full boxes plus 2 loose pencils totaling 26 pencils.  \nThe three full boxes therefore contain \\(26 - 2 = 24\\) pencils.  \nThus each box holds \\(\\frac{24}{3} = 8\\) pencils.\n\nNow calculate how many boxes each person needs:\n\n- **Jam:** 26 pencils \u00f7 8 pencils per box = 3.25 boxes \u2192 she needs \\(\\lceil 3.25 \\rceil = 4\\) boxes (3 full boxes and 1 partial box for the 2 loose pencils).\n- **Meg:** 46 pencils \u00f7 8 pencils per box = 5.75 boxes \u2192 she needs \\(\\lceil 5.75 \\rceil = 6\\) boxes (5 full boxes and 1 partial box for the remaining pencils).\n\nTogether they require \\(4 + 6 = 10\\) boxes.\n\nAnswer: Jam needs 4 boxes, Meg needs 6 boxes (total 10 boxes).<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "9"}
{"qid": "gsm:199", "domain": "gsm8k", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "First, find Ella's hourly rate: $\\$33 \\div 3\\text{ hours} = \\$11$ per hour.\n\nThen, calculate earnings for 12 hours: $\\$11 \\times 12 = \\$132$.\n\nAlternatively, since 12 hours is 4 times 3 hours, she earns $4 \\times \\$33 = \\$132$.\n\nAnswer: $132$", "correct": true, "gold": "132"}
