{"qid": "med:12", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Answer: Administer dantrolene**\n\nThis patient's presentation is consistent with **Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS)**, likely triggered by the anti-emetic given in the ED. Many anti-emetics (e.g., **metoclopramide, prochlorperazine, promethazine**) are dopamine receptor antagonists and can precipitate NMS, especially in a patient who may have also taken other dopaminergic-blocking substances at the party (e.g., recreational drugs, or even undisclosed antipsychotic use).\n\n**Key features supporting NMS:**\n- Muscle rigidity\n- Hyperthermia (103\u00b0F)\n- Altered mental status\n- Autonomic instability (tachycardia)\n- Temporal relationship to administration of a dopamine-blocking anti-emetic\n\n**Why lorazepam and cooling blankets are insufficient:**\nBenzodiazepines and external cooling are appropriate initial supportive measures, but they do not address the underlying pathophysiology of NMS, which involves **central dopamine receptor blockade** leading to dysregulated hypothalamic thermoregulation and increased skeletal muscle rigidity/contraction (which further generates heat).\n\n**Best next step \u2014 Dantrolene:**\nDantrolene is a **direct-acting skeletal muscle relaxant** that works by inhibiting calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum via the ryanodine receptor, reducing muscle rigidity and hyperthermia. It is a mainstay of treatment for NMS (and also malignant hyperthermia) when supportive measures fail.\n\nAn alternative/adjunctive treatment is **bromocriptine**, a dopamine agonist that helps reverse the dopamine blockade at the level of the hypothalamus and nigrostriatal pathways. However, **dantrolene** is generally used first for severe rigidity and hyperthermia due to its rapid onset addressing the muscular component.\n\n---\n\n**Answer: Dantrolene**", "correct": true, "gold": "Dantrolene"}
{"qid": "med:12", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation is consistent with acute serotonergic drug intoxication (most likely MDMA/ecstasy ingestion at a college party). Key diagnostic features include altered mental status, hyperthermia, autonomic hyperactivity, vomiting, and evolution to muscle rigidity.  \n\nSupportive measures (IV fluids, benzodiazepines, external cooling) have already been implemented and have failed to control symptoms. In moderate-to-severe serotonin syndrome that remains refractory, the specific serotonin-receptor antagonist cyproheptadine is the indicated pharmacologic intervention.  \n\nAlternative considerations such as NMS, malignant hyperthermia, or simple sympathomimetic toxicity are far less likely given the rapidity of onset, setting, and lack of an inciting anesthetic or antipsychotic exposure.  \n\nAnswer: administer cyproheptadine", "correct": false, "gold": "Dantrolene"}
