{"qid": "med:14", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient has **zinc deficiency**, likely causing **acrodermatitis enteropathica\u2013like findings**:\n\n- **Perioral bullous/erythematous rash**\n- **Alopecia**\n- **Hypogeusia** (bland taste)\n- History of **Crohn disease** with **multiple small bowel resections**, causing malabsorption\n\nZinc is primarily absorbed in the **small intestine** (especially jejunum), so bowel disease/resection can lead to deficiency.\n\nAnswer: Zinc deficiency due to malabsorption from extensive small-bowel resection in Crohn disease.", "correct": true, "gold": "Zinc"}
{"qid": "med:14", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has Crohn disease with multiple small bowel resections, leading to malabsorption. Her symptoms\u2014hair loss, facial rash (bullous, erythematous perioral plaques), and taste changes (dysgeusia)\u2014point to zinc deficiency. Zinc deficiency causes acrodermatitis enteropathica (periorificial dermatitis), alopecia, and impaired taste. Small bowel resection reduces zinc absorption.\n\nAnswer: Zinc deficiency", "correct": true, "gold": "Zinc"}
{"qid": "med:10", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation of acute chest pain with nausea, diaphoresis, and distress in a young patient with multiple risk factors (untreated hypertension, smoking, recent cocaine use) is most consistent with acute coronary syndrome, possibly cocaine-induced. Differential considerations also include aortic dissection, but the history of progressively (rather than instantaneously) worsening pain makes the latter less likely.\nRegardless of exact etiology, any patient arriving to the ED with this symptom complex must undergo immediate stabilization and risk stratification. The single most time-critical initial action is acquisition of a 12-lead ECG (door-to-ECG goal <10 min). The tracing distinguishes ST-elevation MI (requiring emergent reperfusion) from non-ST-elevation ACS or other patterns (e.g., ischemia, dissection-related infarction) and thereby directs all subsequent pharmacologic and interventional decisions. Adjunctive measures (oxygen if hypoxic, aspirin, benzodiazepines for cocaine sympathomimetic effects, blood-pressure control) follow immediately after the ECG is interpreted.\nAnswer: obtain 12-lead ECG (within 10 min)", "correct": false, "gold": "Reassurance and continuous monitoring"}
{"qid": "med:14", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient's history of Crohn disease with multiple small-bowel resections and fistulas predisposes to malabsorption and excessive gastrointestinal losses of trace minerals. The acute onset of perioral bullous dermatitis, patchy alopecia, and dysgeusia (bland taste) constitutes the classic triad of acquired zinc deficiency (acrodermatitis-enteropathica-like syndrome). No other single micronutrient deficiency reproduces this exact constellation of cutaneous, hair, and gustatory findings.\n\nAnswer: zinc", "correct": true, "gold": "Zinc"}
{"qid": "med:14", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: zinc<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "Zinc"}
{"qid": "med:10", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\nThis patient presents with acute, progressively worsening chest pain radiating to the epigastrium, diaphoresis, and marked distress. His risk factors (untreated hypertension, smoking, and recent cocaine use) significantly increase the likelihood of acute coronary syndrome (ACS), including cocaine-induced myocardial infarction or vasospasm. In any patient with suspected ACS, the most critical and time-sensitive first step is to rapidly assess for ST-segment changes or other ischemic findings to guide immediate management.  \r\n\r\n**Answer:**  \r\nObtain a 12-lead electrocardiogram (ECG) within 10 minutes of arrival.", "correct": false, "gold": "Reassurance and continuous monitoring"}
{"qid": "med:12", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\n\r\nThis patient's presentation is highly consistent with **serotonin syndrome**. Key features include:  \r\n\r\n- **History:** Sudden agitation and unconsciousness at a college party, raising suspicion for substance use (e.g., MDMA, cocaine, amphetamines, or serotonergic drugs).\r\n- **Initial symptoms:** Tachycardia, vomiting, and altered mental status.\r\n- **Progression:** Rapid development of muscle rigidity, hyperthermia (103\u00b0F/39.4\u00b0C), and worsening mental status despite supportive care (IV fluids, antiemetics, analgesics).\r\n- **Refractory to initial measures:** Symptoms persist after lorazepam and cooling blankets, which are first-line supportive therapies. \r\n\r\nSerotonin syndrome is driven by excessive serotonergic activity and can be life-threatening. While benzodiazepines and external cooling help with agitation, rigidity, and hyperthermia, patients with persistent or severe symptoms require a serotonin antagonist. **Cyproheptadine** is the preferred antidote due to its 5-HT2A receptor blockade. \r\n\r\nOther diagnoses (e.g., neuroleptic malignant syndrome, heat stroke, or sympathomimetic toxidrome) are less likely given the rapid onset, prominent gastrointestinal symptoms (vomiting), and lack of exposure to antipsychotics. \r\n\r\n**Answer:** Cyproheptadine", "correct": false, "gold": "Dantrolene"}
{"qid": "med:15", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This adolescent athlete has **tinea cruris (\u201cjock itch\u201d)**: pruritic, erythematous, well-demarcated plaques with scaling in the groin/perineum, often sparing the scrotum.\n\nTo **confirm** the diagnosis, do a **potassium hydroxide (KOH) preparation of skin scrapings from the active/scaly border** of the lesion. Finding **branching septate hyphae** supports dermatophyte infection.\n\nAnswer: Confirm with a KOH prep of skin scrapings from the lesion\u2019s active border, showing branching septate hyphae.", "correct": false, "gold": "KOH examination of lesion scrapings"}
{"qid": "med:15", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The suspected diagnosis is tinea cruris (jock itch), a fungal infection common in athletes due to sweating and friction. To confirm, a potassium hydroxide (KOH) preparation of skin scrapings from the leading edge of the rash can be performed to visualize hyphae under microscopy.\n\nAnswer: Potassium hydroxide (KOH) preparation", "correct": false, "gold": "KOH examination of lesion scrapings"}
