{"qid": "med:14", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient has risk factors for malabsorption (Crohn disease and multiple small\u2011bowel resections). The combination of a periorificial bullous, erythematous rash, well\u2011circumscribed alopecia, and altered taste (hypogeusia) is the classic presentation of zinc deficiency.\n\nAnswer: Zinc deficiency.", "correct": true, "gold": "Zinc"}
{"qid": "med:15", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation of a young athlete with intense groin-area sweating, pruritus, and an annular/arciform erythematous plaque with advancing peripheral scale is classic for tinea cruris (dermatophytosis of the inguinal folds, thighs, and perineum). The scrotum is characteristically spared, further supporting a dermatophyte rather than candidal etiology.\nDefinitive bedside confirmation is obtained by gently scraping the leading edge of scale, placing the material on a glass slide, and applying 10\u201320 % KOH (with or without dimethyl sulfoxide). Microscopic examination under low-power light microscopy reveals branching, septate hyphae traversing the keratinocytes, establishing the diagnosis of dermatophyte infection without need for culture or biopsy in routine cases.\nAnswer: KOH prep of scale (septate hyphae)", "correct": false, "gold": "KOH examination of lesion scrapings"}
