{"qid": "med:16", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient has **infective endocarditis of the aortic valve**, suggested by:\n\n- fever, fatigue, weight loss\n- conjunctival hemorrhages, petechiae\n- painless palm lesions = **Janeway lesions**\n- new **high-pitched blowing diastolic murmur** at the right upper sternal border = **aortic regurgitation**\n- head bobbing with each heartbeat = **de Musset sign**, also due to aortic regurgitation\n- splenomegaly\n\nHe also has **iron deficiency anemia** with a **descending colon cancer**, which strongly suggests **occult gastrointestinal blood loss**. Colon neoplasms are classically associated with **Streptococcus gallolyticus** bacteremia/endocarditis (formerly **Streptococcus bovis**), because this organism can translocate from the colon into the bloodstream in the setting of colonic neoplasia.\n\nThus, the most likely explanation for the cardiac findings is **aortic valve infective endocarditis caused by S gallolyticus from the underlying colon cancer**, leading to **valvular destruction and aortic regurgitation**.\n\nAnswer: Streptococcus gallolyticus (formerly S bovis) bacteremia from his colon adenocarcinoma seeded the aortic valve, causing infective endocarditis with valvular destruction and aortic regurgitation.", "correct": false, "gold": "Group D streptococci"}
{"qid": "med:15", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The clinical presentation is classic for **tinea cruris** (dermatophyte fungal infection): a pruritic, well-demarcated, erythematous patch with peripheral scale in the groin that spares the scrotum, occurring in an athlete during hot, humid conditions. \n\nTo confirm this diagnosis, the appropriate test is a **potassium hydroxide (KOH) preparation of skin scrapings** taken from the active border of the lesion. When examined microscopically, this will reveal branching hyphae characteristic of dermatophytes. While fungal culture can identify the specific organism, it takes weeks to grow. Wood's lamp examination is less useful here because the common causative organisms of tinea cruris (e.g., *Trichophyton* species) typically do not fluoresce.\n\nAnswer: Potassium hydroxide (KOH) preparation of skin scrapings to visualize hyphae under microscopy.", "correct": false, "gold": "KOH examination of lesion scrapings"}
{"qid": "med:16", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has signs of infective endocarditis (fever, new diastolic murmur, conjunctival hemorrhages, petechiae, splinter hemorrhages?\u2014here described as red macules on palms, head bobbing with heartbeat [de Musset's sign], and splenomegaly). He also has iron deficiency anemia (low ferritin, low iron, high TIBC, microcytosis) and a colon mass. Colon cancer can lead to endocarditis from *Streptococcus bovis/gallolyticus* bacteremia, which is associated with colorectal neoplasms. The most likely explanation for his cardiac findings is **infective endocarditis secondary to *Streptococcus bovis* bacteremia from colon cancer**.\n\nAnswer: Infective endocarditis due to *Streptococcus bovis* bacteremia from colon cancer.", "correct": false, "gold": "Group D streptococci"}
{"qid": "med:16", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient's presentation includes constitutional symptoms (fever, fatigue, weight loss), splenomegaly, anemia, and multiple peripheral stigmata of endocarditis (conjunctival hemorrhages, petechiae, Janeway lesions). These are accompanied by a new high-pitched diastolic murmur at the base and de Musset sign, both diagnostic of severe aortic regurgitation. The recently discovered colonic neoplasm supplies the portal of entry for bacteremia; the organism classically linked to this sequence is Streptococcus gallolyticus (formerly S. bovis). Once seeded onto the aortic valve, the organism produces destructive vegetations that prevent coaptation of the cusps, resulting in acute valvular incompetence and the observed physical findings.\nThus the cardiac abnormalities are the direct mechanical consequence of aortic-valve endocarditis driven by S. gallolyticus bacteremia from the underlying colonic malignancy.\nAnswer: aortic regurg from S. gallolyticus IE (colon ca)", "correct": false, "gold": "Group D streptococci"}
{"qid": "med:16", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Most likely explanation:** Infective endocarditis of the aortic valve caused by *Streptococcus gallolyticus* (formerly *S. bovis*) bacteremia originating from the colonic neoplasm.\n\n**Reasoning:**\n- The patient has classic signs of **aortic regurgitation**: a high-pitched, blowing diastolic murmur at the right upper sternal border, wide pulse pressure (145/75), and de Musset's sign (head bobbing with each heartbeat).\n- He also has stigmata of **infective endocarditis**: low-grade fever, conjunctival hemorrhages (Roth spot equivalent), petechiae, and painless erythematous macules on the palms (Janeway lesions).\n- His iron-deficiency anemia (low ferritin, low iron, high TIBC, microcytosis) combined with unintentional weight loss, fatigue, and a new colonic mass strongly suggests **colorectal carcinoma**, causing chronic occult GI blood loss.\n- There is a well-established association between colorectal neoplasms (especially colonic adenocarcinoma) and bacteremia/endocarditis due to ***Streptococcus gallolyticus* (S. bovis biotype I)**. This organism translocates across the disrupted colonic mucosa near the tumor, seeding the bloodstream and subsequently infecting a native heart valve\u2014here, the aortic valve\u2014leading to infective endocarditis and consequent valvular destruction causing aortic regurgitation.\n\n**Answer:** The colonic neoplasm allowed translocation of *Streptococcus gallolyticus* (S. bovis) into the bloodstream, causing infective endocarditis of the aortic valve, which explains his diastolic murmur, wide pulse pressure, and de Musset\u2019s sign (aortic regurgitation from endocarditis-related valve destruction).", "correct": false, "gold": "Group D streptococci"}
{"qid": "med:15", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "The suspected diagnosis is **tinea cruris** (jock itch), given the erythematous, well-demarcated patch with peripheral scale in the groin and thigh of an athlete. \r\n\r\n### How to confirm it: \r\n\r\n- **Potassium hydroxide (KOH) preparation**: Scrape scales from the active peripheral border and examine under microscopy. The presence of branching, septate hyphae confirms a dermatophyte infection.\r\n- **Fungal culture**: If KOH is negative but suspicion remains, a culture on Sabouraud dextrose agar can identify the specific dermatophyte (e.g., *Trichophyton rubrum*). \r\n\r\nAnswer: **KOH preparation of skin scrapings from the lesion's active border showing septate hyphae.**", "correct": false, "gold": "KOH examination of lesion scrapings"}
{"qid": "med:17", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient has **cerebellar dysfunction**: wide-based gait, inability to stand with feet together even with eyes open, intention tremor, and dysdiadochokinesia, with **normal strength and sensation**.\n\nThe most likely cause is **chronic alcohol use causing cerebellar degeneration**, especially of the **anterior/superior vermis**, which classically produces gait ataxia and falls. His daily pint of rum strongly supports this. Normal mental status argues against Wernicke encephalopathy, and intact sensation argues against sensory ataxia from peripheral neuropathy or posterior column disease. Solvent exposure can cause neurologic symptoms, but the classic finding here is alcoholic cerebellar degeneration.\n\nAnswer: Chronic alcohol-induced cerebellar degeneration of the cerebellar vermis.", "correct": true, "gold": "Cerebellar degeneration"}
{"qid": "med:17", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient presents with a wide-based gait, inability to stand with feet together (positive Romberg sign is not mentioned, but this suggests cerebellar ataxia), impaired rapid alternating movements (dysdiadochokinesia), and intention tremor\u2014all pointing to cerebellar dysfunction. He has a history of heavy alcohol use (one pint of rum daily) and works with industrial solvents, both of which can cause cerebellar degeneration. However, the absence of peripheral neuropathy, intact sensation, and normal mental status make alcoholic cerebellar degeneration (typically affecting the anterior vermis, causing gait ataxia) the most likely cause. Solvent exposure could also cause neurological issues, but the classic presentation here fits alcohol.\n\nAnswer: Alcoholic cerebellar degeneration", "correct": true, "gold": "Cerebellar degeneration"}
{"qid": "med:17", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Most likely cause: Chronic alcohol-induced cerebellar degeneration** (affecting primarily the cerebellar vermis/anterior lobe)\n\n**Reasoning:**\n- The patient has a **wide-based, ataxic gait**, **inability to perform tandem stance**, **coarse tremor of the hands**, and **dysdiadochokinesia** (impaired rapid alternating movements) \u2014 all classic signs of **cerebellar dysfunction**, specifically pointing to involvement of the **cerebellar vermis and anterior lobe**.\n- Importantly, **sensation (pain, vibration, position) is intact**, and **muscle strength/tone are normal**, which argues against a peripheral neuropathy or myelopathy (e.g., from solvent toxicity or vitamin deficiency causing subacute combined degeneration) and localizes the problem specifically to the **cerebellum** rather than the dorsal columns or peripheral nerves.\n- Mental status is normal, arguing against Wernicke encephalopathy (which would typically also show confusion, ophthalmoplegia) \u2014 though thiamine deficiency can contribute to cerebellar degeneration, the isolated, chronic, symmetric findings without altered mentation are most consistent with **alcoholic cerebellar degeneration**, a well-known toxic/nutritional complication of **chronic heavy alcohol use** (this patient drinks a pint of rum daily).\n- This condition results from **direct toxic effects of alcohol combined with thiamine deficiency**, leading to **Purkinje cell loss** predominantly in the **anterior superior vermis**, producing truncal and gait ataxia more than limb ataxia, with relatively preserved cognition \u2014 matching this patient's presentation.\n\nAnswer: Chronic alcohol use leading to cerebellar (vermian) degeneration.", "correct": false, "gold": "Cerebellar degeneration"}
{"qid": "med:17", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The combination of a wide\u2011based gait, positive Romberg sign, intention tremor, dysmetria, and preserved muscle strength, sensation, and mental status points to cerebellar dysfunction. In a middle\u2011aged man with heavy daily alcohol intake (one pint of rum) and no other systemic disease, the most common cause of isolated cerebellar ataxia is chronic alcohol\u2011induced cerebellar degeneration. Solvent exposure typically causes peripheral neuropathy rather than pure cerebellar signs, and there are no features of Wernicke\u2019s encephalopathy or other neurologic disorders.\n\n**Answer:** Chronic alcohol consumption leading to alcoholic cerebellar degeneration.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "Cerebellar degeneration"}
{"qid": "med:14", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "This patient\u2019s poorly controlled Crohn disease and multiple segmental small bowel resections have led to **malabsorption of zinc**. Her clinical findings\u2014perioral bullous erythematous plaques, well-circumscribed patches of alopecia, and hypogeusia (bland taste)\u2014are classic for acquired zinc deficiency (resembling acrodermatitis enteropathica).\n\nAnswer: Zinc deficiency", "correct": true, "gold": "Zinc"}
{"qid": "med:17", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient's presentation is dominated by pancerebellar motor signs (wide-based gait, inability to stand feet-together, intention tremor, and impaired rapid alternating movements) with completely preserved strength, tone, proprioception/vibration, and cognition. This pattern localizes to the cerebellum itself rather than to sensory pathways, peripheral nerves, or cerebrum.\nDaily rum intake raises the possibility of alcoholic cerebellar degeneration, yet that entity preferentially affects the midline vermis (legs and gait) while largely sparing the limbs; peripheral neuropathy is also common, yet sensation is normal here. The occupational history therefore becomes decisive: the patient works in a factory that manufactures industrial solvents, the prototype of which is toluene. Chronic toluene exposure produces a diffuse cerebellar syndrome that involves both axial and appendicular coordination, exactly matching the findings, and does not produce sensory loss or encephalopathy when exposure is moderate.\nThus the history, time course, and topographic pattern converge on occupational toluene toxicity as the single most likely etiology.\nAnswer: toluene (solvent) toxicity/exposure", "correct": false, "gold": "Cerebellar degeneration"}
{"qid": "med:12", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "This clinical presentation is classic for **serotonin syndrome**. Key features supporting this diagnosis include:\n\n1. **Clinical triad**: Mental status changes (altered consciousness), autonomic instability (tachycardia, hypertension, hyperthermia), and neuromuscular abnormalities (muscle rigidity)\n2. **Rapid onset**: Symptoms developed within 30 minutes of receiving medications (serotonin syndrome typically occurs within minutes to hours; NMS develops over days)\n3. **Context**: College party setting suggests possible MDMA (ecstasy) use or other serotonergic drugs; the patient likely received additional serotonergic agents in the ED (e.g., tramadol as an analgesic or ondansetron as an anti-emetic), precipitating the syndrome\n4. **Refractory course**: Symptoms persisted despite supportive measures (IV fluids, cooling blanket) and benzodiazepines (lorazepam)\n\n**Differential diagnosis considerations**:\n- **Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS)**: Also causes rigidity, hyperthermia, and autonomic instability, but develops over days (not 30 minutes) and is associated with dopamine antagonists\n- **Malignant hyperthermia**: Requires exposure to volatile anesthetics or succinylcholine (not present here)\n- **Heat stroke**: Lacks the specific neuromuscular findings and medication trigger\n\n**Management**: The specific antidote for serotonin syndrome is **cyproheptadine** (a serotonin antagonist). It is indicated for moderate to severe cases that do not respond to supportive care and benzodiazepines.\n\nAnswer: Administer cyproheptadine.", "correct": false, "gold": "Dantrolene"}
{"qid": "med:19", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Toxic thyroid nodule (toxic adenoma) causing hyperthyroidism\n\nAnswer: Toxic (hyperfunctioning) thyroid adenoma", "correct": false, "gold": "Silent thyroiditis"}
{"qid": "med:19", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Her symptoms suggest **thyrotoxicosis**: sweaty palms/soles, insomnia, irritability, nervousness, and fatigue.\n\nThe neck swelling is:\n- **midline/anterior neck**\n- **painless**\n- **progressively enlarging**\n- no fever or inflammatory signs\n- normal ESR\n- no eye signs\n\nThis makes **subacute (de Quervain) thyroiditis** unlikely because that is usually **painful** with a raised ESR.  \n**Acute suppurative thyroiditis** is also unlikely because there is no fever, tenderness, or marked leukocytosis.  \n**Graves disease** usually causes a **diffuse goiter**, not a small round solitary swelling.  \nA **toxic thyroid adenoma (autonomous functioning thyroid nodule)** fits best: a **solitary thyroid nodule** causing hyperthyroid symptoms.\n\nAnswer: **Toxic adenoma of the thyroid (autonomously functioning solitary thyroid nodule)**", "correct": false, "gold": "Silent thyroiditis"}
