{"qid": "med:16", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:** \r\n\r\nThis patient presents with classic signs of **infective endocarditis (IE)**, including fever, Janeway lesions (painless red macules on the palms), conjunctival hemorrhages, petechiae, and splenomegaly. His new-onset, high-pitched blowing diastolic murmur at the right upper sternal border, along with head bobbing (De Musset's sign), is consistent with **aortic regurgitation**. \r\n\r\nThe underlying descending colon adenocarcinoma is the key predisposing factor, as it is strongly associated with **Streptococcus gallolyticus (formerly S. bovis) bacteremia**. This organism has a high propensity to seed damaged or native valves, most commonly the aortic valve, leading to IE and subsequent valvular insufficiency. \r\n\r\nAnswer: **Infective endocarditis of the aortic valve due to Streptococcus gallolyticus bacteremia, secondary to his underlying colorectal carcinoma.**", "correct": false, "gold": "Group D streptococci"}
{"qid": "med:19", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The presentation of a painless, enlarging neck mass in the anterior triangle, along with symptoms of hypermetabolism (sweaty palms/insulin, insomnia, irritability, nervousness, fatigue) but no fever, lymphadenopathy, or weight loss, points toward a thyroid origin. The normal vital signs and ESR, along with the absence of eosinophilia, make an infectious or inflammatory cause less likely. The most likely diagnosis is a thyroid nodule, possibly a toxic adenoma causing subclinical hyperthyroidism, or early Graves' disease without ophthalmopathy. Given the solitary nodule and lack of diffuse goiter or eye signs, a **toxic thyroid adenoma** is a strong possibility.\n\nAnswer: Toxic thyroid adenoma (or Plummer disease)", "correct": false, "gold": "Silent thyroiditis"}
{"qid": "med:20", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Answer: Imperforate hymen (outflow tract obstruction causing primary amenorrhea with cyclic abdominal pain, despite normal puberty/hormones)", "correct": false, "gold": "Muellerian agenesis"}
{"qid": "med:19", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The swelling\u2019s location in the anterior triangle of the neck, its firm, non\u2011tender, non\u2011fluctuant, non\u2011pulsatile nature, and the accompanying symptoms suggestive of hyperthyroidism (sweaty palms/soles, insomnia, irritability, nervousness, fatigue) point toward a thyroid nodule. The normal white blood cell count and erythrocyte sedimentation rate make infection or acute inflammation less likely, and the absence of systemic signs argues against malignant lymphoma or metastatic disease at this stage. A benign thyroid adenoma or cystic nodule would be the most common cause of such a presentation, but definitive diagnosis requires clinical correlation, imaging (e.g., ultrasound), and possibly fine\u2011needle aspiration cytology.  \n\nAnswer: Thyroid nodule (likely benign thyroid adenoma or cyst) \u2013 requires further clinical evaluation.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Silent thyroiditis"}
{"qid": "med:20", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Primary amenorrhea with **normal secondary sexual characteristics** and **normal FSH/estradiol** suggests that ovarian function is present. That makes gonadal dysgenesis unlikely.\n\nKey clues:\n- **Breast development, pubic and axillary hair present** \u2192 estrogen is being produced and androgens are present.\n- **Normal female testosterone range** \u2192 argues against androgen insensitivity syndrome.\n- **Mild intermittent lower abdominal pain** \u2192 suggests retained menstrual blood from an outflow obstruction.\n- No pelvic exam was done, but the best diagnosis from the information given is an anatomic obstruction rather than endocrine failure.\n\nMost likely diagnosis: **imperforate hymen causing cryptomenorrhea/hematocolpos**.\n\nAnswer: Imperforate hymen causing outflow obstruction (cryptomenorrhea/hematocolpos)", "correct": false, "gold": "Muellerian agenesis"}
{"qid": "med:17", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient presents with a subacute, progressive cerebellar syndrome: wide\u2011based gait, inability to perform tandem stance, intention tremor, and dysdiadochokinesia, with intact sensation and normal mental status. While occupational solvent exposure can cause neurotoxicity, the history of heavy daily alcohol consumption (one pint of rum \u2248 12\u201316 standard drinks) is the classic risk factor for alcoholic cerebellar degeneration. This disorder specifically affects the cerebellar vermis and hemispheres, producing gait and limb ataxia without sensory loss. The solvent history is a distractor; the most likely cause is chronic alcohol use.\n\nAnswer: Alcoholic cerebellar degeneration (chronic heavy alcohol use).", "correct": true, "gold": "Cerebellar degeneration"}
{"qid": "med:20", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has primary amenorrhea with normal secondary sexual characteristics (Tanner stage 5), normal FSH and estradiol, and mild intermittent lower abdominal pain. This suggests an outflow tract obstruction rather than a hormonal cause. The most likely diagnosis is **imperforate hymen** or another M\u00fcllerian anomaly causing hematocolpos/hematometra.\n\nAnswer: Imperforate hymen", "correct": false, "gold": "Muellerian agenesis"}
{"qid": "med:17", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\nThis patient presents with classic cerebellar signs, including a wide-based gait, truncal ataxia (inability to stand with feet together), coarse intention tremor, and dysdiadochokinesia. His sensation, strength, tone, and mental status are all normal, which localizes the lesion to the cerebellum. The most significant risk factor is his heavy chronic alcohol use (one pint of rum daily). Alcoholic cerebellar degeneration typically affects the anterior superior vermis, leading to gait and truncal ataxia without significant cognitive impairment. While occupational solvent exposure is a possible alternative cause, chronic alcohol abuse is far more common and best explains this presentation. \r\n\r\nAnswer: **Chronic alcohol-induced cerebellar degeneration**", "correct": true, "gold": "Cerebellar degeneration"}
{"qid": "med:19", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation includes a localized midline neck mass that has enlarged over 3 weeks together with adrenergic and neuropsychiatric symptoms (palmar/plantar sweating, insomnia, irritability, nervousness, fatigue). These features collectively point to endogenous thyrotoxicosis rather than a non-functional lesion (lipoma, cyst, lymphadenopathy) or an inflammatory process.\nAbsence of fever, tenderness, and cervical nodes, combined with a normal ESR, effectively excludes subacute (de Quervain) thyroiditis. The strictly localized, round 3 \u00d7 3 cm morphology argues against the diffuse enlargement typical of Graves disease. Normal vital signs and the short history make a large multinodular toxic goiter unlikely. The zero eosinophil count further removes rare eosinophilic or parasitic differentials.\nConsequently, the single palpable nodule that is autonomously secreting thyroid hormone is the most parsimonious diagnosis: a toxic (hyperfunctioning) thyroid adenoma.\nAnswer: toxic thyroid adenoma (solitary)", "correct": false, "gold": "Silent thyroiditis"}
{"qid": "med:16", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The patient\u2019s systemic signs (fever, weight loss, night sweats), hematologic abnormalities (microcytic anemia, leukopenia), cutaneous findings (petechiae, conjunctival hemorrhages, Osler nodes), splenomegaly, and the classic diastolic murmur with head bobbing all point to subacute bacterial (infective) endocarditis. Involvement of the aortic valve by vegetations leads to aortic regurgitation, which produces the high\u2011pitched blowing diastolic murmur and Corrigan\u2019s pulse (head bobbing). Thus, the cardiac findings are best explained by aortic regurgitation secondary to infective endocarditis.\n\nAnswer: Aortic regurgitation due to subacute bacterial (infective) endocarditis.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Group D streptococci"}
{"qid": "med:19", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at this clinical presentation, I need to identify the condition causing a thyroid nodule with hyperthyroid symptoms:\n\n**Key findings:**\n- **Physical**: Single 3\u00d73 cm round, non-tender, non-fluctuant, non-pulsatile anterior neck mass (solitary thyroid nodule)\n- **Symptoms**: Hyperthyroid features (sweaty palms/soles, insomnia, irritability, nervousness, fatigue) WITHOUT eye signs\n- **Labs**: Normal ESR (20 mm/hr), normal WBC \u2014 essentially ruling out inflammatory conditions\n- **Absent**: Fever, pain, tenderness, lymphadenopathy (ruling out infectious or inflammatory causes)\n\n**Analysis of differential diagnoses:**\n1. **Subacute thyroiditis (de Quervain's)**: Ruled out by", "correct": false, "gold": "Silent thyroiditis"}
