{"qid": "med:33", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient has an acute **deep venous thrombosis (DVT)** of the popliteal vein. He is young and has a family history of thrombosis, suggesting an **inherited hypercoagulable disorder**.\n\nKey clue: after receiving **unfractionated heparin**, his **aPTT remains essentially unchanged** (26 \u2192 30 sec). Heparin works by **potentiating antithrombin III**, which inactivates thrombin and factor Xa. If there is **antithrombin deficiency**, heparin has little effect, so the aPTT does not appropriately prolong.\n\nInherited **antithrombin III deficiency** causes recurrent venous thrombosis in young patients with otherwise normal PT, aPTT, and platelet count.\n\nAnswer: Inherited antithrombin III deficiency causing heparin resistance and venous thrombosis", "correct": true, "gold": "Antithrombin III deficiency"}
{"qid": "med:33", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has a deep vein thrombosis (DVT) confirmed by ultrasound. He has several risk factors: obesity, smoking, and a family history of pulmonary embolism. After starting heparin, his aPTT is only 30 seconds (therapeutic range typically 60\u201380 seconds), indicating he is not adequately anticoagulated. This suggests heparin resistance, most commonly due to antithrombin III deficiency, which impairs heparin's ability to inactivate thrombin and factor Xa.\n\nAnswer: Antithrombin III deficiency", "correct": true, "gold": "Antithrombin III deficiency"}
{"qid": "med:23", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reason:** This pregnant patient presents with classic features of early disseminated Lyme disease, including erythema migrans, fever, arthralgias, and concerning neurologic symptoms (headache and neck stiffness) suggestive of Lyme meningitis. In pregnancy, doxycycline is contraindicated, and the presence of CNS involvement warrants parenteral therapy. Empiric treatment with intravenous ceftriaxone should be initiated immediately, as serologic tests may be falsely negative early in the disease and delayed treatment increases the risk of maternal and fetal complications. \r\n\r\nAnswer: **Intravenous ceftriaxone.**", "correct": false, "gold": "Amoxicilin"}
{"qid": "med:33", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation (acute unilateral leg pain/swelling, positive Homan sign, ultrasound confirmation of popliteal-vein thrombus) establishes acute DVT. The patient is young, obese, a smoker, and has a first-degree relative with venous thromboembolism, pointing to an underlying inherited thrombophilia rather than a purely acquired risk-factor profile.\nInitial coagulation studies are normal, excluding disseminated intravascular coagulation or lupus anticoagulant. After a standard unfractionated-heparin bolus the aPTT remains essentially unchanged (26 s \u2192 30 s). Heparin exerts its anticoagulant effect only by potentiating antithrombin-III; therefore failure of the aPTT to rise indicates antithrombin-III deficiency. This molecular defect simultaneously explains both the heparin resistance and the patient\u2019s propensity to form thrombus.\nOther common thrombophilias (factor-V Leiden, protein-C or protein-S deficiency) produce normal heparin responsiveness and would not account for the observed laboratory finding.\nAnswer: antithrombin III deficiency (heparin resistance)", "correct": true, "gold": "Antithrombin III deficiency"}
{"qid": "med:33", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Deep venous thrombosis (popliteal vein thrombosis) is the most likely cause of his symptoms.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Antithrombin III deficiency"}
{"qid": "med:29", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "The patient's presentation is classic for **Prinzmetal (variant) angina**. \r\n\r\n### Reasoning:\r\n- **Transient ischemia:** Sudden substernal chest pain at rest with ST-segment elevations on ECG, followed by complete resolution of pain and normalization of ECG within minutes, is characteristic of coronary vasospasm.\r\n- **Risk factors/triggers:** Stress, smoking, and alcohol consumption can all precipitate vasospastic episodes.\r\n- **Exclusion of STEMI:** In acute myocardial infarction, ST elevations persist and cardiac biomarkers would typically rise, neither of which occurred here.\r\n\r\nAnswer: Transient coronary artery vasospasm", "correct": true, "gold": "Coronary artery vasospasm"}
