{"qid": "med:34", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The description of a fever with a pruritic rash that starts on the trunk and spreads to the face and extremities in an unvaccinated child is classic for chickenpox (varicella). The rash typically progresses from macules to papules to vesicles (\"dew drops on a rose petal\") and then crusts.\n\nAnswer: Varicella (chickenpox)", "correct": true, "gold": "Varicella"}
{"qid": "med:34", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "An unvaccinated 18-month-old with **fever** and a **pruritic rash that began on the trunk and spread to the face and extremities** is most consistent with **varicella (chickenpox)**.\n\nKey clues:\n- **Starts on trunk** (\u201ccentripetal\u201d distribution)\n- **Pruritic**\n- Mild fever\n- Unvaccinated child\n\nThis distinguishes it from:\n- **Measles**: starts on the **face** and spreads downward, with cough/coryza/conjunctivitis\n- **Rubella**: face-first spread with postauricular/occipital lymphadenopathy\n- **Roseola**: rash appears **after** high fever resolves\n- **Smallpox**: lesions same stage and more concentrated on face/extremities\n\nAnswer: Varicella (chickenpox)", "correct": true, "gold": "Varicella"}
{"qid": "med:34", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation involves an unvaccinated toddler with concurrent low-grade fever and a pruritic exanthem whose distribution began centrally (trunk) before spreading to the face and limbs. This pattern is the inverse of the centrifugal progression seen in measles or rubella and matches the centripetal spread characteristic of varicella. The lesions are typically polymorphic vesicles on erythematous bases at varying stages of evolution, which are pruritic\u2014features that further distinguish varicella from morbilliform or roseola-like eruptions. Because varicella vaccine is part of the routine childhood schedule, parental refusal directly accounts for susceptibility to primary VZV infection.\nAnswer: varicella (chickenpox)", "correct": true, "gold": "Varicella"}
{"qid": "med:33", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient has an acute deep vein thrombosis (DVT) confirmed by ultrasound. In a young adult with a family history of venous thromboembolism and no obvious acquired triggers, an inherited hypercoagulable disorder is most likely. The critical clue is the failure of unfractionated heparin to prolong the aPTT (remains 30\u202fs after 6\u202fh of infusion). Heparin exerts its anticoagulant effect by potentiating antithrombin; therefore, heparin resistance strongly suggests **antithrombin deficiency**. This autosomal\u2011dominant condition presents with early\u2011onset VTE and a positive family history, matching this patient\u2019s profile.\n\nAnswer: Antithrombin deficiency.", "correct": false, "gold": "Antithrombin III deficiency"}
{"qid": "med:34", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The description of a pruritic rash that begins on the trunk and spreads to the face and extremities, accompanied by low\u2011grade fever in an unvaccinated toddler, is classic for **varicella (chickenpox)**. The rash of varicella is intensely itchy, appears in crops with lesions in various stages (papules, vesicles, crusts), and follows a centripetal distribution (trunk \u2192 face/extremities). Other common childhood exanthems (measles, rubella, roseola, fifth disease) have different rash patterns, lack significant pruritus, or show opposite progression (face to trunk).\n\nAnswer: Varicella (chickenpox).", "correct": true, "gold": "Varicella"}
