{"qid": "med:29", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The sudden onset of rest angina with transient ST\u2011segment elevation that resolves spontaneously within minutes is classic for **Prinzmetal\u2019s (variant) angina**. This condition is caused by focal, reversible spasm of an epicardial coronary artery, producing transient occlusion and transmural ischemia. The patient\u2019s smoking history and stress are risk factors for vasospasm, and the rapid normalization of the ECG makes acute plaque rupture with persistent thrombosis unlikely.\n\nAnswer: Coronary artery vasospasm (Prinzmetal\u2019s angina).", "correct": true, "gold": "Coronary artery vasospasm"}
