{"qid": "med:39", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The presentation includes hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis (suggested by low potassium and chloride), elevated renin and aldosterone, and hypercalciuria. The history of constipation since birth and salt craving points toward a defect in ion transport. This combination\u2014hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis with hypercalciuria and normal blood pressure\u2014is classic for **Bartter syndrome**, which involves a defect in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle.  \n\nAnswer: Bartter syndrome", "correct": false, "gold": "Bartter's syndrome"}
{"qid": "med:39", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This boy has:\n\n- Weakness, polyuria, polydipsia\n- Hypokalemia\n- Mild hypochloremia\n- High renin and high aldosterone\n- Normal blood pressure\n- Hypercalciuria\n- Growth delay\n- Salt craving\n- Constipation since birth\n\nThis pattern is most consistent with **Bartter syndrome**, a defect in the **Na-K-2Cl cotransporter in the thick ascending limb**. Bartter causes renal salt wasting, secondary hyperreninemia/hyperaldosteronism, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis, normal/low blood pressure, and **increased urinary calcium excretion**.\n\nIt is distinguished from Gitelman syndrome by the **hypercalciuria**; Gitelman typically has **hypocalciuria** and often presents later.\n\nAnswer: Bartter syndrome", "correct": false, "gold": "Bartter's syndrome"}
{"qid": "med:39", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation of lifelong polyuria/polydipsia, salt craving, growth failure, chronic hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis, and secondary hyperaldosteronism immediately suggests a renal salt-wasting disorder.  \n\nHypercalciuria (rather than hypocalciuria) and onset in early childhood distinguish classic Bartter syndrome from Gitelman syndrome. Other differentials are excluded by the laboratory pattern: primary aldosteronism and Liddle syndrome suppress renin, while pseudohypoaldosteronism type 1 produces hyperkalemia.  \n\nThus the clinical and biochemical picture is diagnostic of Bartter syndrome.  \n\nAnswer: Bartter syndrome", "correct": false, "gold": "Bartter's syndrome"}
