{"qid": "med:40", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This child most likely has **minimal change disease**, the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children. The biopsy finding of **podocyte foot process effacement on electron microscopy** is classic.\n\nNephrotic syndrome causes:\n- **Massive proteinuria**\n- **Hypoalbuminemia**\n- **Edema**\n- **Hyperlipidemia/lipiduria**\n\nSo the urinalysis would most likely show **heavy proteinuria with fatty casts/oval fat bodies**.\n\nAnswer: **Marked proteinuria with lipiduria (fatty casts/oval fat bodies)**", "correct": false, "gold": "Fatty casts and albumin"}
{"qid": "med:35", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "The patient has chronic hepatitis C infection, as evidenced by a positive HCV IgG and a markedly elevated HCV RNA level (100,000,000 IU/mL). His symptoms of fatigue and arthralgias are consistent with extrahepatic manifestations of HCV. \r\n\r\nGiven his clinical presentation (no signs of cirrhosis on exam), the recommended first-line treatment is a pan-genotypic direct-acting antiviral (DAA) regimen. \r\n\r\n**Answer:** Glecaprevir/pibrentasvir (Mavyret) for 8 weeks.", "correct": false, "gold": "Sofosbuvir and simeprevir"}
{"qid": "med:40", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The presentation of periorbital edema, lower extremity pitting edema, and podocyte effacement on electron microscopy is classic for minimal change disease. This typically presents with nephrotic syndrome, which includes heavy proteinuria, hypoalbuminemia, and edema. On urinalysis, the key finding would be high levels of protein, often measured as 3+ to 4+ on dipstick, and possibly oval fat bodies or lipid droplets due to hyperlipidemia. There is usually no hematuria or significant cellular casts.\n\nAnswer: Heavy proteinuria (3+ to 4+ on dipstick)", "correct": false, "gold": "Fatty casts and albumin"}
{"qid": "med:40", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Urinalysis findings: Selective, heavy proteinuria (predominantly albumin) without hematuria \u2014 likely with fatty/oval fat bodies and possible fatty casts, but no RBCs or RBC casts.**\n\n**Reasoning:** This clinical picture (periorbital and lower extremity edema in a child following an upper respiratory infection, with podocyte foot process effacement on electron microscopy and no immune deposits) is classic for **Minimal Change Disease (MCD)**, the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children. \n\nIn MCD:\n- The glomerular basement membrane loses its negative charge selectivity due to podocyte injury, leading to **selective proteinuria** (mainly albumin, minimal larger proteins like IgG).\n- Since the glomerular capillary architecture (basement membrane structure) remains otherwise intact, there is typically **no hematuria** and **no RBC casts** (distinguishing it from glomerulonephritis).\n- Heavy proteinuria can lead to **lipiduria**, seen as fatty casts or oval fat bodies under polarized light due to associated hyperlipidemia in nephrotic syndrome.\n- Urinalysis is otherwise unremarkable for cellular casts, given the absence of significant inflammation or immune complex deposition.\n\nAnswer: Selective proteinuria (predominantly albumin) without hematuria.", "correct": false, "gold": "Fatty casts and albumin"}
