{"qid": "med:41", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient developed a painful, burning red rash on sun-exposed areas (face, neck, hands) shortly after brief sun exposure, despite wearing a long-sleeved shirt. She is on demeclocycline, a tetracycline antibiotic known to cause photosensitivity. Amoxicillin is less commonly associated with photosensitivity. The rapid onset and distribution strongly point to a phototoxic reaction, which is a known adverse effect of demeclocycline.\n\nAnswer: Demeclocycline-induced phototoxicity.", "correct": false, "gold": "Cell injury due to drug metabolites"}
{"qid": "med:40", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation of periorbital and dependent edema in a school-age child after a nonspecific upper-respiratory illness is the classic setting for idiopathic nephrotic syndrome, most often minimal-change disease. The biopsy finding of diffuse podocyte foot-process effacement on electron microscopy without immune deposits or light-microscopic changes confirms this diagnosis and excludes post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (which would show subepithelial humps and endocapillary proliferation).  \n\nMinimal-change disease produces selective glomerular proteinuria that reaches nephrotic range (>3.5 g/day). Because there is no glomerular inflammation or necrosis, erythrocytes and cellular casts are absent. Consequently, dipstick urinalysis shows heavy protein (typically 3\u20134+) with no blood or RBC casts; lipiduria (free fat droplets or oval fat bodies) may also be seen but is secondary to the hyperlipidemia of nephrotic syndrome.  \n\nThis pattern distinguishes the case from nephritic syndromes, in which hematuria and RBC casts predominate and proteinuria remains sub-nephrotic.  \n\nAnswer: 4+ protein, no RBCs/RBC casts (lipiduria possible)", "correct": false, "gold": "Fatty casts and albumin"}
{"qid": "med:41", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient has an acute **photosensitivity reaction** after brief sun exposure, with burning erythema and papules limited to sun-exposed areas. The most likely culprit is **demeclocycline**, a tetracycline that commonly causes **phototoxicity**.\n\nKey clues:\n- Rash developed **within 30 minutes** of sun exposure\n- Involves **sun-exposed skin** only\n- Symptoms are like an exaggerated sunburn: **painful, burning, red**\n- **Demeclocycline** is well known to cause **phototoxic skin injury**\n\nThis is not due to amoxicillin, which is not a typical cause of photosensitivity.\n\nAnswer: Demeclocycline-induced phototoxic reaction from UV light exposure.", "correct": false, "gold": "Cell injury due to drug metabolites"}
{"qid": "med:41", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Demeclocycline-induced photosensitivity (phototoxic reaction).<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Cell injury due to drug metabolites"}
{"qid": "med:41", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation is an acute phototoxic reaction (erythema, burning pain, and a papular eruption strictly limited to sun-exposed skin after minimal UV exposure). Among the patient\u2019s medications, demeclocycline is a well-documented tetracycline-class photosensitizer that generates reactive oxygen species upon UVA absorption, directly damaging keratinocytes. Amoxicillin can rarely produce photoallergic eruptions, but these are T-cell\u2013mediated, require prior sensitization, and are far less common. The patient\u2019s skin type and brief sun exposure explain the severity only when a potent phototoxic drug is present; the underlying small-cell carcinoma itself does not produce such an immediate, sharply localized cutaneous reaction. Thus the symptoms are attributable to demeclocycline-induced phototoxicity.\n\nAnswer: demeclocycline phototoxicity (drug-induced)", "correct": false, "gold": "Cell injury due to drug metabolites"}
{"qid": "med:40", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "This clinical presentation is classic for **minimal change disease** (MCD), the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children. Key features supporting this diagnosis include:\n- Age (childhood peak 2-6 years)\n- Nephrotic presentation (periorbital and peripheral edema)\n- Recent upper respiratory infection (common trigger)\n- Kidney biopsy showing **podocyte foot process effacement** (the pathognomonic finding of MCD and other podocytopathies)\n\nMinimal change disease causes nephrotic syndrome, which is defined by heavy proteinuria (>50 mg/kg/day in children or >3.5 g/day in adults), hypoalbuminemia, edema, and hyperlipidemia.\n\nOn urinalysis, the hallmark finding is **heavy proteinuria** (3-", "correct": false, "gold": "Fatty casts and albumin"}
