{"qid": "med:46", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "For a **medical abortion at 6 weeks gestation**, the standard medication regimen is **mifepristone**, typically followed by **misoprostol**.\n\n- **Mifepristone** is a **progesterone receptor antagonist** that disrupts the pregnancy.\n- **Misoprostol** is a **prostaglandin E1 analog** that induces uterine contractions to expel the products of conception.\n\nIf the question asks for the key drug used to medically abort an early pregnancy, the best answer is **mifepristone**.\n\nAnswer: **Mifepristone**", "correct": true, "gold": "Mifepristone"}
{"qid": "med:35", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at this case, the patient has chronic hepatitis C infection (HCV RNA 100,000,000 IU/mL) with risk factors (prior IV drug use) and symptoms consistent with chronic HCV (fatigue, arthralgias). He is hepatitis B immune (isolated anti-HBs positive from vaccination) and has no hepatitis A immunity.\n\nKey points:\n- Chronic HCV with high viral load requires treatment regardless of fibrosis stage per current guidelines\n- Physical exam shows no signs of decompensated cirrhosis (no ascites, edema, or jaundice)\n- Current standard of care uses **direct-acting antiviral agents (DAAs)**, not interferon-based therapy\n- Since HCV genotype is unknown, a **pangenotypic regimen** is preferred\n- The patient takes glyburide, which is contraindicated with glecaprevir-containing regimens (OATP1B1 inhibition increases hypoglycemia risk)\n- Safe options include sofosbuvir/velpatasvir (Epclusa) for 12 weeks or glecaprevir/pibrentasvir (Mavyret) for 8 weeks (if diabetes medication is switched)\n\nThe treatment-naive patient without cirrhosis should receive DAA combination therapy to achieve sustained virologic response.\n\nAnswer: Direct-acting antiviral agents (e.g., sofosbuvir/velpatasvir or glecaprevir/pibrentasvir).", "correct": false, "gold": "Sofosbuvir and simeprevir"}
{"qid": "med:46", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Mifepristone (200\u202fmg orally) followed by misoprostol (800\u202fmcg) 24\u201348\u202fhours later.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "Mifepristone"}
{"qid": "med:46", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The standard approach to medical abortion at 6 weeks\u2019 gestation relies on pharmacologic blockade of progesterone action, which is essential for maintaining the early pregnancy. Mifepristone is a competitive antagonist at the progesterone receptor; a single oral dose produces decidual breakdown, cervical softening, and detachment of the conceptus. Twenty-four to forty-eight hours later a prostaglandin analogue (misoprostol) is given to stimulate uterine contractions that complete expulsion. Because the question asks for the drug administered for this purpose and because mifepristone is the agent that specifically interrupts progesterone-dependent pregnancy maintenance, it is the medication indicated in this clinical scenario.\n\nAnswer: mifepristone (RU-486)", "correct": true, "gold": "Mifepristone"}
{"qid": "med:42", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\nThis patient presents with classic signs of acute pyelonephritis, including fever, chills, left costovertebral angle tenderness, and a urinalysis showing pyuria, nitrite positivity, and numerous WBC casts. His risk factors (type 2 diabetes mellitus, chronic kidney disease, and prior foot ulcer) classify this as a complicated urinary tract infection. After obtaining urine and blood cultures, the most appropriate next step is to promptly initiate empiric intravenous antibiotics to prevent progression to sepsis or further renal injury. \r\n\r\nAnswer: Initiate empiric intravenous antibiotic therapy.", "correct": false, "gold": "Inpatient treatment with intravenous ciprofloxacin"}
{"qid": "med:47", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "The infant has **osteogenesis imperfecta**, suggested by:\n\n- **Blue sclerae**\n- **Joint hypermobility**\n- **Hypotonia**\n- **Fracture after minor trauma**\n\nOsteogenesis imperfecta is most commonly due to an **autosomal dominant mutation in type I collagen genes (COL1A1 or COL1A2)**, causing **defective synthesis of type I collagen**. Type I collagen is important in **bone, skin, tendons, dentin, and sclera**, which explains his findings.\n\nAnswer: Defective type I collagen synthesis due to a COL1A1/COL1A2 mutation (osteogenesis imperfecta)", "correct": true, "gold": "Insufficient production of procollagen type 1"}
{"qid": "med:47", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Osteogenesis imperfecta - COL1A1/COL1A2 gene mutation causing defective type I collagen synthesis.\n\nAnswer: Mutation in type I collagen (COL1A1/COL1A2) causing Osteogenesis Imperfecta", "correct": true, "gold": "Insufficient production of procollagen type 1"}
{"qid": "med:46", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Medical abortion at 6 weeks gestation is achieved using a combination regimen. **Mifepristone** (a progesterone receptor antagonist) is administered first to block progesterone, causing decidual necrosis and cervical softening. This is followed 24\u201348 hours later by **misoprostol** (a prostaglandin analog) to induce uterine contractions and expel the products of conception. While both drugs are used, mifepristone is the primary abortifacient that initiates the termination.\n\nAnswer: Mifepristone (followed by misoprostol).", "correct": true, "gold": "Mifepristone"}
{"qid": "med:46", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\nFor a medical abortion at 6 weeks gestation, the standard first-line regimen is a combination of **mifepristone** and **misoprostol**. Mifepristone is a progesterone receptor antagonist that blocks progesterone, leading to decidual breakdown and detachment of the pregnancy. This is followed by misoprostol, a prostaglandin E1 analog, which induces uterine contractions to expel the pregnancy. Mifepristone is the initial drug administered in this regimen. \r\n\r\nAnswer: Mifepristone", "correct": true, "gold": "Mifepristone"}
{"qid": "med:47", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The presentation of a fracture with minimal trauma, blue-gray sclerae, joint hypermobility, and decreased tone in an infant is classic for osteogenesis imperfecta (OI), a genetic disorder of collagen synthesis. The X-ray showing an incomplete fracture (often a \"bowing\" or \"greenstick\" fracture) further supports this. Child abuse is a key differential, but the sclerae and hypermobility point to OI.\n\nAnswer: Osteogenesis imperfecta", "correct": false, "gold": "Insufficient production of procollagen type 1"}
{"qid": "med:47", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The infant\u2019s findings\u2014blue\u2011gray sclerae, fragile bones with an incomplete tibial fracture after minimal trauma, joint hypermobility, and generalized hypotonia\u2014are classic for osteogenesis imperfecta. This condition results from an autosomal\u2011dominant defect in collagen type\u202fI production, usually due to mutations in the COL1A1 or COL1A2 genes, leading to brittle bones, easy bruising, and characteristic ocular findings.\n\n**Answer:** Osteogenesis imperfecta caused by a collagen type\u202fI synthesis defect (COL1A1/COL1A2 mutation).<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "Insufficient production of procollagen type 1"}
{"qid": "med:43", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Answer: \\(\\boxed{\\text{Renal papillary necrosis}}\\)", "correct": true, "gold": "Papillary necrosis"}
{"qid": "med:47", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation of recurrent fractures after minimal trauma, blue sclerae, joint laxity, and hypotonia in an otherwise healthy infant is pathognomonic for osteogenesis imperfecta (most often type I). OI results from quantitative or qualitative defects in type I collagen, the predominant structural protein of bone, sclerae, and ligaments. Defective collagen produces brittle osteopenic bone that fractures under trivial load (as occurred when the infant struck his leg), thin connective tissue that allows the underlying choroid to impart a blue-gray hue to the sclerae, and lax ligaments that manifest as hypermobility. Child abuse, rickets, and hypophosphatasia lack this constellation of extraskeletal findings and are therefore excluded.\nAnswer: defective type I collagen synthesis (OI)", "correct": false, "gold": "Insufficient production of procollagen type 1"}
{"qid": "med:43", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \nThe patient\u2019s presentation of gross hematuria, acute colicky flank pain, and proteinuria in a middle\u2011aged woman with a history of high\u2011dose acetaminophen and aspirin use is classic for **renal papillary necrosis** secondary to analgesic nephropathy. Chronic use of mixed analgesics (especially acetaminophen combined with aspirin/NSAIDs) causes ischemic damage to the renal medulla, leading to papillary necrosis. Sloughed necrotic papillae can obstruct the collecting system, producing colicky pain, while the underlying tubular injury explains the proteinuria. Other diagnoses are less likely: nephrolithiasis usually lacks significant proteinuria and is not linked to this medication history; renal cell carcinoma typically presents with painless hematuria and a palpable mass; pyelonephritis would cause fever; and glomerulonephritis is associated with hypertension and edema.\n\n**Answer:** Renal papillary necrosis (analgesic nephropathy).", "correct": true, "gold": "Papillary necrosis"}
{"qid": "med:48", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "A virus with a **helical capsid** and **segmented genome** causing fever, headache, malaise, and cough in a household cluster is most consistent with **influenza virus** (an orthomyxovirus). Influenza commonly causes outbreaks in families.\n\nThe son being asymptomatic despite exposure is most likely due to **preexisting mucosal immunity**, especially **secretory IgA antibodies** from prior infection or vaccination, which can prevent infection at the respiratory epithelium.\n\nAnswer: Preexisting respiratory mucosal immunity due to secretory IgA antibodies against influenza from prior exposure/vaccination.", "correct": false, "gold": "Serum antibodies against hemagglutinin"}
{"qid": "med:48", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Answer: Prior exposure/vaccination gave him immunity, so he had a subclinical (asymptomatic) infection while still being able to transmit the virus \u2014 i.e., he is an asymptomatic carrier due to pre-existing immunity from prior infection or vaccination (likely influenza).", "correct": false, "gold": "Serum antibodies against hemagglutinin"}
{"qid": "med:47", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Answer: **Osteogenesis imperfecta (OI)**", "correct": false, "gold": "Insufficient production of procollagen type 1"}
{"qid": "med:48", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The son is likely free of symptoms because he may have been previously infected with the same virus or a similar strain, leading to immunity. The virus described (helical capsid, segmented genome) is likely influenza virus. The son could be asymptomatic due to prior vaccination or infection, or he might have a milder or subclinical case.\n\nAnswer: The son is likely immune due to prior infection or vaccination.", "correct": false, "gold": "Serum antibodies against hemagglutinin"}
{"qid": "med:47", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at this case, I need to identify the underlying condition causing bone fragility, blue sclerae, and joint hypermobility in an 11-month-old.\n\n**Key clinical findings:**\n1. **Blue-grayish sclerae** - This is a pathognomonic feature of connective tissue disorders, particularly Osteogenesis Imperfecta (OI)\n2. **Fracture from minimal trauma** - An incomplete tibial fracture resulted from simply hitting the leg against a chair, indicating severe bone fragility\n3. **Joint hypermobility** - Common in collagen disorders\n4. **Normal growth and development** - Length and weight are normal (15th-25th percentile), and milestones are appropriate\n\n**Analysis:**\n- **Child abuse** is always a consideration with fractures in infants, but the presence of blue sclerae and joint hypermobility indicates an underlying genetic condition rather than non-accidental trauma.\n- **Rickets/Vitamin D deficiency** can cause bone fragility but does not cause blue sclerae or joint hypermobility.\n- **Ehlers-Danlos syndrome** causes joint hypermobility but typically does not cause blue sclerae or fractures from such minimal trauma.\n- **Osteogenesis Imperfecta (Type I)** fits perfectly: it is the most common form, presents with blue sclerae, bone fragility leading to fractures with minimal trauma, joint laxity, and normal stature/intelligence. The sclerae appear blue because the underlying collagen defect makes the sclera thin and translucent, allowing the choroid to show through.\n\nThe combination of blue sclerae + fracture from trivial trauma + joint hypermobility is classic for Osteogenesis Imperfecta.\n\nAnswer: Osteogenesis Imperfecta (brittle bone disease)", "correct": false, "gold": "Insufficient production of procollagen type 1"}
{"qid": "med:48", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The described syndrome (acute flu-like illness with fever, malaise, headache, and dry cough) is characteristic of seasonal influenza in the setting of a household outbreak. The causative agent is confirmed by the laboratory clue of a helical nucleocapsid plus a segmented, negative-sense RNA genome, which is pathognomonic for an orthomyxovirus (influenza A or B).\nIn a multigenerational household, symptomatic infection in children and the elderly with sparing of one healthy adult is most commonly explained by vaccine-induced humoral immunity in that adult. Annual influenza vaccination elicits neutralizing IgG and mucosal IgA directed against the viral hemagglutinin, aborting clinical illness even when other family members are infected. Prior natural infection could produce similar protection, but routine adult vaccination is the far more prevalent and therefore most likely explanation.\nAnswer: son vaccinated (protective Abs vs. influenza)", "correct": false, "gold": "Serum antibodies against hemagglutinin"}
{"qid": "med:58", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This pattern suggests **obstructive (posthepatic) jaundice**.\n\nReasoning:\n- **Bilirubin in urine** means there is increased **conjugated bilirubin** in the blood, because only conjugated bilirubin is water-soluble and can be excreted in urine.\n- **Low urobilinogen** indicates that little bilirubin is reaching the intestine, so gut bacteria cannot convert it into urobilinogen.\n- **Pruritus** is also typical of **cholestasis/bile duct obstruction** due to retained bile salts.\n\nSo the most likely cause is **extrahepatic biliary obstruction** (for example, blockage of the common bile duct by a gallstone or pancreatic/biliary tumor).\n\nAnswer: Obstruction of the extrahepatic biliary tract causing conjugated hyperbilirubinemia (posthepatic cholestasis).", "correct": false, "gold": "Defective hepatic bile excretion"}
{"qid": "med:58", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has jaundice with elevated urine bilirubin and low urine urobilinogen. Elevated urine bilirubin indicates conjugated (direct) hyperbilirubinemia, since only conjugated bilirubin is water-soluble and can appear in urine. Low urobilinogen suggests decreased bilirubin reaching the intestine, which occurs with biliary obstruction (obstructive jaundice). In a 65-year-old man, the most likely cause of obstructive jaundice is a malignant obstruction, such as pancreatic cancer (e.g., adenocarcinoma of the pancreatic head).\n\nAnswer: Pancreatic cancer (obstructive jaundice due to malignancy)", "correct": false, "gold": "Defective hepatic bile excretion"}
{"qid": "med:58", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Most likely cause: Extrahepatic biliary obstruction (post-hepatic/obstructive jaundice)** \u2014 e.g., due to a pancreatic head carcinoma or a common bile duct gallstone.\n\n**Reasoning:**\n- **Elevated urine bilirubin** indicates the presence of **conjugated (direct) bilirubin**, which is water-soluble and can be filtered by the kidneys. This means the liver is successfully conjugating bilirubin (hepatocyte function is preserved), but the conjugated bilirubin is not reaching the intestine normally.\n- **Low urine urobilinogen** indicates that bilirubin is not reaching the intestinal lumen in sufficient amounts, so gut bacteria cannot convert it into urobilinogen (which is normally partially absorbed and excreted in urine).\n- Together, these findings point to **obstruction of bile flow** between the liver and the intestine (e.g., in the common bile duct), preventing conjugated bilirubin from being excreted into the gut, while it backs up into the bloodstream and is filtered into the urine.\n\nThis pattern is characteristic of **obstructive (post-hepatic) jaundice**, as opposed to:\n- **Hemolytic jaundice** (unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia \u2014 bilirubin absent from urine, urobilinogen increased)\n- **Hepatocellular jaundice** (both bilirubin and urobilinogen typically present in urine, though patterns can vary)\n\nIn a 65-year-old man, this presentation raises concern for **malignant biliary obstruction**, such as **pancreatic head cancer** or **cholangiocarcinoma**, compressing or invading the common bile duct.\n\nAnswer: Extrahepatic biliary obstruction (obstructive/post-hepatic jaundice), such as from a pancreatic head tumor or bile duct stone, causing impaired excretion of conjugated bilirubin into the intestine.", "correct": false, "gold": "Defective hepatic bile excretion"}
{"qid": "med:48", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The virus described (helical capsid, segmented genome) is most consistent with influenza virus. The son\u2019s lack of symptoms despite exposure suggests he has protective immunity\u2014most commonly from recent influenza vaccination (or prior infection)\u2014that prevented him from developing clinical disease.\n\nAnswer: He likely has protective immunity (e.g., from recent influenza vaccination or prior exposure) that prevented symptomatic infection.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Serum antibodies against hemagglutinin"}
{"qid": "med:58", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation (acute jaundice + pruritus) with lab findings of bilirubinuria + absent/low urobilinogen is the classic pattern of conjugated hyperbilirubinemia caused by impaired biliary outflow. Conjugated bilirubin therefore regurgitates into the bloodstream and spills into urine, while no bilirubin reaches the gut to be converted into urobilinogen. In a 65-year-old this is produced most commonly by mechanical obstruction of the extrahepatic biliary tree (choledocholithiasis or carcinoma of the pancreatic head).\nAnswer: extrahepatic biliary obstruction (e.g., CBD stone/tumor)", "correct": false, "gold": "Defective hepatic bile excretion"}
