{"qid": "med:68", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Staphylococcus aureus\n\n**Answer: Staphylococcus aureus**", "correct": false, "gold": "Staphylococcus epidermidis"}
{"qid": "med:68", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "A prosthetic joint infection occurring **4 months after total knee arthroplasty** is most consistent with a **delayed prosthetic joint infection**. These are classically caused by **low-virulence skin flora that form biofilms**, especially **coagulase-negative staphylococci**, most commonly **Staphylococcus epidermidis**.\n\nAnswer: Staphylococcus epidermidis", "correct": true, "gold": "Staphylococcus epidermidis"}
{"qid": "med:68", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has septic arthritis of a prosthetic joint, which typically occurs within the first year after arthroplasty. The most common causative organism in early prosthetic joint infections (within 3 months to 1 year) is *Staphylococcus aureus*, including coagulase-positive staphylococci. Other possibilities like coagulase-negative staphylococci are more common in later infections, but given the timing and acute presentation, *S. aureus* is most likely.\n\nAnswer: Staphylococcus aureus", "correct": false, "gold": "Staphylococcus epidermidis"}
{"qid": "med:48", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "\\[\n\\textbf{Answer: The son has pre-existing immunity to the virus (from prior infection or vaccination), which protected him from developing symptomatic disease.}\n\\]", "correct": false, "gold": "Serum antibodies against hemagglutinin"}
{"qid": "med:68", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The case involves a prosthetic joint infection (PJI) presenting 4 months after total knee arthroplasty, with the prosthesis ultimately removed. PJIs are timed from the index surgery to guide both microbiology and surgical management:\n\n- Early postoperative PJI (<4 weeks) is typically caused by virulent organisms introduced at implantation (most often S. aureus or aerobic gram-negative rods).  \n- Delayed/chronic PJI (roughly 4 weeks to 12\u201324 months) is caused by less virulent skin flora that were introduced at surgery but produce indolent biofilm disease; coagulase-negative staphylococci (principally S. epidermidis) predominate.  \n- Late hematogenous PJI (>12\u201324 months) arises from distant bacteremia and is again dominated by S. aureus.\n\nFour months places the infection squarely in the delayed category. The decision to explant the prosthesis rather than attempt debridement with implant retention further supports a chronic, biofilm-centered process typical of low-virulence organisms. The modest fever and subacute symptom duration are also consistent with this microbiology.\n\nThus the single most likely pathogen is Staphylococcus epidermidis (coagulase-negative staphylococcus).\n\nAnswer: Staphylococcus epidermidis (CoNS)", "correct": true, "gold": "Staphylococcus epidermidis"}
{"qid": "med:26", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Answer: Aortic stenosis}}\n\\]", "correct": false, "gold": "Increased ventricular wall stiffness"}
{"qid": "med:42", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient has acute pyelonephritis (fever, flank pain/CVA tenderness, pyuria with WBC casts, leukocytosis) complicated by diabetes and chronic kidney disease. Because he is febrile >39\u202f\u00b0C, tachycardic, and has severe infection with comorbidities, he requires hospitalization and prompt empiric intravenous antibiotics after cultures have been drawn. Imaging or surgical intervention is not indicated initially. Ceftriaxone (or another appropriate IV agent such as cefepime or piperacillin\u2011tazobactam if Pseudomonas is a concern) is preferred, avoiding nephrotoxic drugs like aminoglycosides given his CKD.\n\nAnswer: Hospitalize and start empiric intravenous antibiotics (e.g., ceftriaxone).", "correct": false, "gold": "Inpatient treatment with intravenous ciprofloxacin"}
{"qid": "med:70", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "The key clue is the **early systolic murmur with a split S2 at the right upper sternal border**, which is classic for a **bicuspid aortic valve**. Bicuspid aortic valve is strongly associated with **coarctation of the aorta**, a congenital narrowing of the aorta that commonly causes **secondary hypertension** in adults.\n\nIn older patients, coarctation may present mainly as **persistent upper-extremity hypertension** despite therapy. The absence of renal bruits makes renovascular hypertension less likely, and he has no symptoms suggesting obstructive sleep apnea.\n\nThus, the most likely underlying and classic cause is:\n\n**Coarctation of the aorta due to a congenital bicuspid aortic valve association.**\n\nAnswer: Coarctation of the aorta (classically associated with a bicuspid aortic valve)", "correct": false, "gold": "Stiffening of the aorta"}
{"qid": "med:68", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The patient has a prosthetic joint infection presenting four months after total knee arthroplasty, which is classified as a delayed (chronic) infection. In delayed prosthetic joint infections, coagulase\u2011negative staphylococci, especially *Staphylococcus epidermidis*, are the most common causative organisms.\n\nAnswer: Staphylococcus epidermidis<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "Staphylococcus epidermidis"}
