{"qid": "med:70", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Most likely cause: Age-related arterial stiffening (decreased aortic/large vessel compliance) \u2014 i.e., primary (essential) hypertension due to atherosclerotic changes of the vasculature, resulting in isolated systolic hypertension.**\n\n**Reasoning:**\n- This patient is 70 years old with a wide pulse pressure (150/100, and previously up to 170/80), which is characteristic of **isolated systolic hypertension (ISH)** \u2014 a hallmark of aging-related arterial stiffness rather than a secondary cause.\n- Secondary causes of hypertension (renal artery stenosis, primary aldosteronism, pheochromocytoma, OSA, etc.) are less likely here:\n  - No snoring/daytime somnolence \u2192 argues against **obstructive sleep apnea**.\n  - No renal or abdominal bruits \u2192 argues against **renal artery stenosis**.\n  - No electrolyte clues (e.g., hypokalemia) suggesting **hyperaldosteronism**.\n  - No symptoms of catecholamine excess (palpitations, diaphoresis, headaches) suggesting **pheochromocytoma**.\n- The systolic murmur with split S2 at the right upper sternal border is consistent with **aortic sclerosis/stiffening**, a common finding in elderly patients that reflects the same vascular aging process causing systolic hypertension \u2014 not a separate valvular pathology causing secondary hypertension.\n- Given his age and clinical presentation, **the most common cause of hypertension in this age group is loss of arterial elasticity (arteriosclerosis) leading to increased systolic pressure and widened pulse pressure**, rather than a secondary, correctable cause.\n\nAnswer: Age-related arterial stiffness (arteriosclerosis) causing isolated systolic hypertension \u2014 the most common etiology of hypertension in elderly patients.", "correct": false, "gold": "Stiffening of the aorta"}
{"qid": "med:70", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has isolated systolic hypertension (ISH) with a wide pulse pressure (150/100, pulse pressure 50 mmHg). In a 70-year-old, the most common cause is age-related arterial stiffness due to atherosclerosis and loss of vascular compliance. The murmur described (early systolic, right upper sternal border, split S2) is likely an aortic sclerosis murmur, which is also common in older adults and consistent with this pathophysiology. There are no signs of secondary hypertension (no bruits, normal sleep, no snoring).\n\nAnswer: Age-related arterial stiffness (isolated systolic hypertension)", "correct": false, "gold": "Stiffening of the aorta"}
{"qid": "med:70", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The patient\u2019s age, cardiovascular risk factors, lack of specific physical findings suggesting a secondary cause (e.g., bruits, obesity, endocrine features), and the fact that over 90\u202f% of hypertension in adults is idiopathic support the most common etiology.\n\n**Answer:** Essential (primary) hypertension.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Stiffening of the aorta"}
