{"qid": "med:74", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "These are most consistent with **seborrheic keratoses**.\n\nClues:\n- **Older adult**\n- **Brown, greasy/waxy \u201cstuck-on\u201d lesions**\n- Common on the **face and trunk**\n- Patients often say they look like they could be \u201cpeeled off\u201d\n\nAnswer: Seborrheic keratoses", "correct": false, "gold": "Seborrheic keratosis"}
{"qid": "med:46", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient is requesting a medical abortion at 6 weeks gestation. The standard regimen for early medical abortion (up to 10 weeks) is mifepristone followed by misoprostol. Mifepristone is a progesterone receptor antagonist that blocks progesterone, causing decidual necrosis and cervical softening. Misoprostol, a prostaglandin E1 analog, is then given to induce uterine contractions and expel the pregnancy.\n\nAnswer: mifepristone (followed by misoprostol)", "correct": true, "gold": "Mifepristone"}
{"qid": "med:74", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The description of brown, greasy-appearing lesions that cannot be peeled off, especially on the forehead of an older adult, is classic for seborrheic keratoses. These are benign, waxy, \"stuck-on\" lesions common in aging skin.\n\nAnswer: Seborrheic keratosis", "correct": true, "gold": "Seborrheic keratosis"}
{"qid": "med:74", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The description of multiple brown, waxy/greasy, \u201cstuck-on\u201d lesions that cannot be peeled off in an elderly patient is pathognomonic for seborrheic keratoses (benign epidermal proliferations). Differential considerations such as actinic keratoses (rough, scaly, non-greasy) or solar lentigines (flat macules) do not match the clinical features given.\nAnswer: seborrheic keratoses (stuck-on)", "correct": false, "gold": "Seborrheic keratosis"}
{"qid": "med:70", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient is elderly, with persistently elevated BP despite lifestyle measures and two antihypertensive agents. Secondary causes have been excluded by the history (no sleep-disordered breathing) and physical examination (no abdominal or flank bruits).  \n\nAt this age the dominant pathophysiologic mechanism is progressive stiffening of the aorta and other large elastic arteries. Loss of elastin and accumulation of collagen reduce arterial compliance; each stroke volume therefore produces a greater rise in systolic pressure, while the reflected pulse wave returns earlier and augments afterload. The wide pulse-pressure readings recorded on prior visits are the hemodynamic signature of this process.  \n\nThus the most common cause of hypertension in this demographic is decreased large-artery compliance.\n\nAnswer: decreased aortic/large-artery compliance (age-related)", "correct": false, "gold": "Stiffening of the aorta"}
{"qid": "med:74", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Seborrheic keratosis.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "Seborrheic keratosis"}
{"qid": "med:74", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at this clinical presentation, I need to identify the key features:\n- **Patient**: 77-year-old man (elderly)\n- **Lesion characteristics**: Brown, greasy-appearing, \"stuck-on\" look\n- **Symptom**: Cannot be peeled off (though they look like they could be)\n- **Location**: Forehead\n\nThis combination is classic for **seborrheic keratosis**. These are benign epidermal tumors that are extremely common in older adults. They typically present as well-demarcated, waxy, \"stuck-on\" appearing papules or plaques that can range in color from tan to dark brown. The \"greasy\" appearance and the fact that they look like they could be peeled off but are actually firmly adherent are hallmark features.\n\nOther diagnoses to consider but that don't fit as well:\n- **Actinic keratosis**: Usually rough, scaly, erythematous patches, not greasy\n- **Solar lentigo**: Flat macules without the raised, waxy appearance\n- **Melanoma**: Usually solitary, irregular, evolving lesion, not typically multiple and greasy\n- **Skin tags**: Pedunculated, flesh-colored, not brown and greasy\n\nAnswer: Seborrheic keratosis", "correct": true, "gold": "Seborrheic keratosis"}
{"qid": "med:75", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "The presentation is most consistent with **follicular thyroid carcinoma**:\n\n- solitary thyroid nodule\n- suspicion of neoplasia on FNA\n- spread commonly checked in **bone and lungs**\n- treated with **near-total thyroidectomy followed by radioiodine**\n\nFollicular thyroid carcinoma typically spreads **hematogenously** rather than via lymphatics. The **most likely initial metastatic site** is the **lungs**.\n\nAnswer: Lungs", "correct": false, "gold": "Inferior thyroid arteries"}
{"qid": "med:70", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at this case, the question asks for the **most common cause** of this patient's hypertension. This is an epidemiological question about hypertension classification.\n\nKey analysis:\n- The patient is 70 years old with typical cardiovascular risk factors (age, hyperlipidemia)\n- His blood pressure is elevated (150/100 mmHg) despite two antihypertensive medications\n- Physical exam shows an aortic systolic murmur consistent with aortic sclerosis (common in elderly patients with hypertension), but no bruits to suggest renovascular disease\n- There are no clinical features suggesting secondary causes (no hypokalemia, no episodic symptoms, no Cushingoid features, no sleep apnea)\n\n**Essential (primary) hypertension** accounts for approximately 90-95% of all hypertension cases in adults, including the elderly. While secondary causes like renal artery stenosis become more prevalent with age, they still represent only 5-10% of hypertension cases. The presence of atherosclerotic risk factors and the absence of specific findings pointing to secondary causes support this being essential hypertension.\n\nThe aortic murmur is a distractor representing aortic sclerosis (a marker of cardiovascular disease associated with hypertension but not a cause of it). The unremarkable vascular exam helps exclude secondary renovascular hypertension, but regardless, essential hypertension remains the most common cause by far.\n\nAnswer: Essential hypertension (or primary hypertension)", "correct": false, "gold": "Stiffening of the aorta"}
{"qid": "med:75", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The description points to a thyroid malignancy, most likely papillary thyroid carcinoma given the presentation (fixed, non-tender nodule, hypoechoic on ultrasound, and likely neoplasia on cytology). For papillary thyroid carcinoma, the most common initial route of metastasis is via lymphatics to regional lymph nodes in the neck.\n\nAnswer: Regional lymph nodes (cervical lymph nodes)", "correct": false, "gold": "Inferior thyroid arteries"}
{"qid": "med:68", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Answer: \\(\\textbf{Staphylococcus epidermidis}\\)", "correct": true, "gold": "Staphylococcus epidermidis"}
{"qid": "med:48", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The virus with a helical capsid and segmented genome is influenza virus (family Orthomyxoviridae). In a household outbreak, an asymptomatic contact is most likely protected by pre\u2011existing immunity. Annual influenza vaccination is recommended for all adults, especially those living with high\u2011risk individuals (such as the elderly), and prior infection can also confer protection. Therefore, the son\u2019s lack of symptoms is best explained by immunity from prior vaccination or previous infection.\n\nAnswer: He has pre\u2011existing immunity (most likely from prior vaccination or previous infection).", "correct": false, "gold": "Serum antibodies against hemagglutinin"}
{"qid": "med:75", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Lungs<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Inferior thyroid arteries"}
{"qid": "med:75", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "## Analysis\n\n**Clinical clues pointing to Follicular Thyroid Carcinoma:**\n- Solitary, fixed, hypoechoic thyroid nodule\n- Cytology showing \"suspicion of neoplasia\" (a classic limitation of FNA for follicular lesions \u2014 capsular/vascular invasion, which defines malignancy, **cannot be assessed on FNA alone** and requires histological examination of the resected specimen)\n- CT performed to screen for **bone and lung metastases** \u2014 the classic distant metastatic sites for follicular thyroid carcinoma\n- Treatment plan: near-total thyroidectomy followed by **radioiodine (I-131) therapy** \u2014 follicular carcinoma retains iodine-avidity, making RAI effective for treating metastases\n\n**Key pathophysiological point:**\nUnlike papillary thyroid carcinoma (which spreads primarily via **lymphatic** channels to cervical lymph nodes), follicular thyroid carcinoma has a strong propensity for **hematogenous spread** due to its vascular invasiveness. This is why distant metastases (lung, bone) are more characteristic of follicular carcinoma even at initial presentation, whereas lymph node involvement is less common.\n\nBecause tumor cells access the bloodstream directly (via vascular invasion) and travel through the venous system to the right heart and then to the pulmonary capillary bed first, the **lungs** are the most likely initial site of hematogenous metastasis before spreading further systemically (e.g., to bone).\n\n---\n\n**Answer: The lungs** (via hematogenous spread, characteristic of follicular thyroid carcinoma)", "correct": false, "gold": "Inferior thyroid arteries"}
{"qid": "med:74", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\nThis 77-year-old man presents with several brown, greasy-appearing lesions on his forehead that have a \"stuck-on\" appearance and cannot be easily peeled off. These are classic features of seborrheic keratoses, which are benign epidermal tumors commonly seen in older adults. They often appear on the face, trunk, and upper extremities and have a waxy, verrucous, or greasy surface. \r\n\r\nAnswer: **Seborrheic keratosis**", "correct": true, "gold": "Seborrheic keratosis"}
{"qid": "med:78", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Isolated prolonged aPTT with normal PT and normal CBC/platelets suggests an intrinsic pathway coagulation factor problem rather than thrombocytopenia. In a boy with easy bruising/petechiae and otherwise normal exam, the most likely inherited cause is hemophilia. Hemophilia A (factor VIII deficiency) is the most common and presents with prolonged aPTT and normal PT.\n\nAnswer: Hemophilia A (factor VIII deficiency)", "correct": false, "gold": "Von Willebrand disease"}
{"qid": "med:78", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The normal PT and prolonged aPTT suggest an intrinsic pathway coagulation defect. In a 9-year-old with bruising and petechiae but normal platelet count, the most likely cause is hemophilia A (factor VIII deficiency) or hemophilia B (factor IX deficiency). Given the age and presentation, hemophilia A is more common.  \n\nAnswer: Hemophilia A (factor VIII deficiency)", "correct": false, "gold": "Von Willebrand disease"}
{"qid": "med:75", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The clinical details point to a differentiated thyroid carcinoma, most likely follicular type. This conclusion follows from the FNA result (\u201csuspicion of neoplasia\u201d), which is the typical cytologic phrasing for a follicular neoplasm; definitive distinction between adenoma and carcinoma requires histologic demonstration of capsular or vascular invasion after thyroidectomy. Papillary carcinoma is less likely because its nuclear features usually allow a definitive cytologic diagnosis on FNA.\nFollicular thyroid carcinoma spreads hematogenously rather than via lymphatics. Venous effluent from the thyroid gland first reaches the pulmonary capillary bed; consequently, the lungs are the earliest and most frequent site of distant metastasis. Bone lesions occur later and less often, either by passage through pulmonary shunts or via vertebral venous plexuses. This sequence explains why staging CT is performed to survey both lungs and bones, yet the initial (and statistically most common) metastatic focus remains pulmonary.\nThus the most likely initial metastatic site is the lung.\nAnswer: lungs", "correct": false, "gold": "Inferior thyroid arteries"}
{"qid": "med:75", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "The most likely initial metastatic site in this patient is the **cervical lymph nodes**. \r\n\r\n### Reasoning:\r\n- This presentation is highly suggestive of **papillary thyroid carcinoma (PTC)**, the most common thyroid malignancy. \r\n- PTC has a strong propensity for **lymphatic spread**, with cervical lymph node metastases being the most frequent initial site.\r\n- The nodule is fixed and immobile, which can indicate local invasion or involvement of adjacent lymph nodes.\r\n- While lungs and bones are common distant metastatic sites, they typically occur later in the disease course. \r\n\r\nAnswer: Cervical lymph nodes", "correct": false, "gold": "Inferior thyroid arteries"}
{"qid": "med:79", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Answer: Microtubule disruption impairing axonal transport (vincristine inhibits tubulin polymerization, causing axonal degeneration of peripheral nerves)", "correct": false, "gold": "Inhibition of beta-tubulin polymerization"}
{"qid": "med:78", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Most likely diagnosis: von Willebrand Disease (vWD)**\n\n**Reasoning:**\n\n- **Normal PT (12 sec)**: This indicates the extrinsic and common coagulation pathways (Factors VII, X, V, II, fibrinogen) are functioning normally.\n\n- **Prolonged aPTT (60 sec)**: This indicates a defect in the intrinsic pathway (Factors VIII, IX, XI, XII).\n\n- **Normal platelet count (normal CBC)**: Rules out thrombocytopenia as the cause of bruising/petechiae, but does not rule out a **platelet function defect**.\n\n- **Clinical presentation (petechiae + ecchymoses, mucocutaneous-type bleeding)**: This pattern is characteristic of **platelet-type/primary hemostasis disorders** rather than the deep bleeding (hemarthroses, muscle hematomas) typically seen in classic hemophilias (Factor VIII or IX deficiency).\n\n**Why vWD fits best:**\n\nvon Willebrand factor (vWF) has two main functions:\n1. It facilitates platelet adhesion to the subendothelium at sites of vascular injury (primary hemostasis)\n2. It acts as a carrier protein for Factor VIII, protecting it from degradation\n\nIn vWD, deficient or dysfunctional vWF leads to:\n- **Reduced Factor VIII levels** \u2192 prolongs aPTT (since Factor VIII is part of the intrinsic pathway)\n- **Impaired platelet adhesion** \u2192 causes mucocutaneous bleeding, petechiae, and easy bruising despite normal platelet counts\n\nThis combination \u2014 **normal PT, prolonged aPTT, normal platelet count, but petechiae and bruising** \u2014 is the classic presentation of von Willebrand disease, the most common inherited bleeding disorder.\n\n(Hemophilia A would show similar coagulation profile findings, but typically presents with hemarthroses and deep tissue bleeding rather than petechiae, making vWD more likely given this clinical picture.)\n\nAnswer: Von Willebrand disease", "correct": true, "gold": "Von Willebrand disease"}
{"qid": "med:78", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The normal PT indicates the extrinsic pathway is intact, while the prolonged aPTT points to an intrinsic pathway factor deficiency. Normal CBC rules out thrombocytopenia. The child has easy bruising without joint bleeds, which is typical of mild intrinsic pathway deficiencies such as factor\u202fXI deficiency (Hemophilia\u202fC).  \n\nAnswer: Factor XI deficiency (Hemophilia C)<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Von Willebrand disease"}
{"qid": "med:70", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Answer: \\(\\boxed{\\text{Essential (primary) hypertension}}\\)", "correct": false, "gold": "Stiffening of the aorta"}
{"qid": "med:79", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Vincristine commonly causes a **length-dependent peripheral neuropathy** due to its effect on **microtubules in neurons**.\n\nMechanism:\n- Vincristine **binds \u03b2-tubulin and inhibits microtubule polymerization**\n- This disrupts the **mitotic spindle** in dividing cells, but in neurons it also impairs **axonal transport**\n- Peripheral nerves, especially long axons to the hands and feet, are particularly vulnerable, leading to **distal sensory loss and decreased reflexes**\n\nAnswer: Impaired axonal transport due to inhibition of microtubule polymerization (vincristine binding to \u03b2-tubulin)", "correct": false, "gold": "Inhibition of beta-tubulin polymerization"}
{"qid": "med:79", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient's symptoms are consistent with vincristine-induced peripheral neuropathy. Vincristine is a vinca alkaloid that works by binding to tubulin, inhibiting microtubule formation. This disrupts axonal transport in neurons, leading to a length-dependent, symmetric sensorimotor polyneuropathy. The decreased reflexes and distal sensory loss are classic findings.\n\nAnswer: Inhibition of microtubule assembly (or disruption of axonal transport due to tubulin binding).", "correct": false, "gold": "Inhibition of beta-tubulin polymerization"}
{"qid": "med:79", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Inhibition of microtubule polymerization, which impairs axonal transport and leads to distal axonal degeneration.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Inhibition of beta-tubulin polymerization"}
{"qid": "med:78", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation of easy bruising with petechiae implies a disorder of primary hemostasis (i.e., platelet plug formation). The normal CBC excludes thrombocytopenia, leaving a qualitative platelet disorder or von Willebrand disease (vWD) as leading possibilities.\nThe coagulation profile shows an isolated, markedly prolonged aPTT with a normal PT. This pattern localizes the defect to the intrinsic pathway (factors VIII, IX, XI, or XII). Among these, only deficiencies that also impair platelet adhesion produce both petechiae and a prolonged aPTT. Severe (type 3) vWD lowers factor VIII sufficiently to prolong the aPTT while simultaneously abolishing vWF-mediated platelet adhesion, thereby explaining the cutaneous findings.\nClassic hemophilia A or B produces the same aPTT/PT pattern but causes deep bleeding (hemarthroses, intramuscular hematomas) rather than petechiae, because platelet function remains intact. Factor XII deficiency prolongs the aPTT yet causes no clinical bleeding. Thus the combination of superficial skin bleeding plus isolated aPTT prolongation points specifically to severe vWD.\nAnswer: von Willebrand disease (severe/type 3)", "correct": true, "gold": "Von Willebrand disease"}
{"qid": "med:79", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "Vincristine is a vinca alkaloid chemotherapeutic that binds \u03b2-tubulin and thereby prevents microtubule polymerization. In mitotic cells this blocks spindle formation and arrests division, which is the intended antineoplastic effect. In post-mitotic neurons the same biochemical action disrupts the dynamic microtubule network (neurotubules) that supports fast anterograde and retrograde axonal transport. Because long peripheral axons are most dependent on continuous transport of organelles, vesicles, and trophic factors, their distal segments undergo a length-dependent \u201cdying-back\u201d axonal degeneration. This produces the classic sensory symptoms (paresthesias in fingers and toes) and loss of deep-tendon reflexes observed clinically.  \n\nThus the proximate mechanism of the neuropathy is microtubule disassembly leading to impaired axonal transport.\n\nAnswer: impaired axonal transport (via microtubule depolymerization)", "correct": false, "gold": "Inhibition of beta-tubulin polymerization"}
{"qid": "med:58", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The urinalysis findings provide key diagnostic clues. Elevated urinary bilirubin indicates conjugated hyperbilirubinemia (conjugated bilirubin is water-soluble and filtered by the kidneys), while low urobilinogen indicates that bilirubin is not reaching the intestines to be converted by gut bacteria. This pattern is characteristic of **obstructive (post-hepatic) jaundice**, where bile flow is blocked proximal to the intestine. In a 65-year-old patient, this is most commonly caused by extrahepatic biliary obstruction from choledocholithiasis or malignancy (e.g., pancreatic head cancer, cholangiocarcinoma).\n\nAnswer: Extrahepatic biliary obstruction (obstructive jaundice).", "correct": false, "gold": "Defective hepatic bile excretion"}
{"qid": "med:82", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This presentation is classic for **lymphogranuloma venereum (LGV)** due to **_Chlamydia trachomatis_ serovars L1\u2013L3**:\n\n- **Transient painless genital ulcer** 3 weeks earlier\n- Followed by **painful inguinal lymphadenopathy (\u201cbuboes\u201d)**\n- Nodes can **rupture and ulcerate**\n- Exposure in **Southeast Asia**, where it is more prevalent\n\nThe finding most likely associated with this condition is:\n\n- **Obligate intracellular organism**\n- **Lacks muramic acid in its cell wall**\n- Seen as **intracytoplasmic inclusions**\n\nAnswer: Infection with *Chlamydia trachomatis* (L1\u2013L3), an obligate intracellular bacterium with intracytoplasmic inclusions and no muramic acid in its cell wall.", "correct": false, "gold": "PCR positive for Chlamydia trachomatis"}
{"qid": "med:82", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient's presentation\u2014a painless ulcer on the labia that resolved, followed by painful, ulcerative inguinal lymph nodes (buboes) with dark discoloration, along with recent travel to Southeast Asia and unprotected sex\u2014is classic for lymphogranuloma venereum (LGV), caused by *Chlamydia trachomatis* serovars L1\u2013L3. The most characteristic finding in LGV is the presence of **intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies** on biopsy or culture of the bubo or ulcer.\n\nAnswer: Intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies (or Chlamydia trachomatis serovars L1\u2013L3).", "correct": false, "gold": "PCR positive for Chlamydia trachomatis"}
{"qid": "med:78", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\nThis 9-year-old boy presents with mucocutaneous bleeding (petechiae and bruising) but is otherwise healthy. His labs show an isolated prolonged activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) of 60 seconds with a normal prothrombin time (PT) and normal complete blood count. This pattern is consistent with a defect in the intrinsic coagulation pathway. \r\n\r\nThe most common inherited cause in a young male with bleeding and isolated aPTT prolongation is **hemophilia A** (factor VIII deficiency). Hemophilia B (factor IX deficiency) is less common but presents similarly. Von Willebrand disease can also prolong aPTT, but it more classically causes mucosal bleeding and platelet-type symptoms, while hemophilia tends to present with deeper bruising and hemarthrosis. \r\n\r\nAnswer: Hemophilia A", "correct": false, "gold": "Von Willebrand disease"}
{"qid": "med:68", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at this case, the key is the **timing of the infection relative to surgery** (4 months post-total knee arthroplasty).\n\nProsthetic joint infections are classified by time:\n- **Early** (<3 months): Usually virulent organisms like *Staphylococcus aureus* acquired during surgery\n- **Delayed** (3\u201312 months): Typically less virulent organisms like **coagulase-negative staphylococci** (*Staphylococcus epidermidis*) or *Cutibacterium acnes* that form biofilms and cause indolent infections\n- **Late** (>12 months): Usually hematogenous spread, commonly *S. aureus*\n\nAt **4 months**, this falls into the **delayed** category. While the patient has fever and acute pain (which might suggest a more virulent organism), the specific timing of 3\u201312 months post-operatively is the classic window for **coagulase-negative staphylococci** (particularly *S. epidermidis*), which are the most common pathogens for delayed prosthetic joint infections. These organisms are skin flora that contaminate the prosthesis during surgery but have slow-growing, biofilm-associated infections that present months later.\n\nAlthough *S. aureus* is the most common cause overall and causes early/late infections, the 4-month timeframe specifically points to coagulase-negative staphylococcus as the most likely culprit.\n\nAnswer: *Staphylococcus epidermidis* (or coagulase-negative staphylococcus)", "correct": true, "gold": "Staphylococcus epidermidis"}
{"qid": "med:79", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Vincristine is a vinca alkaloid that binds to \u03b2\u2011tubulin and blocks the polymerization of microtubules. In neurons, this disrupts the microtubule\u2011based axonal transport system, leading to accumulation of organelles and neurofilaments, axonal swelling, and a length\u2011dependent \u201cdying\u2011back\u201d peripheral neuropathy. The symmetric distal sensory loss and diminished reflexes are classic for this drug effect.\n\nAnswer: Inhibition of microtubule polymerization (disruption of axonal microtubules and transport).", "correct": false, "gold": "Inhibition of beta-tubulin polymerization"}
{"qid": "med:82", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Answer: Intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies (on Giemsa or immunofluorescent staining) \u2014 consistent with *Chlamydia trachomatis* (serovars L1\u2013L3) causing Lymphogranuloma Venereum (LGV)**\n\n**Reasoning:**\nThis patient\u2019s presentation is classic for **Lymphogranuloma Venereum (LGV)**:\n- History of unprotected sex in **Southeast Asia** (an LGV-endemic region)\n- **Painless genital ulcer** that spontaneously resolved (primary stage)\n- Followed weeks later by **painful, unilateral inguinal lymphadenopathy** that enlarges, ulcerates, and shows dark discoloration (secondary stage \u2014 \"buboes\")\n- Absence of systemic symptoms (fever, dysuria) makes other STIs less likely\n\nLGV is caused by invasive serovars (L1, L2, L3) of ***Chlamydia trachomatis***. Diagnosis is often clinical, but laboratory confirmation can involve:\n- **NAAT (PCR)** for *C. trachomatis* \u2014 the gold standard\n- **Giemsa or Gram stain of lymph node aspirate**, which may reveal **intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies** within infected epithelial or mononuclear cells \u2014 a classic (though insensitive) finding\n- Serology (complement fixation or microimmunofluorescence) showing elevated chlamydial antibody titers\n\nAnswer: Intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies (Chlamydia trachomatis) seen on Giemsa/Gram stain of lymph node aspirate, confirming Lymphogranuloma Venereum.", "correct": false, "gold": "PCR positive for Chlamydia trachomatis"}
{"qid": "med:82", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Tender, ulcerative lymph nodes (buboes) in the groin, often with dark discoloration.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "PCR positive for Chlamydia trachomatis"}
{"qid": "med:84", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has clinical and laboratory features of Cushing's syndrome (hypertension, obesity, striae, amenorrhea, hyperglycemia, elevated urinary and serum cortisol). The low ACTH (<5 pg/mL) indicates an ACTH-independent cause. The lack of suppression with high-dose dexamethasone points away from a pituitary adenoma and toward an adrenal source. The best next step is adrenal imaging (CT or MRI) to localize the tumor.\n\nAnswer: Adrenal imaging (CT or MRI)", "correct": false, "gold": "MRI of the adrenal glands"}
{"qid": "med:84", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "She has **Cushing syndrome** with:\n- proximal muscle weakness, amenorrhea, hypertension, obesity, striae, hyperglycemia\n- elevated serum and urinary cortisol\n- **low ACTH**, indicating **ACTH-independent** cortisol excess\n- **no suppression with high-dose dexamethasone**, which supports an **adrenal source** rather than pituitary Cushing disease\n\nThe best next step is to **localize the adrenal lesion with adrenal imaging**, typically **CT scan of the adrenal glands**. If an adrenal tumor is found, treatment is adrenalectomy.\n\nAnswer: Obtain a CT scan of the adrenal glands to evaluate for an adrenal cortisol-secreting tumor.", "correct": false, "gold": "MRI of the adrenal glands"}
{"qid": "med:84", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Answer: CT scan of the abdomen (adrenal glands)**\n\n**Reasoning:** This patient has clinical (central obesity, striae, hypertension, hyperglycemia, proximal muscle weakness, menstrual irregularity) and biochemical (elevated 24-hr urinary cortisol, elevated serum cortisol) evidence of Cushing syndrome. The **suppressed ACTH level (<5 pg/mL)** indicates that the cortisol excess is **ACTH-independent**, pointing to a primary adrenal source (adenoma, carcinoma, or bilateral hyperplasia) rather than a pituitary or ectopic ACTH-secreting tumor. The failure to suppress cortisol with high-dose dexamethasone is consistent with this, as adrenal tumors are autonomous and do not respond to negative feedback.\n\nSince the source is localized to the adrenal gland (low ACTH), the appropriate next step is **imaging of the adrenal glands** with a **CT scan of the abdomen** to identify an adrenal adenoma, carcinoma, or nodular hyperplasia \u2014 this will guide further management (e.g., surgical resection if a unilateral adenoma is found).\n\nAnswer: CT scan of the abdomen/adrenal glands to localize the adrenal lesion causing ACTH-independent Cushing syndrome.", "correct": false, "gold": "MRI of the adrenal glands"}
{"qid": "med:84", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Perform adrenal imaging (CT or MRI) to locate the source of autonomous cortisol production.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "MRI of the adrenal glands"}
{"qid": "med:79", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Vincristine is a vinca alkaloid that binds to \u03b2-tubulin and **inhibits microtubule polymerization**. This disrupts the formation of axonal microtubules, impairing anterograde and retrograde axonal transport in peripheral nerves. The resulting axonal degeneration leads to the length-dependent sensory and motor neuropathy seen in this patient.\n\nAnswer: **Inhibition of microtubule polymerization**", "correct": false, "gold": "Inhibition of beta-tubulin polymerization"}
{"qid": "med:84", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation (central obesity, striae, proximal weakness, hypertension, hyperglycemia, and secondary amenorrhea) plus markedly elevated urinary and serum cortisol establishes endogenous Cushing syndrome.  \n\nLow plasma ACTH together with failure of cortisol to suppress on high-dose dexamethasone localizes the source to the adrenal gland itself (autonomous cortisol secretion by adenoma or carcinoma).  \n\nOnce ACTH-independent Cushing syndrome is confirmed biochemically, the immediate next management step is cross-sectional imaging of the adrenals to identify and characterize the causative lesion.  \n\nAnswer: CT abdomen (adrenal protocol)", "correct": false, "gold": "MRI of the adrenal glands"}
{"qid": "med:82", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The vignette describes a transient, painless genital ulcer (primary phase) followed weeks later by unilateral suppurative inguinal lymphadenopathy that ulcerates and shows dark discoloration of the overlying skin. This temporal sequence and the epidemiologic clue of travel to Southeast Asia are diagnostic of lymphogranuloma venereum (LGV) caused by Chlamydia trachomatis serovars L1\u2013L3.\nLGV produces a distinctive lymph-node reaction consisting of stellate (star-shaped) micro-abscesses surrounded by palisading histiocytes and granulomatous inflammation. These stellate abscesses are the histologic hallmark that would be seen on lymph-node biopsy and distinguish LGV from other ulcerative sexually transmitted diseases.\nThus the finding most characteristic of the patient\u2019s condition is stellate microabscesses within inguinal lymph nodes.\nAnswer: stellate microabscesses (LN bx)", "correct": false, "gold": "PCR positive for Chlamydia trachomatis"}
{"qid": "med:85", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Pyridostigmine", "correct": true, "gold": "Pyridostigmine"}
{"qid": "med:85", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient has **myasthenia gravis**, suggested by:\n\n- **Diplopia and ptosis**\n- Symptoms that **worsen with use / later in the day**\n- Normal pupils and normal visual acuity\n- Association with **autoimmune disease** history\n\nMyasthenia gravis is caused by autoantibodies against **postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors** at the neuromuscular junction. First-line symptomatic treatment is an **acetylcholinesterase inhibitor**, most commonly **pyridostigmine**.\n\nAnswer: **Pyridostigmine**", "correct": true, "gold": "Pyridostigmine"}
{"qid": "med:85", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient's symptoms\u2014fluctuating double vision, ptosis worse at the end of the day, and fatigue\u2014are classic for myasthenia gravis. The history of type I diabetes increases the risk for other autoimmune disorders. The first-line symptomatic treatment for myasthenia gravis is an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor, typically pyridostigmine.\n\nAnswer: Pyridostigmine", "correct": true, "gold": "Pyridostigmine"}
{"qid": "med:85", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient's presentation of fatigable diplopia (worse later in the day), bilateral ptosis, and generalized fatigue is classic for myasthenia gravis, an autoimmune disorder of the neuromuscular junction. This is further supported by her personal history of type 1 diabetes and family history of Sj\u00f6gren syndrome (both autoimmune). Other differentials (e.g., diabetic cranial neuropathy, thyroid orbitopathy) lack the characteristic diurnal fluctuation and fatigability.\nInitial symptomatic therapy for MG uses an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor to prolong acetylcholine action at the neuromuscular junction. Pyridostigmine is the preferred agent in this class due to its oral bioavailability and favorable side-effect profile relative to neostigmine.\nImmunomodulatory therapy (e.g., corticosteroids) or thymectomy may be added later for disease control, but they are not first-line for a new outpatient diagnosis.\nAnswer: pyridostigmine", "correct": true, "gold": "Pyridostigmine"}
{"qid": "med:84", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient has ACTH\u2011independent Cushing\u2019s syndrome (low ACTH, elevated cortisol that fails to suppress with high\u2011dose dexamethasone). The next step is to localize the source within the adrenal glands. Imaging with a CT (or MRI) of the abdomen/adrenals is required to identify a unilateral adenoma, carcinoma, or bilateral hyperplasia before planning definitive therapy.\n\nAnswer: CT of the adrenal glands (abdominal CT).", "correct": false, "gold": "MRI of the adrenal glands"}
{"qid": "med:84", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Answer: **CT scan of the abdomen to evaluate for an adrenal mass.**", "correct": false, "gold": "MRI of the adrenal glands"}
{"qid": "med:85", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Pyridostigmine (acetylcholinesterase inhibitor) is the most appropriate medication for symptomatic management of suspected ocular myasthenia gravis in this patient.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "Pyridostigmine"}
{"qid": "med:87", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "During dynamic exercise, sympathetic outflow increases and parasympathetic tone decreases. This raises **heart rate** and **contractility**, increasing **cardiac output**. At the same time, active skeletal muscle causes **local metabolic vasodilation** (eg, due to adenosine, K+, CO2, lactate), which lowers total peripheral resistance, so **diastolic pressure changes little** while **systolic pressure rises** due to the increased cardiac output.\n\nAnswer: Increased sympathetic stimulation with \u03b21-mediated rise in cardiac output and local metabolic vasodilation in exercising skeletal muscle (\u2193TPR), causing \u2191systolic pressure with little change in diastolic pressure.", "correct": false, "gold": "Sympathetic nervous system activation"}
{"qid": "med:87", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The increase in heart rate and blood pressure during exercise is primarily due to increased sympathetic nervous system activity. This leads to higher cardiac output (from increased heart rate and stroke volume) and vasoconstriction in non-essential vascular beds, while skeletal muscle arterioles dilate. The net effect is a rise in both heart rate and systolic blood pressure, with diastolic pressure remaining relatively stable.\n\nAnswer: Increased sympathetic nervous system activity.", "correct": false, "gold": "Sympathetic nervous system activation"}
{"qid": "med:87", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Increased sympathetic nervous system activity (release of catecholamines such as epinephrine and norepinephrine) leading to tachycardia and elevated systolic blood pressure.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Sympathetic nervous system activation"}
{"qid": "med:85", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Answer: Pyridostigmine", "correct": true, "gold": "Pyridostigmine"}
{"qid": "med:87", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Increased sympathetic nervous system activity (with simultaneous vagal/parasympathetic withdrawal) in response to exercise.**\n\nDuring dynamic exercise, the central command from the motor cortex together with feedback from muscle mechanoreceptors and chemoreceptors activates the sympathetic nervous system while withdrawing vagal tone. This autonomic shift produces:\n\n- **\u2191 Heart rate** \u2013 sympathetic stimulation of the SA node increases firing rate (chronotropy), while vagal withdrawal removes the resting parasympathetic \"brake.\"\n- **\u2191 Systolic BP** \u2013 sympathetic stimulation increases myocardial contractility (inotropy) and stroke volume, raising cardiac output and systolic pressure.\n- **Unchanged/slightly widened pulse pressure (diastolic BP stays about the same)** \u2013 exercising skeletal muscle undergoes local metabolic vasodilation (mediated by adenosine, K\u207a, H\u207a, nitric oxide, etc.), which lowers peripheral vascular resistance in the exercising muscle beds. This offsets the sympathetic vasoconstriction occurring in non-exercising tissues (splanchnic, renal, cutaneous circulation), keeping diastolic pressure relatively stable despite the rise in systolic pressure.\n\nThis pattern\u2014an increase in systolic BP and heart rate with a stable diastolic BP\u2014is the normal, expected physiologic response to aerobic exercise in a healthy person, reflecting increased cardiac output and decreased total peripheral resistance due to muscle vasodilation.\n\nAnswer: Increased sympathetic tone (and decreased vagal tone) causing increased heart rate and cardiac output, combined with exercise-induced skeletal muscle vasodilation lowering peripheral vascular resistance \u2014 the normal physiologic response to aerobic exercise.", "correct": false, "gold": "Sympathetic nervous system activation"}
{"qid": "med:87", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The observed post-exercise changes (HR rising from 70 to 130 beats/min, systolic BP rising from 114 to 145 mm Hg, diastolic BP essentially unchanged) reflect the integrated cardiovascular response to sustained aerobic work. At rest, cardiac vagal tone predominates and sympathetic outflow is modest. During prolonged treadmill running, central command and muscle metaboreflexes produce two concurrent autonomic adjustments:\n- withdrawal of parasympathetic (vagal) tone to the sino-atrial node, and\n- increased sympathetic outflow to the heart and resistance vessels.\nThe net effect on the heart is a rise in both rate and contractility, elevating cardiac output and therefore systolic pressure. In parallel, sympathetic vasoconstrictor activity is offset by local metabolic vasodilation in the large muscle vascular beds, so total peripheral resistance falls enough to keep diastolic pressure nearly constant. These autonomic shifts persist for minutes after exercise ceases, accounting for the values recorded immediately upon stopping.\nThus the single most proximate mechanism responsible for the measured changes is the combination of parasympathetic withdrawal and sympathetic activation.\nAnswer: \u2191 symp. + \u2193 parasymp. outflow (to heart/vessels)", "correct": false, "gold": "Sympathetic nervous system activation"}
{"qid": "med:88", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Anti-HAV **IgM** indicates an **acute hepatitis A infection**, which fits her current symptoms of fever, malaise, and decreased appetite.\n\nA positive **anti-HBsAg (anti-HBs)** means she has **immunity to hepatitis B** from either **prior vaccination or past resolved infection**, not active HBV disease.\n\nSo her presentation is most likely due to **acute hepatitis A virus infection**, probably from **fecal-oral transmission**.\n\nAnswer: Acute hepatitis A infection (fecal-oral HAV infection); anti-HBs indicates prior immunity to hepatitis B, not active disease.", "correct": false, "gold": "Shellfish"}
{"qid": "med:88", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has positive Anti-HBsAg (antibody to hepatitis B surface antigen) and Anti-HAV IgM (IgM antibody to hepatitis A virus). Anti-HBsAg indicates past hepatitis B infection or vaccination (immunity), not active disease. Anti-HAV IgM indicates acute hepatitis A infection. Given her symptoms (decreased appetite, malaise, fever) and serology, the most likely cause is acute hepatitis A.\n\nAnswer: Hepatitis A virus", "correct": false, "gold": "Shellfish"}
{"qid": "med:75", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The clinical picture (fixed thyroid nodule, indeterminate FNA, planned radioiodine therapy) points to differentiated thyroid carcinoma. The explicit note that bone and lung are common metastatic targets indicates the tumor is most likely **follicular thyroid carcinoma**, which spreads hematogenously. Among distant sites, the lungs are the most frequent initial metastatic location for this histology.\n\nAnswer: Lungs (pulmonary metastasis).", "correct": false, "gold": "Inferior thyroid arteries"}
{"qid": "med:88", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Acute Hepatitis A infection**\n\n**Reasoning:**\n\n- **Anti-HAV IgM** = Marker of **acute/recent Hepatitis A infection**. IgM antibodies appear early in infection and indicate active or very recent illness. This explains her acute symptoms (decreased appetite, malaise, fever).\n\n- **Anti-HBsAg (Anti-HBs)** = Antibody against Hepatitis B surface antigen. This indicates **immunity** to Hepatitis B, which occurs either from:\n  - Prior vaccination, or\n  - Prior resolved/cleared Hepatitis B infection\n\nImportantly, Anti-HBs does **NOT** indicate active infection \u2014 in fact, it signifies the opposite (immunity/protection). If she had active Hepatitis B, you would expect to see **HBsAg** (the antigen itself) positive, not the antibody against it.\n\n**Conclusion:** Her clinical presentation (fever, malaise, anorexia) is due to **acute Hepatitis A virus infection**, while the positive Anti-HBs simply reflects immune protection against Hepatitis B (from vaccination or past infection) and is incidental to her current illness.\n\nAnswer: Acute Hepatitis A infection (indicated by positive Anti-HAV IgM); the positive Anti-HBs reflects immunity to Hepatitis B, not active disease.", "correct": false, "gold": "Shellfish"}
{"qid": "med:88", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient's symptoms (anorexia, malaise, fever) are nonspecific but consistent with acute viral hepatitis. Serology shows two key findings:\n- Anti-HAV IgM is present only during acute infection and directly documents recent HAV exposure as the cause of the current illness.\n- Anti-HBs (without HBsAg) indicates resolved prior HBV infection or successful vaccination; it confers immunity and excludes active hepatitis B as the etiology.\nThus the only active infection that explains the clinical picture is hepatitis A.\nAnswer: acute HAV infection (anti-HAV IgM+)", "correct": false, "gold": "Shellfish"}
{"qid": "med:88", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Acute hepatitis A virus infection.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Shellfish"}
{"qid": "med:90", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "The patient likely has COPD from a heavy smoking history, chronic progressive dyspnea, home oxygen use, and recent exacerbation. A medication with **both mucoactive and antioxidant properties** is **N-acetylcysteine**.\n\nIn the lungs, **N-acetylcysteine works primarily by breaking disulfide bonds in mucus glycoproteins**, which decreases mucus viscosity and helps clear secretions. Its antioxidant effect comes from replenishing glutathione, but the main pulmonary mechanism is mucolysis via disulfide bond disruption.\n\nAnswer: Breaks disulfide bonds in mucus glycoproteins, reducing mucus viscosity.", "correct": false, "gold": "Disulfide bond cleavage"}
{"qid": "med:82", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Answer: Positive nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT) for *Chlamydia trachomatis* serovars L1\u2013L3.", "correct": false, "gold": "PCR positive for Chlamydia trachomatis"}
{"qid": "med:85", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at this case, the key clinical features are:\n1. **Fatigable muscle weakness**: Symptoms worsen in the evenings and after sustained activity (long work day)\n2. **Ocular manifestations**: Bilateral ptosis (worse on left) and diplopia with intact pupillary reflexes\n3. **Demographics**: Young female (peak incidence 20-30 years for women)\n4. **Autoimmune history**: Type 1 diabetes and family history of Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome (autoimmune clustering)\n\nThis presentation is classic for **myasthenia gravis (MG)**, an autoimmune disorder of the neuromuscular junction where antibodies against acetylcholine receptors cause fatigable muscle weakness. The ocular muscles are affected first in ~85% of cases.\n\n**Why not other diagnoses:**\n- **Diabetic neuropathy**: Would not cause fatigable, fluctuating weakness\n- **Thyroid eye disease**: Causes lid retraction/proptosis, not ptosis\n- **CPEO** (mitochondrial): Slowly progressive, NOT fatigable\n- **MS**: Does not cause fatigable ptosis with diurnal variation\n\n**Management:**\nFor mild to moderate MG with ocular symptoms, the first-line symptomatic treatment is an **acetylcholinesterase inhibitor** to increase acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction. **Pyridostigmine** is the drug of choice (preferred over neostigmine due to longer duration and fewer CNS side effects). Immunosuppressants (e.g., corticosteroids) are used for refractory cases or severe generalized disease, but pyridostigmine is the appropriate initial medication for this presentation.\n\nAnswer: Pyridostigmine (an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor)", "correct": true, "gold": "Pyridostigmine"}
{"qid": "med:90", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The medication described is likely N-acetylcysteine (NAC), which has mucoactive and anti-oxidative properties. Its primary mechanism in the lungs is to reduce mucus viscosity by breaking disulfide bonds in mucoproteins, thereby facilitating mucus clearance. Additionally, it acts as a precursor to glutathione, enhancing antioxidant defenses.\n\n**Answer:** It reduces mucus viscosity by breaking disulfide bonds in mucoproteins.", "correct": false, "gold": "Disulfide bond cleavage"}
{"qid": "med:90", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Medication: N-acetylcysteine (NAC)**\n\n**Primary mechanism of action:** NAC exerts its mucoactive effect by cleaving disulfide bonds between mucin glycoproteins via its free thiol (sulfhydryl) group. This disrupts the cross-linked structure of mucus, reducing its viscosity and elasticity, thereby facilitating easier clearance of airway secretions (mucolytic action).\n\nAdditionally, NAC serves as a precursor for L-cysteine, which is a rate-limiting substrate for the synthesis of **glutathione**, one of the lung's most important endogenous antioxidants. By replenishing intracellular glutathione stores, NAC helps neutralize reactive oxygen species (ROS) and reduce oxidative stress-induced damage to airway epithelium, which is particularly relevant in patients with chronic lung disease (e.g., COPD) exacerbated by oxidative injury from smoking.\n\nAnswer: NAC works as a mucolytic by breaking disulfide bonds in mucus glycoproteins to decrease sputum viscosity, and as an antioxidant by serving as a glutathione precursor to reduce oxidative stress in the lungs.", "correct": false, "gold": "Disulfide bond cleavage"}
{"qid": "med:90", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient's presentation (advanced age, extensive smoking history, chronic progressive dyspnea, home oxygen requirement, and recent exacerbation) is diagnostic of COPD, most likely with a chronic bronchitis component. In this setting, the only medication that simultaneously possesses clinically relevant mucoactive and antioxidant properties is N-acetylcysteine (NAC).\nNAC exerts its mucoactive effect directly within the airways by donating free sulfhydryl groups; these groups reduce the disulfide cross-links that stabilize mucin multimers, thereby lowering mucus viscosity and facilitating clearance. The same sulfhydryl donation also replenishes intracellular glutathione, accounting for the drug\u2019s antioxidant activity. Because the question specifies action \u201cin the lungs,\u201d the dominant local pharmacologic effect is the cleavage of mucin disulfide bonds.\nThus the primary mechanism of NAC in pulmonary tissue is reduction of disulfide linkages within mucus glycoproteins.\nAnswer: breaks disulfide bonds in mucins (NAC)", "correct": false, "gold": "Disulfide bond cleavage"}
{"qid": "med:88", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The presence of Anti\u2011HAV IgM indicates an acute hepatitis\u202fA infection, which explains the patient\u2019s prodromal symptoms (anorexia, malaise, fever). \u201cAnti\u2011HBsAg\u201d refers to antibody against the hepatitis\u202fB surface antigen (anti\u2011HBs); a positive result signifies immunity (prior vaccination or resolved infection) rather than active hepatitis\u202fB disease. Thus, the current illness is caused by hepatitis\u202fA.\n\nAnswer: Hepatitis A virus (acute hepatitis A infection).", "correct": false, "gold": "Shellfish"}
{"qid": "med:93", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This obese adolescent boy with hip/groin/knee pain, inability to bear weight, and limited hip ROM most likely has **slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE)**. Referred pain to the knee is common. The appropriate treatment is **urgent orthopedic management with in situ screw fixation**, and he should be made **non\u2013weight bearing immediately** to prevent further slippage and avascular necrosis.\n\nAnswer: Immediate non\u2013weight bearing and urgent orthopedic surgical pinning (in situ fixation) for slipped capital femoral epiphysis.", "correct": false, "gold": "Surgical pinning of the femoral head"}
{"qid": "med:78", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at this clinical presentation, I need to identify the bleeding disorder based on the pattern of bleeding and coagulation studies.\n\n**Key findings:**\n- 9-year-old boy with bruising and **petechiae**\n- Normal platelet count (CBC normal)\n- Normal PT (12 seconds)\n- Prolonged aPTT (60 seconds; normal ~25-35 seconds)\n\n**Analysis of the coagulation profile:**\nAn isolated prolonged aPTT with normal PT indicates a defect in the **intrinsic pathway** (factors XII, XI, IX, VIII) or common pathway, while the extrinsic pathway is intact.\n\n**Differential diagnosis:**\n1. **Hemophilia A (Factor VIII deficiency)** or **Hemophilia B (Factor IX deficiency)**: These cause prolonged aPTT with normal PT and platelets. However, hemophilia typically causes **deep tissue bleeding, hemarthrosis, and large hematomas**\u2014not petechiae. Petechiae are characteristic of **primary hemostasis defects** (platelet or vascular problems), not coagulation factor deficiencies.\n\n2. **Von Willebrand Disease (vWD)**: This is crucial because vWD affects **both primary and secondary hemostasis**:\n   - **Primary hemostasis**: vWF is essential for platelet adhesion to damaged endothelium. Deficiency causes mucocutaneous bleeding including **petechiae**, epistaxis, and easy bruising.\n   - **Secondary hemostasis**: vWF carries Factor VIII. Low vWF leads to low Factor VIII, causing **prolonged aPTT**.\n   - Labs show: Normal platelet count, normal PT, prolonged or normal aPTT (depending on Factor VIII level), and the clinical picture matches mucocutaneous bleeding.\n\n3. **Factor XI deficiency**: Rare, usually mild bleeding, not associated with petechiae.\n\n**Why vWD is the answer:**\nThe presence of **petechiae** with **normal platelets** localizes the problem to primary hemostasis (platelet function/vWF), while the **prolonged aPTT** indicates secondary hemostasis involvement. Only vWD explains both findings simultaneously. Hemophilia would not cause petechiae.\n\nAnswer: Von Willebrand Disease", "correct": true, "gold": "Von Willebrand disease"}
{"qid": "med:90", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: It serves as a precursor to glutathione, replenishing intracellular antioxidant stores and directly scavenging free radicals, while also cleaving disulfide bonds in mucus to reduce its viscosity.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Disulfide bond cleavage"}
{"qid": "med:87", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reason:**  \r\nDuring dynamic exercise (running), the body undergoes acute cardiovascular adjustments driven primarily by the autonomic nervous system:  \r\n\r\n- **Increased heart rate:** Central command and muscle chemoreceptors/mechanoreceptors trigger decreased parasympathetic (vagal) tone and increased sympathetic tone to the sinoatrial node, leading to a rapid rise in heart rate.\r\n- **Increased systolic blood pressure:** Sympathetic activation also increases myocardial contractility and heart rate, raising cardiac output (CO). Meanwhile, local metabolites (e.g., adenosine, CO\u2082, lactate, K\u207a) cause vasodilation in active skeletal muscles (functional sympatholysis), which prevents a large rise in total peripheral resistance (TPR). As a result, systolic BP increases significantly while diastolic BP remains near baseline \u2014 as seen here (145/75 mm Hg).\r\n\r\nThese effects persist immediately after exercise cessation before autonomic tone and metabolite clearance return to resting levels.\r\n\r\nAnswer: **Increased sympathetic nervous system activity and withdrawal of parasympathetic tone.**", "correct": false, "gold": "Sympathetic nervous system activation"}
{"qid": "med:93", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Diagnosis:** Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis (SCFE)\n\nThis presentation is classic for SCFE: an obese adolescent male with acute/chronic hip pain that may be referred to the groin, thigh, or knee, along with limited internal rotation and abduction of the hip, and inability to bear weight.\n\n**Best Management:**\n1. **Immediate non-weight bearing** on the affected limb (wheelchair or crutches) to prevent further slippage of the epiphysis.\n2. **Urgent orthopedic surgery consultation** for **surgical stabilization with in situ percutaneous screw fixation** of the femoral head, which is the definitive treatment.\n\nThis is considered an orthopedic emergency (especially if unstable/acute), as delayed treatment risks avascular necrosis of the femoral head, chondrolysis, and progression to a more severe slip.\n\nAnswer: Immediate non-weight bearing status and urgent orthopedic surgery consultation for in situ surgical screw fixation of the femoral epiphysis (treatment for slipped capital femoral epiphysis).", "correct": false, "gold": "Surgical pinning of the femoral head"}
{"qid": "med:93", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The presentation of a 13-year-old obese boy with acute hip, knee, and groin pain, inability to bear weight, and limited hip range of motion is classic for a slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE). This is an orthopedic emergency. The best management is immediate non-weight-bearing status and urgent orthopedic consultation for surgical stabilization (typically in situ pinning). Delaying treatment can lead to avascular necrosis of the femoral head.\n\nAnswer: Urgent orthopedic consultation for surgical fixation (in situ pinning) and strict non-weight-bearing.", "correct": false, "gold": "Surgical pinning of the femoral head"}
{"qid": "med:93", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation of an obese adolescent with acute non-traumatic hip/groin/knee pain, inability to bear weight, and restricted hip motion is classic for slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE). Because the patient cannot bear weight, the slip is classified as unstable; unstable SCFE carries a high risk of avascular necrosis and therefore requires urgent operative stabilization rather than non-operative measures or temporizing interventions (e.g., aspiration, antibiotics, or simple non-weight-bearing). The definitive management is prompt orthopedic consultation for in-situ percutaneous screw fixation under fluoroscopic guidance, performed after the patient is made non-weight-bearing and NPO.\nAnswer: emergent ortho consult for in-situ percutaneous pinning", "correct": false, "gold": "Surgical pinning of the femoral head"}
{"qid": "med:88", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Acute hepatitis A virus infection}}\n\\]", "correct": false, "gold": "Shellfish"}
{"qid": "med:94", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Answer: Choledocholithiasis \u2014 a gallstone impacted in the common bile duct causing obstruction (biliary colic with cholestatic liver enzymes and dilated CBD)", "correct": true, "gold": "Choledocholithiasis"}
{"qid": "med:93", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Obtain immediate plain radiographs of the pelvis and hip (AP pelvis and frog\u2011leg lateral) and arrange emergent orthopedic consultation; if a slipped capital femoral epiphysis is confirmed, manage with non\u2011weight\u2011bearing, appropriate analgesia, and prompt stabilization (spica cast or surgical fixation) as directed by orthopedics.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Surgical pinning of the femoral head"}
{"qid": "med:94", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient has **intermittent biliary colic with obstructive jaundice** and a **dilated common bile duct**, which strongly suggests **choledocholithiasis** (a gallstone in the common bile duct).  \n\nWhy she formed gallstones:\n- She is female and obese, which are risk factors for gallstones.\n- Most importantly, she had **terminal ileum resection** for Crohn disease.\n- The **terminal ileum is where bile acids are reabsorbed** in the enterohepatic circulation.\n- Ileal resection causes **bile acid malabsorption**, reducing the bile acid pool.\n- With fewer bile acids available to solubilize cholesterol, bile becomes **supersaturated with cholesterol**, leading to **cholesterol gallstone formation**.\n\nSo the underlying cause is **decreased bile acid reabsorption after terminal ileal resection**, causing **cholesterol gallstones** that can migrate into the common bile duct.\n\nAnswer: Decreased enterohepatic reabsorption of bile acids after terminal ileal resection, causing cholesterol supersaturation of bile and gallstone formation (choledocholithiasis).", "correct": true, "gold": "Choledocholithiasis"}
{"qid": "med:94", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has episodic severe right upper quadrant pain, nausea, vomiting, scleral icterus, elevated bilirubin and liver enzymes (especially alkaline phosphatase), and a dilated common bile duct with gallstones but no gallbladder wall thickening. This points to choledocholithiasis (a stone lodged in the common bile duct) causing obstructive jaundice. Her history of terminal ileum resection for Crohn\u2019s disease is key: the ileum is the primary site of bile salt reabsorption, so resection leads to bile salt malabsorption, supersaturation of bile with cholesterol, and increased risk of cholesterol gallstones. These stones can migrate into the common bile duct.\n\nAnswer: Choledocholithiasis due to cholesterol gallstones from bile salt malabsorption after ileal resection.", "correct": true, "gold": "Choledocholithiasis"}
{"qid": "med:93", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "This patient presents with classic signs of **slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE)**: an obese 13-year-old boy with severe hip, groin, and knee pain, inability to bear weight, and limited hip range of motion. SCFE is an orthopedic emergency due to the risk of avascular necrosis of the femoral head.  \n\nThe best management is **immediate non-weight-bearing** and **emergent orthopedic consultation for surgical stabilization** (typically in situ percutaneous pinning). Imaging (AP and frog-leg lateral hip radiographs) should be obtained promptly to confirm the diagnosis, but management should not be delayed if SCFE is strongly suspected.  \n\nAnswer: Emergent orthopedic consultation for surgical stabilization of the slipped capital femoral epiphysis.", "correct": false, "gold": "Surgical pinning of the femoral head"}
{"qid": "med:94", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Obstruction of the common bile duct by a gallstone (choledocholithiasis).<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "Choledocholithiasis"}
