{"qid": "med:95", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This boy has **cryptorchidism** (an undescended testis), specifically a **palpable testis in the inguinal canal**. \n\nKey management point:\n- Testes often descend spontaneously in the first **6 months** of life.\n- If still undescended **after 6 months**, spontaneous descent is unlikely.\n- The recommended treatment is **orchiopexy**, ideally performed between **6 and 18 months** of age to reduce risks of infertility and testicular malignancy.\n\nBecause he is **11 months old** and the testis is still undescended, the next step is **surgical referral for orchiopexy**.\n\nAnswer: Refer for orchiopexy (surgical correction of cryptorchidism).", "correct": true, "gold": "Orchiopexy"}
{"qid": "med:95", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Continue observation (reassurance and follow-up), since spontaneous descent can still occur up to 12 months of age \u2014 surgical referral (orchiopexy) if it hasn't descended by then.\n\nAnswer: Observation/reassurance with follow-up until 12 months of age; refer for orchiopexy if the testicle has not descended by then.", "correct": true, "gold": "Orchiopexy"}
{"qid": "med:94", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient's presentation (recurrent self-limited episodes of biliary colic, transient conjugated hyperbilirubinemia, mixed cholestatic/hepatocellular enzyme elevation, and sonographic dilation of the common bile duct in the presence of gallstones) indicates intermittent mechanical obstruction of the extrahepatic biliary tree by calculi that originated in the gallbladder and migrated distally.\nThe key pathophysiologic driver is the remote terminal-ileum resection. The ileum is the sole site of active bile-acid reabsorption; its removal interrupts the enterohepatic circulation, contracts the total bile-acid pool, and lowers the intra-biliary concentration of bile salts. Because bile salts and phospholipids normally keep cholesterol in micellar solution, their deficiency produces bile supersaturated with cholesterol and thereby promotes cholesterol gallstone formation. The patient\u2019s additional risk factors (female sex, obesity, hyperlipidemia) amplify hepatic cholesterol secretion, but the ileal resection is the dominant, disease-specific mechanism that explains the stones responsible for the current findings.\nThus the proximate cause of the entire picture is choledocholithiasis secondary to ileal-resection\u2013induced cholesterol cholelithiasis.\nAnswer: decr. bile acid reabs. (ileal resect.) \u2192 chol. stones/choledocholith.", "correct": false, "gold": "Choledocholithiasis"}
