{"qid": "med:95", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The infant has an undescended testicle (cryptorchidism) that is palpable in the inguinal canal. At 11 months, spontaneous descent is unlikely after 6 months of age. The standard of care is surgical correction (orchiopexy) before 12\u201318 months to preserve fertility and reduce the risk of testicular malignancy. Observation is no longer appropriate, and hormonal therapy is not first-line.  \n\nAnswer: Referral for orchiopexy.", "correct": true, "gold": "Orchiopexy"}
{"qid": "med:95", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The case describes unilateral cryptorchidism (undescended testis) in an 11-month-old boy. Spontaneous descent becomes unlikely after 6 months of age, and current guidelines (AAP, AUA) therefore recommend orchiopexy between 6 and 12 months to optimize fertility, reduce malignancy risk, and prevent torsion. Because the gonad is palpable in the inguinal canal, the diagnosis is clinical; imaging is unnecessary and hormonal therapy (hCG) is no longer first-line. Consequently, the indicated next management step is prompt referral for surgical orchiopexy.\nAnswer: referral for orchiopexy (surgical repair)", "correct": true, "gold": "Orchiopexy"}
