{"qid": "med:95", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "This 11-month-old boy has a unilateral undescended testicle (cryptorchidism) with the testis palpable in the inguinal canal. Key management points:\n\n1. **Spontaneous descent is unlikely**: Testes that haven't descended by 6 months of age rarely descend spontaneously thereafter.\n\n2. **Optimal timing for surgery**: Current guidelines (AAP/AUA) recommend orchiopexy (surgical placement of the testis into the scrotum) between **6-12 months of age**, ideally before 12 months. This timing optimizes future fertility and reduces malignancy risk while minimizing anesthesia risks.\n\n3. **No role for imaging**: Since the testis is palpable, ultrasound or other imaging is unnecessary and not recommended.\n\n4. **Hormonal therapy is not first-line**: hCG or GnRH agonists have low success rates and are not standard initial management.\n\n5. **Observation is inappropriate**: At 11 months, waiting further would delay surgery beyond the optimal window.\n\nThe child is approaching the upper limit of the ideal surgical window (12 months), so prompt surgical referral for orchiopexy is indicated.\n\nAnswer: Surgical orchiopexy (referral for surgical correction).", "correct": true, "gold": "Orchiopexy"}
{"qid": "med:97", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "An older adult with **marked lymphocytosis** found incidentally, no symptoms, normal hemoglobin and platelets, and a family history of hematologic malignancy is most consistent with **chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL)**.\n\nWhy:\n- **Age 75**: CLL is the most common leukemia in older adults.\n- **Very high absolute lymphocyte count**: CLL is diagnosed when there are **\u22655,000 clonal B lymphocytes/\u00b5L** in the blood; this patient has **50,000/mm\u00b3**.\n- **Asymptomatic/incidental finding**: CLL is often discovered on routine blood work.\n- **Normal hemoglobin and platelets**: suggests early-stage disease.\n- Flow cytometry in CLL typically shows **clonal mature B cells** expressing **CD5, CD19, CD20, and CD23**.\n\nThis is not reactive lymphocytosis, and not acute leukemia because there are no blasts or cytopenias.\n\nAnswer: Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL)", "correct": true, "gold": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL)"}
{"qid": "med:97", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has a markedly elevated lymphocyte count (50,000/mm\u00b3) with no symptoms other than osteoarthritis, and flow cytometry confirms a clonal lymphocytosis. In an older adult, this presentation is classic for chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL), which often is an incidental finding on routine blood work.\n\nAnswer: Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL)", "correct": true, "gold": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL)"}
{"qid": "med:97", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The patient\u2019s complete blood count shows a marked leukocytosis (58,000\u202fmm\u00b3) driven almost entirely by lymphocytes (absolute lymphocyte count 50,000\u202fmm\u00b3). The hemoglobin and platelet counts are normal, and he has no systemic symptoms. This pattern of isolated lymphocytosis in an older adult, discovered incidentally, is classic for chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL), especially in its early, asymptomatic stage.  \n\nAnswer: Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL).<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL)"}
