{"qid": "med:97", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient is an older adult with an incidental finding of marked peripheral lymphocytosis (ALC 50k) on routine labs, with otherwise normal CBC values and no symptoms or physical findings to suggest an acute or aggressive process. This presentation is characteristic of an indolent B-cell lymphoproliferative disorder. Flow cytometry is the key diagnostic test that would confirm the clonal B-cell population with the classic immunophenotype (CD5+/CD23+).  \nAmong possible lymphoproliferative disorders, CLL is by far the most prevalent in this age group and matches the laboratory and clinical picture exactly. Diagnostic thresholds are met (ALC well above the 5k clonal B-cell cutoff that distinguishes CLL from monoclonal B-cell lymphocytosis). Acute leukemias, mantle-cell lymphoma, and reactive lymphocytoses are far less likely given the patient\u2019s age, lack of systemic symptoms, normal hemoglobin/platelets, and the decision to perform flow cytometry in the first place.\nAnswer: chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL, Rai stage 0)", "correct": true, "gold": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL)"}
{"qid": "med:98", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This cirrhotic patient with progressive confusion, asterixis, and ascites most likely has **hepatic encephalopathy** from elevated ammonia.\n\nThe **best next treatment** is **lactulose**. It acidifies the gut lumen, converting ammonia (NH3) to ammonium (NH4+), which is trapped in the colon and excreted. It also acts as a cathartic to reduce ammonia absorption.\n\nHis hypokalemia can worsen ammonia production, so potassium should also be corrected, but the primary treatment is **lactulose**.\n\nAnswer: **Lactulose**", "correct": false, "gold": "Potassium"}
{"qid": "med:97", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at this case, the key findings are:\n- **75-year-old asymptomatic man** with an **incidental finding** of marked leukocytosis (58,000/mm\u00b3)\n- **Absolute lymphocytosis of 50,000/mm\u00b3** (lymphocytes account for the vast majority of the elevated WBC)\n- **Normal hemoglobin (15 g/dL) and platelets (250,000/mm\u00b3)**\n- **Family history** of hematologic malignancy\n\n**Analysis:**\nThe marked lymphocytosis (>5,000/mm\u00b3) in an elderly patient with preserved red blood cells and platelets is the classic presentation of **Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL)**. CLL is the most common leukemia in adults in Western countries, with a median age at diagnosis of 70-72 years. Most patients are asymptomatic at diagnosis and are identified incidentally on routine blood work.\n\nThe differential diagnosis includes:\n- **Reactive lymphocytosis**: Usually due to viral infections (EBV, CMV), but counts rarely exceed 20,000/mm\u00b3 and patients typically have symptoms\n- **Acute lymphoblastic leukemia**: Usually presents with cytopenias and symptoms\n- **Prolymphocytic leukemia**: Rare, presents with massive splenomegaly and very high counts\n- **Other lymphomas** with leukemic phase: Less likely given the classic presentation\n\nThe flow cytometry (mentioned but not detailed) would confirm clonal B-cells with the typical CLL immunophenotype (CD5+, CD19+, CD23+, CD20 dim, sIg dim).\n\n**Risk factors present:** Advanced age and family history of hematologic malignancy (CLL shows familial clustering).\n\nAnswer: Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL)", "correct": true, "gold": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL)"}
{"qid": "med:90", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient\u2019s history (45 pack\u2011year smoking, chronic progressive dyspnea, home oxygen) indicates severe COPD. The drug described as having both mucoactive and antioxidant properties is **N\u2011acetylcysteine (NAC)**. In the airways, NAC\u2019s principal pharmacologic action is mucolytic: it reduces the viscosity of mucus by **cleaving disulfide bonds in mucus glycoproteins**, thereby facilitating clearance. Its antioxidant effect (via glutathione precursor activity) is a secondary benefit.\n\nAnswer: It acts primarily as a mucolytic by cleaving disulfide bonds in mucus glycoproteins, reducing mucus viscosity.", "correct": false, "gold": "Disulfide bond cleavage"}
{"qid": "med:98", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has hepatic encephalopathy, likely triggered by hypokalemia (K\u207a 3.3 mEq/L) and possibly hypoglycemia (glucose 59 mg/dL). The best next step is to correct these reversible factors and start lactulose.\n\n**Answer:** Lactulose and correction of hypokalemia/hypoglycemia.", "correct": false, "gold": "Potassium"}
{"qid": "med:94", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient has episodic right\u2011upper\u2011quadrant pain, jaundice (scleral icterus, total bilirubin 4.1\u202fmg/dL), a cholestatic laboratory pattern (markedly elevated alkaline phosphatase, modest transaminitis), and sonographic evidence of a dilated common bile duct (10\u202fmm) with gallstones but no signs of acute cholecystitis. These findings are classic for a gallstone that has migrated into and is obstructing the common bile duct. The prior terminal ileal resection predisposes to cholesterol gallstones (bile\u2011acid malabsorption), but the immediate cause of the current presentation is the stone\u2011induced biliary obstruction.\n\nAnswer: Choledocholithiasis (a gallstone obstructing the common bile duct).", "correct": true, "gold": "Choledocholithiasis"}
{"qid": "med:82", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "This patient has **lymphogranuloma venereum (LGV)**, caused by *Chlamydia trachomatis* serotypes L1-L3. The clinical presentation is classic:\n- **Primary stage** (3 weeks ago): Small, painless, transient genital ulcer (often unnoticed or healed)\n- **Secondary stage** (current): Painful inguinal lymphadenopathy (buboes) 2-6 weeks after exposure, with nodes that become matted, necrotic, and rupture through the skin forming ulcers/sinuses\n- **Risk factor**: Unprotected sex in Southeast Asia (an endemic region)\n- **Key distinction**: Painless primary ulcer but **painful** secondary lymphadenopathy (unlike syphilis, which has painless lymphadenopathy)\n\nThe \"dark discoloration\" indicates necrosis of the lymph node tissue. On histopathological examination of the affected lymph nodes, LGV characteristically produces **necrotizing granulomatous inflammation** (stellate necrotizing granulomas with central suppuration). \n\nClinically, the pathognomonic **\"groove sign\"** (a linear depression along the inguinal ligament separating the superficial inguinal and deep femoral lymph node chains) may also be seen, though the histological finding of necrotizing granulomas explains the physical finding of ulcerative nodes with dark discoloration.\n\nAnswer: Necrotizing granulomatous inflammation (necrotizing granulomas) in the lymph nodes.", "correct": false, "gold": "PCR positive for Chlamydia trachomatis"}
