{"qid": "med:101", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This is a **hydatidiform mole**, most likely a **complete mole**:\n\n- 4 months amenorrhea\n- uterus larger than dates\n- no fetal heart sounds\n- no fetal parts on ultrasound\n- grape-like vesicles per vaginum\n- early hypertension\n\nA **complete mole** typically has an **androgenetic diploid genotype**, most commonly formed when an empty ovum is fertilized by one sperm that duplicates.\n\nMost common genotype:\n**46,XX, entirely paternal in origin**\n\nAnswer: **46,XX (complete hydatidiform mole; paternal/androgenetic diploid genome)**", "correct": true, "gold": "46XX"}
{"qid": "med:97", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\nThis 75-year-old asymptomatic man is found to have marked leukocytosis (58,000/mm\u00b3) with an absolute lymphocyte count of 50,000/mm\u00b3 on flow cytometry. His hemoglobin and platelet counts are normal, and he has no B symptoms (fever, weight loss, night sweats), lymphadenopathy, or splenomegaly on exam. These findings are classic for **chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL)**, which is the most common leukemia in older adults and is often diagnosed incidentally. \r\n\r\nAnswer: Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL)", "correct": true, "gold": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL)"}
{"qid": "med:101", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The presentation suggests a hydatidiform mole (complete mole), given the uterine size larger than dates, absence of fetal parts, grape-like vaginal mass, and hypertension. In a complete mole, the genotype is typically 46,XX, all paternal in origin (androgenetic), usually from duplication of a haploid sperm's DNA after fertilization of an empty ovum.\n\nAnswer: 46,XX", "correct": true, "gold": "46XX"}
