{"qid": "med:101", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation (secondary amenorrhea, vaginal expulsion of grape-like vesicles, uterine enlargement without fetal parts or heart sounds, and early-onset hypertension) is diagnostic of complete hydatidiform mole. Complete moles arise from fertilization of an empty ovum by a single haploid sperm that then duplicates its genome (or, less often, by two separate sperm). Consequently the molar tissue is diploid and entirely paternal in origin; the most common karyotype is therefore 46 XX (paternal). Dilation and evacuation yields this tissue, whose genotype is accordingly 46 XX (paternal).\nAnswer: 46XX (paternal)", "correct": true, "gold": "46XX"}
