{"qid": "med:87", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "During exercise, activation of the sympathetic nervous system and withdrawal of vagal tone increase heart rate and myocardial contractility. This raises cardiac output, which is the primary determinant of systolic blood pressure. Diastolic pressure remains relatively unchanged because metabolic vasodilation in active skeletal muscle offsets sympathetic vasoconstriction, keeping peripheral resistance low. Thus, the combined rise in heart rate and systolic pressure with a stable diastolic pressure is best explained by an increase in cardiac output.\n\nAnswer: Increased cardiac output (secondary to sympathetic activation).", "correct": false, "gold": "Sympathetic nervous system activation"}
