{"qid": "med:109", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient has a **deep sternal wound infection/mediastinitis** after recent median sternotomy for CABG, suggested by:\n\n- **Wound dehiscence**\n- **Necrotic tissue with pus**\n- **CT showing fluid collection and fat stranding**\n- Risk factors: **diabetes** and recent cardiac surgery\n\nThis is not managed with simple dressings alone. The key next step is **urgent operative management** with:\n\n- **Surgical debridement** of necrotic/infected tissue\n- **Drainage** of the collection\n- **Broad-spectrum IV antibiotics** covering likely organisms (especially *Staphylococcus aureus*, including MRSA, and gram-negatives as needed)\n\nAnswer: Urgent surgical debridement and drainage of the infected sternal wound with initiation of broad-spectrum IV antibiotics.", "correct": true, "gold": "Surgical debridement"}
{"qid": "med:109", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has a sternal wound dehiscence with necrotic tissue and pus, along with CT findings of fluid collection and fat stranding, indicating a deep sternal wound infection or mediastinitis. Given the presence of necrotic tissue, the most appropriate next step is surgical debridement to remove all devitalized tissue, control infection, and allow for proper healing.\n\nAnswer: Surgical debridement", "correct": true, "gold": "Surgical debridement"}
{"qid": "med:109", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient is 3 weeks post-sternotomy and now exhibits clear local signs of infection (dehiscence, necrotic tissue, pus) together with CT evidence of a localized peristernal fluid collection and fat stranding. These findings indicate a sternal wound infection that requires prompt source control. Because necrotic tissue and pus are already visible, non-operative measures such as antibiotics alone or simple dressing changes will not remove the infected, non-viable material. Therefore the immediate next management step is operative wound exploration, thorough debridement of all necrotic tissue, drainage of the fluid collection, and acquisition of deep cultures to guide subsequent antibiotic therapy. Once source control has been achieved, intravenous antibiotics and local wound care can be tailored to culture results.\nAnswer: surgical debridement & drainage (IV abx after cultures)", "correct": true, "gold": "Surgical debridement"}
{"qid": "med:109", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Immediate surgical debridement of the infected sternal wound combined with initiation of broad\u2011spectrum intravenous antibiotics and wound cultures.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "Surgical debridement"}
{"qid": "med:114", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Assess alcohol use (screen for alcohol use disorder/counsel on reducing alcohol intake).\n\nAnswer: Screen for alcohol use disorder / advise reduction of alcohol consumption", "correct": false, "gold": "Dual-energy x-ray bone absorptiometry"}
{"qid": "med:114", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This 62-year-old woman is asymptomatic and up to date on several routine screenings, but she has a major additional preventive need: **screening for osteoporosis with bone mineral density testing (DEXA scan)**.\n\nWhy:\n- **All women age \u226565** should be screened, and **younger postmenopausal women with increased fracture risk** should also be screened.\n- She is **62** and has several risk factors:\n  - **Low body weight/BMI 18**\n  - **Current smoker**\n  - **Heavy alcohol use**\n- These risk factors make osteoporosis screening appropriate now.\n\nOther options are less appropriate:\n- **Mammography**: last done 1 year ago and normal; not the best next step over DEXA.\n- **Pap smear**: normal 2 years ago; routine interval screening is longer.\n- **Colonoscopy**: normal 2 years ago; repeat not needed yet.\n- **Abdominal aortic aneurysm screening**: generally for men 65\u201375 with smoking history, not this patient.\n- **Lung cancer screening**: requires at least a 20 pack-year history; she has 9 pack-years.\n- **Influenza vaccine**: already received 6 months ago.\n\nAnswer: Order bone mineral density testing with a DEXA scan to screen for osteoporosis.", "correct": false, "gold": "Dual-energy x-ray bone absorptiometry"}
{"qid": "med:114", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has a low BMI (18), heavy alcohol use (4\u20135 glasses of wine daily), and is a postmenopausal woman, placing her at high risk for osteoporosis. She also has hypertension and a smoking history, but the most pressing preventive issue given her risk factors is bone density screening. A DEXA scan is indicated to assess for osteoporosis.\n\nAnswer: DEXA scan (bone density testing)", "correct": false, "gold": "Dual-energy x-ray bone absorptiometry"}
{"qid": "med:109", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at this case, we have a 62-year-old man with a deep sternal wound infection following CABG surgery 3 weeks ago. Key findings include:\n- Wound dehiscence in the lower sternum\n- Visible necrotic tissue with pus (indicating devitalized tissue and abscess formation)\n- CT showing fluid collection with fat stranding in perisurgical tissues (confirming deep infection/mediastinitis)\n- Risk factors: long-standing diabetes mellitus\n\n**Analysis:**\nThis represents a **deep sternal wound infection (DSWI)** or post-sternotomy mediastinitis. The presence of necrotic tissue, purulent drainage, and CT evidence of fluid collections indicates this is not a superficial infection but involves deep tissues.\n\n**Management principles:**\n1. **Necrotic tissue must be removed** - It acts as a nidus for infection, prevents wound healing, and antibiotics cannot penetrate avascular necrotic tissue effectively.\n2. **Source control is essential** - The pus and fluid collection require drainage.\n3. **Antibiotics are adjunctive** - While broad-spectrum antibiotics (covering Staphylococcus aureus including MRSA, and gram-negatives) are necessary, they are insufficient alone when necrotic tissue and abscess are present.\n\n**Why surgical debridement is the answer:**\n- Antibiotics alone cannot clear established infection in the presence of necrotic tissue\n- The wound is dehisced with visible necrosis requiring operative exploration\n- CT findings suggest deep extension requiring thorough debridement\n- This is a surgical emergency/urgency requiring operative drainage and removal of all devitalized tissue, often followed by vacuum-assisted closure (VAC) or muscle flap reconstruction\n\nOther options like needle aspiration, antibiotics alone, or local wound care are inadequate because they don't address the necrotic tissue and deep infection.\n\nAnswer: Surgical debridement of the wound (operative debridement).", "correct": true, "gold": "Surgical debridement"}
{"qid": "med:109", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "This patient presents with signs of a deep sternal wound infection (DSWI) following a recent CABG, including wound dehiscence, necrotic tissue with pus, and a small fluid collection with fat stranding on CT. In such cases, the priority is source control. \r\n\r\nSurgical debridement is essential to remove necrotic tissue, drain any collections, and obtain wound cultures to guide antibiotic therapy. While broad-spectrum antibiotics are important, they are not sufficient without debridement in the presence of devitalized tissue and an infected fluid collection. \r\n\r\nAnswer: **Surgical debridement and drainage of the wound.**", "correct": true, "gold": "Surgical debridement"}
{"qid": "med:107", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient has macrocytic anemia (MCV 109 fL) with pancytopenia, a low reticulocyte count, and hypersegmented neutrophils\u2014classic for megaloblastic anemia. The presence of neurological deficits (wide\u2011based ataxic gait, behavioral changes/paranoia) points specifically to vitamin\u202fB12 deficiency (subacute combined degeneration), as folate deficiency does not cause neurologic disease. Anti\u2011intrinsic factor antibodies are negative, making pernicious anemia unlikely. Given his long\u2011standing vegetarian diet, the most plausible etiology is inadequate dietary intake of vitamin\u202fB12.\n\nAnswer: Vitamin B12 deficiency (due to dietary insufficiency).", "correct": true, "gold": "Vitamin B12 deficiency"}
{"qid": "med:108", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Answer: \\(\\boxed{\\text{Clonidine}}\\)", "correct": false, "gold": "Methadone"}
{"qid": "med:115", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This presentation is most consistent with an **acute ischemic stroke** causing a **left-sided upper motor neuron lesion** (right arm and leg weakness, hyperreflexia, Babinski). Given the vascular risk factors and gradual progression over 1 hour, the lesion is most likely a **cerebral infarction** rather than hemorrhage.\n\nIn an acute ischemic infarct, the characteristic pathologic change is:\n\n- **Liquefactive necrosis of brain tissue**\n- Early ischemic neuronal injury shows **\u201cred neurons\u201d**\n- Later there is **macrophage/microglial infiltration** and eventual **cystic cavitation**\n\nAnswer: Liquefactive necrosis due to cerebral infarction (ischemic stroke)", "correct": true, "gold": "Liquefactive necrosis"}
{"qid": "med:115", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Most likely pathologic change: Lipohyalinosis (hyaline arteriolosclerosis) of small penetrating arteries leading to a lacunar infarct**\n\n**Reasoning:**\n- This patient has a classic **pure motor hemiparesis** (face/arm/leg weakness without cortical signs like aphasia, neglect, or visual field defects), which is the hallmark presentation of a **lacunar stroke**.\n- Lacunar strokes result from occlusion of small, deep **penetrating arteries** (e.g., lenticulostriate arteries) that supply the internal capsule, basal ganglia, thalamus, or pons.\n- The major risk factors for lacunar infarcts are **long-standing hypertension and diabetes mellitus** \u2014 both present in this patient for decades.\n- Chronic hypertension and diabetes cause a specific vascular pathology in these small vessels called **lipohyalinosis** (also called hyaline arteriolosclerosis), characterized by:\n  - Deposition of hyaline (eosinophilic, PAS-positive) material in the vessel wall\n  - Smooth muscle cell loss and replacement by collagen\n  - Vessel wall thickening and luminal narrowing\n  - Eventually leading to vessel occlusion or rupture (small vessel occlusion in this case)\n- The CT scan would show a small, well-defined hypodense lesion in a deep brain structure (e.g., internal capsule or basal ganglia), consistent with an acute or subacute lacunar infarct.\n- This contrasts with **atherosclerosis**, which affects larger vessels and typically causes cortical infarcts with more prominent focal deficits (aphasia, neglect, visual field cuts).\n\nAnswer: Lipohyalinosis (hyaline arteriolosclerosis) of the small penetrating cerebral arteries, resulting in a lacunar infarct", "correct": false, "gold": "Liquefactive necrosis"}
{"qid": "med:102", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient\u2019s use of a high\u2011dose inhaled corticosteroid (fluticasone) predisposes to oral candidiasis (thrush), which presents as white, removable plaques on the oral mucosa and a sore mouth. Because the examination shows no posterior extension and the patient is immunocompetent, the infection is limited to the oral cavity. The immediate management is to treat the fungal infection with a topical antifungal agent such as nystatin suspension or clotrimazole troches. In addition, the patient should be counseled to rinse his mouth after each inhaler use to prevent recurrence, but the next therapeutic step is antifungal therapy.\n\nAnswer: Prescribe topical antifungal therapy (e.g., nystatin suspension or clotrimazole troches).", "correct": true, "gold": "Nystatin"}
{"qid": "med:106", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "**Chronic active gastritis with *H. pylori* organisms** (curved gram\u2011negative rods visible on the mucosal surface, often with neutrophilic and lymphoplasmacytic infiltrates in the lamina propria).  \n\n*Rationale*: The patient\u2019s epigastric pain that is relieved by eating, weight gain, and occult GI bleeding are classic for a duodenal peptic ulcer. In the absence of chronic NSAID use, the most common etiology is *Helicobacter pylori* infection (present in >90\u202f% of duodenal ulcers). Endoscopic biopsy therefore most likely demonstrates the organism and associated active inflammation.  \n\nAnswer: Chronic active gastritis with *H. pylori* organisms (curved gram\u2011negative rods on the mucosal surface).", "correct": false, "gold": "Urease-producing organism in the small intestine"}
{"qid": "med:115", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation of acute-onset focal neurologic deficits (right-sided hemiparesis) together with upper-motor-neuron signs localizes the lesion to the left corticospinal tract, most often within the MCA territory or internal capsule. The patient\u2019s long-standing vascular risk factors (hypertension, diabetes, ischemic heart disease) favor in-situ arterial thrombosis rather than hemorrhage or embolism; the stuttering progression over one hour is also characteristic of evolving thrombotic ischemia.\n\nAt one hour no light-microscopic change is yet visible, but the inevitable tissue response to ischemic infarction in the central nervous system is liquefactive necrosis. This distinguishes brain infarction from the coagulative necrosis seen in other solid organs and from the hematoma that would form after hemorrhage.\n\nThus the most probable pathologic change is liquefactive necrosis.\n\nAnswer: liquefactive necrosis (brain infarct)", "correct": true, "gold": "Liquefactive necrosis"}
{"qid": "med:114", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient is a 62-year-old woman presenting for routine preventive care. All age-appropriate cancer screenings (mammography, Pap smear, colonoscopy) and vaccinations are already up to date per USPSTF intervals. The history instead highlights multiple risk factors that warrant earlier osteoporosis screening: Asian ethnicity, low BMI, current tobacco use, and consumption of >3 alcoholic drinks daily. These factors raise her 10-year fracture probability to the threshold at which USPSTF recommends bone-density testing even before age 65. Therefore, the single most appropriate next management step is to obtain a DEXA scan.\nAnswer: DEXA (osteoporosis screening)", "correct": false, "gold": "Dual-energy x-ray bone absorptiometry"}
{"qid": "med:115", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient presents with acute onset of right-sided weakness, hypertonia, and a positive Babinski sign, suggesting an upper motor neuron lesion, likely in the left hemisphere. The gradual increase in weakness over 1 hour is typical of an evolving ischemic stroke. Given the history of ischemic heart disease, diabetes, and hypertension, the most likely cause is thromboembolic occlusion of a cerebral artery. The CT scan (not shown here, but implied to be non-contrast) would typically show no hemorrhage in the first few hours, but may show early signs of infarction. The most likely pathologic change is **cerebral infarction due to thromboembolism**.\n\nAnswer: Cerebral infarction (ischemic stroke) due to thromboembolism.", "correct": false, "gold": "Liquefactive necrosis"}
