{"qid": "med:122", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The patient\u2019s acute confusion, fluctuating level of consciousness, reversed sleep\u2011wake pattern, inattention, and disorientation without focal neurologic or systemic abnormalities are classic features of delirium. In older adults shortly after major surgery, the most common cause of such a presentation is postoperative delirium, often triggered by a combination of factors such as pain, opioid analgesia, sleep disruption, and the surgical stress response.\n\nAnswer: Postoperative delirium.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "Delirium"}
{"qid": "med:123", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Low-molecular-weight heparin plus low-dose aspirin (this patient has antiphospholipid syndrome, likely secondary to SLE).\n\nAnswer: Start prophylactic low-molecular-weight heparin (e.g., enoxaparin) combined with low-dose aspirin for the duration of pregnancy.", "correct": false, "gold": "Aspirin and heparin"}
{"qid": "med:122", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The vignette describes an acute change in mental status in a hospitalized elderly patient. Key diagnostic features include inattention, altered level of consciousness with a reversed sleep\u2013wake cycle, disorientation, and a fluctuating course\u2014all developing days after surgery. These elements fulfill the diagnostic criteria for delirium.\nAlternative explanations are excluded by the details supplied:\n- Minimal alcohol intake and onset six days after surgery make alcohol withdrawal improbable (typical peak is 48\u201372 h).\n- Normal vital signs and a negative urine dipstick make systemic infection or metabolic crisis unlikely precipitants.\n- Absence of focal neurologic findings makes stroke improbable.\n- Lack of any premorbid cognitive or functional decline makes an underlying dementia improbable.\nConsequently, the symptoms are produced by an acute, fluctuating confusional state (delirium) rather than by focal brain injury, chronic neurodegeneration, or withdrawal.\nAnswer: delirium (postoperative)", "correct": true, "gold": "Delirium"}
{"qid": "med:123", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient likely has **antiphospholipid syndrome secondary to SLE**, suggested by:\n\n- **Recurrent pregnancy losses** before 10\u201315 weeks\n- **History of DVTs and PE**\n- Underlying **systemic lupus erythematosus**\n\nIn pregnant patients with antiphospholipid syndrome, the best management to reduce miscarriage risk is:\n\n- **Low-dose aspirin**\n- **Heparin** during pregnancy\n\nBecause she is pregnant, **warfarin is contraindicated** due to teratogenicity.\n\nAnswer: Start low-dose aspirin plus heparin (typically low-molecular-weight heparin) throughout pregnancy.", "correct": false, "gold": "Aspirin and heparin"}
{"qid": "med:115", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Answer: Spontaneous intracerebral hemorrhage (most likely due to hypertensive rupture of a Charcot\u2013Bouchard microaneurysm in the basal ganglia).", "correct": false, "gold": "Liquefactive necrosis"}
{"qid": "med:123", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has a history of recurrent pregnancy loss, systemic lupus erythematosus, and thrombotic events (DVTs and pulmonary embolism). This strongly suggests antiphospholipid syndrome (APS). In pregnant women with APS and prior pregnancy loss, the standard treatment to improve pregnancy outcomes is low-dose aspirin plus low molecular weight heparin (or unfractionated heparin). Warfarin is avoided in pregnancy due to teratogenicity. Corticosteroids are not first-line for APS in pregnancy.\n\n**Answer:** Low-dose aspirin plus low molecular weight heparin.", "correct": false, "gold": "Aspirin and heparin"}
{"qid": "med:108", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient\u2019s presentation\u2014anxiety, restlessness, autonomic hyperactivity (tachycardia, hypertension, diaphoresis, dilated pupils, piloerection), and GI symptoms (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, cramping) in the setting of chronic opioid use for back pain\u2014is classic for **opioid withdrawal**.  \n\nThe most effective pharmacologic treatment for moderate\u2011to\u2011severe opioid withdrawal is **opioid substitution therapy** to relieve symptoms and prevent complications. In the outpatient setting, **buprenorphine** (a partial \u03bc\u2011agonist) is preferred over methadone because it has a ceiling effect for respiratory depression, a lower overdose risk, and can be prescribed in primary care (office\u2011based treatment). It alleviates both the autonomic and GI symptoms of withdrawal and can serve as maintenance therapy for opioid use disorder or chronic pain.\n\nAnswer: Buprenorphine", "correct": false, "gold": "Methadone"}
{"qid": "med:122", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Answer: Opioid-induced delirium (secondary to oxycodone).", "correct": true, "gold": "Delirium"}
{"qid": "med:123", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "This patient has SLE plus a classic history of recurrent miscarriages and venous thromboembolic events, meeting diagnostic criteria for antiphospholipid syndrome (APS). In a pregnant patient with thrombotic APS, low-dose aspirin plus therapeutic-dose low-molecular-weight heparin is indicated from early gestation through the puerperium; this regimen improves live-birth rates and prevents further maternal thrombosis. Warfarin is contraindicated because of teratogenicity, and no other agent (e.g., hydroxychloroquine alone) addresses the thrombotic mechanism of fetal loss.\nAnswer: LMWH + low-dose aspirin (therapeutic-dose LMWH)", "correct": false, "gold": "Aspirin and heparin"}
{"qid": "med:123", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Begin prophylactic low\u2011molecular\u2011weight heparin (e.g., enoxaparin 40\u202fmg SC daily) combined with low\u2011dose aspirin (81\u202fmg daily) throughout pregnancy, continue hydroxychloroquine for SLE, screen for antiphospholipid antibodies, and consider adjunctive vaginal progesterone if indicated; provide close monitoring and preconception counseling.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Aspirin and heparin"}
