{"qid": "med:127", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has recurrent infections with *Neisseria gonorrhoeae* (diplococci on Thayer-Martin agar) and *Streptococcus pneumoniae* (meningitis). These bacteria are typically cleared by opsonization and complement-mediated killing. Recurrent infections with encapsulated organisms and *Neisseria* species point to a deficiency in the complement system, specifically the membrane attack complex (MAC) pathway (C5\u2013C9) or terminal complement components. This defect impairs lysis of *Neisseria* and also affects opsonization for *Streptococcus pneumoniae*.\n\n**Answer:** Deficiency in a terminal complement component (e.g., C5, C6, C7, C8, or C9).", "correct": false, "gold": "Creates pores in the cell membrane"}
{"qid": "med:127", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "The patient\u2019s vaginal infection is **Neisseria gonorrhoeae** (gram-negative diplococci growing on **Thayer-Martin agar**). Her history of **meningitis** suggests possible **Neisseria meningitidis** infection, and recurrent infection with **Streptococcus pneumoniae** also points to a problem with clearance of encapsulated bacteria.\n\nA defect in the **terminal complement pathway (C5\u2013C9)** causes recurrent **Neisseria** infections, and complement defects are part of the **innate immune system**. The membrane attack complex normally forms pores in bacterial membranes.\n\nSo the deficient component is:\n\n**Terminal complement proteins that form the membrane attack complex (MAC), especially C5\u2013C9.**\n\nAnswer: Deficiency of the terminal complement pathway (C5\u2013C9), which forms the membrane attack complex (MAC).", "correct": false, "gold": "Creates pores in the cell membrane"}
{"qid": "med:127", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "## Analysis\n\n**Clinical clues:**\n- **Diplococci on Thayer-Martin agar** \u2192 *Neisseria gonorrhea* (confirmed by symptoms of dysuria, itching, and vaginal discharge)\n- **History of meningitis** \u2192 likely due to *Neisseria meningitidis*, another Neisseria species\n- **History of *Streptococcus pneumoniae* infection** \u2192 an encapsulated organism\n- **Recurrent infections with Neisseria species AND encapsulated organisms (S. pneumoniae)** \u2192 points toward a **complement deficiency**\n\n**Key reasoning:**\n\nRecurrent **Neisseria** infections (gonorrhea, meningitidis) classically point to a **terminal complement deficiency (C5\u2013C9)**, since these components form the **Membrane Attack Complex (MAC)**, which is critical for lysing gram-negative diplococci like *Neisseria*.\n\nHowever, this patient *also* has a history of **Streptococcus pneumoniae** infection, an **encapsulated organism** that is normally cleared via **opsonization**, a process dependent on **C3b** deposition on the pathogen surface (facilitating phagocytosis by neutrophils/macrophages via complement receptors).\n\nThe **common link** between:\n1. Susceptibility to encapsulated organisms (opsonization defect)\n2. Susceptibility to Neisseria species (MAC defect)\n\n...is a deficiency in **C3**, since C3 is the **central convergence point** of all three complement pathways (classical, alternative, lectin) and is required for:\n- **Opsonization** (via C3b) \u2014 protecting against encapsulated organisms like *S. pneumoniae*\n- **Downstream MAC formation** (C3b \u2192 C5 convertase \u2192 C5-9) \u2014 protecting against *Neisseria* species\n\nA deficiency in an isolated terminal complement component (C5-C9) would only explain the Neisseria susceptibility, not the *S. pneumoniae* infections. Therefore, **C3 deficiency** best explains the combined susceptibility pattern.\n\nAnswer: The patient likely has a deficiency in **complement protein C3**, which serves as the central component linking all complement activation pathways\u2014required both for opsonization (protecting against encapsulated organisms like *S. pneumoniae*) and for downstream membrane attack complex formation (protecting against *Neisseria* species).", "correct": false, "gold": "Creates pores in the cell membrane"}
{"qid": "med:121", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at this 3-year-old boy with symptoms since age 2 months, the key features are:\n- **Recurrent pyogenic skin lesions** (raised, erythematous, purulent abscesses) on face, neck, groin, and extremities\n- **Recurrent respiratory infections** and fevers\n- **Generalized lymphadenopathy** (cervical and axillary)\n- **Failure to thrive** (5th percentile for length, 10th for weight)\n- **Onset in early infancy**\n\nThe differential diagnosis includes primary immunodeficiencies:\n\n**Chronic Granulomatous Disease (CGD)** fits best because:\n- Defective NADPH oxidase \u2192 inability to kill catalase-positive organisms (S. aureus, Burkholderia, Serratia, Aspergillus)\n- Characterized by recurrent **pyogenic skin abscesses** (often perianal/perioral), pneumonia, lymphadenitis, and granuloma formation\n- Presents in infancy/early childhood with the exact pattern described\n- The lesions are \"erythematous\" (inflamed/\"hot\"), consistent with active infection, unlike the \"cold\" abscesses of Hyper-IgE syndrome\n\n**Why not other options:**\n- **Hyper-IgE (Job) syndrome**: Features \"cold\" (non-erythematous, non-tender) abscesses, eczema, retained primary teeth, and pneumatoceles\u2014none of which are described here\n- **Leukocyte Adhesion Deficiency**: Characterized by infections **without** pus formation (impaired neutrophil migration), but this patient has lesions that \"secrete pus\"\n- **X-linked Agammaglobulinemia**: Would have absent/small lymph nodes, not enlarged ones\n- **Severe Combined Immunodeficiency**: Would present with severe opportunistic infections and usually fatal without transplant earlier\n\nThe combination of recurrent purulent skin abscesses, respiratory infections, lymphadenopathy, and onset in infancy is classic for CGD.\n\nAnswer: Chronic Granulomatous Disease", "correct": true, "gold": "Chronic granulomatous disease"}
{"qid": "med:122", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient\u2019s acute, fluctuating confusion with inattention, disorientation, and reversal of the sleep\u2011wake cycle in the setting of recent major surgery (hip fracture repair) is classic for **delirium**. Infection is unlikely given the normal urine dipstick and vital signs; alcohol withdrawal is improbable with minimal alcohol use; opioid toxicity would produce more consistent sedation and possible respiratory depression; and the absence of focal deficits makes a cerebrovascular event unlikely. In elderly patients, postoperative delirium is common after orthopedic procedures and is multifactorial (advanced age, surgical stress, pain, sleep deprivation, and medications such as opioids).\n\nAnswer: Postoperative delirium.", "correct": true, "gold": "Delirium"}
