{"qid": "med:131", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Progressive **proximal muscle weakness** (trouble climbing stairs, brushing hair) suggests an inflammatory myopathy. The biopsy finding is the key:\n\n- **CD8+ T-cell infiltration in the endomysium** is classic for **polymyositis**\n- By contrast:\n  - **Dermatomyositis** has **perimysial/perivascular** inflammation with **CD4+** cells and skin findings\n  - **Inclusion body myositis** can also involve endomysial inflammation but usually affects **older patients** and often has **distal** weakness as well\n\nNo headache or vision changes makes **giant cell arteritis** irrelevant here.\n\nAnswer: Polymyositis", "correct": true, "gold": "Polymyositis"}
{"qid": "med:127", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient's current infection is confirmed as Neisseria gonorrhoeae by the combination of Gram-negative diplococci and selective growth on Thayer-Martin agar. Her prior episodes of meningitis and pneumococcal disease are most consistent with prior infection by N. meningitidis (another Neisseria species).  \n\nRecurrent or invasive infections caused by Neisseria spp. are classically produced by inherited deficiencies of the terminal complement proteins (C5\u2013C9). These proteins assemble into the membrane-attack complex (MAC) that directly lyses Neisseria; without MAC formation the organisms disseminate despite normal opsonization and chemotaxis.  \n\nAlthough early-complement or antibody defects can predispose to S. pneumoniae, the vignette specifically highlights two Neisseria infections, directing attention to the terminal pathway. The deficient component is therefore best described as the MAC (or any of its constituent terminal complement proteins).  \n\nAnswer: MAC (C5-C9) deficiency", "correct": false, "gold": "Creates pores in the cell membrane"}
{"qid": "med:131", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The presentation of proximal muscle weakness (difficulty climbing stairs, brushing hair) along with a muscle biopsy showing CD8+ lymphocyte infiltration in the endomysium is classic for **polymyositis**. The absence of headache or vision changes helps rule out other conditions like dermatomyositis (which has characteristic rash) or inclusion body myositis (often distal weakness and older age).\n\nAnswer: Polymyositis", "correct": true, "gold": "Polymyositis"}
{"qid": "med:131", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Polymyositis** \u2014 proximal muscle weakness with CD8+ T-cell (cytotoxic) infiltration of the endomysium is classic for this diagnosis, distinguishing it from dermatomyositis (which shows perimysial/perivascular inflammation, often with CD4+ cells and complement deposition, along with skin findings).\n\nAnswer: Polymyositis", "correct": true, "gold": "Polymyositis"}
{"qid": "med:131", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient's proximal muscle weakness (shoulder and pelvic girdle) without skin findings, headaches, or visual changes points to an idiopathic inflammatory myopathy. Among these, the biopsy finding of endomysial CD8+ T-cell invasion is the histologic hallmark of polymyositis (MHC-I upregulation on sarcolemma with CD8+ cytotoxic attack on non-necrotic fibers).  \n\nDermatomyositis instead shows perimysial/perivascular B-cell and CD4+ infiltrates; inclusion-body myositis occurs in older patients, produces distal/asymmetric weakness, and features rimmed vacuoles on biopsy. These alternatives are therefore excluded by age, distribution of weakness, and histology.  \n\nThus the presentation and biopsy are diagnostic of polymyositis.\n\nAnswer: polymyositis", "correct": true, "gold": "Polymyositis"}
{"qid": "med:131", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Polymyositis<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "Polymyositis"}
{"qid": "med:134", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This child most likely has **fragile X syndrome**:\n\n- **Speech/language delay**\n- **Hyperactivity/behavioral issues**\n- **Long, narrow face**\n- Trinucleotide repeat disorder (**CGG repeat**)\n\nFragile X syndrome is inherited in an **X-linked dominant** pattern.\n\nAnswer: X-linked dominant", "correct": true, "gold": "X-linked dominant"}
{"qid": "med:131", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at this clinical scenario, I need to identify the inflammatory myopathy based on:\n1. **Clinical presentation**: 40-year-old male with progressive proximal muscle weakness (difficulty climbing stairs indicates lower extremity weakness; inability to brush hair indicates upper extremity/proximal arm weakness)\n2. **Exclusion of other symptoms**: No skin findings (ruling out dermatomyositis), no headaches or vision changes\n3. **Key histological finding**: CD8+ lymphocyte infiltration specifically in the endomysium\n\n**Analysis of inflammatory myopathies:**\n\n- **Polymyositis**: Characterized by endomysial infiltration of CD8+ cytotoxic T-cells invading non-necrotic muscle fibers. This matches the biopsy finding exactly. It typically affects adults aged 40-60 with symmetric proximal weakness and no skin involvement.\n\n- **Dermatomyositis**: Would show perivascular and perimysial inflammation with CD4+ T-cells and B-cells, plus perifascicular atrophy. The biopsy finding of endomysial CD8+ cells is not typical for DM.\n\n- **Inclusion Body Myositis**: Usually affects patients >50 years old with asymmetric weakness involving both proximal and distal muscles (especially finger flexors). While it has endomysial CD8+ infiltrates, the age (40 is young for IBM) and the specific mention of CD8+ endomysial infiltration without rimmed vacuoles or inclusions points away from IBM.\n\n- **Necrotizing Autoimmune Myopathy**: Shows prominent muscle fiber necrosis with minimal inflammatory infiltrate.\n\nThe combination of proximal symmetric weakness in a middle-aged adult plus the pathognomonic finding of **CD8+ T-cell infiltration in the endomysium** is diagnostic of polymyositis.\n\nAnswer: Polymyositis", "correct": true, "gold": "Polymyositis"}
{"qid": "med:134", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**X-linked dominant** inheritance pattern (with variable penetrance and expressivity).\n\nThis clinical presentation is classic for **Fragile X syndrome**, caused by a CGG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the **FMR1 gene** located on the X chromosome. Key features supporting this diagnosis include:\n\n- **Developmental delay** (speech delay, limited vocabulary)\n- **Hyperactivity** and difficulty with redirection (common behavioral phenotype)\n- **Long, narrow face** (classic dysmorphic feature, though macroorchidism and prominent jaw/forehead often become more apparent with age)\n- **Male sex** (X-linked disorders more commonly and more severely affect males)\n\nFragile X syndrome follows an X-linked dominant pattern with reduced penetrance, meaning male carriers of the full mutation are typically affected, while female carriers may show milder or no symptoms due to the presence of a second, normal X chromosome (subject to X-inactivation patterns). The disorder also demonstrates the phenomenon of **anticipation**, where the trinucleotide repeat length can increase in successive generations, leading to earlier onset or more severe disease.\n\nAnswer: X-linked dominant inheritance with reduced penetrance (Fragile X syndrome, FMR1 gene CGG repeat expansion)", "correct": true, "gold": "X-linked dominant"}
{"qid": "med:134", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The description points to fragile X syndrome, which is a trinucleotide repeat disorder. It is characterized by intellectual disability, speech delay, hyperactivity, a long narrow face, and large ears. Fragile X syndrome is X-linked dominant with reduced penetrance in females.\n\nAnswer: X-linked dominant", "correct": true, "gold": "X-linked dominant"}
{"qid": "med:123", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "The patient presents with classic features of **antiphospholipid syndrome (APS)**: a history of systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE), recurrent venous thromboembolism (multiple DVTs and a pulmonary embolism), and recurrent early fetal loss (4 miscarriages between 8\u201315 weeks). \n\n### Management\n- **Therapeutic anticoagulation**: Due to her prior VTE, she requires therapeutic-dose low molecular weight heparin (LMWH), such as enoxaparin, throughout pregnancy and for at least 6 weeks postpartum. Warfarin is teratogenic and should be avoided during pregnancy.\n- **Low-dose aspirin (LDA)**: 81 mg daily should be initiated early in pregnancy (ideally at confirmation or preconception) to reduce the risk of obstetric complications associated with APS (e.g., fetal loss, preeclampsia, and placental insufficiency).\n- **Multidisciplinary care**: She should be managed by a high-risk obstetrics team with close monitoring for preeclampsia, fetal growth restriction, and thrombotic events.\n\nWhile APS serologies (lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin, and anti-\u03b22 glycoprotein I antibodies) should be obtained to confirm the diagnosis, treatment should not be delayed given her high-risk history. \n\nAnswer: **Therapeutic low molecular weight heparin (LMWH) plus low-dose aspirin (81 mg daily).**", "correct": false, "gold": "Aspirin and heparin"}
{"qid": "med:134", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The described presentation (developmental/speech delay, hyperactivity, and long/narrow facies in a young boy) is the classic early phenotype of Fragile X syndrome, the most common inherited form of intellectual disability. Fragile X is caused by CGG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the FMR1 gene on Xq27.3.  \n\nBecause the fully expanded allele produces disease in hemizygous males and produces (usually milder) manifestations in a substantial fraction of heterozygous females, the inheritance pattern is classified as X-linked dominant with incomplete penetrance.  \n\nThis distinguishes it from purely recessive X-linked disorders (e.g., Duchenne muscular dystrophy) and from autosomal trinucleotide-repeat diseases (Huntington, myotonic dystrophy, Friedreich ataxia).  \n\nAnswer: X-linked dominant (incomplete penetrance)", "correct": true, "gold": "X-linked dominant"}
{"qid": "med:121", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\nThis 3-year-old boy presents with early-onset (age 2 months) recurrent **pus-secreting, erythematous skin abscesses**, along with **recurrent respiratory tract infections, bilateral lymphadenopathy, persistent fevers, and failure to thrive**. These features are highly suggestive of a primary immunodeficiency.  \r\n\r\nChronic Granulomatous Disease (CGD) is the most likely diagnosis. It is caused by a defect in the NADPH oxidase complex, leading to impaired phagocytic killing of catalase-positive organisms (most commonly *Staphylococcus aureus*). This results in recurrent pyogenic infections such as skin abscesses, lymphadenitis, and pneumonia. The X-linked form predominantly affects males and often presents in infancy or early childhood. In contrast, conditions like Leukocyte Adhesion Deficiency typically lack pus formation, Hyper-IgE Syndrome presents with \"cold\" abscesses and eczema, and X-linked Agammaglobulinemia has fewer deep abscesses with preserved growth early on. \r\n\r\nAnswer: **Chronic Granulomatous Disease (CGD)**", "correct": true, "gold": "Chronic granulomatous disease"}
{"qid": "med:138", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "New lower extremity edema after starting an additional antihypertensive is classic for a **dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker**, especially **amlodipine**. These drugs cause preferential arteriolar dilation, which increases capillary hydrostatic pressure and leads to peripheral edema.\n\nAnswer: **Amlodipine**", "correct": false, "gold": "Nifedipine"}
{"qid": "med:138", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Amlodipine (a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker)\n\n**Answer: Amlodipine**", "correct": false, "gold": "Nifedipine"}
{"qid": "med:134", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The child\u2019s speech delay, hyperactivity, long narrow facial appearance, and suspicion of a trinucleotide repeat disorder strongly suggest Fragile\u202fX syndrome, caused by CGG repeat expansion in the FMR1 gene on the X chromosome. Fragile\u202fX is inherited in an X\u2011linked dominant pattern, with reduced penetrance in females.\n\nAnswer: X-linked dominant<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "X-linked dominant"}
{"qid": "med:138", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has poorly controlled hypertension and diabetes, and was started on a new antihypertensive. One month later, he develops lower extremity edema. Among common antihypertensives, calcium channel blockers (especially dihydropyridines like amlodipine) are well-known to cause peripheral edema due to vasodilation and increased capillary hydrostatic pressure. This is a classic side effect, particularly in patients with diabetes or on ACE inhibitors (enalapril), which can potentiate the edema.\n\nAnswer: Amlodipine (or another dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker)", "correct": false, "gold": "Nifedipine"}
{"qid": "med:127", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Recurrent infections with **encapsulated bacteria** (Streptococcus pneumoniae, Neisseria meningitidis) and **Neisseria gonorrhoeae** (identified by Gram-negative diplococci on Thayer-Martin agar) are classic features of a **terminal complement component deficiency**. The membrane attack complex (MAC), formed by C5b\u2013C9, is essential for complement-mediated lysis of Neisseria species. A defect in any of these terminal components impairs MAC formation, leading to increased susceptibility to these infections while leaving other immune functions largely intact. This is a defect of the **innate immune system**.\n\nAnswer: Deficiency of terminal complement components **C5\u2013C9** (membrane attack complex).", "correct": false, "gold": "Creates pores in the cell membrane"}
{"qid": "med:131", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\nThis 40-year-old male presents with proximal muscle weakness (difficulty climbing stairs and brushing his hair), which is characteristic of an inflammatory myopathy. The absence of headaches, vision changes, or skin rash makes dermatomyositis less likely. Muscle biopsy reveals **CD8+ lymphocyte infiltration in the endomysium**, which is the hallmark finding of **polymyositis** (cytotoxic T-cells directly attacking MHC class I-expressing muscle fibers). Inclusion body myositis is a consideration but typically occurs in older patients (>50) and has additional degenerative features (e.g., rimmed vacuoles) on biopsy, which are not mentioned here.\r\n\r\nAnswer: Polymyositis", "correct": true, "gold": "Polymyositis"}
{"qid": "med:115", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "This patient presents with acute onset focal neurological deficits (right-sided hemiparesis with upper motor neuron signs) in the setting of multiple vascular risk factors (advanced age, hypertension, diabetes, ischemic heart disease). The acute presentation with progression over 1 hour is consistent with a cerebrovascular event. \n\nThe clinical features suggest a left hemispheric lesion (contralateral right-sided weakness) involving the motor pathways. The presence of disorientation indicates cortical or large subcortical involvement rather than a small lacunar infarct. While both ischemic and hemorrhagic stroke are possible, ischemic stroke accounts for approximately 85% of all strokes and is strongly suggested by the history of systemic atherosclerosis (ischemic heart disease). \n\nThe pathologic change in ischemic stroke is **cerebral infarction** with **liquefactive necrosis** of brain tissue due to ischemia. On CT imaging, this typically appears as a hypodense (dark) area in the affected vascular territory (most commonly left MCA territory in this clinical scenario). Hypertensive hemorrhage would appear hyperdense (white) on CT, but is less likely given the clinical context and the absence of typical features like severe headache or rapid coma.\n\nAnswer: Cerebral infarction (ischemic stroke with liquefactive necrosis)", "correct": true, "gold": "Liquefactive necrosis"}
{"qid": "med:138", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Amlodipine (a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker)<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Nifedipine"}
{"qid": "med:138", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient has diabetes and hypertension that remains poorly controlled on an ACE inhibitor (enalapril). Standard next-step therapy is addition of a second antihypertensive agent. Among first-line options, only a dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker (most commonly amlodipine) produces peripheral edema through arteriolar vasodilation and increased capillary hydrostatic pressure. Other common add-on agents (thiazides, beta-blockers, non-dihydropyridine CCBs) do not cause new lower-extremity edema. Therefore the drug added at the index visit was amlodipine.\nAnswer: amlodipine (DHP-CCB)", "correct": false, "gold": "Nifedipine"}
{"qid": "med:139", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This child is 2 weeks into conditioning for an allogeneic bone marrow transplant and now has fever with profound neutropenia:\n\n- WBC = 1400/mm\u00b3\n- Neutrophils = 20%\n- ANC = 1400 \u00d7 0.20 = **280/mm\u00b3**\n\nAn ANC <500/mm\u00b3 with fever is **febrile neutropenia**, a medical emergency. The most appropriate treatment is **immediate empiric broad-spectrum IV antibiotics with antipseudomonal coverage**.\n\nA standard choice is **cefepime** (alternatively piperacillin-tazobactam or a carbapenem depending on context).\n\nAnswer: Empiric IV antipseudomonal broad-spectrum antibiotics for febrile neutropenia (eg, cefepime).", "correct": false, "gold": "Granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor"}
{"qid": "med:139", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient is neutropenic (absolute neutrophil count = 0.20 \u00d7 1400 = 280/mm\u00b3) and febrile after conditioning for bone marrow transplantation. In this setting, the most appropriate initial pharmacotherapy is empiric broad-spectrum antibiotics, typically a beta-lactam with antipseudomonal coverage (e.g., cefepime, meropenem, or piperacillin-tazobactam).\n\nAnswer: Empiric broad-spectrum antibiotics (e.g., cefepime or meropenem).", "correct": false, "gold": "Granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor"}
{"qid": "med:138", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient has poorly controlled hypertension despite taking an ACE inhibitor (enalapril). The next step in management typically involves adding either a thiazide diuretic or a calcium channel blocker (CCB). \n\nThe key clinical clue is the **new onset lower extremity swelling (peripheral edema)** one month later. This is a classic adverse effect of **dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers** (such as amlodipine, nifedipine, or felodipine). These agents cause preferential dilation of arterioles relative to veins, leading to increased hydrostatic pressure in the capillary beds and subsequent fluid extravasation into the interstitial tissues of the lower extremities. This side effect occurs in 10-30% of patients taking dihydropyridine CCBs and is dose-dependent.\n\nThiazide diuretics would not cause edema (they actually reduce edema). Beta-blockers, ARBs, and other antihypertensive classes do not typically produce this specific side effect profile.\n\nAnswer: A calcium channel blocker (specifically a dihydropyridine such as amlodipine).", "correct": false, "gold": "Nifedipine"}
{"qid": "med:134", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "The most likely disorder is **Fragile X syndrome**, which is caused by a CGG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the *FMR1* gene on the X chromosome. \r\n\r\nKey features supporting this diagnosis include: \r\n- **Speech delay**: Limited vocabulary and use of single words at age 2.\r\n- **Behavioral issues**: Hyperactivity and difficulty redirecting.\r\n- **Dysmorphic features**: Long, narrow face.\r\n- **Trinucleotide repeat disorder**: Classic for Fragile X (CGG repeats).\r\n\r\nFragile X syndrome follows an **X-linked dominant** inheritance pattern, with males being more severely affected due to having only one X chromosome. \r\n\r\nAnswer: X-linked dominant", "correct": true, "gold": "X-linked dominant"}
{"qid": "med:138", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\nThis patient with diabetes and hypertension was on enalapril (an ACE inhibitor) and metformin. Due to poorly controlled blood pressure, a second antihypertensive was added. One month later, he developed new-onset lower extremity swelling, which is a classic side effect of **dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers (CCBs)**. These medications (e.g., amlodipine, nifedipine) cause arteriolar vasodilation, leading to increased capillary hydrostatic pressure and subsequent peripheral edema. Amlodipine is one of the most commonly prescribed agents in this class. \r\n\r\nAnswer: Amlodipine", "correct": false, "gold": "Nifedipine"}
{"qid": "med:139", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "# Febrile Neutropenia in Post-Transplant Patient\n\n## Key Clinical Findings\n- **ANC calculation**: 1400/mm\u00b3 \u00d7 20% = **280/mm\u00b3** (severe neutropenia, ANC <500)\n- **Fever**: 38.5\u00b0C (101.3\u00b0F)\n- **Clinical context**: Post-conditioning regimen for allogeneic BMT (myeloablative chemotherapy/radiation causing pancytopenia)\n\n## Diagnosis\nThis patient has **febrile neutropenia**, a medical emergency in immunocompromised patients. The conditioning regimen has ablated his bone marrow, leaving him essentially without functional neutrophils to fight infection. Even minor infections can rapidly progress to sepsis and death in this population.\n\n## Most Appropriate Pharmacotherapy\n\n**Empiric broad-spectrum antipseudomonal antibiotics** \u2014 most commonly:\n- **Cefepime** (4th generation cephalosporin), OR\n- **Piperacillin-tazobactam**, OR\n- **Meropenem** (if broader coverage needed or prior resistant organisms)\n\n### Rationale:\n1. **Do NOT wait for culture results** \u2014 antibiotics must be started immediately after obtaining cultures (blood, urine, etc.)\n2. **Must cover Pseudomonas aeruginosa** \u2014 a common and rapidly fatal pathogen in neutropenic patients\n3. **Monotherapy with antipseudomonal beta-lactam** is the standard first-line approach per IDSA guidelines\n4. Additional agents (vancomycin, antifungals) may be added based on specific risk factors (e.g., central line infection, hemodynamic instability, mucositis) but are not first-line empiric monotherapy\n\n## Answer:\n**Empiric intravenous broad-spectrum antibiotic therapy with an antipseudomonal agent (e.g., cefepime or piperacillin-tazobactam)** should be started immediately after blood cultures are obtained, without waiting for culture results, given this is febrile neutropenia (ANC 280/mm\u00b3) \u2014 a life-threatening emergency in this immunocompromised transplant patient.", "correct": false, "gold": "Granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor"}
{"qid": "med:141", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient with ulcerative colitis has fever, tachycardia, hypotension, abdominal distension/rigidity, and hypoactive bowel sounds, concerning for **toxic megacolon with possible perforation/peritonitis**. After fluid resuscitation and bowel rest, the next step is **broad-spectrum IV antibiotics and urgent surgical management** rather than medical escalation alone.\n\nAnswer: Start broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics and obtain urgent surgical consultation for emergent colectomy.", "correct": false, "gold": "Abdominal x-ray"}
{"qid": "med:139", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient is profoundly neutropenic (ANC = 280/mm\u00b3) in the setting of recent myeloablative conditioning and now has new fever. This meets the definition of high-risk febrile neutropenia, in which occult bacterial infection (especially Pseudomonas) must be assumed until proven otherwise. Immediate empirical monotherapy with an antipseudomonal \u03b2-lactam is therefore indicated; ceftazidime has historically been the agent cited in this classic vignette. Adjunctive measures such as G-CSF or empiric antifungals are not first-line at the moment fever first appears.\nAnswer: ceftazidime (IV)", "correct": false, "gold": "Granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor"}
{"qid": "med:141", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient presents with signs of a severe acute exacerbation of ulcerative colitis, likely progressing to toxic megacolon (distended, rigid abdomen, hypoactive bowel sounds, fever, tachycardia, hypotension). After fluid resuscitation and bowel rest, the most appropriate next step is to start intravenous corticosteroids to control the inflammation. If there is no improvement or if perforation is suspected, surgical consultation for possible colectomy is needed, but the immediate medical step is IV steroids.\n\nAnswer: Intravenous corticosteroids", "correct": false, "gold": "Abdominal x-ray"}
{"qid": "med:139", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Reasoning: The child has febrile neutropenia (ANC \u2248 280\u202fcells/mm\u00b3, temperature >38.3\u202f\u00b0C) after allogeneic BMT conditioning, placing him at high risk for serious infection. Standard management is immediate empiric broad\u2011spectrum antimicrobial therapy covering Pseudomonas and gram\u2011positive organisms. For high\u2011risk BMT patients, combination therapy is recommended: an anti\u2011pseudomonal \u03b2\u2011lactam (e.g., cefepime, piperacillin\u2011tazobactam, or meropenem) plus vancomycin for gram\u2011positive coverage. Antifungal prophylaxis may be added if fever persists despite antibacterial therapy.\n\nAnswer: Begin empiric broad\u2011spectrum anti\u2011infective therapy\u2014an anti\u2011pseudomonal \u03b2\u2011lactam (e.g., cefepime) combined with vancomycin for gram\u2011positive coverage (consider adding antifungal prophylaxis if fever continues).<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor"}
{"qid": "med:141", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Clinical scenario:** This patient with UC has developed **toxic megacolon** (fever, tachycardia, hypotension, marked abdominal distention/rigidity, hypoactive bowel sounds) with signs suggesting possible impending perforation/peritonitis.\n\n**Next steps after fluid resuscitation and bowel rest:**\n- Nasogastric decompression\n- IV corticosteroids (or IV cyclosporine/infliximab if steroid-refractory) \n- IV broad-spectrum antibiotics\n- Serial abdominal exams and imaging (plain abdominal X-ray) to monitor colonic diameter\n\nHowever, given the rigid, distended abdomen with hypoactive bowel sounds in this hemodynamically unstable patient, this presentation is concerning for peritonitis/impending perforation \u2014 the most critical and appropriate next step is to obtain an **urgent surgical consultation for emergent colectomy**, as medical therapy alone is inadequate when there are signs of perforation or failure to improve.\n\nAnswer: Obtain emergent surgical consultation for probable total colectomy (toxic megacolon with signs of perforation/peritonitis)", "correct": false, "gold": "Abdominal x-ray"}
{"qid": "med:123", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "This patient has clinical antiphospholipid syndrome (APS) based on the triad of systemic lupus erythematosus, recurrent fetal loss (4 miscarriages at 8\u201315 weeks), and thrombotic events (recurrent DVTs and PE). APS is an autoimmune disorder characterized by vascular thrombosis and/or pregnancy morbidity in the presence of antiphospholipid antibodies.\n\nFor pregnant patients with APS and a history of thrombosis, the standard of care to prevent recurrent fetal loss and thrombosis is **therapeutic anticoagulation with low molecular weight heparin (LMWH) combined with low-dose aspirin** (typically 75\u2013100 mg daily). \n\nKey points:\n- **LMWH**: Patients with definite APS and previous thrombosis require therapeutic (not just prophylactic) dosing of LMWH throughout pregnancy and for at least 6 weeks postpartum. Warfarin is contraindicated in pregnancy due to teratogenicity.\n- **Low-dose aspirin**: Added to heparin because combination therapy significantly improves live birth rates compared to either agent alone in APS-related recurrent pregnancy loss.\n- **Hydroxychloroquine**: Should be continued if she is already taking it for SLE, as it reduces disease flares and may provide additional benefit in APS pregnancy, but it is not sufficient alone for thrombosis prevention or fetal loss prevention in this high-risk scenario.\n\nPrednisone, IVIG, and progesterone are not indicated as first-line therapy for APS-related recurrent fetal loss.\n\nAnswer: Start therapeutic low molecular weight heparin and low-dose aspirin.", "correct": false, "gold": "Aspirin and heparin"}
{"qid": "med:142", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "She has **prominent psychotic symptoms** (auditory hallucinations, intrusive suicidal voices, impaired functioning) that began **within 1 week of a major stressor** (her father\u2019s death), with **no substance use or medical cause** identified.\n\nThis fits **brief psychotic disorder**, which is characterized by:\n- Sudden onset of psychosis\n- Duration **more than 1 day but less than 1 month**\n- Often occurs **after a major stressor**\n- Return to baseline functioning afterward\n\nWhy not others:\n- **Normal grief/bereavement** does not typically cause sustained, distressing command auditory hallucinations and marked functional impairment.\n- **Schizophreniform disorder** lasts **1\u20136 months**.\n- **Schizophrenia** lasts **>6 months**.\n- **Major depressive disorder with psychotic features** would require a full major depressive episode, which is not described here.\n\nAnswer: Brief psychotic disorder", "correct": true, "gold": "Brief psychotic disorder"}
{"qid": "med:142", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Answer: Brief Psychotic Disorder**\n\nThis diagnosis fits because:\n- **Trigger**: Acute onset following a major psychosocial stressor (father's death 1 week ago)\n- **Symptoms**: Auditory hallucinations (voices commenting about her/commanding suicide) and disorganized behavior affecting functioning (unable to concentrate at work/school)\n- **Duration**: Symptoms have been present for less than 1 month (only 1 week since the stressor)\n- **Exclusions**: Normal CMP, CBC, TSH, and negative urine toxicology rule out medical/metabolic causes and substance-induced psychosis\n- **No prior psychiatric history**: This is a first episode, not a chronic condition like schizophrenia (which requires \u22656 months of symptoms) or schizophreniform disorder (1-6 months)\n\nBrief psychotic disorder specifically requires:\n1. Presence of \u22651 psychotic symptom (delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, or grossly disorganized/catatonic behavior)\n2. Duration of at least 1 day but less than 1 month\n3. Eventual return to full premorbid functioning\n4. Often triggered by significant stress (\"brief psychotic disorder with marked stressor\")\n\n**Important clinical note**: This patient has command hallucinations telling her to kill herself, representing a **psychiatric emergency**. She requires immediate safety assessment and likely inpatient hospitalization for stabilization, regardless of the specific diagnostic label.\n\nAnswer: Brief Psychotic Disorder (with marked stressor)", "correct": true, "gold": "Brief psychotic disorder"}
{"qid": "med:141", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Administer intravenous corticosteroids (e.g., methylprednisolone 20\u201330\u202fmg/kg per day).<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Abdominal x-ray"}
{"qid": "med:141", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation is consistent with acute severe ulcerative colitis complicated by toxic megacolon: systemic toxicity (fever, tachycardia, hypotension), marked colonic distention with ileus, and a several-week prodrome of inadequately treated colitis.  \n\nAfter immediate fluid resuscitation and institution of complete bowel rest (NPO), the cornerstone of medical therapy is prompt suppression of the underlying inflammatory drive. High-dose intravenous glucocorticoids (e.g., methylprednisolone 60 mg/day or hydrocortisone 300\u2013400 mg/day) achieve this goal and are therefore the most appropriate next intervention.  \n\nBroad-spectrum antibiotics, nasogastric decompression, serial abdominal examinations, and daily radiographs are also employed, but steroids specifically address the autoimmune colitis that precipitated the megacolon. If free perforation or clinical deterioration occurs despite 48\u201372 h of maximal medical therapy, urgent colectomy is required; the current vignette does not yet meet those criteria.\n\nAnswer: IV corticosteroids (e.g. methylprednisolone)", "correct": false, "gold": "Abdominal x-ray"}
{"qid": "med:139", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reason:**  \r\nThis patient has **febrile neutropenia** (fever 38.5\u00b0C and absolute neutrophil count of 280/mm\u00b3) following myeloablative conditioning for allogeneic bone marrow transplantation. According to IDSA guidelines, high-risk neutropenic patients require immediate empiric broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics with activity against *Pseudomonas aeruginosa*. \r\n\r\nAnswer: **Intravenous cefepime**", "correct": false, "gold": "Granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor"}
