{"qid": "med:155", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Answer: Dobutamine stress echocardiography", "correct": false, "gold": "Myocardial perfusion scan under pharmacological stress"}
{"qid": "med:156", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation describes bilateral physiologic genu varum (bow-legs). This is a normal developmental variant that appears with the onset of independent walking, is symmetric, produces an unremarkable gait, and occurs in a child whose linear growth, weight, and developmental milestones are all within normal limits. The closed fontanelles and absence of rachitic features further exclude metabolic bone disease, while the family history of developmental dysplasia of the hip is unrelated to the current lower-limb alignment.\nBecause physiologic varus spontaneously resolves by 24\u201336 months of age, the evidence-based next step is expectant observation together with parental reassurance that no growth disorder is present and that routine follow-up at future well-child visits is sufficient. Imaging or laboratory evaluation is reserved for atypical features (unilateral deformity, persistence beyond age 3, short stature, or gait disturbance), none of which are present here.\nAnswer: reassurance/observe (physiologic genu varum)", "correct": false, "gold": "Reassurance and follow-up"}
{"qid": "med:158", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient most likely has **overflow incontinence due to diabetic autonomic neuropathy causing a neurogenic bladder**.\n\nWhy:\n- She has **diabetes** (metformin, insulin)\n- **Decreased pinprick sensation** in the lower extremities suggests **diabetic neuropathy**\n- She has **small-volume frequent urination** with leakage when she delays voiding and **soaked underwear overnight**, which is classic for **overflow incontinence** from incomplete bladder emptying\n\nBest treatment:\n- **Scheduled intermittent self-catheterization** to relieve chronic urinary retention and prevent overflow leakage\n\nAntimuscarinics are for urge incontinence; sling/surgery is for stress incontinence.\n\nAnswer: Intermittent self-catheterization for overflow incontinence due to diabetic neurogenic bladder.", "correct": false, "gold": "Bethanechol and intermittent straight catheterization"}
{"qid": "med:156", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Reassure the mother that physiologic bowing of the legs is common at this age and will likely correct spontaneously; schedule a follow\u2011up examination in 6\u202fmonths to reassess alignment and growth.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Reassurance and follow-up"}
{"qid": "med:158", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has symptoms of urinary incontinence with features of both stress incontinence (leakage with missed bathroom breaks, suggesting increased intra-abdominal pressure) and possibly overflow incontinence (small volumes, frequent urination). The decreased pinprick sensation in the lower extremities points to diabetic neuropathy, which can cause neurogenic bladder and overflow incontinence. Her blood pressure is very high (167/108), and she has a systolic murmur at the right upper sternal border (suggestive of aortic stenosis). However, the key issue is likely diabetic neurogenic bladder causing overflow incontinence. The best initial treatment for overflow incontinence due to neurogenic bladder is clean intermittent catheterization.\n\nAnswer: Clean intermittent catheterization", "correct": false, "gold": "Bethanechol and intermittent straight catheterization"}
{"qid": "med:158", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Best treatment: Clean intermittent self-catheterization**\n\n**Reasoning:**\n\nThis patient has long-standing diabetes (on metformin + insulin) with evidence of diabetic peripheral neuropathy (decreased pinprick sensation in lower extremities). Her urinary symptoms\u2014frequent small-volume voids, constant dribbling/leakage (soaked underwear), and needing scheduled bathroom breaks to avoid leakage\u2014are classic for **overflow incontinence due to diabetic autonomic neuropathy causing a neurogenic (atonic) bladder**.\n\nIn diabetic cystopathy, autonomic neuropathy impairs bladder sensation and detrusor contractility, leading to incomplete emptying, urinary retention, and overflow incontinence (leakage of small amounts around a chronically distended bladder). The hypertension, sternal murmur (likely aortic sclerosis/stenosis), and neuropathy all fit with long-standing vascular/diabetic end-organ damage, but the key finding driving the urinary complaint is the neurogenic bladder from diabetic autonomic neuropathy.\n\nThe definitive treatment for overflow incontinence due to an underactive/atonic bladder is **regular bladder emptying via clean intermittent self-catheterization**, which prevents overdistension, reduces residual volume, and decreases leakage and risk of infection or renal damage. Pharmacologic options like bethanechol are sometimes used but are less effective and less reliable than catheterization for significant retention.\n\nAnswer: Clean intermittent self-catheterization (to ensure complete bladder emptying due to diabetic neurogenic/atonic bladder causing overflow incontinence).", "correct": false, "gold": "Bethanechol and intermittent straight catheterization"}
{"qid": "med:150", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "This clinical presentation is classic for **slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE)**. The key diagnostic features include:\n- Adolescent obese male (BMI 32) - highest risk demographic\n- Insidious onset of hip pain with acute worsening\n- Pain referred to the knee (common in hip pathology)\n- External rotation of the affected leg with limited internal rotation\n- Radiographic confirmation: posterior-inferior displacement of the femoral epiphysis relative to the neck\n\nSCFE is an orthopedic emergency because continued slippage can compromise the blood supply to the femoral head, leading to avascular necrosis. The patient has an acute-on-chronic slip given the 3-week history with sudden deterioration.\n\n**Treatment rationale:**\n- Non-surgical management (casting, traction, or observation) is contraindicated as it risks further slippage and avascular necrosis\n- The standard of care is **urgent surgical stabilization with in-situ percutaneous pinning** using a cannulated screw\n- Forceful closed reduction is avoided to prevent disrupting the tenuous blood supply; gentle positioning for fluoroscopy is acceptable\n- The goal is to stabilize the physis and prevent further displacement\n- Contralateral prophylactic pinning may be considered later given the high risk of bilateral involvement and obesity, but the immediate priority is the symptomatic hip\n\nAnswer: Urgent surgical stabilization with in-situ percutaneous pinning (single screw fixation) of the right hip.", "correct": false, "gold": "Surgical pinning of the right hip"}
{"qid": "med:158", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient's presentation is consistent with overflow incontinence due to diabetic cystopathy (detrusor underactivity from autonomic neuropathy). Key supporting features include long-standing diabetes with documented peripheral sensory neuropathy, frequent small-volume voids, nocturnal enuresis, and leakage only when voiding is postponed. These findings indicate chronic urinary retention with overflow rather than urge incontinence (which would feature sudden urgency without neuropathy) or stress incontinence (which would be provoked by increased intra-abdominal pressure).\nAnticholinergic agents (e.g., oxybutynin) are therefore contraindicated, as they would further impair detrusor contractility and worsen retention. Pharmacologic detrusor stimulants such as bethanechol have limited efficacy and are not first-line. The definitive management for incomplete emptying from an atonic bladder is mechanical drainage to prevent recurrent overflow and upper-tract complications.\nThus, the indicated therapy is clean intermittent self-catheterization.\nAnswer: clean intermittent self-catheterization", "correct": false, "gold": "Bethanechol and intermittent straight catheterization"}
{"qid": "med:160", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Ethosuximide is first-line for childhood absence seizures; parents should be advised about GI side effects (nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain).\n\nAnswer: Gastrointestinal distress (nausea/vomiting/abdominal pain) \u2014 the classic side effect of ethosuximide, the first-line treatment for absence seizures.", "correct": false, "gold": "GI upset"}
{"qid": "med:160", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "A 5-year-old with frequent \u201cspacing out\u201d episodes and a **3-Hz spike-and-wave EEG** has **absence seizures**.  \n**First-line treatment** is **ethosuximide**.\n\nA classic adverse effect of ethosuximide is **gastrointestinal distress**, especially **abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting**, and it can also cause **fatigue, headache, itching, and urticaria**.\n\nAnswer: **Gastrointestinal upset (especially nausea/vomiting/abdominal pain) from ethosuximide.**", "correct": false, "gold": "GI upset"}
{"qid": "med:158", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Begin non\u2011pharmacologic management\u2014bladder training with timed voiding and pelvic\u2011floor muscle exercises; if drug therapy is needed, use mirabegron (\u03b2\u2083\u2011agonist) rather than an antimuscarinic because of her hypertension and existing constipation.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Bethanechol and intermittent straight catheterization"}
{"qid": "med:160", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The EEG finding of a 3-Hz spike and wave pattern is classic for childhood absence epilepsy. The first-line medication for this condition is ethosuximide. A common and important side effect of ethosuximide is gastrointestinal distress (nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain) and, less commonly, drowsiness or headache. However, a more serious potential side effect is aplastic anemia or other blood dyscrasias, so parents should be advised to watch for signs of infection, easy bruising, or bleeding.\n\nAnswer: Gastrointestinal upset (nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain) or risk of blood dyscrasias (aplastic anemia).", "correct": false, "gold": "GI upset"}
