{"qid": "med:173", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Atropine (organophosphate/pesticide toxicity from cholinesterase inhibitor exposure)\n\n**Answer: Atropine**", "correct": false, "gold": "Atropine and pralidoxime"}
{"qid": "med:173", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This mechanic likely has **organophosphate poisoning** from pesticide exposure while working on a tractor. Clues:\n- **Acute dyspnea with wheezing** from bronchospasm/bronchorrhea\n- **Miosis** (contracted pupils)\n- **Diaphoresis**\n- Exposure setting consistent with agricultural chemicals\n\nOrganophosphates inhibit **acetylcholinesterase**, causing excess acetylcholine at muscarinic and nicotinic receptors.\n\nBest treatment:\n- **Atropine** to block muscarinic effects\n- **Pralidoxime (2-PAM)** to regenerate acetylcholinesterase, especially for nicotinic toxicity and to reverse the poisoning itself\n\nAnswer: **Administer atropine and pralidoxime for organophosphate poisoning.**", "correct": true, "gold": "Atropine and pralidoxime"}
{"qid": "med:173", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient's presentation\u2014acute shortness of breath, wheezing, diaphoresis, miosis (contracted pupils), and exposure to a tractor (likely a diesel engine)\u2014suggests organophosphate poisoning from inhaling or absorbing pesticide residue. Organophosphates inhibit acetylcholinesterase, causing cholinergic crisis (SLUDGE syndrome and muscarinic effects like wheezing, miosis, and diaphoresis). The best initial treatment is atropine to block muscarinic overstimulation, along with pralidoxime to reactivate acetylcholinesterase.\n\nAnswer: Atropine and pralidoxime", "correct": true, "gold": "Atropine and pralidoxime"}
{"qid": "med:173", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation (acute dyspnea, diffuse wheezing, diaphoresis, and miosis without prior respiratory disease) is the classic cholinergic toxidrome produced by acetylcholinesterase inhibition. In the described occupational setting the most likely etiology is organophosphate (or carbamate) pesticide exposure.  \nExcess acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors is competitively antagonized by atropine, which rapidly dries bronchial secretions and relieves bronchospasm; this is therefore the immediate life-saving intervention. At the same time, the phosphorylated (or carbamylated) acetylcholinesterase must be reactivated by pralidoxime (2-PAM) to restore normal neuromuscular transmission and prevent the nicotinic effects (including respiratory-muscle weakness). Both agents are therefore administered together as definitive therapy; supportive measures (oxygen, decontamination, benzodiazepines for seizures) follow but do not replace the antidotes.\nAnswer: atropine + pralidoxime (2-PAM)", "correct": false, "gold": "Atropine and pralidoxime"}
{"qid": "med:162", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient has lower urinary tract symptoms (hesitancy, nocturia, frequency) due to benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH). The serum PSA of 6\u202fng/mL indicates a significantly enlarged prostate volume (PSA\u202f\u2248\u202f0.1\u202fng/mL per gram of tissue). For men with moderate\u2011to\u2011severe symptoms and a PSA\u202f>\u202f1.5\u202fng/mL (or prostate volume\u202f>\u202f30\u202fmL), a 5\u2011alpha\u2011reductase inhibitor (e.g., finasteride or dutasteride) is indicated to reduce prostate size, improve symptoms, and prevent disease progression (acute urinary retention, need for surgery). While an alpha\u2011blocker provides rapid symptomatic relief, the optimal regimen for this patient must include a 5\u2011alpha\u2011reductase inhibitor.\n\nAnswer: A 5\u2011alpha\u2011reductase inhibitor (e.g., finasteride or dutasteride).", "correct": true, "gold": "Finasteride"}
{"qid": "med:160", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The EEG pattern (3\u2011Hz spike\u2011and\u2011wave) is classic for childhood absence epilepsy. The first\u2011line medication for typical absence seizures is **ethosuximide**. The most common adverse effects of ethosuximide are gastrointestinal, so parents should be warned that the child may experience nausea, vomiting, or loss of appetite.\n\nAnswer: Gastrointestinal upset (nausea, vomiting, anorexia).", "correct": false, "gold": "GI upset"}
