{"qid": "med:170", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reason:**  \r\nThis patient has chronic hepatitis C virus (HCV) infection confirmed by positive HCV RNA and genotype 1. His liver biopsy shows portal mononuclear infiltrates and periportal hepatocyte necrosis, consistent with active chronic hepatitis without evidence of cirrhosis. Current guidelines (AASLD/IDSA) recommend initiating direct-acting antiviral (DAA) therapy for all patients with chronic HCV, regardless of symptoms or disease stage, to prevent progression to cirrhosis, hepatocellular carcinoma, and other complications. Genotype 1 is highly treatable with pan-genotypic or genotype-specific DAA regimens.\r\n\r\nAnswer: \\(\\boxed{\\text{Initiate direct-acting antiviral (DAA) therapy for chronic HCV genotype 1.}}\\)", "correct": false, "gold": "Sofosbuvir and ledipasvir therapy"}
{"qid": "med:173", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "This patient presents with the classic **cholinergic toxidrome** characterized by the combination of:\n- **Miosis** (constricted pupils)\n- **Bronchospasm** (diffuse wheezing)\n- **Diaphoresis** (sweating)\n- **Bradycardia** (pulse 65/min)\n- Acute onset during mechanical/pesticide exposure (plowing tractor)\n\nThese findings are pathognomonic for **organophosphate or carbamate poisoning** (insecticides/pesticides), which cause acetylcholinesterase inhibition leading to excessive acetylcholine at muscarinic and nicotinic receptors.\n\nThe immediate life-threatening concerns are bronchospasm and respiratory secretions. The antidote is **atropine**, which competitively antagonizes acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors to dry secretions, relieve bronchospasm, and reverse bradycardia. Pralidoxime (2-PAM) is also administered to regenerate acetylcholinesterase activity, particularly for organophosphates.\n\nAnswer: Atropine (with pralidoxime and decontamination)", "correct": false, "gold": "Atropine and pralidoxime"}
{"qid": "med:174", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This child\u2019s asthma is **not well controlled on as-needed albuterol alone**.\n\nKey clues:\n- **Symptoms requiring SABA 4 times/week**\n- **3 nighttime awakenings/month**\n- **Activity limitation** (can\u2019t keep up with friends)\n\nIn a child age **5\u201311 years**, this is consistent with **mild persistent asthma**, so he should be stepped up from **Step 1** therapy (PRN SABA only) to **Step 2** therapy:\n\n- **Add a daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid**\n- **Continue albuterol as needed for relief**\n\nBecause he also has **allergic rhinitis**, managing that can further improve asthma control.\n\n**Appropriate regimen change:**\n- Start a **low-dose ICS** such as **fluticasone or budesonide daily**\n- Keep **albuterol inhaler PRN**\n- Consider treatment of allergic rhinitis as adjunctive care\n\nAnswer: Add a daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid for controller therapy while continuing as-needed albuterol.", "correct": false, "gold": "Add fluticasone daily"}
{"qid": "med:174", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Classification:** Based on the symptom frequency (SABA use 4\u00d7/week) and nocturnal awakenings (3\u00d7/month), this presentation is consistent with **mild persistent asthma** (symptoms >2 days/week but not daily, and nighttime awakenings 3-4\u00d7/month), which represents a step up from intermittent asthma given his previous reliance on albuterol alone.\n\n**Treatment Change:**\nThe patient should be stepped up from **Step 1 (SABA only as needed)** to **Step 2 therapy**, which involves:\n\n- **Initiation of a daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid (ICS)**, such as fluticasone or budesonide, as the preferred controller medication.\n- **Continue albuterol (SABA) as needed** for acute symptom relief.\n\nThis addition of a daily low-dose ICS addresses the underlying airway inflammation driving his increased symptom frequency, nocturnal awakenings, and exercise limitation, and is the guideline-recommended step-up therapy (per NAEPP/GINA guidelines) for a child with mild persistent asthma.\n\nAnswer: The patient should be stepped up to Step 2 asthma therapy by adding a daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid (ICS) as a controller medication, while continuing albuterol as needed for symptom relief \u2014 reflecting his change in classification from intermittent to mild persistent asthma.", "correct": false, "gold": "Add fluticasone daily"}
{"qid": "med:174", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has symptoms consistent with **mild persistent asthma** (daytime symptoms >2 days/week but not daily, nighttime awakenings 3\u20134 times per month, and some activity limitation). Current guidelines recommend starting a **low-dose inhaled corticosteroid (ICS)** as a daily controller medication for this severity level, while continuing the albuterol inhaler for rescue.\n\n**Answer:** Add a daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid (e.g., fluticasone) to his regimen.", "correct": false, "gold": "Add fluticasone daily"}
{"qid": "med:171", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Both the right and left pupils would constrict (a normal direct response in the right eye and a normal consensual response in the left eye). \r\n\r\nAnswer: \\(\\textbf{\\text{Right pupil constricts; left pupil constricts (direct and consensual reflexes intact).}}}\\)", "correct": false, "gold": "Bilateral miosis"}
{"qid": "med:174", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The child\u2019s asthma is not well controlled (rescue inhaler use\u202f>\u202f2\u202fdays/week, \u22653 nighttime awakenings/month, activity limitation). He should move from a rescue\u2011only regimen to a stepwise approach that adds a daily controller medication.\n\n**Recommended changes**\n1. **Initiate a low\u2011dose inhaled corticosteroid (ICS)** (e.g., budesonide 200\u2013400\u202f\u00b5g BID or fluticasone propionate 100\u2013250\u202f\u00b5g BID) as the primary controller, taken every day regardless of symptoms.  \n2. Continue albuterol as needed for breakthrough symptoms, but aim to reduce its use to \u22642\u202fdays/week once the ICS takes effect.  \n3. **Ensure proper inhaler technique** and provide a spacer device for optimal delivery.  \n4. Address comorbid allergic rhinitis with a daily intranasal corticosteroid (e.g., mometasone furoate 200\u202f\u00b5g daily) to improve overall control and reduce airway inflammation.  \n5. Reassess after 4\u20136\u202fweeks. If control remains suboptimal, consider stepping up to a medium\u2011dose ICS or adding a leukotriene receptor antagonist (e.g., montelukast) before escalating further.  \n\nAnswer: Start a low\u2011dose daily inhaled corticosteroid (with proper technique/spacer), continue albuterol PRN, treat allergic rhinitis with an intranasal steroid, and re\u2011evaluate control in 4\u20136\u202fweeks; consider stepping up therapy if symptoms persist.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Add fluticasone daily"}
{"qid": "med:173", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\nThis patient's presentation is classic for **organophosphate poisoning**, likely from exposure while repairing the tractor (organophosphates are commonly found in pesticides used in agriculture). Key findings supporting this diagnosis include:  \r\n\r\n- **Acute onset shortness of breath** with diffuse wheezes (due to bronchospasm and bronchorrhea).\r\n- **Pinpoint pupils (miosis)**, a hallmark of cholinergic toxicity.\r\n- **Cholinergic symptoms**: pale, diaphoretic skin, along with potential SLUDGE (Salivation, Lacrimation, Urination, Defecation, GI upset, Emesis) effects.\r\n- **Exposure history**: working on farming equipment, a common source of pesticide exposure. \r\n\r\nThe best treatment is **atropine**, a muscarinic antagonist that blocks the excess acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors, reversing bronchospasm, bronchorrhea, bradycardia, and miosis. If organophosphate poisoning is confirmed, **pralidoxime (2-PAM)** should also be administered to reactivate acetylcholinesterase at nicotinic sites, but atropine is the immediate first-line therapy. \r\n\r\nAnswer: Atropine", "correct": false, "gold": "Atropine and pralidoxime"}
{"qid": "med:175", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient has **new atrial fibrillation** (irregularly irregular rhythm with palpitations), and his recent ischemic stroke strongly suggests a **cardioembolic source**. The key intervention to prevent future strokes is **long-term anticoagulation**.\n\nBecause his stroke was **1 month ago** and CT shows **no hemorrhage**, anticoagulation is appropriate now. Antiplatelet therapy alone is inferior for stroke prevention in atrial fibrillation.\n\nThe best intervention is **chronic oral anticoagulation**, classically **warfarin** (or a DOAC in many modern settings, but if choosing the classic answer: warfarin).\n\nAnswer: Start long-term oral anticoagulation for atrial fibrillation (eg, warfarin) to prevent recurrent embolic stroke.", "correct": false, "gold": "Begin warfarin and heparin"}
{"qid": "med:174", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "This patient (age 5\u201311 y) was previously managed with SABA rescue only (Step 1). Frequency of daytime SABA use (>2 d/wk), nocturnal awakenings (3\u00d7/mo), and activity limitation now meet criteria for not-well-controlled asthma and correspond to mild persistent disease. Guidelines therefore require stepping up therapy to Step 2 by adding a daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid (ICS) while continuing albuterol PRN. Allergic rhinitis is a comorbidity that may be addressed separately (e.g., nasal corticosteroid or montelukast), but the preferred controller for the asthma component itself remains low-dose ICS. No indication exists for LABA, medium-dose ICS, or oral steroids at this visit.\n\nAnswer: add daily low-dose ICS (continue albuterol PRN)", "correct": false, "gold": "Add fluticasone daily"}
{"qid": "med:175", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has atrial fibrillation (irregularly irregular rhythm, no P waves) and a history of recent ischemic stroke. For long-term stroke prevention in atrial fibrillation, anticoagulation is indicated. However, given his recent stroke (1 month ago) with residual deficits and the fact that he has been in atrial fibrillation for at least 2 days, the key decision is timing of anticoagulation. Current guidelines recommend starting anticoagulation within 4 weeks after an acute ischemic stroke, but not emergently if the stroke was moderate-to-severe. His NIH Stroke Scale is not given, but he has residual motor and sensory deficits and a visual field cut, suggesting a moderate stroke. A noncontrast CT shows no hemorrhage. The most appropriate intervention to prevent future CVA is to start oral anticoagulation (e.g., apixaban, rivaroxaban, edoxaban, or warfarin) after ensuring no contraindications. Among the options, direct oral anticoagulants are preferred over warfarin unless contraindicated.\n\nAnswer: Start a direct oral anticoagulant (e.g., apixaban).", "correct": false, "gold": "Begin warfarin and heparin"}
{"qid": "med:175", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Most appropriate intervention: Initiate long-term oral anticoagulation (e.g., a direct oral anticoagulant such as apixaban, or warfarin with bridging) rather than antiplatelet therapy alone.**\n\n**Reasoning:**\nThis patient has developed new-onset atrial fibrillation (irregular rate/rhythm on exam, confirmed by ECG showing absence of P waves with irregularly irregular QRS complexes), which is a common and important cause of cardioembolic stroke \u2014 consistent with his recent ACA territory infarct. \n\nGiven:\n- New-onset AFib with rapid ventricular response\n- History of an embolic-appearing ischemic stroke (ACA territory, a classic site for cardioembolism)\n- CHA2DS2-VASc score is clearly elevated (age >65, hypertension, prior stroke) \u2014 his stroke risk is high\n\nThe single best strategy to prevent future embolic strokes is **systemic anticoagulation**, not antiplatelet therapy (e.g., aspirin) or rate/rhythm control alone. While rate control (e.g., beta-blocker) may be used for symptomatic control of his AFib, it does not address stroke prevention. Anticoagulation directly targets the thromboembolic risk from atrial fibrillation, which is the most likely cause of his prior and potential future strokes.\n\nSince he is more than 2 weeks post-tPA administration and there is no evidence of hemorrhagic transformation on repeat CT, initiating anticoagulation now is considered safe and is the guideline-recommended approach for secondary stroke prevention in the setting of atrial fibrillation.\n\nAnswer: Initiate long-term oral anticoagulation (e.g., a DOAC such as apixaban) for secondary stroke prevention due to newly diagnosed atrial fibrillation.", "correct": false, "gold": "Begin warfarin and heparin"}
{"qid": "med:175", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Initiate anticoagulation (e.g., a direct oral anticoagulant such as apixaban) for stroke prophylaxis in newly diagnosed atrial fibrillation.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Begin warfarin and heparin"}
{"qid": "med:177", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This elderly man has **depression presenting as pseudodementia**, not primary dementia.\n\nKey clues:\n- **Subacute onset** over 5 weeks\n- **Sleep disturbance**\n- **Social withdrawal**\n- **Anhedonia**: no longer enjoys piano\n- **Psychomotor retardation** and **blunted affect**\n- He is **concerned about his memory problems**, which is more typical of depression than dementia\n- Orientation is preserved, and labs for reversible cognitive causes are normal\n\nThe most appropriate treatment is an **antidepressant**, especially an **SSRI** in an older adult.\n\n**Answer:** Start treatment for major depressive disorder with an SSRI (eg, sertraline).", "correct": false, "gold": "Fluoxetine"}
{"qid": "med:177", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Most appropriate treatment: Initiate an antidepressant (SSRI, e.g., sertraline)**\n\n**Rationale:**\nThis patient's presentation is classic for **pseudodementia (depression-related cognitive impairment)** rather than a true neurodegenerative dementia. Key clues supporting depression as the underlying cause include:\n\n- **Subacute onset** (5 weeks) \u2014 true dementias (Alzheimer's, vascular, etc.) typically progress insidiously over months to years\n- **Psychomotor retardation and blunted affect** \u2014 classic neuropsychiatric features of depression\n- **Anhedonia** \u2014 he has stopped playing piano, an activity he used to enjoy\n- **Social withdrawal** \u2014 rarely leaves his room, minimal conversation\n- **Sleep disturbance** \u2014 frequent nighttime awakenings\n- **Impaired attention/concentration** contributing to memory complaints, rather than primary amnestic impairment\n- **Patient's high level of concern** about his memory \u2014 patients with true dementia often lack insight or minimize deficits, whereas depressed patients tend to be acutely distressed about their cognitive lapses\n- **Normal metabolic/endocrine workup** (electrolytes, TSH, B12) and **no focal neurologic findings**, ruling out reversible metabolic causes and focal structural lesions\n\nDepression in the elderly (\"pseudodementia\") can mimic dementia with impaired memory, attention, and executive function, but it is reversible with appropriate treatment of the underlying mood disorder.\n\n**Answer:** Start an **SSRI antidepressant** (e.g., sertraline), which is first-line therapy for depression in elderly patients given its favorable side-effect profile and safety in this population. Cognitive symptoms should improve as the depression resolves, confirming the diagnosis of pseudodementia.\n\nAnswer: Treatment with an antidepressant medication (SSRI such as sertraline) for underlying depression/pseudodementia.", "correct": false, "gold": "Fluoxetine"}
{"qid": "med:177", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient presents with subacute cognitive decline over 5 weeks, impaired memory, attention, psychomotor retardation, blunted affect, sleep disturbance, and social withdrawal. He is distressed by his symptoms. Key features include the relatively rapid onset (weeks, not months or years), lack of focal neurologic signs, normal labs, and preserved orientation. This presentation is more consistent with depression (pseudodementia) than a neurodegenerative dementia like Alzheimer's disease. In elderly patients, depression can present with prominent cognitive complaints and impairment, often with insight and concern. The most appropriate treatment is an antidepressant, such as a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI).\n\nAnswer: Antidepressant therapy (e.g., SSRI)", "correct": false, "gold": "Fluoxetine"}
{"qid": "med:175", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient has new\u2011onset atrial fibrillation (AF) with a rapid ventricular response and a recent ischemic stroke. His CHA\u2082DS\u2082\u2011VASc score is at least 3 (age\u202f62\u202f=\u202f1 point, prior stroke\u202f=\u202f2 points), giving him a high risk of recurrent cardioembolic stroke. The most effective strategy to reduce that risk is long\u2011term oral anticoagulation. Because he is one month out from his stroke with no hemorrhagic transformation on imaging, it is safe to start anticoagulation now. Direct oral anticoagulants (DOACs) such as apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran, or edoxaban are preferred over warfarin in non\u2011valvular AF because they have similar efficacy and lower intracranial bleeding risk. Rhythm control (cardioversion) does not eliminate stroke risk without anticoagulation, and antiplatelet therapy is inferior for AF\u2011related stroke prevention.\n\nAnswer: Initiate long\u2011term oral anticoagulation with a DOAC (e.g., apixaban) or warfarin.", "correct": false, "gold": "Begin warfarin and heparin"}
{"qid": "med:175", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient has new atrial fibrillation (evident from the history of palpitations with irregular rhythm on exam and the diagnostic ECG), which confers a high risk of cardioembolism. His CHA\u2082DS\u2082-VASc score is at least 3 (age, prior stroke, vascular disease), placing him in a stratum where long-term therapeutic anticoagulation is indicated to prevent recurrent ischemic stroke. Rate or rhythm control alone does not reduce stroke risk; antiplatelet agents are inferior to anticoagulation in this setting. One month has elapsed since his index stroke, so the risk of hemorrhagic conversion is acceptably low and anticoagulation can be initiated safely. Therefore the single intervention that most effectively lowers the likelihood of future cerebrovascular events is therapeutic oral anticoagulation (warfarin with target INR 2\u20133 or an approved direct oral anticoagulant).\nAnswer: long-term anticoagulation (warfarin INR 2-3)", "correct": false, "gold": "Begin warfarin and heparin"}
{"qid": "med:174", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:** \r\n\r\nThis patient has **not well-controlled asthma** on as-needed albuterol alone. His symptoms meet criteria for persistent asthma, including: \r\n\r\n- Increased SABA use (4x/week) over the past month\r\n- Nighttime awakenings (3x/month)\r\n- Activity limitation (unable to keep up with friends)\r\n- Bilateral late expiratory wheezes on exam\r\n\r\nAccording to the NHLBI EPR-4 guidelines for children ages 5-11, this warrants **Step 2 therapy**, which includes a daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid (ICS) in addition to as-needed albuterol for quick relief. His comorbid allergic rhinitis should also be addressed, as it can exacerbate his asthma. \r\n\r\n**Recommended Changes:** \r\n\r\n- **Controller therapy:** Initiate a daily low-dose ICS (e.g., fluticasone propionate 44 mcg, 1-2 puffs twice daily via a spacer).\r\n- **Rescue therapy:** Continue albuterol as needed for acute symptoms.\r\n- **Comorbidity management:** Optimize treatment for allergic rhinitis (e.g., intranasal corticosteroids) and allergen avoidance.\r\n- **Education:** Provide an asthma action plan, review inhaler/spacer technique, and discuss trigger avoidance.\r\n- **Follow-up:** Reassess control in 1-3 months to evaluate response and adjust therapy as needed. \r\n\r\nAnswer: Initiate daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid therapy (e.g., fluticasone propionate 44 mcg 2 puffs BID via spacer) and continue albuterol as needed for rescue.", "correct": false, "gold": "Add fluticasone daily"}
