{"qid": "med:199", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient's symptoms (agitation, photophobia, dysphagia, flu-like prodrome) and history of spelunking in Mexico point to rabies. Rabies virus spreads from the site of inoculation (likely a bat bite in the cave) via retrograde axonal transport along peripheral nerves to the CNS.\n\nAnswer: Retrograde axonal transport along peripheral nerves.", "correct": false, "gold": "Retrograde migration up peripheral nerve axons"}
{"qid": "med:199", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient's presentation (flu-like prodrome followed by agitation, photophobia, dysphagia, coma, and death) is pathognomonic for rabies encephalitis. The epidemiologic clue of recent spelunking in Mexico points to exposure to an aerosolized or unnoticed bat bite, the most common source of indigenous rabies in North America.\nRabies virions are inoculated into muscle or subcutaneous tissue at the bite site. They replicate locally in myocytes, then bind nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at neuromuscular junctions. From that point they are taken up into unmyelinated peripheral axons and travel centripetally to the dorsal-root ganglia and spinal cord exclusively by fast retrograde axonal transport (dynein-mediated, ~50\u2013100 mm day\u207b\u00b9). Once inside the CNS the virus replicates in neurons and spreads trans-synaptically, but the initial neuroinvasion step is strictly retrograde axonal.\nThus the virus reaches the CNS by retrograde axonal transport along peripheral nerves.\nAnswer: retrograde axonal transport (via peripheral nerves)", "correct": false, "gold": "Retrograde migration up peripheral nerve axons"}
