{"qid": "med:195", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient presents with the classic triad of normal pressure hydrocephalus (NPH): gait apraxia (broad\u2011based, slow, short\u2011stepped gait), urinary urgency/incontinence, and subcortical dementia (inattention, impaired recall). NPH is caused by impaired CSF reabsorption leading to ventriculomegaly without elevated intracranial pressure. The definitive treatment for symptomatic NPH is surgical diversion of CSF.\n\n**Answer:** Ventriculoperitoneal shunt placement.", "correct": false, "gold": "Ventricular shunting"}
{"qid": "med:200", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "They started with **outcome status**:\n\n- **Cases**: BRCA-positive women **with** breast cancer\n- **Controls**: BRCA-positive women **without** breast cancer\n\nThen they looked back at prior **exposure**:\n\n- Whether they had undergone **bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy**\n\nThey also reported an **odds ratio**, which is the classic measure of association for this design.\n\nAnswer: **Case-control study**", "correct": true, "gold": "Case-control"}
{"qid": "med:199", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The clinical picture (acute agitation, photophobia, dysphagia, hydrophobia\u2011like symptoms, prodromal flu\u2011like illness, recent spelunking in bat\u2011infested caves, and fatal encephalitis) is classic for rabies virus infection. Rabies virus enters the body through a bite or mucosal contact with infected bat saliva, replicates locally in muscle, then invades peripheral nerve endings and travels to the CNS by retrograde axonal transport.\n\nAnswer: Retrograde axonal transport along peripheral nerves.", "correct": false, "gold": "Retrograde migration up peripheral nerve axons"}
{"qid": "med:200", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "This is a case-control study. The researchers identified women with breast cancer (cases) and without breast cancer (controls) from a database of BRCA-mutation positive women, then looked back at their surgical history to compare the proportion who had undergone bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy.\n\nAnswer: case-control study", "correct": true, "gold": "Case-control"}
{"qid": "med:200", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The described study begins by identifying a population of BRCA-positive women and partitioning them according to the presence or absence of breast cancer (i.e., by outcome status). Historical exposure information\u2014whether bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy had been performed\u2014is then ascertained retrospectively from registry records and surveys. The measure of association reported is an odds ratio, which is the statistic classically generated by this sampling scheme.\nThese design features match the definition of a case-control study: cases (women with breast cancer) and controls (women without breast cancer) are sampled on the basis of outcome, after which prior exposure prevalence is compared. The approach is distinct from a cohort design, which would have begun with exposure status and followed participants forward for incident cancer. Because outcome information already exists at the time exposure is assessed, the study is inherently retrospective, but the essential label remains \u201ccase-control.\u201d\nAnswer: case-control study", "correct": true, "gold": "Case-control"}
{"qid": "med:200", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The researchers identified groups based on disease status (cases with breast cancer vs. controls without) and looked back at their past exposure (previous surgery). This retrospective comparison of exposed vs. unexposed proportions between cases and controls is characteristic of a **case\u2011control study**.\n\nAnswer: case-control study<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "Case-control"}
{"qid": "med:200", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "This is a **case-control study** (specifically a retrospective case-control study).\n\nHere's the reasoning: The researchers started by identifying two groups based on the **outcome** status (women with breast cancer vs. women without breast cancer) and then looked backward in time to compare the **exposure** (history of bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy) between these groups. This \"outcome-first, look back at exposure\" approach is the hallmark of case-control design. The use of an odds ratio as the measure of association is also characteristic of case-control studies, as they sample based on disease status rather than exposure status.\n\nAnswer: Case-control study (or retrospective case-control study)", "correct": true, "gold": "Case-control"}
{"qid": "med:185", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient\u2019s presentation of a **stony\u2011hard, asymmetric, enlarged thyroid** causing compressive symptoms (dysphagia), together with **primary hypothyroidism** (low\u2011normal T4, elevated TSH) and a **cold nodule** on radionuclide scan, is classic for **Riedel\u2019s thyroiditis** (invasive fibrous thyroiditis). This rare IgG4\u2011related fibrosing disorder replaces thyroid parenchyma with dense fibrous tissue, producing a rock\u2011like (\u201cstony hard\u201d) gland that is often fixed and can cause hypothyroidism and low radioactive iodine uptake. While anaplastic carcinoma or lymphoma can present with a rapidly enlarging hard mass, the specific descriptor \u201cstony hard,\u201d the hypothyroid state, and the relatively young age (48\u202fyears) make Riedel\u2019s thyroiditis the most likely pathology.\n\nAnswer: Riedel's thyroiditis (invasive fibrous thyroiditis).", "correct": false, "gold": "Reidel thyroiditis"}
{"qid": "med:201", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient's presentation after treatment\u2014sudden severe respiratory distress, tracheal deviation to the left, absent breath sounds on the right, distended neck veins, and hypotension\u2014is classic for tension pneumothorax. This is a life-threatening emergency requiring immediate decompression.\n\nThe best next step is **needle decompression** of the right chest, followed by chest tube placement.\n\nAnswer: Needle decompression of the right chest (second intercostal space, midclavicular line).", "correct": false, "gold": "Needle thoracostomy"}
{"qid": "med:201", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Needle decompression (needle thoracostomy) of the right chest \u2014 immediate needle decompression at the 2nd intercostal space, midclavicular line, followed by chest tube placement.\n\nAnswer: Emergency needle decompression of the right chest (tension pneumothorax) at the 2nd intercostal space, midclavicular line.", "correct": false, "gold": "Needle thoracostomy"}
{"qid": "med:201", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This HIV-positive patient with fever, dry cough, hypoxemia, and low CD4 count likely has Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia. After treatment, she acutely develops hypotension, severe respiratory distress, tracheal deviation away from the right side, absent right-sided breath sounds, and JVD \u2014 classic for a right-sided tension pneumothorax, a known complication of PCP due to ruptured subpleural cysts.\n\nTension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis and must be treated immediately without waiting for imaging.\n\nAnswer: Immediate needle decompression of the right chest followed by chest tube thoracostomy.", "correct": false, "gold": "Needle thoracostomy"}
{"qid": "med:200", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Type of Study Design:** Case-control study \r\n\r\n**Reasoning:** \r\n- **Outcome-based selection:** The researchers divided participants into two groups based on whether they had breast cancer (cases) or not (controls).\r\n- **Retrospective exposure assessment:** They looked back at surgical history (bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy) using a registry and surveys to determine exposure status.\r\n- **Measure of association:** An odds ratio (0.46) was reported, which is the standard measure used in case-control studies. \r\n\r\nAnswer: Case-control study", "correct": true, "gold": "Case-control"}
{"qid": "med:197", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Answer: Clarithromycin 500 mg PO BID, ethambutol 15 mg/kg PO daily, and rifabutin 300 mg PO daily.", "correct": false, "gold": "Azithromycin and ethambutol"}
{"qid": "med:201", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation is consistent with PCP pneumonia (HIV, low CD4 count, non-compliance with prophylaxis, subacute fever/dry cough/hypoxemia). Standard initial therapy (antibiotics + steroids) was appropriately begun, but rapid hemodynamic collapse with unilateral absent breath sounds, tracheal deviation, and jugular venous distention is diagnostic of tension pneumothorax (a known complication of PCP). This is a clinical diagnosis that requires immediate intervention before imaging or other measures.\nAnswer: immediate needle decompression, right 2nd intercostal space, mid-clavicular line (followed by tube thoracostomy)", "correct": false, "gold": "Needle thoracostomy"}
