{"qid": "med:203", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Halothane hepatitis (immune-mediated hepatotoxicity from halogenated inhalational anesthetic exposure)\n\nAnswer: Halothane-induced hepatotoxicity from the general inhalational anesthesia used during her appendectomy", "correct": false, "gold": "Adverse effect of anesthetic"}
{"qid": "med:203", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Massive centrilobular hepatic necrosis occurring **2 weeks after surgery under general inhalational anesthesia** is classic for **halothane-induced hepatitis**.\n\nWhy this fits:\n- Recent exposure to **inhaled anesthetic** during appendectomy\n- Fever, vomiting, abdominal pain, jaundice\n- Markedly elevated **AST**\n- **Negative viral hepatitis workup** for acute HAV, HBV, and HCV\n- Biopsy showing **massive centrilobular necrosis**, a classic pathology finding\n\nMechanism:\n- **Halothane** is metabolized by the liver to reactive trifluoroacetylated intermediates\n- These can trigger an **immune-mediated hepatotoxic reaction**, especially after prior sensitization/exposure\n\nThe blood transfusion is less likely:\n- **Hepatitis C antibodies** may not yet be positive this early, but transfusion-related hepatitis would not classically cause this specific pattern of **massive centrilobular necrosis** so soon after surgery\n- The anesthesia exposure is the key clue\n\nAnswer: Halothane-induced immune-mediated hepatic necrosis due to toxic anesthetic metabolites.", "correct": false, "gold": "Adverse effect of anesthetic"}
