{"qid": "med:206", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at this case, I need to identify the diagnosis based on:\n1. **Clinical presentation**: 45-year-old man with chronic pelvic pain (2 years), irritative voiding symptoms, hematospermia, and painful ejaculation\n2. **Failed antibiotic therapy**: No response to ciprofloxacin and other antimicrobials\n3. **Laboratory findings**: \n   - Mid-stream urine: WBC negative, culture negative\n   - Expressed prostatic secretion (EPS): WBC positive, culture negative\n4. **Associated conditions**: GI symptoms suggesting IBS (abdominal pain, alternating constipation/diarrhea, fatigue)\n\n**Key analysis:**\nThe NIH classification system categorizes prostatitis into four types:\n- **Category I**: Acute bacterial prostatitis (fever, acute illness, positive culture)\n- **Category II**: Chronic bacterial prostatitis (recurrent UTIs, positive culture)\n- **Category III**: Chronic Prostatitis/Chronic Pelvic Pain Syndrome (CP/CPPS) - pelvic pain >3 months without bacterial infection\n  - **IIIA**: Inflammatory (WBCs in semen/EPS/voided bladder 3)\n  - **IIIB**: Non-inflammatory (no WBCs)\n- **Category IV**: Asymptomatic inflammatory prostatitis\n\n**This patient has:**\n- Symptoms >3 months (2 years)\n- Pelvic pain + voiding symptoms + sexual dysfunction\n- **Critical finding**: WBCs present in EPS but culture negative\n- No response to antibiotics\n\nThis pattern is pathognomonic for **Category IIIA Chronic Prostatitis/Chronic Pelvic Pain Syndrome (CP/CPPS)** - the inflammatory subtype. The associated IBS-like symptoms support this diagnosis, as CP/CPPS is a functional somatic syndrome frequently comorbid with irritable bowel syndrome, fibromyalgia, and chronic fatigue syndrome.\n\nChronic bacterial prostatitis (Category II) is ruled out by negative cultures. Interstitial cystitis is less likely given the prostatic-specific findings (painful ejaculation, hematospermia, WBCs in EPS).\n\nAnswer: Chronic Prostatitis/Chronic Pelvic Pain Syndrome (CP/CPPS) - NIH Category IIIA (Inflammatory chronic pelvic pain syndrome) or Chronic nonbacterial prostatitis.", "correct": false, "gold": "Chronic, non-bacterial prostatitis"}
