{"qid": "med:208", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient is only 12 hours removed from an upper-abdominal laparotomy. At this early interval the dominant physiologic insults are incisional pain and diaphragmatic irritation, both of which produce shallow tidal breathing, failure to clear bronchial secretions, and progressive alveolar collapse\u2014most often in the dependent lower lobes. Physical findings of reduced breath sounds at one base and a supine chest radiograph that typically shows volume loss or platelike opacity corroborate segmental atelectasis rather than later-onset processes such as pneumonia or pulmonary embolism. Pre-existing smoking and asthma increase the risk but do not alter the temporal pattern that points to postoperative atelectasis as the proximate cause of dyspnea.\nAnswer: atelectasis (left lower lobe)", "correct": true, "gold": "Atelectasis"}
