{"qid": "med:250", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The case describes intentional overdose by a young man with known MDD, resulting in coma plus a borderline-prolonged QT interval (450 ms).  \n\nTricyclic antidepressants are unlikely because their classic ECG signature is QRS widening (not isolated QT prolongation) and the vignette does not mention this finding. Most SSRIs are relatively safe in overdose and do not appreciably lengthen the QT interval. Citalopram, however, is well-documented to produce dose-dependent QT prolongation; in large ingestions it also causes coma, seizures, and depressed consciousness. The modest therapeutic response noted by the mother is consistent with an SSRI trial that was only partially effective, after which the patient retained access to the medication for overdose.  \n\nThus the clinical picture\u2014coma plus QT prolongation without QRS widening\u2014points directly to citalopram.\n\nAnswer: citalopram", "correct": false, "gold": "Clomipramine"}
{"qid": "med:252", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient has **hepatic encephalopathy**: somnolence, confusion, asterixis, hyperreflexia, cirrhosis, ascites, and elevated ammonia.\n\nThe key question is the **precipitating factor**. Common triggers include:\n- GI bleeding\n- Infection, especially **spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP)**\n- Constipation\n- Electrolyte disturbances\n- Sedatives\n- Renal failure\n\nHere, there is:\n- **Fever**\n- **Ascites**\n- No evidence of active GI bleeding on endoscopy\n- No major electrolyte abnormality\n- No constipation history\n- No structural brain lesion on MRI\n\nIn a cirrhotic patient with ascites and fever, the most likely trigger is **spontaneous bacterial peritonitis**, which commonly precipitates hepatic encephalopathy.\n\nAnswer: Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis causing hepatic encephalopathy.", "correct": true, "gold": "Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis"}
{"qid": "med:252", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has hepatic encephalopathy (altered mental status, asterixis, hyperreflexia, elevated ammonia) in the setting of cirrhosis and ascites. The most common precipitating factors include GI bleeding, infection, electrolyte abnormalities, constipation, medications (sedatives), and high protein intake.  \n\nHere, there is no active bleeding on endoscopy, no electrolyte disturbances, and no obvious infection from vitals (though temperature is slightly elevated). However, the presence of ascites and low-grade fever raises the possibility of spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP), a common infection in cirrhotic patients that can precipitate encephalopathy.  \n\n**Answer:** Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP)", "correct": true, "gold": "Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis"}
{"qid": "med:252", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Most likely precipitating factor: Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis (SBP)**\n\nThis patient has decompensated cirrhosis with new-onset ascites and now presents with hepatic encephalopathy (altered mentation, asterixis, hyperreflexia, elevated ammonia) along with a low-grade fever (38.0\u00b0C). In a cirrhotic patient with ascites, fever plus new/worsening encephalopathy should immediately raise suspicion for **spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP)**, which is one of the most common precipitants of hepatic encephalopathy.\n\nSBP causes systemic inflammation and increased ammonia production/absorption, along with bacterial translocation across the gut wall, which impairs hepatic and renal clearance of nitrogenous waste products, precipitating encephalopathy. A diagnostic paracentesis with ascitic fluid analysis (cell count with differential, culture) is warranted to confirm SBP (PMN count \u2265250 cells/mm\u00b3).\n\nOther common precipitants (GI bleeding, hypokalemia, medication noncompliance, dehydration) were reasonably excluded \u2014 endoscopy showed no active variceal bleeding, potassium is only mildly low (unlikely to be the primary trigger), and there's no history of excess diuretic use or renal failure indicated.\n\nAnswer: Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP)", "correct": true, "gold": "Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis"}
{"qid": "med:245", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: In immunocompetent individuals the peptide would normally be generated by antigen processing (proteolysis) and loaded onto MHC class\u202fII molecules on antigen\u2011presenting cells for presentation to CD4\u207a T cells \u2013 i.e., antigen processing and presentation.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Isotype switching"}
{"qid": "med:252", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient has decompensated alcoholic cirrhosis with new/worsening ascites and subacute alteration in mental status. The presence of asterixis, hyperreflexia, and elevated serum ammonia in the absence of any structural brain lesion on MRI establishes the diagnosis of hepatic encephalopathy (HE).\nAmong the classic precipitants of HE (GI bleed, constipation, electrolyte disturbance, sedatives, uremia, infection), only infection is supported by the data. Low-grade fever plus ascites is the classic presentation of spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP); SBP is the single most common trigger of HE in this setting. Endoscopy shows non-bleeding gastric varices, electrolytes and glucose are normal, bowel habits are unchanged, and arterial blood gas shows no acid\u2013base disorder, eliminating the other common precipitants.\nThus the most likely precipitating factor is spontaneous bacterial peritonitis.\nAnswer: spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP)", "correct": true, "gold": "Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis"}
