{"qid": "med:257", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient reports a gestational age of 10 weeks, but the measured fundal height (10 cm above the symphysis) is markedly larger than expected. At 10 weeks the uterus remains a pelvic organ and is not normally palpable on abdominal examination; a fundal height of this magnitude is consistent with approximately 16 weeks\u2019 gestation. This size\u2013date discrepancy raises the possibility of inaccurate menstrual dating, multiple gestation, or gestational trophoblastic disease.\nBecause the discrepancy is already evident on physical examination, the immediate next step is pelvic ultrasonography. Ultrasound will confirm or revise gestational age, determine fetal number, assess viability, and evaluate for molar changes, thereby guiding all subsequent prenatal management.\nAnswer: pelvic ultrasonography (to evaluate size-date discrepancy)", "correct": false, "gold": "Cell-free DNA testing"}
