{"qid": "med:258", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "**Reaction:** Acute hemolytic transfusion reaction (AHTR) \u2013 an intravascular hemolysis usually due to ABO incompatibility.\n\n**Best treatment:** Immediately stop the transfusion, keep the IV line open with normal saline, and provide aggressive supportive care (IV fluid resuscitation, vasopressors if needed) to maintain blood pressure and urine output; monitor for acute kidney injury and notify the blood bank to check for clerical error and perform a direct antiglobulin test.\n\nAnswer: Acute hemolytic transfusion reaction; stop the transfusion immediately and give IV fluids/supportive care to maintain blood pressure and renal perfusion.", "correct": false, "gold": "Hemolytic transfusion reaction; discontinue transfusion"}
{"qid": "med:264", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Methimazole is generally avoided in the 1st trimester because it is associated with teratogenic effects (eg, aplasia cutis, choanal/esophageal atresia). In early pregnancy, women with hyperthyroidism who require antithyroid therapy should usually be switched to propylthiouracil (PTU), which is preferred in the 1st trimester; methimazole can be resumed later (typically after the 1st trimester) due to PTU\u2019s risk of hepatotoxicity.\n\nHer TSH is normal, but in a patient with known hyperthyroidism on treatment, pregnancy itself does not mean stopping therapy outright without assessment; the immediate management change is to replace methimazole with PTU and monitor thyroid function closely.\n\nAnswer: Discontinue methimazole and switch to propylthiouracil during the first trimester, with close thyroid-function monitoring.", "correct": false, "gold": "Discontinue methimazole, start propylthiouracil"}
{"qid": "med:262", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "This patient presents with a subacute cough (2 weeks duration) following an upper respiratory infection, now with scant hemoptysis (blood-tinged sputum). While post-infectious cough is common and can persist for 3\u20138 weeks, the presence of hemoptysis requires further evaluation to rule out serious underlying pathology such as pneumonia, tuberculosis (particularly relevant given his diabetes), bronchiectasis, or malignancy.\n\nKey considerations:\n- **Hemoptysis**: Even small amounts of blood in sputum warrant imaging to exclude underlying lung disease, infection, or mass lesions.\n- **Risk factors**: Diabetes increases susceptibility to infections including tuberculosis.\n- **Physical exam**: Clear lung sounds do not rule out parenchymal disease, cavitary lesions, or endobronchial pathology.\n- **Stability**: The patient is hemodynamically stable with normal vitals, making advanced imaging (CT) or bronchoscopy premature without initial screening.\n\n**Chest X-ray** is the appropriate first step to evaluate for infiltrates, cavitary lesions, masses, or other abnormalities. Further management (sputum cultures, CT scan, or bronchoscopy) would be guided by the radiographic findings.\n\nAntibiotics are not indicated as there are no signs of bacterial pneumonia (afebrile, clear lungs, improving condition). Reassurance alone is inappropriate given the hemoptysis.\n\nAnswer: Order a chest X-ray.", "correct": false, "gold": "Supportive care"}
{"qid": "med:263", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Answer: Postoperative pneumonia", "correct": true, "gold": "Pneumonia"}
{"qid": "med:264", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Next best step: Switch from methimazole to propylthiouracil (PTU) for the first trimester.**\n\n**Rationale:**\n\nThis patient is euthyroid (TSH 2.0 \u03bcU/mL, within normal range), indicating her hyperthyroidism is currently well-controlled. However, she is now in early pregnancy (first trimester), which changes the risk-benefit calculation of her antithyroid medication.\n\n- **Methimazole** is associated with a rare but serious embryopathy when used during the **first trimester**, including aplasia cutis, choanal atresia, esophageal atresia, and other malformations (collectively sometimes called \"methimazole embryopathy\").\n- **Propylthiouracil (PTU)** is preferred during the **first trimester** because it does not carry this teratogenic risk. However, PTU carries a small but serious risk of **hepatotoxicity**, which is why it is not used long-term.\n- Therefore, the standard recommendation is to **switch to PTU for the first trimester**, then **transition back to methimazole for the second and third trimesters** to minimize the risk of PTU-associated liver injury.\n\nSince her thyroid function is currently normal, no dose escalation or additional monitoring beyond routine thyroid function testing (typically each trimester) is needed at this time\u2014the priority is changing the medication itself to protect the fetus during the critical period of organogenesis.\n\nAnswer: Switch the patient from methimazole to propylthiouracil (PTU) for the duration of the first trimester, then transition back to methimazole in the second and third trimesters.", "correct": false, "gold": "Discontinue methimazole, start propylthiouracil"}
{"qid": "med:264", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has well-controlled hyperthyroidism on methimazole and is now pregnant. Methimazole is associated with teratogenic effects, especially during the first trimester (e.g., aplasia cutis, choanal atresia). The current guidelines recommend switching to propylthiouracil (PTU) during the first trimester to minimize fetal risk, as PTU has a lower teratogenic risk. Her TSH is normal (2.0 \u03bcU/mL), indicating good control, so no dose adjustment for active hyperthyroidism is needed\u2014just the medication change.\n\nAnswer: Switch from methimazole to propylthiouracil (PTU).", "correct": false, "gold": "Discontinue methimazole, start propylthiouracil"}
{"qid": "med:264", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Switch from methimazole to propylthiouracil (PTU) for hyperthyroidism management during the first trimester of pregnancy, and monitor thyroid function tests regularly.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Discontinue methimazole, start propylthiouracil"}
{"qid": "med:264", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient is confirmed to be in the first trimester of pregnancy (approximately 6 weeks gestational age) with previously well-controlled hyperthyroidism. Methimazole is a known teratogen (associated with aplasia cutis congenita, choanal atresia, and esophageal atresia) and is therefore contraindicated once pregnancy is recognized. Propylthiouracil is the preferred antithyroid agent during the first trimester because it does not carry the same teratogenic risk; after organogenesis is complete it can be switched back to methimazole if desired. The current TSH value of 2.0 \u00b5U/mL indicates euthyroidism, so an immediate change from methimazole to an equivalent dose of propylthiouracil is both safe and necessary. No other laboratory adjustments or specialist referrals supersede this medication change at the present visit.\nAnswer: switch methimazole to PTU (1st-trimester)", "correct": false, "gold": "Discontinue methimazole, start propylthiouracil"}
{"qid": "med:263", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at this clinical scenario, I need to identify the most likely diagnosis based on the timing and clinical features:\n\n**Key Clinical Features:**\n- Postoperative day 3 after open cholecystectomy\n- High fever (39\u00b0C/102.2\u00b0F) with chills\n- Right-sided pleuritic chest pain (worse with inspiration)\n- Productive cough\n- Physical exam: Inspiratory crackles at the right lung base\n- Risk factors: Obesity, smoking history (17 pack-years), recent upper abdominal surgery\n\n**Analysis using postoperative fever timeline (the \"5 W's\"):**\n- **Day 1-2**: Atelectasis (\"Wind\") - typically causes low-grade fever, decreased breath sounds, minimal cough\n- **Day 3-5**: Pneumonia (\"Wind\") or UTI (\"Water\")\n- **Day 5-7**: Wound infection\n- **Day 7-10**: DVT/PE (\"Walking\")\n\n**Why this is pneumonia, not atelectasis:**\nWhile atelectasis is common on postoperative days 1-2, this patient is on day 3 with:\n- High fever (39\u00b0C) rather than low-grade fever\n- Productive cough with purulent sputum\n- Pleuritic chest pain\n- Crackles at the lung base (atelectasis typically causes decreased breath sounds)\n\n**Why not other diagnoses:**\n- **Pulmonary embolism**: Usually causes tachypnea, tachycardia, and low-grade fever; the high fever (39\u00b0C) and productive cough point to infection, not embolism\n- **Subphrenic abscess**: Typically occurs 7-10 days post-op with abdominal pain/tenderness; the abdomen here is soft and nontender\n- **Wound infection**: Usually occurs days 5-7 with local wound findings\n- **UTI**: Would present with urinary symptoms, not pulmonary findings\n\n**Pathophysiology:**\nThe patient has multiple risk factors for postoperative pneumonia: obesity, smoking, upper abdominal surgery (causing splinting and shallow breathing), and possible aspiration during the perioperative period. The right lower lobe is commonly affected due to gravitational effects and the anatomy of the right mainstem bronchus.\n\nAnswer: Postoperative pneumonia (right lower lobe pneumonia)", "correct": true, "gold": "Pneumonia"}
{"qid": "med:265", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has multiple risk factors for atherosclerosis (smoking, diabetes, hypertension, hyperlipidemia) and presents with a carotid bruit and symptoms of left hand weakness/numbness, suggesting possible transient ischemic attacks (TIAs) referable to the right carotid territory. Before proceeding with elective vascular surgery, further evaluation of the carotid arteries is indicated to assess for significant stenosis and stroke risk.\n\n**Answer:** Carotid duplex ultrasound", "correct": false, "gold": "Ultrasonography of the neck"}
{"qid": "med:265", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient has **symptomatic carotid artery disease**: he has a **right carotid bruit** and **transient episodes of left hand weakness/numbness**, which are concerning for **transient ischemic attacks** from **right carotid stenosis**.\n\nIn a patient with **symptomatic carotid stenosis**, the next step is to **evaluate the carotid arteries with duplex ultrasonography**. If significant stenosis is confirmed (typically **70\u201399%**, or sometimes 50\u201369% depending on circumstances), he would benefit from **carotid endarterectomy**, especially since he is already being evaluated preoperatively and has good functional status.\n\nThis is more urgent and relevant than the incidental inguinal hernia or additional cardiac testing. He has no symptoms suggesting unstable coronary disease, and routine preoperative stress testing is not indicated here.\n\n**Most appropriate next step:** **carotid duplex ultrasonography**.\n\nAnswer: Perform carotid duplex ultrasonography to evaluate suspected symptomatic carotid artery stenosis.", "correct": false, "gold": "Ultrasonography of the neck"}
{"qid": "med:265", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Answer: Duplex ultrasonography of the carotid arteries**\n\nThis patient has a right-sided carotid bruit and transient neurologic symptoms (episodic left hand weakness/numbness) that are referable to the right cerebral hemisphere \u2014 consistent with **transient ischemic attacks (TIAs)** from right carotid artery stenosis. Given his significant risk factors (long-standing smoking, hypertension, diabetes, hyperlipidemia, peripheral arterial disease), carotid atherosclerosis is highly likely.\n\nBefore proceeding with elective femoropopliteal bypass surgery, it is critical to evaluate the carotid circulation, since:\n- Significant carotid stenosis increases the perioperative risk of stroke, especially with the hemodynamic changes associated with anesthesia and surgery.\n- If severe stenosis (>70%) is found, carotid revascularization (endarterectomy or stenting) may need to be performed before or concurrently with the peripheral vascular surgery to reduce stroke risk.\n\n**Carotid duplex ultrasonography** is the most appropriate initial, non-invasive next step to assess the degree of carotid artery stenosis and guide further management (e.g., need for carotid endarterectomy) prior to proceeding with the elective bypass surgery.\n\nAnswer: Duplex ultrasonography of the carotid arteries", "correct": false, "gold": "Ultrasonography of the neck"}
{"qid": "med:265", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient has multiple atherosclerotic risk factors and is undergoing preoperative evaluation for elective vascular surgery. He also reports transient left-hand sensory and motor symptoms (consistent with carotid-territory TIAs) and has a right carotid bruit on examination. These findings indicate the need to evaluate for hemodynamically significant carotid stenosis before proceeding with surgery. Non-invasive carotid duplex ultrasonography is the appropriate initial diagnostic study; invasive angiography or cross-sectional brain imaging is not required at this stage.\nAnswer: carotid duplex ultrasonography", "correct": false, "gold": "Ultrasonography of the neck"}
{"qid": "med:265", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Reasoning: The patient has a neck bruit and intermittent left-hand weakness/numbness, suggesting possible carotid artery stenosis. Before a major vascular procedure such as a femoropopliteal bypass, evaluating for clinically significant carotid disease is important to reduce the risk of perioperative stroke. The appropriate initial, non\u2011invasive test is carotid duplex ultrasonography. If substantial stenosis is found, further imaging and possible carotid endarterectomy would be considered.\n\nAnswer: Obtain a carotid duplex ultrasound to evaluate for carotid artery stenosis.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Ultrasonography of the neck"}
{"qid": "med:266", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Caudal regression syndrome (sacral agenesis)**\n\n**Reasoning:** Maternal type 1 diabetes with poor glycemic control\u2014especially preconception and in early pregnancy\u2014is a well-established teratogenic risk factor. While diabetic embryopathy can cause multiple anomalies (cardiac defects like transposition of the great vessels or VSDs, neural tube defects, and renal anomalies), **caudal regression syndrome** is the most *specific* and classic congenital abnormality associated with poorly controlled maternal diabetes, even though cardiac defects are the most *common* anomaly overall. \n\nCaudal regression syndrome results from disruption of the caudal mesoderm during early embryogenesis (weeks 3\u20137), leading to a spectrum of abnormalities including:\n- Sacral/lumbosacral agenesis\n- Lower limb hypoplasia or fusion (sirenomelia in severe cases)\n- Renal anomalies\n- Anorectal malformations\n- Neurogenic bladder\n\nOn ultrasound, this would appear as absence or hypoplasia of the sacrum/lumbosacral spine, which is a hallmark finding highly specific for maternal diabetic embryopathy.\n\nAnswer: Caudal regression syndrome (sacral agenesis)", "correct": false, "gold": "Ventricular septal defect"}
{"qid": "med:264", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient is approximately 6 weeks pregnant (gestational age calculated from last menstrual period: 4-week cycle + 2 weeks missed period). Methimazole is associated with teratogenic effects, specifically aplasia cutis and choanal atresia, when used during the first trimester (particularly weeks 6\u201310). Current guidelines recommend using propylthiouracil (PTU) during the first trimester because it has a lower risk of congenital malformations. Although PTU carries a risk of hepatotoxicity, it is the preferred agent until the second trimester, after which patients can be switched back to methimazole. Since the patient is well-controlled but early in pregnancy, the priority is changing to the safer medication.\n\nAnswer: Switch from methimazole to propylthiouracil (PTU).", "correct": false, "gold": "Discontinue methimazole, start propylthiouracil"}
{"qid": "med:254", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "", "correct": false, "gold": "Check serum electrolytes"}
{"qid": "med:266", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Poorly controlled **pregestational type 1 diabetes**, especially during **organogenesis in the 1st trimester**, is strongly associated with fetal congenital malformations. The classic defect most specifically linked to maternal diabetes is **caudal regression syndrome**.\n\nClues here:\n- **Poor diabetic control before pregnancy**\n- **Fundal height larger than dates** suggests possible **fetal macrosomia/polyhydramnios**, also common in diabetic pregnancy\n- The congenital anomaly most classically tested is **sacral agenesis/caudal regression**\n\nOn ultrasound, this would appear as **absence or malformation of the lower spine/sacrum**, sometimes with abnormal lower extremities.\n\nAnswer: **Caudal regression syndrome (sacral agenesis)**", "correct": false, "gold": "Ventricular septal defect"}
{"qid": "med:263", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient is on postoperative day 3 after upper-abdominal surgery and now exhibits acute systemic and focal respiratory findings (high fever with chills, pleuritic pain, productive cough, and basal crackles). These features localize the process to the lung parenchyma.\nPostoperative pulmonary complications follow a predictable temporal pattern:\n- Atelectasis peaks within the first 24\u201348 h and is typically associated with low-grade fever and minimal or non-purulent secretions.\n- Hospital-acquired (or postoperative) pneumonia emerges after 48 h, produces high fever, rigors, and frankly purulent sputum, and is favored by the same risk factors present here (obesity, smoking, upper-abdominal incision causing splinting and impaired clearance).\nAlternative considerations are less consistent with the vignette:\n- Pulmonary embolism may cause pleuritic pain but rarely produces high fever, chills, or copious sputum.\n- Subphrenic abscess or wound infection would be expected later (\u22655\u20137 days) and would feature abdominal or incisional signs that are absent.\n- Simple atelectasis is chronologically too early and does not explain the frankly productive cough or rigors.\nThus the timing, the character of the sputum, and the degree of fever converge on hospital-acquired pneumonia (most often right-lower-lobe) as the single most likely diagnosis.\nAnswer: hospital-acquired (post-op) pneumonia (RLL)", "correct": true, "gold": "Pneumonia"}
{"qid": "med:265", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "This patient has several important findings that require evaluation before his elective vascular surgery:\n\n1. **Focal neurological symptoms**: Episodic weakness and numbness of the left hand suggest transient ischemic attacks (TIAs). These symptoms localize", "correct": false, "gold": "Ultrasonography of the neck"}
{"qid": "med:262", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "The patient presents with a persistent cough and occasional blood-streaked sputum following a recent upper respiratory infection. While this is most likely due to post-infectious airway irritation (post-bronchitic cough), the presence of hemoptysis warrants further evaluation to rule out more serious causes such as pneumonia, tuberculosis, bronchiectasis, or a pulmonary mass. \r\n\r\nA chest X-ray is the most appropriate initial test to assess for these potential pathologies, especially in a patient with new hemoptysis. \r\n\r\nAnswer: Chest X-ray", "correct": false, "gold": "Supportive care"}
{"qid": "med:266", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "Maternal pregestational diabetes with hyperglycemia during the first trimester (organogenesis) markedly elevates the risk of fetal structural malformations. While cardiac defects are numerically the most frequent anomalies observed, caudal regression syndrome (sacral agenesis) is the lesion with the strongest and most specific epidemiologic link to maternal diabetes; its relative risk is increased several-hundred-fold and it is considered nearly pathognomonic in this clinical setting. At the 18-week anatomic survey, ultrasound can directly demonstrate absence or hypoplasia of the sacrum, shortened lower extremities, and abrupt termination of the spinal column\u2014findings that distinguish caudal regression from other anomalies (e.g., isolated neural-tube defects) whose association with diabetes is weaker. Consequently, the ultrasound finding most characteristic of this patient\u2019s poorly controlled diabetes is caudal regression/sacral agenesis.\nAnswer: caudal regression (sacral agenesis)", "correct": false, "gold": "Ventricular septal defect"}
{"qid": "med:266", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Poor glycemic control in pregestational diabetes markedly increases the risk of fetal congenital malformations, especially cardiac defects. Among these, a ventricular septal defect is the most frequently observed abnormality on mid\u2011trimester ultrasound in infants of diabetic mothers.\n\nAnswer: Ventricular septal defect (VSD)<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "Ventricular septal defect"}
{"qid": "med:267", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "She has **hoarding disorder**: persistent difficulty discarding possessions, clutter compromising living spaces, distress/impairment, and insight that it is excessive. She has already had **6 months of CBT without improvement**, so the next step is **pharmacotherapy**.\n\nFirst-line medication treatment for hoarding disorder/OCD-spectrum symptoms is an **SSRI** (eg, fluoxetine, sertraline, paroxetine).\n\nAnswer: Start an SSRI (eg, fluoxetine/sertraline).", "correct": true, "gold": "Fluoxetine"}
{"qid": "med:267", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Answer: Start an SSRI (e.g., paroxetine)", "correct": false, "gold": "Fluoxetine"}
{"qid": "med:267", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has hoarding disorder, which has not improved with cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) alone. The most appropriate next step is to add pharmacotherapy, typically a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI), as combination treatment is often more effective for refractory cases.\n\nAnswer: Add an SSRI (e.g., fluoxetine or paroxetine).", "correct": true, "gold": "Fluoxetine"}
{"qid": "med:265", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Answer: \\(\\boxed{\\text{Carotid duplex ultrasonography}}\\)", "correct": false, "gold": "Ultrasonography of the neck"}
{"qid": "med:266", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has poorly controlled type 1 diabetes in early pregnancy, which is associated with an increased risk of congenital anomalies, particularly when glycemic control is poor during the first trimester (organogenesis). The most characteristic congenital abnormality linked to maternal diabetes is **sacral agenesis** (caudal regression syndrome). The fundal height being larger than expected (20 cm at 18 weeks) may suggest polyhydramnios, which can also occur with poor diabetic control or fetal anomalies.\n\nAnswer: Sacral agenesis (caudal regression syndrome)", "correct": false, "gold": "Ventricular septal defect"}
