{"qid": "med:270", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Answer: Bipolar I disorder", "correct": false, "gold": "Bipolar II disorder"}
{"qid": "med:270", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "He has had:\n- A **5-day period** of elevated energy, **decreased need for sleep**, and **impulsive spending** causing social/functional concern, but not described as severe enough to require hospitalization or include psychosis during the episode \u2192 **hypomanic episode**\n- Multiple **month-long depressive episodes** with hypersomnia, anhedonia, low energy, and guilt \u2192 **major depressive episodes**\n\nThis combination is most consistent with **bipolar II disorder**.\n\nThe prior hallucinations during severe meningoencephalitis are best explained by **delirium/medical illness**, not a primary psychotic disorder.\n\nAnswer: **Bipolar II disorder**", "correct": true, "gold": "Bipolar II disorder"}
{"qid": "med:266", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Poorly controlled pregestational diabetes markedly increases the risk of fetal structural malformations. Although several anomalies (neural tube defects, caudal regression syndrome, renal anomalies) are associated with diabetic embryopathy, **cardiovascular defects** are the most common, occurring in roughly 3\u20135\u202f% of infants of diabetic mothers (versus <1\u202f% in the general population). Typical lesions include ventricular septal defects, transposition of the great arteries, and coarctation of the aorta. At 18\u202fweeks\u2019 gestation, a detailed anatomy scan can readily identify these cardiac abnormalities.\n\nAnswer: Congenital heart disease (cardiac defect).", "correct": false, "gold": "Ventricular septal defect"}
{"qid": "med:270", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient's presentation includes a clear hypomanic episode (5 days of decreased sleep, increased energy, impulsive spending) and recurrent major depressive episodes (month-long episodes of sadness, hypersomnia, anhedonia, fatigue, guilt). This pattern meets criteria for bipolar II disorder, as there is no history of full mania (the hypomanic episode was only 5 days, which is the minimum duration, and there was no psychosis or severe impairment). The history of meningoencephalitis with visual hallucinations (monkeys) is likely a separate episode of delirium or psychosis due to the infection, not a primary mood or psychotic disorder.\n\nAnswer: Bipolar II disorder", "correct": true, "gold": "Bipolar II disorder"}
{"qid": "med:270", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Bipolar II disorder.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "Bipolar II disorder"}
{"qid": "med:267", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "This vignette describes a patient with classic hoarding disorder (persistent difficulty discarding possessions, excessive accumulation that congests living areas, marked distress, onset in adolescence, and functional impairment). The patient has already completed an adequate trial of CBT without improvement and now reports worsening impairment and depressed mood. Because first-line psychotherapy has failed, pharmacologic augmentation is indicated. Hoarding disorder, although nosologically distinct from OCD, shares a serotonergic pathophysiology; therefore an SSRI is the evidence-based next step. Any SSRI (e.g., sertraline, fluoxetine) at standard antidepressant dosing is appropriate; choice among agents is individualized by side-effect profile and comorbidities.\nAnswer: initiate SSRI (e.g., sertraline)", "correct": false, "gold": "Fluoxetine"}
{"qid": "med:270", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient's history includes recurrent, month-long periods meeting full symptomatic criteria for major depressive episodes (anhedonia, hypersomnia, fatigue, and excessive guilt). These alternate with a distinct 5-day period of decreased need for sleep, elevated energy, and impulsive spending that was clearly noticeable to others. Because the elevated period lasted only 5 days, produced no marked functional impairment, required no hospitalization, and was unaccompanied by psychotic features, it meets DSM-5 criteria for hypomania rather than mania. The combination of major depressive episodes and hypomania without any history of full mania establishes the diagnosis of bipolar II disorder.\nThe remote episode of meningoencephalitis with transient visual hallucinations is best interpreted as a delirium that resolved years before the onset of the mood disorder; it does not explain the current, temporally separated, stereotyped affective episodes. Other differentials (cyclothymic disorder, substance-induced mood changes, or mood disorder due to another medical condition) are excluded by the severity and duration of the depressive episodes and by the absence of ongoing medical or substance-related precipitants.\nAnswer: bipolar II disorder", "correct": true, "gold": "Bipolar II disorder"}
{"qid": "med:271", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This college student most likely has **delayed sleep-wake phase disorder**, a circadian rhythm disorder common in adolescents and young adults. He has a **persistent delay in sleep onset** with difficulty falling asleep until very late, followed by marked morning sleepiness and daytime impairment.\n\nKey clues:\n- Falls asleep **2\u20133 hours after going to bed**\n- Trouble waking in the morning\n- Daytime sleepiness and class impairment\n- Normal physical exam\n- Has already reduced nighttime phone use\n- Melatonin \u201cwithout success\u201d likely because timing matters\n\nBest treatment:\n- **Bright light therapy in the morning** to shift his circadian rhythm earlier\n- Also helpful: consistent wake time, avoid naps/caffeine late in the day\n\nExercise at 8 p.m. can worsen delayed sleep onset, and nonspecific melatonin use is less reliable unless carefully timed in the early evening.\n\nAnswer: Recommend **morning bright light therapy (with a fixed early wake time) to advance his sleep phase**.", "correct": false, "gold": "Stop evening exercise"}
{"qid": "med:267", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Answer: Initiate pharmacotherapy with a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI).", "correct": false, "gold": "Fluoxetine"}
{"qid": "med:271", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "This patient has symptoms consistent with **delayed sleep-wake phase disorder (DSWPD)**. He cannot fall asleep until late (2\u20133 hours after going to bed at 11 p.m.\u20131 a.m.) and has extreme difficulty waking in the morning, with excessive daytime sleepiness and long naps. He has already tried good sleep hygiene measures (avoiding screens, melatonin, evening exercise) without success. The most appropriate next step is **timed morning bright light therapy** to advance his circadian phase, often combined with a strict, consistent wake time and avoiding late afternoon/evening naps.\n\nAnswer: Timed morning bright light therapy.", "correct": false, "gold": "Stop evening exercise"}
{"qid": "med:270", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at this case, I need to identify the pattern of mood episodes:\n\n**Key Clinical Features:**\n1. **Depressive episodes**: Multiple month-long episodes over 2 years with sadness, hypersomnia (sleeping more), anhedonia (loss of interest), fatigue, and guilt - meeting criteria for major depressive episodes.\n\n2. **Elevated episode**: A distinct 5-day period with decreased need for sleep, increased energy, and impulsive behavior (spending $2,000 without permission). This lasted 5 days and resolved without hospitalization.\n\n3. **No psychosis** in the current episode (the hallucinations of \"monkeys\" occurred 5 years ago during acute meningoencephalitis/delirium, not during the mood episode).\n\n**Diagnostic Analysis:**\n- **Bipolar I** requires a manic episode (\u22657 days or any duration if hospitalized). This episode lasted only 5 days without hospitalization, so it does not meet mania duration criteria.\n- **Bipolar II** requires at least one major depressive episode AND at least one hypomanic episode (\u22654 days but <7 days, with symptoms similar to mania but causing less severe impairment). The 5-day duration fits hypomania perfectly.\n- **Cyclothymia** is ruled out because the depressive episodes meet full criteria for major depression (lasting months with multiple symptoms).\n- **Major Depressive Disorder** is ruled out by the presence of the hypomanic episode.\n- **Mood disorder due to medical condition** (meningoencephalitis) is unlikely because the mood episodes began 3 years after the infection resolved, and the pattern is classic for primary bipolar disorder.\n\nThe 5-day duration is the critical clue distinguishing hypomania (4+ days) from mania (7+ days), making Bip", "correct": false, "gold": "Bipolar II disorder"}
{"qid": "med:270", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\nThis patient presents with a history of both hypomanic and major depressive episodes, which is characteristic of Bipolar II Disorder. \r\n\r\n- **Hypomanic episode:** The 5-day period of minimal sleep, unusual energy, and impulsive spending ($2,000) meets DSM-5 criteria for hypomania (lasting \u22654 days, no marked impairment or psychosis, and not requiring hospitalization).\r\n- **Major depressive episodes:** Over the past 2 years, he has experienced multiple month-long episodes of sadness, hypersomnia, anhedonia, fatigue, and guilt, consistent with Major Depressive Disorder.\r\n- **No full manic episode:** There is no history of symptoms lasting \u22657 days, severe impairment, or hospitalization due to mania, ruling out Bipolar I Disorder.\r\n- **Past meningoencephalitis:** While his history of severe meningoencephalitis with visual hallucinations is notable, it does not explain the recurrent, episodic mood fluctuations independent of acute infection at this time. \r\n\r\nAnswer: Bipolar II Disorder", "correct": true, "gold": "Bipolar II disorder"}
{"qid": "med:271", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Most appropriate recommendation: Morning bright light therapy (phototherapy) immediately upon waking, combined with a strict, consistent sleep-wake schedule and avoidance of daytime naps.**\n\n**Reasoning:**\n\nThis patient's presentation is classic for **Delayed Sleep-Wake Phase Disorder (DSPD)**, a circadian rhythm sleep disorder common in adolescents/young adults. Key features supporting this diagnosis:\n\n- Chronic difficulty falling asleep at a \"conventional\" bedtime (11 pm\u20131 am), taking 2\u20133 hours to fall asleep\n- Extreme difficulty waking at a socially required time (8:30 am) with significant morning grogginess (sleep inertia)\n- Compensatory afternoon napping and stimulant (caffeine) use\n- Failure of behavioral interventions that don't address the underlying circadian misalignment (melatonin taken incorrectly, evening exercise, avoiding blue light\u2014none of which corrected the phase delay)\n- No other medical/psychiatric red flags on history or exam\n\nIn DSPD, the patient's endogenous circadian rhythm is phase-delayed relative to societal norms\u2014he is not sleep-deprived or insomniac in the traditional sense; his internal clock simply runs \"late.\" Treatment aims to advance (shift earlier) the circadian phase:\n\n1. **Morning bright light exposure** (10,000 lux light box, or simply outdoor light) immediately upon waking helps advance the circadian clock by suppressing melatonin and resetting the SCN (suprachiasmatic nucleus) clock earlier.\n2. **Strict wake time** every day (including weekends) to reinforce the new schedule.\n3. **Elimination of naps**, which perpetuate the delayed phase by reducing sleep pressure at night.\n4. **Timed melatonin** (low-dose, several hours before desired bedtime \u2014 not at bedtime) can complement light therapy, but the patient already tried melatonin incorrectly (likely at the wrong time/dose).\n\nAvoiding evening light (already implemented) and evening exercise are reasonable adjuncts but insufficient alone. The cornerstone intervention for DSPD is **timed bright light therapy in the morning** to shift the circadian phase earlier.\n\nAnswer: Morning bright light therapy with a fixed, consistent wake-up time (chronotherapy for delayed sleep-wake phase disorder).", "correct": false, "gold": "Stop evening exercise"}
{"qid": "med:266", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Caudal regression syndrome (sacral agenesis)}}\n\\]", "correct": false, "gold": "Ventricular septal defect"}
{"qid": "med:272", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Propranolol\n\nAnswer: Propranolol", "correct": true, "gold": "Propranolol"}
{"qid": "med:271", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The vignette describes a young adult with a several-month history of sleep-onset difficulty, short sleep duration on a socially imposed schedule, profound morning sleep inertia, and excessive daytime sleepiness that is only partially offset by caffeine and long afternoon naps. These features are characteristic of delayed sleep-wake phase disorder, a circadian-rhythm misalignment common in college students.\nBecause the patient has already implemented basic sleep-hygiene measures (elimination of evening screen light) and has tried melatonin without benefit, the next evidence-based intervention is timed morning bright-light exposure. Light delivered shortly after the desired wake time (approximately 8:30 a.m.) advances the phase of the suprachiasmatic pacemaker, gradually allowing both sleep onset and final awakening to occur earlier. Adjunctive recommendations include strict avoidance of long afternoon naps and maintenance of a fixed wake time every day, but morning bright light is the specific, physiologically targeted therapy for this circadian disorder.\nAnswer: morning bright-light therapy (advance circadian phase)", "correct": false, "gold": "Stop evening exercise"}
{"qid": "med:272", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient's tremor is bilateral, high-frequency, occurs with action (finger-to-nose testing, brushing teeth, pouring coffee), improves as the day progresses, and is not accompanied by other Parkinsonian features (rigidity, bradykinesia, gait disturbance). This pattern is classic for **essential tremor**, not Parkinson disease. First-line treatment for essential tremor is a **beta-blocker**, typically **propranolol**. Given his hypertension, propranolol may also help lower blood pressure.\n\nAnswer: Propranolol", "correct": true, "gold": "Propranolol"}
{"qid": "med:272", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient most likely has **essential tremor**:\n\n- **Action/postural tremor**: occurs when brushing teeth, making coffee, and on finger-to-nose testing\n- **Bilateral, high-frequency hand tremor**\n- **Improves with alcohol**, which is classic\n- No features suggesting Parkinson disease such as **resting tremor, bradykinesia, rigidity, or postural instability**\n\nFirst-line treatment for essential tremor is:\n- **Propranolol** or\n- **Primidone**\n\nBecause this patient also has **hypertension**, **propranolol** is an especially good choice.\n\n**Answer: Propranolol**", "correct": true, "gold": "Propranolol"}
{"qid": "med:271", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Implement a consistent sleep\u2011wake schedule (fixed wake time daily), get bright light exposure in the morning and dim light in the evening, avoid caffeine after noon, limit afternoon naps to \u226430\u202fminutes, and consider taking melatonin 5\u202fhours before the desired bedtime to advance his circadian rhythm.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Stop evening exercise"}
{"qid": "med:272", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Propranolol<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "Propranolol"}
{"qid": "med:272", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient's presentation is consistent with essential tremor (bilateral high-frequency kinetic/postural tremor that is worse after overnight abstinence from alcohol and improves as the day progresses with ongoing modest alcohol intake). Parkinson disease is unlikely given the absence of resting tremor, bradykinesia, rigidity, or other neurologic findings; the family history of PD is therefore a distractor.  \n\nFirst-line pharmacologic options for essential tremor are propranolol or primidone. Because the patient also has untreated hypertension, propranolol simultaneously addresses both problems and is the preferred initial agent.  \n\nAnswer: propranolol", "correct": true, "gold": "Propranolol"}
{"qid": "med:277", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient has:\n\n- **>6 months** of symptoms\n- **Social withdrawal** and impaired functioning\n- **Delusions** (\u201caliens are watching him\u201d)\n- **Thought insertion/withdrawal** (\u201cstealing his thoughts\u201d)\n- **Disorganized thought process**\n- **Flat affect** (negative symptom)\n\nThis is most consistent with **schizophrenia**.\n\nNot schizophreniform disorder because that lasts **1\u20136 months**.  \nNot brief psychotic disorder because that lasts **<1 month**.  \nNot schizoid personality disorder because that does **not** involve psychosis.  \nNot substance-induced psychosis because marijuana use stopped a year ago.\n\nAnswer: Schizophrenia", "correct": true, "gold": "Schizophrenia"}
{"qid": "med:264", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\nIn pregnancy, thyroid function is best monitored with **free T4** rather than TSH alone, as TSH can be suppressed by hCG in the first trimester and may not accurately reflect control in patients on antithyroid drugs. Additionally, this patient is in early pregnancy (within organogenesis) and is currently taking methimazole, which carries a teratogenic risk. Checking free T4 will assess whether she remains euthyroid and guide the decision to either continue or adjust therapy (including switching to propylthiouracil in the first trimester per current guidelines). \r\n\r\nAnswer: \\(\\boxed{\\text{Measure serum free thyroxine (FT4) level.}}\\)", "correct": false, "gold": "Discontinue methimazole, start propylthiouracil"}
{"qid": "med:277", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Answer: Schizophrenia**\n\nKey features supporting this diagnosis:\n- **Duration**: Symptoms have been present for 7 months (>6 months required for schizophrenia diagnosis)\n- **Delusions**: Paranoid/persecutory delusions (aliens watching him, stealing his thoughts) \u2014 this is also a form of \"thought withdrawal,\" a first-rank symptom of schizophrenia\n- **Disorganized thinking**: Impossible to follow his sequence of thoughts (formal thought disorder)\n- **Negative symptoms**: Social withdrawal, flat affect\n- **Functional decline**: Withdrawal from family/friends, inability to leave the house\n- **Exclusion of substance-induced psychosis**: He quit marijuana use 1 year ago, and symptoms began 7 months ago and have persisted \u2014 timeline doesn't fit substance-induced psychotic disorder\n- **No alcohol use**, normal vital signs, and unremarkable physical exam make organic/toxic causes less likely\n\nThe combination of positive symptoms (delusions, disorganized thought), negative symptoms (flat affect, social withdrawal), and functional decline lasting more than 6 months in a young adult male (typical age of onset) is classic for schizophrenia.\n\nAnswer: Schizophrenia", "correct": true, "gold": "Schizophrenia"}
{"qid": "med:277", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient presents with a 7-month history of social withdrawal, delusions (aliens watching him, stealing his thoughts), disorganized thinking (impossible to follow his thoughts), and flat affect. These symptoms are consistent with a psychotic disorder. The duration of over 6 months points toward schizophrenia rather than a brief psychotic disorder or schizophreniform disorder. The absence of mood symptoms rules out a mood disorder with psychotic features. Substance-induced psychosis is less likely given he quit marijuana a year ago and has no other substance use.\n\n**Answer:** Schizophrenia", "correct": true, "gold": "Schizophrenia"}
{"qid": "med:277", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Schizophrenia.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "Schizophrenia"}
{"qid": "med:277", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation includes a clear functional decline with progressive social withdrawal over 7 months, plus active-phase psychotic symptoms (delusions of alien surveillance and thought withdrawal, plus disorganized speech evidenced by loose associations) and negative symptoms (flat affect). These meet DSM-5 criteria for schizophrenia: two or more characteristic symptoms present for a sufficient duration, continuous signs of illness for >6 months (including prodromal features), and exclusion of substance-induced or mood-related psychoses. Differentials such as schizophreniform disorder (duration 1\u20136 months), brief psychotic disorder (<1 month), schizotypal personality disorder (no frank delusions or disorganization), or drug-induced psychosis (remote marijuana use, ongoing symptoms) are thereby ruled out.\nAnswer: schizophrenia", "correct": true, "gold": "Schizophrenia"}
{"qid": "med:277", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at this clinical vignette, I need to identify the key features:\n\n**Key findings:**\n- **Duration**: 7 months of symptoms (exceeds the 6-month threshold)\n- **Positive symptoms**: Bizarre delusions (aliens watching him, \"stealing his thoughts\" - indicating thought withdrawal/insertion), disorganized speech (\"impossible to follow the patient's sequence of thoughts\")\n- **Negative symptoms**: Flat affect, social withdrawal, loss of interest in relationships\n- **Functional decline**: Unable to leave house, complete isolation from friends/family (change from baseline)\n- **Age**: 23-year-old male (typical onset age for schizophrenia)\n- **No substance cause**: Quit marijuana 1 year ago, so current symptoms are not acute intoxication/withdrawal\n- **No mood symptoms**: No mania or depression described to suggest schizoaffective disorder\n\n**Analysis of options:**\n- **Schizophrenia**: Fits perfectly - >6 months duration, active phase symptoms (delusions, disorganized speech), negative symptoms (flat affect, withdrawal), and significant functional impairment.\n- **Schizophreniform disorder**: Requires 1-6 months duration; this is 7 months.\n- **Delusional disorder**: Features non-bizarre delusions with relatively preserved functioning; this patient has bizarre delusions and prominent negative/disorganized symptoms.\n- **Schizoaffective disorder**: Requires a major mood episode concurrent with psychotic symptoms; no mood symptoms are described here.\n- **Substance-induced**: Unlikely given 1 year of abstinence from marijuana and 7-month symptom duration.\n\nThe presence of first-rank symptoms (thought interference), bizarre delusions, disorganized thinking, negative symptoms, and duration >6 months in a young adult is classic for schizophrenia.\n\nAnswer: Schizophrenia", "correct": true, "gold": "Schizophrenia"}
{"qid": "med:271", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\nThis patient meets the criteria for chronic insomnia disorder (difficulty initiating sleep \u22653 nights/week for \u22653 months with significant daytime impairment). He has already attempted appropriate sleep hygiene measures (avoiding screens at night, exercising earlier, and trying OTC melatonin) without success. In young, otherwise healthy individuals, the first-line treatment for chronic insomnia is **cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I)**. CBT-I addresses maladaptive behaviors (irregular bedtimes, long afternoon naps, caffeine use) and unhelpful thoughts about sleep, and it has long-lasting efficacy without the risks associated with hypnotic medications. \r\n\r\nAnswer: **Referral for cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I).**", "correct": false, "gold": "Stop evening exercise"}
{"qid": "med:267", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient presents with classic features of **Hoarding Disorder**: persistent difficulty discarding possessions regardless of value, accumulation of clutter that compromises living spaces, significant distress and functional impairment, and onset during adolescence. While cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) is first-line treatment, she has had no improvement after a 6-month trial.\n\nWhen psychotherapy fails or is insufficiently effective, the next step is **pharmacotherapy with a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI)**. Although hoarding disorder may be less responsive to SSRIs than obsessive-compulsive disorder, serotonergic agents (such as sertraline, fluoxetine, or paroxetine) are the recommended pharmacologic intervention for moderate-to-severe or treatment-resistant cases. The patient\u2019s depressed mood further supports the use of an SSRI, which can address both the hoarding symptoms and comorbid depression.\n\nOther options are less appropriate: continuing CBT alone is unlikely to help given the failed trial; antipsychotics are not indicated without psychotic symptoms; and hospitalization is unnecessary as she is stable and functional.\n\nAnswer: Start an SSRI (e.g., sertraline, fluoxetine, or paroxetine).", "correct": true, "gold": "Fluoxetine"}
{"qid": "med:278", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient has **ovarian torsion**: sudden severe unilateral pelvic/abdominal pain with nausea/vomiting, adnexal mass/tenderness, and ultrasound showing ovarian edema/free fluid. **Presence of Doppler flow does not rule out torsion** because the ovary has dual blood supply. Persistent symptoms despite analgesics also support urgent intervention.\n\nThe best next step is **urgent diagnostic laparoscopy with detorsion** (and ovarian cystectomy if a cyst is present), with attempts to **preserve the ovary** if viable.\n\nAnswer: Urgent laparoscopic surgical exploration with ovarian detorsion (despite preserved Doppler flow)\ufffc", "correct": false, "gold": "Laparoscopy"}
{"qid": "med:272", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at this case, the patient has a **bilateral, high-frequency action tremor** (worse with fine motor tasks like brushing teeth and finger-to-nose testing) that has been present for several years with an otherwise normal neurological exam. This pattern is classic for **essential tremor**, not Parkinson disease (which typically presents with a unilateral, low-frequency resting tremor and other signs like rigidity or bradykinesia). The family history and the fact that the tremor improves over the day (likely due to self-medication with alcohol, as alcohol often transiently improves essential tremor) further support this diagnosis.\n\nFirst-line medications for essential tremor are **propranolol** (a non-selective beta-blocker) or primidone. Given that this patient also has **hypertension** (BP 159/84) and is not currently on antihypertensive therapy, propranolol is the ideal choice as it treats both conditions. Propranolol is the most commonly used initial therapy for essential tremor.\n\nAnswer: Propranolol", "correct": true, "gold": "Propranolol"}
{"qid": "med:278", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The presentation of sudden severe abdominal pain with nausea and vomiting, along with an adnexal mass and tenderness, suggests ovarian torsion. The ultrasound findings of free fluid, ovarian edema, and preserved Doppler flow do not rule out torsion, as flow can still be present. The negative pregnancy test and lack of response to analgesics further support this. The best next step is surgical intervention, typically laparoscopic detorsion.\n\nAnswer: Laparoscopic detorsion.", "correct": false, "gold": "Laparoscopy"}
{"qid": "med:272", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "The patient's presentation is most consistent with **essential tremor**. Key features supporting this diagnosis include a bilateral, high-frequency action tremor that worsens with fine motor tasks (e.g., brushing teeth, preparing coffee), improves throughout the day, and the absence of parkinsonian features (such as rest tremor, rigidity, or bradykinesia). \r\n\r\nThe first-line treatment for essential tremor is a nonselective beta-blocker, such as **propranolol**. \r\n\r\nAnswer: Propranolol", "correct": true, "gold": "Propranolol"}
{"qid": "med:278", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Best next step: Emergent laparoscopy (surgical exploration) for detorsion/ovarian sparing surgery**\n\n**Reasoning:** This patient's presentation is classic for ovarian torsion:\n- Sudden onset severe unilateral pelvic/abdominal pain\n- Nausea/vomiting\n- Adnexal mass with tenderness\n- Ultrasound showing free fluid, ovarian edema, and adnexal mass\n\nImportantly, the presence of Doppler flow does **not** rule out torsion \u2014 it is a common misconception. Intermittent or partial torsion can still show flow due to the dual blood supply of the ovary (ovarian and uterine arteries) or because torsion is intermittent. Given her clinical presentation strongly suggests torsion, and her pain persists despite analgesics, imaging findings should not delay definitive management, as delayed treatment risks ovarian necrosis and loss of the ovary.\n\nThe gold standard next step is **emergent gynecologic surgical consultation for laparoscopy**, allowing for:\n- Direct visualization of the ovary\n- Detorsion if viable\n- Ovarian-sparing surgery when possible (especially important given her age and fertility considerations)\n\nDelaying surgery for further imaging or reliance on Doppler findings alone can result in unnecessary loss of ovarian tissue.\n\nAnswer: Emergent laparoscopy for ovarian detorsion (surgical exploration)", "correct": true, "gold": "Laparoscopy"}
{"qid": "med:278", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Immediate surgical evaluation for suspected ovarian torsion with urgent laparoscopic detorsion (or oophorectomy if necrosis is found).<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Laparoscopy"}
{"qid": "med:278", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation of sudden-onset severe pelvic pain with nausea/vomiting, adnexal tenderness/mass, and sonographic ovarian edema plus free fluid is highly suggestive of ovarian torsion, even though arterial Doppler signals are still detectable (Doppler flow has imperfect sensitivity and does not exclude intermittent or partial torsion). Negative pregnancy testing removes ectopic pregnancy from the differential, and the absence of peritoneal signs makes appendicitis or other acute abdominal processes unlikely. Because the patient remains tachycardic and symptomatic after simple analgesics, the definitive next step is prompt surgical evaluation rather than further imaging, additional analgesics, or expectant management.\nAnswer: emergent diagnostic laparoscopy (gynecology)", "correct": true, "gold": "Laparoscopy"}
{"qid": "med:280", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient has **small bowel obstruction** with **pneumobilia** (branching radiolucencies in the right subdiaphragmatic region = air in the biliary tree). In an older woman with recurrent episodes of upper abdominal pain, this is classic for **gallstone ileus**.\n\nGallstone ileus occurs when repeated cholecystitis causes a **cholecystoenteric fistula** (usually **cholecystoduodenal**), allowing a large gallstone to enter the bowel and obstruct it, often at the ileum.\n\nAnswer: Small bowel obstruction from a gallstone that entered through a cholecystoenteric (usually cholecystoduodenal) fistula due to recurrent cholecystitis.", "correct": false, "gold": "Inflammation of the gallbladder wall"}
{"qid": "med:280", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient presents with signs of small bowel obstruction (abdominal pain, vomiting, obstipation, distended abdomen, high-pitched bowel sounds, dilated bowel with air-fluid levels). The branching radiolucencies in the right infra-diaphragmatic region are key\u2014this describes air in the biliary tree (pneumobilia), which is not normal. Pneumobilia combined with small bowel obstruction strongly suggests gallstone ileus, where a gallstone erodes into the bowel (usually duodenum) via a cholecystenteric fistula and becomes impacted in the distal ileum. Her risk factors (older age, female, smoking, prior episodes of upper abdominal pain) support gallstone disease.\n\nAnswer: Gallstone ileus", "correct": false, "gold": "Inflammation of the gallbladder wall"}
{"qid": "med:278", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at this case, I need to identify the diagnosis and determine the appropriate management.\n\n**Key clinical features:**\n- 25-year-old woman with sudden, severe unilateral left adnexal pain onset during physical activity (walking up stairs)\n- Associated nausea and vomiting\n- Tachycardia (HR 120/min)\n- Physical exam: Left adnexal mass and tenderness without diffuse abdominal tenderness\n- Transvaginal ultrasound: Free fluid, ovarian edema, but **presence of Doppler flow**\n- Negative pregnancy test (rules out ectopic pregnancy)\n- Persistent symptoms despite analgesics\n\n**Diagnosis: Ovarian torsion**\n\nThis presentation is classic for ovarian torsion: sudden onset unilateral pain with nausea/vomiting in a patient with an adnexal mass. The \"absence of abdominal tenderness\" with isolated adnexal findings localizes the pathology to the adnexa.\n\n**Critical point about the ultrasound:** The presence of Doppler flow does NOT exclude ovarian torsion. This is a common distractor. Because the ovary has dual blood supply (ovarian and uterine arteries) and torsion may be intermittent or partial, arterial flow may still be detectable in 50-70% of torsion cases. Venous flow is typically compromised first. The key ultrasound findings here are free fluid and ovarian edema in the setting of classic symptoms.\n\n**Why this is urgent:** Ovarian torsion is a surgical emergency. The ovary becomes ischemic, and without prompt intervention, it can progress to necrosis and infarction. Time is critical for ovarian salvage.\n\n**Management:** Since the patient has persistent symptoms, an adnexal mass, and classic findings despite negative pregnancy test, the next step is **emergent diagnostic laparoscopy** with detorsion of the ovary. This is both diagnostic and therapeutic. Further imaging (CT/MRI) or observation would delay definitive treatment and risk ovarian loss.\n\nAnswer: Laparoscopy (surgical exploration and detorsion)", "correct": true, "gold": "Laparoscopy"}
{"qid": "med:280", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Diagnosis: Gallstone ileus**\n\nThe clinical picture and imaging findings point to gallstone ileus as the cause of this patient's small bowel obstruction:\n\n- **History of biliary colic**: Multiple episodes of upper abdominal pain over the past year suggest chronic cholelithiasis/cholecystitis.\n- **Branching radiolucencies in the right infra-diaphragmatic region**: This represents **pneumobilia** (air in the biliary tree), which occurs when a gallstone erodes through the inflamed gallbladder wall into the adjacent duodenum, creating a **cholecystoenteric (cholecystoduodenal) fistula**. This fistula allows air to reflux from the bowel into the biliary tree.\n- **Signs of small bowel obstruction**: Distended abdomen, high-pitched bowel sounds, air-fluid levels, and dilated bowel loops occur when the gallstone migrates through the GI tract and becomes lodged\u2014most commonly at the **ileocecal valve** (the narrowest part of the small intestine)\u2014causing mechanical obstruction.\n\n**Pathophysiology summary:**\n1. Chronic cholelithiasis \u2192 gallbladder wall inflammation and erosion\n2. Formation of a cholecystoduodenal fistula\n3. Passage of a large gallstone into the small bowel lumen\n4. Impaction of the stone (typically at the ileocecal valve) \u2192 mechanical bowel obstruction\n5. Air enters the biliary tree via the fistula \u2192 pneumobilia (seen as branching radiolucencies on x-ray)\n\nThis classic triad\u2014small bowel obstruction, pneumobilia, and an ectopic gallstone\u2014is known as **Rigler's triad**, and is highly suggestive of gallstone ileus, even though the actual stone is not always visualized on plain films.\n\nAnswer: Gallstone ileus due to a cholecystoduodenal fistula, with the gallstone impacted in the small bowel (commonly at the ileocecal valve), causing mechanical obstruction \u2014 evidenced by pneumobilia (branching radiolucencies) and signs of bowel obstruction.\n\nAnswer: Gallstone ileus (mechanical small bowel obstruction caused by a gallstone that entered the intestine via a cholecystoduodenal fistula, with pneumobilia seen as branching radiolucencies in the right upper quadrant).", "correct": false, "gold": "Inflammation of the gallbladder wall"}
{"qid": "med:280", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation of acute small-bowel obstruction (distention, high-pitched bowel sounds, dilated loops with air-fluid levels) in an elderly woman with prior episodes of biliary colic immediately raises the possibility of gallstone ileus. The key radiographic clue\u2014central, branching lucencies beneath the right hemidiaphragm\u2014represents pneumobilia and completes Rigler\u2019s triad, confirming that a gallstone has entered the intestinal lumen through a cholecysto-enteric fistula (most often cholecystoduodenal). Once inside the gut the stone migrates until it lodges in the narrowest segment (usually distal ileum), producing mechanical obstruction. No other common cause of SBO (adhesions, hernia, malignancy) produces pneumobilia; therefore the fistula and subsequent intraluminal gallstone constitute the proximate cause of the patient\u2019s acute illness.\nAnswer: cholecystoenteric fistula \u2192 gallstone ileus (impacted intraluminal gallstone)", "correct": false, "gold": "Inflammation of the gallbladder wall"}
{"qid": "med:287", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Fever and chills **2 hours after surgery** are most consistent with an **acute hemolytic transfusion reaction** from the unit of packed RBCs, especially given prior sensitization from her **hysterectomy/pregnancy history** possibility and the very rapid onset after transfusion. Postop wound infection, catheter infection, or drug fever would be less likely this soon.\n\nAnswer: Acute hemolytic transfusion reaction due to ABO incompatibility from the transfused packed red blood cells.", "correct": true, "gold": "Transfusion reaction"}
{"qid": "med:287", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient developed fever and chills within two hours of surgery, which is too early for a surgical site infection or pneumonia. Given she received a blood transfusion during the procedure, the most likely cause is an acute hemolytic transfusion reaction or a febrile non-hemolytic transfusion reaction. The rapid onset of fever and chills points toward a transfusion reaction rather than infection.\n\nAnswer: Acute hemolytic transfusion reaction (or febrile non-hemolytic transfusion reaction).", "correct": true, "gold": "Transfusion reaction"}
{"qid": "med:280", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Acute mesenteric ischemia (e.g., superior mesenteric artery thrombosis/embolism) leading to bowel necrosis.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Inflammation of the gallbladder wall"}
{"qid": "med:280", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient presents with signs of small bowel obstruction (SBO): vomiting, obstipation, abdominal distension, and air\u2011fluid levels on X\u2011ray. The critical clue is the \u201cbranching radiolucencies in the right infra\u2011diaphragmatic region,\u201d which represents pneumobilia (air in the biliary tree). In an elderly woman with a history of upper abdominal pain (suggesting chronic cholelithiasis) and no prior abdominal surgery, the triad of SBO, pneumobilia, and a history of biliary symptoms is classic for gallstone ileus\u2014obstruction caused by a gallstone that has entered the intestinal lumen via a cholecystoenteric fistula.\n\nAnswer: Gallstone ileus.", "correct": false, "gold": "Inflammation of the gallbladder wall"}
{"qid": "med:287", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Most likely cause: Febrile non-hemolytic transfusion reaction (FNHTR)**\n\n**Reasoning:**\n- Timing is key: fever/chills occurred only **2 hours after surgery**, which is too early for surgical site infection, UTI, or line infection (these typically manifest 48\u201372+ hours postoperatively, following the classic \"5 W's\" timeline of postoperative fever).\n- The patient received a **unit of packed RBCs** during the procedure. FNHTR is the most common transfusion reaction, occurring in up to 1\u20133% of transfusions, and is caused by **cytokines released from donor leukocytes** during storage, or recipient antibodies reacting to donor leukocyte antigens.\n- Symptoms classically include **fever, chills, and rigors** occurring **during or within 1\u20136 hours** of transfusion, without hemolysis, hypotension, or respiratory distress \u2014 consistent with this patient's presentation.\n- Her surgical wound is only mildly tender without erythema or discharge, arguing against wound infection.\n- No signs of hemolysis (no hypotension, back pain, hemoglobinuria) argue against acute hemolytic transfusion reaction.\n- No respiratory symptoms (clear lungs, normal respiratory rate) argue against TRALI (transfusion-related acute lung injury) or TACO (transfusion-associated circulatory overload).\n- Atelectasis (a common cause of early postoperative fever) does not typically cause chills, and her lungs are clear.\n\n**Answer:** Febrile non-hemolytic transfusion reaction due to the intraoperative PRBC transfusion.\n\nAnswer: Febrile non-hemolytic transfusion reaction from the blood transfusion", "correct": true, "gold": "Transfusion reaction"}
{"qid": "med:277", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\nThis 23-year-old man presents with key features of schizophrenia:  \r\n\r\n- **Duration:** Symptoms have been present for 7 months, exceeding the 1-month minimum for schizophreniform disorder and meeting the >6-month criterion for schizophrenia.\r\n- **Positive symptoms:** Delusions of persecution and control (aliens are watching him and \"stealing his thoughts\"), which is consistent with thought withdrawal.\r\n- **Disorganized thinking:** His thought process is incoherent, making it impossible to follow.\r\n- **Negative symptoms:** Social withdrawal, loss of interest in friends/family, and a flat affect.\r\n- **Functional decline:** A clear change from his premorbid baseline (previously social at least once a week).\r\n- **Exclusion:** No recent substance use (marijuana quit 1 year ago), no alcohol, and no medical abnormalities to explain his symptoms. \r\n\r\nThese findings align most closely with schizophrenia. \r\n\r\nAnswer: **Schizophrenia**", "correct": true, "gold": "Schizophrenia"}
{"qid": "med:288", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient has severe **hypothyroidism with altered mental status, hypothermia, bradycardia, hypotension, hypoxemia, hyponatremia, nonpitting edema (myxedema), and very high TSH with low thyroid hormone levels**. In an elderly patient presenting obtunded/collapsed, this is classic for **myxedema coma**.\n\nAnswer: Myxedema coma", "correct": true, "gold": "Myxedema coma"}
{"qid": "med:288", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Answer: Myxedema coma (severe primary hypothyroidism)", "correct": true, "gold": "Myxedema coma"}
{"qid": "med:278", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "The patient's presentation is highly suggestive of **ovarian torsion**, given the sudden onset of severe abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting, left adnexal mass and tenderness, and transvaginal ultrasound findings of free fluid, ovarian edema, and preserved Doppler flow. While Doppler flow does not rule out torsion, this is a surgical emergency, and prompt intervention is necessary to preserve ovarian viability. \r\n\r\n**Answer: Emergent laparoscopy.**", "correct": true, "gold": "Laparoscopy"}
{"qid": "med:287", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient developed fever and chills within a few hours after surgery, but vital signs are otherwise stable (no hypotension or marked tachycardia) and physical examination shows clear lungs with a benign-appearing wound. Among the many potential sources of postoperative fever, the temporal relationship to intraoperative transfusion of a single unit of PRBCs is the key finding.\n- Wound infection, catheter-associated UTI, and pneumonia/atelectasis all require hours to days to produce systemic symptoms and are inconsistent with the normal examination findings and the extremely short time interval.\n- Acute hemolytic transfusion reaction or bacterial contamination of the unit would typically produce hemodynamic instability, hemolysis, or overt sepsis; these are absent here.\n- TRALI produces prominent respiratory distress and pulmonary infiltrates, which are not present.\nThis leaves febrile non-hemolytic transfusion reaction (FNHTR), a common, cytokine- or antibody-mediated response to donor leukocytes or plasma components that produces isolated fever and rigors while leaving the patient otherwise stable. Prior surgery (hysterectomy) may have sensitized the patient to leukocyte antigens, further supporting this mechanism.\nAnswer: febrile nonhemolytic transfusion rxn (anti-leukocyte Abs)", "correct": false, "gold": "Transfusion reaction"}
{"qid": "med:288", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at this clinical presentation, I need to identify the unifying diagnosis that explains all findings:\n\n**Key Clinical Features:**\n- **Severe hypothermia** (33\u00b0C/91.4\u00b0F) with bradycardia (40/min), hypotension (80/50), and bradypnea\n- **Altered mental status** progressing to coma (GCS ~7: eyes to pain, incomprehensible speech, decorticate posturing)\n- **Physical signs of hypothyroidism**: dry hair, coarse/waxy skin, and non-pitting edema (myxedema) of face/extremities\n- **Hyponatremia** (Na 120)\n- **Thyroid function**: Markedly elevated TSH (110 \u00b5U/mL) with low T4 (1.2 \u00b5g/dL) and low T3 (70 ng/dL)\n\n**Analysis:**\nThe laboratory studies confirm **severe primary hypothyroidism** (high TSH, low thyroid hormones). The combination of:\n1. Altered mental status/coma\n2. Profound hypothermia (pathognomonic)\n3. Hypotension and bradycardia\n4. Myxedema (non-pitting edema with skin changes)\n5. Hyponatremia\n\nThis constellation represents the life-threatening end-stage complication of untreated hypothyroidism known as **myxedema coma**. The patient was found wandering outdoors (cold exposure), which is a common precipitating factor.\n\nWhile sepsis or adrenal crisis could cause hypotension and altered mental status, they would not explain the severe bradycardia, hypothermia, myxedema, or the characteristic thyroid function abnormalities. The TSH of 110 with low T4 definitively establishes severe hypothyroidism as the cause.\n\nAnswer: Myxedema coma", "correct": true, "gold": "Myxedema coma"}
{"qid": "med:288", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient presents with severe hypothermia, bradycardia, hypotension, altered mental status, and physical findings of dry hair, coarse/waxy skin, and non-pitting edema. Labs show markedly elevated TSH with low T4 and T3, confirming primary hypothyroidism. The constellation of myxedema coma is most likely.\n\nAnswer: Myxedema coma", "correct": true, "gold": "Myxedema coma"}
{"qid": "med:280", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\nThis elderly woman presents with classic features of small bowel obstruction (SBO) \u2014 intermittent abdominal pain, vomiting, obstipation, abdominal distension, and high-pitched bowel sounds. The key radiographic finding is **branching radiolucencies in the right infra-diaphragmatic region**, which indicates **pneumobilia** (air within the biliary tree). Combined with a history of recurrent upper abdominal pain over the past year (suggestive of chronic cholecystitis or biliary colic), this is highly characteristic of **gallstone ileus**. Gallstone ileus occurs when a large gallstone erodes through the gallbladder wall, forming a cholecystoenteric fistula, and subsequently lodges in the small intestine (most commonly the terminal ileum), causing mechanical obstruction. Rigler's triad (pneumobilia, SBO, and an ectopic gallstone) is classically seen, though the stone may not always be radiopaque.\r\n\r\nAnswer: Gallstone ileus", "correct": false, "gold": "Inflammation of the gallbladder wall"}
{"qid": "med:287", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient\u2019s fever and chills occurring within a few hours of surgery are most consistent with a reaction to the blood product she received. Febrile non\u2011hemolytic transfusion reaction (FNHTR) is the most common transfusion complication and typically presents with fever and chills 1\u20136\u202fhours after transfusion, without hemodynamic instability, hemolysis, or respiratory distress. Other immediate postoperative causes (atelectasis, malignant hyperthermia, drug allergy, surgical site infection, UTI) are less likely given the timing, the absence of localizing findings, and the stable vital signs.\n\nAnswer: Febrile non\u2011hemolytic transfusion reaction.", "correct": true, "gold": "Transfusion reaction"}
{"qid": "med:288", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The patient\u2019s presentation of severe hypothermia (\u2264\u202f33\u202f\u00b0C), bradycardia, hypotension, altered mental status, dry waxy skin, and non\u2011pitting edema, together with laboratory evidence of primary hypothyroidism (markedly elevated TSH, low free T4, low T3) strongly point to myxedema coma\u2014a life\u2011threatening decompensation of severe hypothyroidism.\n\nAnswer: Myxedema coma<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "Myxedema coma"}
{"qid": "med:287", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\nThis patient's fever and chills developed just 2 hours after surgery, which is too early for most postoperative infectious causes (e.g., wound infection, pneumonia, or UTI). Key findings include:  \r\n\r\n- **Timing:** Symptoms occurred within 1\u20136 hours of receiving a unit of packed red blood cells during the procedure, which is classic for a febrile nonhemolytic transfusion reaction (FNHTR).\r\n- **Clinical presentation:** High fever (39.5\u00b0C) and chills without signs of hemolysis (stable BP, no jaundice, no hemoglobinuria), infection (wound without erythema, clear lungs, no catheter site infection), or severe allergic reaction.\r\n- **Exclusion of other causes:** Atelectasis can cause early fever but is typically milder and not associated with chills; drug fever from cephalexin is less abrupt and less common. \r\n\r\nFNHTR is caused by recipient antibodies against donor leukocyte antigens, leading to cytokine release. It is the most common transfusion reaction. \r\n\r\nAnswer: **Febrile nonhemolytic transfusion reaction (FNHTR).**", "correct": true, "gold": "Transfusion reaction"}
{"qid": "med:288", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation includes profound hypothermia, bradycardia, hypotension, hyponatremia, depressed consciousness with decorticate posturing, and non-pitting myxedematous edema. These features, together with markedly elevated TSH and depressed free T4, establish decompensated primary hypothyroidism. The syndrome is termed myxedema coma when life-threatening thermoregulatory and mental-status failure supervene; no other endocrine or metabolic emergency reproduces this exact constellation of physical and laboratory findings.\nAnswer: myxedema coma (severe hypothyroidism)", "correct": true, "gold": "Myxedema coma"}
{"qid": "med:287", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Reasoning: The patient\u2019s fever and chills began within two hours after surgery, shortly after receiving a packed red blood cell transfusion. The surgical wound shows no signs of infection, the lungs are clear, and there are no urinary symptoms despite the Foley catheter. These findings are most consistent with a febrile non\u2011hemolytic transfusion reaction (FNHTR), which commonly presents with fever and chills within hours of transfusion and lacks hypotension or hemolysis.\n\nAnswer: Febrile non\u2011hemolytic transfusion reaction (FNHTR) due to the intraoperative RBC transfusion.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "Transfusion reaction"}
{"qid": "med:289", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient most likely has **acute pancreatitis**: severe epigastric pain radiating to the back, nausea, decreased bowel sounds, elevated amylase, hypocalcemia, hemoconcentration, and hypotension.\n\nHer **hypotension with warm extremities and tachycardia** is most consistent with **distributive/hypovolemic shock due to acute pancreatitis causing a systemic inflammatory response**. In pancreatitis, pancreatic enzymes and inflammatory mediators increase vascular permeability, leading to **massive third-spacing of fluid into the retroperitoneum and peritoneal cavity**. This causes **intravascular volume depletion**, reflected by her elevated hematocrit and BUN.\n\nSo the underlying cause of the vital sign abnormalities is:\n\n**loss of intravascular volume from increased vascular permeability and third spacing due to acute pancreatitis.**\n\nAnswer: Intravascular volume depletion from pancreatic inflammation causing capillary leak and third-spacing of fluid (acute pancreatitis\u2013induced shock).", "correct": false, "gold": "Capillary leakage"}
{"qid": "med:289", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient presents with severe epigastric pain radiating to the back, nausea, hypotension, tachycardia, and elevated amylase, consistent with acute pancreatitis. The low serum calcium (8.0 mg/dL) and elevated BUN suggest significant volume loss and possible saponification (calcium binding in necrotic fat). Her vital sign abnormalities (hypotension, tachycardia) are most likely due to **hypovolemia** from third-spacing of fluids into the retroperitoneum and peritoneal cavity, a hallmark of severe pancreatitis. Other causes like sepsis or cardiac issues are less likely given warm extremities and clear lungs.\n\nAnswer: Hypovolemia due to third-spacing of fluids in acute pancreatitis.", "correct": false, "gold": "Capillary leakage"}
