{"qid": "med:317", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This 15-year-old has **secondary amenorrhea** after previously irregular cycles soon after menarche. Key clues:\n\n- Menarche only **1 year ago**\n- Cycles were **irregular from the start (15\u201345 days)**\n- **Progesterone is low**, suggesting she has **not ovulated**\n- **Prolactin is normal**, so risperidone-induced hyperprolactinemia is unlikely\n- **TSH is normal**\n- **FSH/LH are normal**\n- **Testosterone is normal**, making PCOS less likely\n- Pregnancy test is negative\n\nIn adolescents during the first 1\u20132 years after menarche, the most common cause of irregular menses or temporary amenorrhea is **immaturity of the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis**, leading to **anovulatory cycles**.\n\nAnswer: Immaturity of the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis causing physiologic adolescent anovulation.", "correct": true, "gold": "Anovulation"}
{"qid": "med:312", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient\u2019s presentation (acute fever, nausea, vomiting, photophobia, nuchal rigidity) is classic for meningitis. The CSF findings\u2014moderate pleocytosis (175 cells/mm\u00b3), normal glucose (\u224849\u202fmg/dL), mildly elevated protein (55\u202fmg/dL), and normal opening pressure\u2014are most consistent with **viral meningitis** (bacterial meningitis would typically show a very high neutrophil count, markedly low glucose [<40\u202fmg/dL], and markedly elevated protein).\n\nViral meningitis is usually self\u2011limited and managed with supportive measures. There is no specific antiviral therapy for the most common enteroviral causes; treatment focuses on symptom control and hydration.\n\n**Answer:** Supportive care (analgesics, antiemetics, IV fluids, and rest).", "correct": false, "gold": "IV fluids and monitoring"}
{"qid": "med:316", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at this clinical scenario, I need to identify the infectious agent causing fetal microcephaly based on the maternal history and exposures.\n\n**Key clinical features:**\n- 32-week fetus with microcephaly\n- Maternal illness in early 1st trimester (critical period for fetal brain development)\n- Symptoms: fever, headache, myalgias, arthralgias, and pruritic maculopapular rash\n- Epidemiological exposure: Travel to Brazil with outdoor evening activity and no mosquito repellent\n- Negative toxoplasmosis serology\n\n**Analysis:**\nThe combination of congenital microcephaly with maternal first-trimester febrile illness featuring a maculopapular rash and arthralgias in a traveler to Brazil strongly suggests a mosquito-borne infection. \n\n**Differential considerations:**\n- **Toxoplasmosis**: Ruled out by negative IgM/IgG titers\n- **CMV**: Can cause microcephaly, but maternal symptoms are usually asymptomatic or mononucleosis-like without the characteristic pruritic rash and arthralgias\n- **Rubella**: Patient was vaccinated; rash is typically non-pruritic\n- **Dengue/Chikungunya**: Endemic to Brazil but less specifically associated with microcephaly as a hallmark finding\n- **Zika virus**: Fits perfectly - causes mild febrile illness with pruritic maculopapular rash, arthralgias, and myalgias; transmitted by Aedes mosquitoes (active in evenings); Brazil was the epicenter of the Zika epidemic; first-trimester infection is strongly associated with congenital microcephaly and other neurological abnormalities\n\nThe travel history to Brazil, the specific symptom constellation (fever + pruritic maculopapular rash + arthralgias), the timing of infection in early pregnancy, and the finding of fetal microcephaly form the classic presentation of congenital Zika syndrome.\n\nAnswer: Zika virus", "correct": true, "gold": "Zika virus"}
{"qid": "med:317", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has secondary amenorrhea with irregular cycles since menarche, obesity (BMI 27), and a family history of PCOS. Her labs show normal prolactin, TSH, FSH, LH, and testosterone, with low progesterone (consistent with anovulation). The negative pregnancy test and normal pelvic exam rule out pregnancy and structural issues. Given her irregular cycles since menarche, obesity, and family history, the most likely cause is anovulation due to polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS), even though her testosterone is not elevated\u2014PCOS can present without hyperandrogenemia by lab criteria.\n\nAnswer: Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS)", "correct": false, "gold": "Anovulation"}
{"qid": "med:318", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Image-guided percutaneous aspiration of the liver lesion, with Gram stain/culture (drainage) plus IV antibiotics \u2014 i.e., percutaneous drainage of the hepatic abscess.\n\nAnswer: Ultrasound-guided percutaneous drainage (aspiration) of the liver abscess with antibiotics", "correct": false, "gold": "Perform percutaneous drainage\n\""}
{"qid": "med:318", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient has **fever, leukocytosis, tender hepatomegaly, right-sided abdominal pain, and decreased breath sounds at the right base**, which is classic for a **liver abscess**, especially a **pyogenic liver abscess**. Her diabetes is a risk factor. Lack of travel makes amebic abscess less likely.\n\nThe most appropriate next step is **drainage plus antibiotics**. Pyogenic liver abscesses are treated with **broad-spectrum IV antibiotics** covering enteric gram-negatives and anaerobes, along with **percutaneous drainage**.\n\nAnswer: **Begin broad-spectrum IV antibiotics and perform image-guided percutaneous drainage of a pyogenic liver abscess.**", "correct": false, "gold": "Perform percutaneous drainage\n\""}
