{"qid": "med:316", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\nThis patient presents with fetal microcephaly at 32 weeks gestation, along with a self-limited first-trimester illness characterized by fever, headache, myalgias, arthralgias, and a pruritic maculopapular rash. Her travel history to Brazil (an endemic area for Zika virus) one week prior to symptom onset, combined with evening hiking and lack of mosquito repellent, puts her at high risk for mosquito-borne infection. Zika virus is a flavivirus transmitted by *Aedes* mosquitoes and is classically associated with congenital Zika syndrome, which includes microcephaly, particularly when infection occurs in the first or early second trimester. Negative toxoplasmosis IgM/IgG serologies help rule out that alternative etiology. \r\n\r\nAnswer: Zika virus", "correct": true, "gold": "Zika virus"}
