{"qid": "med:317", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Most likely cause: Physiologic anovulation due to immaturity of the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian (HPO) axis (adolescent anovulation)**\n\n**Reasoning:**\n\n- **Menstrual history is key:** Since menarche (only 1 year ago), she has had irregular cycles (15\u201345 days) with variable flow \u2014 this is the *typical pattern* of anovulatory cycles seen in the first 1\u20132 years after menarche, when the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis has not yet fully matured to support consistent ovulation.\n\n- **Hormone studies support anovulation, not another specific pathology:**\n - **Progesterone 0.8 ng/mL** \u2014 low, consistent with an anovulatory (non-luteal) cycle, since ovulation and corpus luteum formation (which produce progesterone) have not occurred.\n - **FSH and LH are within normal, prepubertal-appropriate ranges** with a normal LH:FSH ratio (~1), arguing against PCOS (which typically shows LH:FSH ratio >2\u20133).\n - **Testosterone is normal (2.2 nmol/L, <3.5)** \u2014 against PCOS-associated hyperandrogenism, despite her mother's history of PCOS.\n - **Prolactin is normal (16 ng/mL)** \u2014 argues against risperidone-induced hyperprolactinemia as the cause (though risperidone can cause hyperprolactinemia, it is not doing so here).\n - **TSH normal** \u2014 rules out thyroid dysfunction as a cause of menstrual irregularity.\n - **Pregnancy test negative** \u2014 rules out pregnancy as the cause of amenorrhea.\n\n- **Other distractors:**\n - Risperidone-induced hyperprolactinemia is excluded by normal prolactin.\n - PCOS, while a family history exists, is not supported biochemically (normal LH:FSH, normal testosterone), though her BMI and weight concerns overlap with PCOS risk factors \u2014 she may still be at risk for developing PCOS later, but it does not fit the current lab picture.\n - Pregnancy is ruled out by a negative urine test.\n\nThus, the clinical picture \u2014 recent menarche, persistent irregular cycles, normal hormone panel except low progesterone (indicating anovulation) \u2014 is most consistent with the normal, self-limited **physiologic anovulation of adolescence**, due to immaturity of the HPO axis, which can persist for 1\u20132 years after menarche before regular ovulatory cycles are established.\n\n---\n\n**Answer: Immaturity of the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis, causing physiologic anovulatory cycles of adolescence.**", "correct": false, "gold": "Anovulation"}
