{"qid": "med:318", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has fever, right upper quadrant pain, tender hepatomegaly, elevated liver enzymes (especially alkaline phosphatase), and leukocytosis. The ultrasound likely shows an abscess (e.g., pyogenic liver abscess). Given her diabetes and oral contraceptive use (possible biliary stasis), pyogenic abscess is most common. The next step is percutaneous drainage (or aspiration) for diagnosis and treatment, along with antibiotics.  \n\nAnswer: Percutaneous drainage of the liver abscess.", "correct": false, "gold": "Perform percutaneous drainage\n\""}
{"qid": "med:321", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This older man has **iron deficiency anemia** from **chronic gastrointestinal blood loss** (fatigue, pallor, low hemoglobin, **microcytosis**, intermittent hematochezia). In a 65-year-old with painless, intermittent lower GI bleeding and iron deficiency anemia, the most likely cause is **colonic angiodysplasia**.\n\nHis **end-stage renal disease** is a key clue because uremia causes **platelet dysfunction**, which increases bleeding from vascular malformations. Angiodysplasia consists of **dilated, tortuous submucosal vessels** in the colon, most often in the cecum/right colon, that can bleed intermittently.\n\nAnswer: Colonic angiodysplasia causing intermittent lower GI bleeding due to degenerative dilation of submucosal vessels (with ESRD-associated platelet dysfunction contributing)", "correct": false, "gold": "Arteriovenous malformation in the colonic wall"}
{"qid": "med:318", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation of fever, rigors, right-upper-quadrant pain, tender hepatomegaly, and basal atelectasis/pleural reaction in a diabetic patient points to a pyogenic liver abscess. Mild leukocytosis with neutrophilia and only modest transaminase/alkaline-phosphatase elevations further support an acute bacterial intrahepatic collection rather than viral hepatitis, cholangitis, or amebic disease (the last of which is unlikely given the absence of travel or risk factors). The already-performed right-upper-quadrant ultrasound is presumed to demonstrate one or more hypoechoic hepatic lesions consistent with abscess.\nBecause the patient is frankly septic, source control must be obtained promptly. For abscesses larger than 3 cm, the standard next management step is image-guided percutaneous needle aspiration or catheter drainage combined with broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics; blood cultures are drawn first but do not replace drainage. Surgical consultation or ERCP would be considered only if the abscess were multiloculated, ruptured, or associated with biliary obstruction\u2014none of which are suggested here.\nThus the single most appropriate next procedural step is CT- or ultrasound-guided percutaneous drainage.\nAnswer: CT-guided percutaneous drainage (pyogenic abscess)", "correct": false, "gold": "Perform percutaneous drainage\n\""}
{"qid": "med:321", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has microcytic anemia (MCV 68 \u03bcm\u00b3) with iron deficiency likely from chronic blood loss (blood in stools). Given his age, the most concerning cause is a gastrointestinal malignancy, such as colon cancer. He also has end-stage renal disease, but the microcytosis points away from anemia of chronic disease. Alcohol use could cause GI bleeding from gastritis or varices, but the lack of organomegaly and unremarkable rectal exam make chronic liver disease less likely. The most likely underlying cause of his bleeding is a colonic neoplasm.\n\nAnswer: Colorectal carcinoma", "correct": false, "gold": "Arteriovenous malformation in the colonic wall"}
{"qid": "med:321", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Angiodysplasia of the colon (GI vascular ectasias), related to his end-stage renal disease/uremic platelet dysfunction**\n\n**Reasoning:**\n- Chronic, intermittent lower GI bleeding (occult blood in stool) with a completely unremarkable rectal exam and no palpable masses/organomegaly points away from a mass lesion (e.g., colorectal cancer) as the most likely cause, though this should always be excluded with colonoscopy.\n- The patient has **end-stage renal disease (ESRD)**, which is strongly associated with **angiodysplasia** (acquired vascular ectasias) of the GI tract \u2014 these fragile, dilated submucosal vessels are prone to slow, recurrent bleeding.\n- Additionally, uremia causes **platelet dysfunction** (impaired adhesion/aggregation due to accumulated uremic toxins), which further predisposes to mucosal bleeding from these lesions.\n- The resulting chronic blood loss has led to **iron-deficiency anemia**, reflected by the low MCV (68 fL, microcytic) and low hemoglobin \u2014 consistent with ongoing occult GI blood loss rather than anemia of chronic kidney disease alone (which is typically normocytic).\n\n**Answer: Angiodysplasia of the colon, related to his underlying end-stage renal disease (uremia-induced vascular fragility and platelet dysfunction), causing chronic occult GI bleeding and iron-deficiency anemia.**", "correct": false, "gold": "Arteriovenous malformation in the colonic wall"}
{"qid": "med:321", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient presents with symptomatic iron-deficiency anemia (microcytic, Hb 7.2 g/dL) in the setting of intermittent hematochezia. Visible rectal bleeding plus chronic occult blood loss in an older adult immediately implicates a colonic source. Among the common colonic lesions capable of producing this pattern (diverticula, neoplasia, hemorrhoids, angiodysplasia), the history supplies a key epidemiologic clue: end-stage renal disease. ESRD is strongly associated with acquired vascular ectasias (angiodysplasia), which are prone to chronic, low-grade or intermittent bleeding. Other risk factors for angiodysplasia (aortic stenosis, hereditary von Willebrand disease) are absent, and competing explanations are less plausible: diverticular bleeding is typically acute and voluminous; alcohol use is modest and there are no stigmata of portal hypertension; rectal examination is normal, excluding hemorrhoids; and no constitutional features suggest malignancy. Thus the most probable lesion accounting for the observed gastrointestinal blood loss is colonic angiodysplasia.\nAnswer: colonic angiodysplasia (vascular ectasia)", "correct": false, "gold": "Arteriovenous malformation in the colonic wall"}
{"qid": "med:317", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient meets clinical criteria for secondary amenorrhea (\u22653 months without menses). Pregnancy, hyperprolactinemia, thyroid dysfunction, and primary ovarian insufficiency have already been excluded by the negative urine hCG and the normal prolactin, TSH, and FSH values.  \nThe history of oligomenorrhea dating back to menarche, together with overweight BMI, maternal PCOS, and paternal diabetes, points to underlying insulin resistance and chronic anovulation\u2014the cardinal features of PCOS. In obese adolescents the LH:FSH ratio and total testosterone may remain within the reference range, so their normality does not exclude the diagnosis.  \nRisperidone can raise prolactin, but the level here is normal, eliminating drug-induced hyperprolactinemia. Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea is unlikely given the patient\u2019s BMI and the long-standing menstrual irregularity that predates any recent psychosocial stressors.  \nThus the presentation is explained by PCOS.\nAnswer: polycystic ovary syndrome (chronic anovulation)", "correct": true, "gold": "Anovulation"}
{"qid": "med:317", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\nThis 15-year-old girl presents with secondary amenorrhea (3 months) after a history of irregular menstrual cycles. Key findings supporting the diagnosis include:  \r\n\r\n- **Anovulation:** Low progesterone (0.8 ng/mL) indicates she is not ovulating.\r\n- **Risk factors:** Overweight (BMI 27.02 kg/m\u00b2), family history of PCOS (mother), and a father with diabetes mellitus.\r\n- **Exclusion of other causes:** Pregnancy is ruled out by a negative urine test. Thyroid dysfunction (TSH normal), hyperprolactinemia (prolactin normal at 16 ng/mL, despite risperidone use), and ovarian failure (normal FSH/LH) are unlikely.\r\n- **Absence of significant hyperandrogenism:** Testosterone is within normal limits (2.2 nmol/L), which is common in early or mild PCOS. \r\n\r\nThese features align with polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS), the most common cause of anovulatory secondary amenorrhea in adolescents with this clinical profile. \r\n\r\nAnswer: Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS)", "correct": false, "gold": "Anovulation"}
