{"qid": "med:323", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient most likely has a **rotator cuff tear**, specifically involving the **supraspinatus tendon**.\n\nWhy:\n- **Drop arm test positive**: inability to slowly lower the arm from an abducted position suggests **supraspinatus tear**\n- **Abduction strength 1/5**: the **supraspinatus** initiates abduction\n- Older patient with acute worsening after lifting is classic for rotator cuff injury\n\nAlthough **external rotation weakness** can suggest **infraspinatus** involvement, the key exam finding here is the **positive drop arm test** with severe weakness in abduction, making **supraspinatus** the most likely injured structure.\n\nAnswer: **Supraspinatus tendon**", "correct": true, "gold": "Supraspinatus"}
{"qid": "med:323", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient's presentation\u2014weakness of external rotation, a positive drop arm test, and prominent weakness of abduction\u2014points to a rotator cuff tear. Given the acute onset after heavy lifting and exacerbation by weightlifting, a full-thickness tear of the **supraspinatus** tendon is most likely. The drop arm test specifically indicates a large or complete tear of the supraspinatus, and external rotation weakness suggests possible involvement of the infraspinatus as well.\n\nAnswer: Supraspinatus tendon (rotator cuff tear)", "correct": true, "gold": "Supraspinatus"}
{"qid": "med:318", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient presents with high fever, leukocytosis, right\u2011upper\u2011quadrant pain, tender hepatomegaly, and an elevated alkaline phosphatase\u2014findings classic for a pyogenic liver abscess. Diabetes is a major risk factor for pyogenic (especially Klebsiella) abscesses, and the absence of travel makes amebic disease unlikely. The ultrasound (showing a hypoechoic or complex hepatic lesion) confirms the diagnosis. In a septic patient with a documented abscess, definitive source control is required; percutaneous drainage (ultrasound\u2011 or CT\u2011guided) is the first\u2011line intervention, combined with empiric broad\u2011spectrum antibiotics.\n\nAnswer: Percutaneous drainage of the liver abscess.", "correct": false, "gold": "Perform percutaneous drainage\n\""}
{"qid": "med:323", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Supraspinatus tendon tear (rotator cuff injury)**\n\n**Key findings supporting this diagnosis:**\n- **Positive drop arm test**: Inability to smoothly lower the arm from an overhead position, with sudden dropping and pain, is classic for a supraspinatus tear.\n- **Weakness in abduction (1/5 strength)**: The supraspinatus initiates the first 15-20 degrees of shoulder abduction and assists throughout the arc.\n- **Weakness in external rotation**: While the infraspinatus is the primary external rotator, the supraspinatus also contributes to external rotation, and large rotator cuff tears often involve both the supraspinatus and infraspinatus tendons.\n- **Mechanism of injury**: Acute strain from heavy lifting (moving furniture) followed by worsening symptoms with further loading (weightlifting) is a common precipitant for rotator cuff tears, especially in older patients with likely pre-existing tendon degeneration.\n- **Risk factors**: Age (69 years old), obesity, and diabetes are all associated with increased risk of rotator cuff pathology due to degenerative changes and impaired tendon healing/vascularity.\n\nThe supraspinatus is the most commonly injured rotator cuff tendon due to its anatomic vulnerability (it passes through the subacromial space and has a relatively poor blood supply in its distal portion, known as the \"critical zone\").\n\nAnswer: The supraspinatus tendon (rotator cuff tear)", "correct": true, "gold": "Supraspinatus"}
{"qid": "med:323", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The patient\u2019s findings\u2014severe weakness of external rotation, a \u201cdropping arm\u201d sign when lowering the arm from overhead, and marked loss of abduction strength\u2014are classic for a massive rotator cuff tear, most commonly involving the supraspinatus tendon.  \n\n**Answer:** Rotator cuff tear (likely supraspinatus tendon rupture).<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "Supraspinatus"}
{"qid": "med:321", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Colonic angiodysplasia (intestinal vascular ectasia)<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Arteriovenous malformation in the colonic wall"}
{"qid": "med:324", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This 5-day-old neonate has **poor feeding, irritability, trismus/clenched fists, and umbilical stump infection**, which is classic for **neonatal tetanus**. Lack of prenatal care suggests the mother may not have been immunized, so the infant lacked protective maternal antibodies. Infection occurs through the **umbilical stump**, where **Clostridium tetani** spores germinate in devitalized tissue.\n\nPathogenesis:\n- **C tetani** produces **tetanospasmin**, an **A-B exotoxin**\n- The toxin is taken up at peripheral nerves and transported **retrograde** to the CNS\n- It cleaves **synaptobrevin (a SNARE protein)** in inhibitory interneurons\n- This blocks release of **GABA and glycine**\n- Loss of inhibitory input causes **muscle rigidity and spasms**\n\nAnswer: Retrograde axonal transport of tetanospasmin from an infected umbilical stump to the CNS, where it cleaves SNARE proteins and blocks release of inhibitory neurotransmitters (GABA and glycine).", "correct": false, "gold": "Decreased release of glycine"}
{"qid": "med:323", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The vignette describes an older patient with multiple risk factors (age, diabetes, obesity) who develops acute shoulder pain/weakness after mechanical loading; symptoms then markedly worsen with further loading. These elements are classic for acute rotator cuff tear.\nPhysical-examination findings localize the injury:\n- Marked weakness in initiation of abduction and a positive drop-arm test both indicate loss of supraspinatus function (the tendon that both initiates abduction and eccentrically controls humeral head descent during lowering).\n- Weakness in external rotation indicates concomitant infraspinatus involvement, which commonly occurs once a tear becomes large enough to extend beyond the supraspinatus.\nBecause the supraspinatus tendon is the most frequently torn rotator-cuff structure and accounts for the cardinal positive tests (drop-arm and abduction weakness), it is the single structure whose injury best explains the overall picture.\nAnswer: supraspinatus tendon (rotator cuff tear)", "correct": true, "gold": "Supraspinatus"}
{"qid": "med:324", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The presentation\u2014poor feeding, irritability, noisy breathing, sunken fontanelles (dehydration), clenched fists, and umbilical erythema in a 5-day-old\u2014suggests neonatal tetanus. This is caused by *Clostridium tetani* infection, typically through the umbilical stump when hygiene is poor (e.g., no prenatal care, non-sterile delivery). The pathogenesis involves tetanus toxin (tetanospasmin) binding to presynaptic nerve terminals, blocking release of inhibitory neurotransmitters (GABA and glycine), leading to unchecked muscle spasticity and autonomic instability.\n\n**Answer:** Tetanus toxin blocks release of inhibitory neurotransmitters (GABA/glycine) at spinal synapses, causing unopposed muscle contraction and spasm.", "correct": false, "gold": "Decreased release of glycine"}
{"qid": "med:323", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at this clinical vignette, I need to identify the key findings:\n\n**Key clinical features:**\n1. **Patient**: 69-year-old male with risk factors (diabetes, obesity, age >40)\n2. **Mechanism**: Acute trauma from lifting heavy objects, worsened by weightlifting\n3. **Physical exam findings**:\n   - Prominent **weakness of external rotation** (infraspinatus/teres minor)\n   - **Positive drop arm test**: Patient cannot slowly lower arm from overhead; it drops suddenly with pain\n   - **Severe abduction weakness** (1/5 strength)\n\n**Analysis:**\n- The **drop arm test** is highly specific for a **complete rotator cuff tear**, particularly involving the supraspinatus tendon. This test assesses the patient's ability to hold the arm at 90\u00b0 abduction against gravity.\n- **External rotation weakness** indicates involvement of the infraspinatus muscle (suprascapular nerve innervated).\n- **Severe abduction weakness** (1/5) suggests the supraspinatus is torn and unable to initiate or maintain abduction.\n- The combination of these findings indicates a **massive rotator cuff tear** involving the supraspinatus and likely the infraspinatus.\n\n**Differential considerations:**\n- Axillary nerve injury: Would affect abduction (deltoid) and external rotation (teres minor), but the drop arm test is specific for rotator cuff integrity, not nerve function.\n- Adhesive capsulitis: Common in diabetics, but causes stiffness without this degree of weakness or positive drop arm sign.\n- Cervical radiculopathy: Would not produce the specific pattern of weakness with a positive drop arm test.\n\n**Risk factors:** Age, diabetes, obesity, and acute trauma all predispose to rotator cuff tears.\n\nThe supraspinatus tendon is the most commonly injured rotator cuff tendon, and the drop arm test is pathognomonic for a complete tear.\n\nAnswer: Rotator cuff (supraspinatus tendon)", "correct": true, "gold": "Supraspinatus"}
{"qid": "med:323", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:** \r\n\r\nThis patient's presentation is classic for a rotator cuff tear, most commonly involving the **supraspinatus tendon**. \r\n\r\nKey supporting findings include: \r\n\r\n- **Mechanism:** Acute onset after heavy lifting (moving furniture), a common inciting event in older adults.\r\n- **Risk factors:** Age (69), diabetes, and obesity, all of which increase the risk of rotator cuff degeneration and tears.\r\n- **Weakness:** 1/5 strength with abduction (primarily supraspinatus) and prominent weakness with external rotation (supraspinatus and infraspinatus).\r\n- **Positive drop arm test:** Sudden dropping of the arm when lowering from overhead is highly suggestive of a large supraspinatus tear.\r\n- **Symptom progression:** Worsening with weightlifting, which is consistent with an underlying tear. \r\n\r\nAnswer: **Supraspinatus tendon tear.**", "correct": true, "gold": "Supraspinatus"}
{"qid": "med:324", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "## Diagnosis: Neonatal Tetanus\n\n**Clinical clues pointing to this diagnosis:**\n- No prenatal care (likely no maternal tetanus immunization)\n- Unhygienic umbilical cord care (erythema/infection at stump = portal of entry)\n- Trismus equivalent (clenched fists suggest generalized muscle rigidity)\n- Poor feeding (risus sardonicus/inability to suckle due to facial muscle spasm)\n- Irritability and noisy breathing (laryngospasm)\n\n## Pathogenesis\n\n**1. Entry and Infection:**\n*Clostridium tetani* spores contaminate the umbilical stump (especially with non-sterile cutting instruments or unhygienic post-natal care). In an anaerobic, devitalized wound environment, spores germinate into vegetative bacilli.\n\n**2. Toxin Production:**\nThe vegetative bacteria produce **tetanospasmin** (tetanus toxin), an exotoxin encoded on a plasmid.\n\n**3. Toxin Transport:**\n- Tetanospasmin is taken up by peripheral motor neuron terminals at the site of infection\n- It undergoes **retrograde axonal transport** to the cell bodies in the spinal cord and brainstem\n- It then transcytoses to **presynaptic inhibitory interneurons** (Renshaw cells)\n\n**4. Mechanism of Toxicity:**\n- The toxin acts as a **zinc-dependent endopeptidase**\n- It cleaves **synaptobrevin (VAMP)**, a protein required for synaptic vesicle docking and fusion\n- This **blocks release of inhibitory neurotransmitters \u2014 GABA and glycine** \u2014 from Renshaw cells and other inhibitory interneurons\n\n**5. Clinical Consequence:**\nLoss of inhibitory input results in **unopposed, sustained motor neuron firing**, causing:\n- Generalized muscle rigidity and spasms\n- Trismus (lockjaw)\n- Opisthotonus\n- Risus sardonicus\n- Autonomic instability (in severe cases)\n\n---\n\n**Answer:** *Clostridium tetani* infects the umbilical stump, and the released tetanospasmin toxin undergoes retrograde transport to the CNS, where it cleaves synaptobrevin to block release of the inhibitory neurotransmitters **glycine and GABA** from Renshaw cells, leading to unopposed motor neuron activity and the characteristic muscle rigidity/spasms of tetanus.", "correct": false, "gold": "Decreased release of glycine"}
{"qid": "med:324", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Neonatal tetanus; pathogenesis involves infection of the umbilical stump by *Clostridium tetani* spores, production of tetanospasmin toxin that travels retrograde up motor neurons to the spinal cord and brainstem, where it blocks inhibitory neurotransmitters (GABA, glycine), causing uncontrolled muscle contractions and spastic paralysis.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Decreased release of glycine"}
{"qid": "med:324", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The newborn's presentation (onset at age 5 days, trismus, clenched fists, irritability, and umbilical-stump erythema) plus absent maternal prenatal care is diagnostic of neonatal tetanus from Clostridium tetani introduced via the umbilical stump. Tetanospasmin is retrogradely transported into the CNS, where it cleaves synaptobrevin and thereby prevents vesicular release of the inhibitory transmitters glycine and GABA from Renshaw interneurons. Loss of this inhibition produces unopposed motor-neuron firing and the observed muscle spasms.\nAnswer: blocks release of GABA/glycine from spinal interneurons (tetanospasmin)", "correct": false, "gold": "Decreased release of glycine"}
{"qid": "med:318", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Answer: Percutaneous catheter drainage of the liver abscess}}\n\\]", "correct": false, "gold": "Perform percutaneous drainage\n\""}
{"qid": "med:325", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "He has **hypertonic dehydration from osmotic diuresis due to uncontrolled diabetes mellitus**.\n\nWhy:\n- **Hypernatremia (Na 150) and high serum osmolality (354)** indicate loss of free water.\n- He has signs of **volume depletion**: orthostatic dizziness, low BP, dry mucosa, poor skin turgor, weakness.\n- He reports **polyuria with normal-volume voids**, which suggests increased total urine output.\n- **HbA1c 10.5%** strongly suggests poorly controlled diabetes with **glucosuria**, causing **osmotic diuresis**.\n- **Urine osmolality 400 mOsm/kg** shows the kidneys are appropriately concentrating urine, so this is **not diabetes insipidus**.\n- Vomiting and thiazide use can contribute to volume loss and metabolic alkalosis, but they do not best explain the **polyuria plus hyperosmolar hypernatremia**.\n\nMechanism:\n- Excess filtered glucose exceeds proximal tubular reabsorptive capacity.\n- Glucose remains in the tubular lumen and **pulls water into the urine**.\n- This causes **loss of water in excess of sodium**, leading to **hypernatremia and hyperosmolarity**.\n\nAnswer: Osmotic diuresis from uncontrolled diabetes mellitus causing free-water loss (hypertonic volume depletion).", "correct": true, "gold": "Osmotic diuresis"}
{"qid": "med:325", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient has longstanding uncontrolled type 2 diabetes (BMI 32, HbA1c 10.5 %). Chronic hyperglycemia produces an osmotic diuresis once the filtered load of glucose exceeds the renal reabsorptive threshold. The resulting urinary loss of water exceeds sodium loss, raising serum sodium and effective osmolality. Continued obligatory solute excretion keeps urine osmolality only modestly above plasma osmolality (400 vs 354 mOsm/kg) rather than the several-hundred-fold concentration that would be expected in pure water deprivation. Volume depletion is confirmed by orthostatic vital signs, delayed skin turgor, and dry mucous membranes; the modest contraction alkalosis is consistent with both diuretic use and volume loss. Other causes of hypernatremia (central DI, nephrogenic DI, primary polydipsia) produce either frankly dilute urine or maximal urine concentration and are therefore excluded by the laboratory values.\nAnswer: osmotic diuresis from glucosuria (uncontrolled DM)", "correct": true, "gold": "Osmotic diuresis"}
{"qid": "med:325", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient presents with hypernatremia (Na+ 150 mEq/L), high serum osmolality (354 mOsm/kg), and a history of polyuria, polydipsia, and weakness. He has hypertension treated with hydrochlorothiazide, which can cause hyperglycemia, and his HbA1c of 10.5% indicates poorly controlled diabetes. The urine osmolality (400 mOsm/kg) is inappropriately low relative to the high serum osmolality, suggesting a renal concentrating defect. This combination\u2014hypernatremia, hyperosmolality, polyuria, and inappropriately dilute urine\u2014points to diabetes insipidus. Given his diabetes and thiazide use, the most likely cause is hyperglycemia-induced osmotic diuresis leading to water loss and hypernatremia, consistent with diabetes insipidus (likely nephrogenic due to osmotic diuresis from glucosuria).\n\nAnswer: Hyperglycemia-induced osmotic diuresis causing nephrogenic diabetes insipidus.", "correct": true, "gold": "Osmotic diuresis"}
{"qid": "med:326", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Answer: NSAIDs (e.g., ibuprofen) started at onset of menses, \u00b1 hormonal contraceptives if inadequate relief", "correct": true, "gold": "NSAIDs"}
{"qid": "med:321", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient\u2019s microcytic anemia (MCV\u202f68\u202f\u03bcm\u00b3) indicates iron deficiency from chronic blood loss. In a patient with end\u2011stage renal disease (ESRD), the most common structural lesion causing recurrent lower gastrointestinal bleeding is **colonic angiodysplasia** (vascular ectasia). ESRD predisposes to the development of these fragile, dilated submucosal vessels, which bleed intermittently and produce occult or visible blood in the stool. While colon cancer is a common cause of iron\u2011deficiency anemia in the general elderly population, the presence of ESRD strongly favors angiodysplasia as the underlying source.\n\nAnswer: Angiodysplasia (vascular ectasia) of the colon.", "correct": false, "gold": "Arteriovenous malformation in the colonic wall"}
{"qid": "med:317", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient has secondary amenorrhea with a background of irregular cycles, obesity (BMI 27), and a strong family history of PCOS. Laboratory studies show normal prolactin, normal TSH, and normal gonadotropin levels (FSH \u2248 LH) with a low progesterone, indicating anovulation. There is no biochemical hyperandrogenism (testosterone is normal), but PCOS can present with normal androgens and is the most common cause of anovulatory amenorrhea in adolescents, especially in the setting of obesity and a first\u2011degree relative with the disorder. Risperidone can cause hyperprolactinemia, but the prolactin level is normal, ruling that out. Pregnancy, thyroid disease, and ovarian failure are also excluded by the labs. Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea typically presents with low gonadotropins and is associated with low body weight or extreme stress, neither of which is present here.\n\nAnswer: Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS).", "correct": false, "gold": "Anovulation"}
{"qid": "med:324", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The clinical picture\u2014poor feeding, irritability, muscle rigidity (clenched fists), noisy breathing (laryngeal spasm), dehydration, and umbilical erythema in a newborn whose mother had no prenatal care\u2014is classic for **neonatal tetanus**. The pathogen, *Clostridium tetani*, enters through the infected umbilical stump. Its exotoxin, tetanospasmin, binds peripheral nerve terminals and is transported retrograde to the spinal cord and brainstem, where it cleaves SNARE proteins and prevents the release of inhibitory neurotransmitters (glycine and GABA) from interneurons. This loss of inhibition produces unopposed \u03b1\u2011motor neuron activity, resulting in the characteristic muscle spasms and rigidity.\n\nAnswer: *Clostridium tetani* spores infect the umbilical stump and produce tetanospasmin, which travels retrograde to the CNS and blocks the release of inhibitory neurotransmitters (glycine and GABA) in the spinal cord, leading to unopposed motor neuron firing and muscle spasms.", "correct": false, "gold": "Decreased release of glycine"}
{"qid": "med:326", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient's history of severe dysmenorrhea starting after cycles became regular, along with a family history of endometriosis, raises concern for endometriosis. However, in an adolescent with normal exam and no red flags (e.g., bowel/bladder symptoms, abnormal bleeding), the best next step is to initiate empiric treatment with NSAIDs and combined hormonal contraceptives (CHC) rather than proceeding directly to diagnostic laparoscopy. This aligns with guidelines for managing primary dysmenorrhea or suspected endometriosis in adolescents.\n\n**Answer:** Start NSAIDs and combined hormonal contraceptives.", "correct": true, "gold": "NSAIDs"}
{"qid": "med:326", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This adolescent has **primary dysmenorrhea**, which is common after ovulatory cycles become established. Her pain is crampy, occurs with menses, she has a normal exam, and there are no strong signs of secondary causes on presentation. Although her mother has endometriosis, the **best initial management** for typical adolescent dysmenorrhea is **NSAID therapy**, especially since she has only tried heating pads so far.\n\nNSAIDs reduce prostaglandin production and are first-line treatment. They work best when started **1\u20132 days before the onset of menses (or at the first sign of bleeding/pain)** and continued on a scheduled basis for the first 2\u20133 days of the period.\n\nAnswer: Start scheduled NSAID therapy (eg, ibuprofen or naproxen), ideally beginning 1\u20132 days before menses/onset of symptoms.", "correct": false, "gold": "NSAIDs"}
{"qid": "med:325", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Most likely explanation: Hyperglycemia-induced osmotic diuresis causing free water loss in excess of sodium loss, resulting in hypernatremic dehydration**\n\n**Reasoning:**\n\nThis patient has poorly controlled diabetes mellitus (HbA1c 10.5%) leading to significant hyperglycemia. Key findings supporting osmotic diuresis as the cause:\n\n1. **Polyuria with normal-appearing urine volume** (\"normal amount\" each time, but increased frequency) \u2014 classic for glucosuria-driven osmotic diuresis, distinct from primary polydipsia or diabetes insipidus.\n\n2. **Hypernatremia (Na+ 150) with elevated serum osmolality (354)** \u2014 indicates a *hypertonic* fluid loss state, where water loss exceeds sodium loss.\n\n3. **Inappropriately dilute urine osmolality (400 mOsm/kg)** relative to the degree of hyperosmolality/dehydration \u2014 in appropriate ADH response, urine osmolality should be maximally concentrated (>600\u2013800 mOsm/kg) in the setting of hypernatremia and volume depletion. This \"relative\" inability to concentrate urine reflects an **osmotic diuresis** (glucosuria) that obligates water excretion despite the body's attempt to conserve water via ADH \u2014 the glucose in the tubule prevents maximal urine concentration even though ADH is appropriately elevated.\n\n4. **Volume depletion signs**: orthostatic dizziness, tachycardia, low-normal BP, dry mucous membranes (dry tongue), decreased skin turgor (poor skin recoil) \u2014 despite high oral water intake (9 glasses/day), indicating that fluid losses are outpacing intake.\n\n5. **HCO3- of 30 (mild metabolic alkalosis)** \u2014 consistent with volume contraction (contraction alkalosis) from GI losses (vomiting) and diuretic use (HCTZ), which also contributes to volume depletion and electrolyte disturbance, but the primary driver of the *hypernatremia and hyperosmolar state* is the glucose-induced osmotic diuresis.\n\n6. **Altered mental status (slowed speech)** \u2014 reflects cellular dehydration from severe hyperosmolality, consistent with evolving **Hyperosmolar Hyperglycemic State (HHS)**.\n\nThe combination of markedly elevated glucose (implied by HbA1c and osmolar gap \u2014 measured osmolality 354 is much higher than calculated from Na, urea, and normal glucose, suggesting significant hyperglycemia contributing to the gap), inappropriate urine concentration, and hypernatremic volume depletion all point to **osmotic diuresis from uncontrolled hyperglycemia** as the underlying mechanism, compounded by vomiting and thiazide-induced volume loss.\n\nAnswer: Uncontrolled hyperglycemia (poorly controlled type 2 diabetes) causing osmotic diuresis, leading to excess free water loss relative to sodium and resulting in hypernatremic volume depletion (early hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state).", "correct": true, "gold": "Osmotic diuresis"}
{"qid": "med:326", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient's history is classic for primary dysmenorrhea (cyclic midline pain beginning 1\u20132 years after menarche, associated low-back ache, no red-flag symptoms of secondary causes). She is not sexually active, has a normal abdominal and external genital examination, and has already tried only non-pharmacologic measures. First-line therapy is therefore empiric NSAID treatment begun at the onset of menses (or 1\u20132 days before expected pain); NSAIDs simultaneously reduce prostaglandin-mediated pain and menstrual blood loss. If symptoms remain refractory after 2\u20133 cycles, hormonal suppression or further evaluation for secondary dysmenorrhea can be considered next.\nAnswer: trial of NSAIDs (e.g., ibuprofen at menses onset)", "correct": true, "gold": "NSAIDs"}
{"qid": "med:327", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Reassurance and routine follow-up (no further workup needed) \u2014 this is physiologic neonatal withdrawal (pseudomenstruation) from maternal estrogen.\n\nAnswer: Reassure the mother that this is a normal, self-limited physiologic withdrawal bleed and provide routine outpatient follow-up\u2014no further intervention or abuse workup is indicated.", "correct": false, "gold": "Reassurance"}
{"qid": "med:321", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Answer: \\(\\boxed{\\text{Colonic angiodysplasia (vascular ectasia)}}\\)", "correct": false, "gold": "Arteriovenous malformation in the colonic wall"}
{"qid": "med:325", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The patient\u2019s labs show hypernatremia (Na\u202f150\u202fmEq/L), an elevated serum osmolality (354\u202fmOsm/kg), metabolic alkalosis (HCO\u2083\u207b\u202f30\u202fmEq/L), and a relatively concentrated urine (urine osmolality 400\u202fmOsm/kg). Physical findings (delayed skin turgor, white tongue) and the history of 7\u202fdays of vomiting plus regular use of the diuretic hydrochlorothiazide point to substantial water and electrolyte losses. Vomiting causes loss of gastric HCl \u2192 metabolic alkalosis and water loss; thiazide diuretics increase urinary water excretion, worsening volume depletion. In response to volume loss, ADH is released, allowing the kidneys to concentrate urine (high urine osmolality). The resulting hypertonic dehydration explains the hypernatremia, high serum osmolality, orthostatic dizziness, weakness, and slowed mental status.  \n\n**Answer:** Hypertonic dehydration (volume depletion) secondary to vomiting and thiazide diuretic use.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Osmotic diuresis"}
{"qid": "med:327", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The presentation is consistent with physiologic leukorrhea and a withdrawal bleed from maternal estrogen, which is normal in the first week of life. Given the well appearance, normal vital signs, and lack of signs of infection or trauma, no further workup or intervention is needed.\n\nAnswer: Reassure the mother that this is normal and no treatment is necessary.", "correct": false, "gold": "Reassurance"}
{"qid": "med:326", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Begin a trial of NSAIDs (e.g., ibuprofen 400\u2013600\u202fmg every 6\u20138\u202fhours) starting 1\u20132\u202fdays before expected menses and continuing through the first few days of bleeding, while continuing heat therapy; if pain remains uncontrolled after 2\u20133 cycles, consider initiating combined oral contraceptives.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "NSAIDs"}
{"qid": "med:327", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This 4-day-old neonate has scant pink mucoid vaginal discharge without signs of trauma, irritation, infection, or systemic illness. In newborn girls, this is most consistent with **physiologic neonatal withdrawal bleeding** due to the sudden drop in maternal estrogen after birth. It is benign and self-limited.\n\nThere is no indication here for STI testing, abuse evaluation, or treatment, as the exam is otherwise normal and the discharge is classic for this normal newborn finding.\n\nAnswer: Reassure the mother that this is normal neonatal withdrawal bleeding from maternal estrogen and requires no treatment.", "correct": false, "gold": "Reassurance"}
{"qid": "med:332", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Answer: Amphotericin B plus flucytosine (induction therapy for cryptococcal meningitis)", "correct": false, "gold": "Amphotericin B and flucytosine"}
{"qid": "med:327", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation of scant mucoid or lightly blood-tinged vaginal discharge beginning on day 2\u20133 of life in a well-appearing term female neonate is the classic time course and appearance of physiologic estrogen-withdrawal bleeding. Maternal estrogen stimulates the neonatal endometrium in utero; after delivery the abrupt drop in circulating estrogen produces a self-limited endometrial shedding that resolves within a few days without sequelae. No laboratory evaluation, imaging, or antimicrobial therapy is indicated when the infant is afebrile, feeding normally, and free of vulvar erythema, purulent discharge, or signs of trauma.\nThe remote maternal chlamydia history (treated, test-of-cure negative) and the presence of a new caregiver are distractors that do not alter management in the absence of suggestive physical findings. Child-protection reporting or STI testing would be appropriate only if overt injury, purulent discharge, or other red-flag features were present; none exist here. Therefore the appropriate next step is simple reassurance with routine newborn follow-up.\nAnswer: reassurance (physiologic estrogen withdrawal)", "correct": true, "gold": "Reassurance"}
{"qid": "med:332", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient has advanced HIV/AIDS with oral candidiasis and a CD4 count of 80/mm\u00b3, plus subacute headache, fever, somnolence, memory deficits, and CSF showing lymphocytic pleocytosis, mildly elevated protein, low glucose, and **India ink with encapsulated organisms**. This is classic **cryptococcal meningitis** due to *Cryptococcus neoformans*.\n\nInitial treatment for cryptococcal meningitis in patients with AIDS is **induction therapy with amphotericin B plus flucytosine**, followed later by fluconazole for consolidation/maintenance.\n\nAnswer: Amphotericin B plus flucytosine.", "correct": false, "gold": "Amphotericin B and flucytosine"}
{"qid": "med:324", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reason:**  \r\nThis 5-day-old boy presents with classic features of **neonatal tetanus**, including poor feeding, irritability, noisy breathing (due to laryngeal spasms), sunken fontanelles (dehydration), tightly clenched fists (muscle rigidity/spasms), and erythema around the umbilical cord stump (the portal of entry). In the absence of prenatal care, non-sterile cord handling is a major risk factor.  \r\n\r\nThe disease is caused by *Clostridium tetani*, which produces the neurotoxin **tetanospasmin**. This toxin is retrogradely transported to the spinal cord, where it cleaves **synaptobrevin** (a SNARE protein) in inhibitory interneurons. This prevents the release of the inhibitory neurotransmitters **GABA and glycine**, leading to disinhibition of motor neurons and resulting in sustained muscle contractions and spasms.  \r\n\r\nAnswer: Tetanospasmin cleaves synaptobrevin, blocking GABA and glycine release from spinal inhibitory interneurons.", "correct": false, "gold": "Decreased release of glycine"}
{"qid": "med:325", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Answer:** Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS) precipitated by dehydration in a patient with poorly controlled type 2 diabetes mellitus.", "correct": false, "gold": "Osmotic diuresis"}
{"qid": "med:327", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Reassure the mother that the pink mucoid discharge is most likely physiologic estrogen\u2011withdrawal discharge; no further diagnostic testing or treatment is needed at this time\u2014advise gentle cleaning, close monitoring, and appropriate follow\u2011up.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Reassurance"}
{"qid": "med:332", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has AIDS (CD4 count 80) and symptoms of meningitis (headache, fever, somnolence, memory deficits). CSF shows low glucose, elevated protein, lymphocytic pleocytosis, and India ink stain positive for encapsulated organisms\u2014classic for cryptococcal meningitis. The most appropriate pharmacotherapy for cryptococcal meningitis is induction therapy with amphotericin B (often liposomal) plus flucytosine.\n\nAnswer: Amphotericin B plus flucytosine", "correct": false, "gold": "Amphotericin B and flucytosine"}
{"qid": "med:327", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The blood\u2011tinged mucoid discharge in a 4\u2011day\u2011old female infant is classic for **pseudomenstruation** (withdrawal bleeding), a benign physiologic response to the sudden drop in maternal estrogen after delivery. The infant is well\u2011appearing, afebrile, and has no signs of infection, trauma, or vulvar irritation. The mother\u2019s prior chlamydia was adequately treated with a negative test of cure, making neonatal infection unlikely, and there are no physical findings to suggest abuse. No laboratory tests, imaging, or treatment are indicated; the condition resolves spontaneously within days.\n\nAnswer: Reassure the mother that this is normal physiologic pseudomenstruation and observe; no further workup or treatment is needed.", "correct": false, "gold": "Reassurance"}
{"qid": "med:334", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This 6-day-old infant has **neonatal hyperthyroidism** from **transplacental passage of maternal TSH-receptor\u2013stimulating antibodies** due to maternal Graves disease.\n\nKey clues:\n- Irritability, restlessness\n- Frequent stools\n- Poor weight gain / weight loss\n- Tachycardia, diaphoresis\n- Goiter\n- Maternal Graves disease\n\nMaternal **propylthiouracil** can cross the placenta and temporarily suppress the fetal thyroid, which may delay presentation until several days after birth; symptoms often appear once the maternal drug effect wears off while stimulatory antibodies remain.\n\nMost appropriate management:\n- **Confirm with thyroid function tests** and\n- **Start antithyroid therapy**, typically **methimazole**; symptomatic control can include a **beta-blocker** if needed.\n\nThis is not neonatal opioid withdrawal because the goiter and maternal Graves history strongly indicate thyrotoxicosis.\n\nAnswer: Obtain neonatal thyroid function tests and begin treatment for neonatal Graves disease with methimazole.", "correct": false, "gold": "Methimazole and propranolol therapy"}
{"qid": "med:332", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The patient\u2019s presentation, CSF findings, and India ink\u2013positive encapsulated yeast identify cryptococcal meningitis, a common opportunistic infection in advanced HIV. The standard of care for induction is amphotericin\u202fB (preferably liposomal formulation) combined with flucytosine for at least 2\u202fweeks, followed by consolidation with high\u2011dose fluconazole for \u22658\u202fweeks and long\u2011term suppressive therapy with fluconazole. Elevated opening pressure should be addressed with therapeutic lumbar punctures (and possibly acetazolamide), but the core pharmacotherapy for his neurological infection is antifungal induction and consolidation.\n\n**Answer:** Induction with liposomal amphotericin\u202fB plus flucytosine, followed by consolidation with fluconazole (and continued fluconazole for maintenance).<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Amphotericin B and flucytosine"}
{"qid": "med:334", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Diagnosis:** Neonatal (transient) Graves' disease/thyrotoxicosis due to transplacental passage of maternal TSH-receptor stimulating antibodies (TRAb), presenting with irritability, tachycardia, diaphoresis, poor weight gain/failure to thrive, increased stooling, and a goiter.\n\n**Most appropriate next step:** Obtain a thyroid function panel (serum free T4 and TSH) to confirm the diagnosis, and then promptly initiate treatment with an antithyroid drug (methimazole) plus a beta-blocker (propranolol) to control the hyperthyroid symptoms \u2014 this is a medical emergency given the risk of high-output cardiac failure in neonates.\n\nAnswer: Check serum free T4/TSH to confirm neonatal thyrotoxicosis, then start methimazole and propranolol.", "correct": false, "gold": "Methimazole and propranolol therapy"}
{"qid": "med:332", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Answer: Intravenous amphotericin B and oral flucytosine", "correct": false, "gold": "Amphotericin B and flucytosine"}
{"qid": "med:332", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient has advanced untreated HIV/AIDS (CD4 count 80/mm\u00b3) with oral candidiasis and subacute headache, altered mentation, and short-term memory loss. CSF demonstrates lymphocytic pleocytosis, hypoglycorrhachia, and yeast forms with prominent capsules on India ink, confirming Cryptococcus neoformans meningitis. Standard induction therapy for cryptococcal meningitis in AIDS is therefore required to sterilize the CSF and reverse neurologic deficits. This consists of intravenous amphotericin B (which binds ergosterol in the fungal membrane) combined with oral flucytosine (which inhibits fungal DNA/RNA synthesis) for at least 2 weeks, followed by fluconazole consolidation. The measured opening pressure is normal, so no additional pressure-lowering maneuvers are indicated at this time. Antiretroviral therapy will be started later, once the acute infection is controlled, but does not directly treat the neurologic manifestations.\nAnswer: amphotericin B + flucytosine (induction)", "correct": false, "gold": "Amphotericin B and flucytosine"}
{"qid": "med:326", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "This patient presents with classic features of **primary dysmenorrhea**, including crampy midline abdominal pain with back pain that begins around the time of menstruation, regular ovulatory cycles, and a normal pelvic exam. \r\n\r\n### Reasoning: \r\n\r\n- **Most likely diagnosis:** Primary dysmenorrhea, caused by increased prostaglandin production during ovulation. \r\n- **First-line treatment:** Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), such as ibuprofen or naproxen sodium, are the best initial step. They work by inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis and are highly effective.\r\n- **Timing matters:** NSAIDs should be started at the onset of cramps (or 1-2 days before menses) and taken on a scheduled basis for the first 1-3 days of bleeding, rather than as needed alone.\r\n- **Red flags absent:** Her exam is normal, she's not sexually active, and there are no concerning bowel or bladder symptoms. While her mother's history of endometriosis increases her risk for secondary causes, a trial of NSAIDs is still appropriate first. \r\n\r\nAnswer: **Trial of scheduled NSAIDs (e.g., ibuprofen 400\u2013600 mg every 6\u20138 hours or naproxen sodium 440\u2013550 mg every 12 hours) at the onset of menstruation.**", "correct": true, "gold": "NSAIDs"}
{"qid": "med:327", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Answer: Reassurance and observation", "correct": true, "gold": "Reassurance"}
{"qid": "med:334", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The presentation suggests neonatal hyperthyroidism due to maternal Graves' disease, likely from transplacental passage of thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins. The infant is tachycardic, irritable, diaphoretic, and has a goiter with weight loss despite frequent feeding. Propylthiouracil given to the mother can cross the placenta but may not fully suppress fetal thyroid function. The most appropriate next step is to confirm the diagnosis with thyroid function tests (TFTs) including TSH, free T4, and T3, and then initiate antithyroid medication (e.g., methimazole or propylthiouracil) and supportive care (e.g., beta-blockers for tachycardia). However, the question asks for the *most appropriate next step in management*, which is to obtain thyroid function tests to confirm hyperthyroidism before treatment.\n\nAnswer: Obtain serum thyroid function tests.", "correct": false, "gold": "Methimazole and propranolol therapy"}
{"qid": "med:335", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient has **recurrent deep venous thrombosis at a young age**, suggesting an **inherited thrombophilia**. In an otherwise healthy Caucasian woman, the most likely inherited cause is **Factor V Leiden**.\n\n**Pathophysiology:** Factor V Leiden is a **point mutation in factor V** that makes factor V **resistant to inactivation by activated protein C**. Normally, activated protein C degrades factors Va and VIIIa to limit clotting. When factor V is resistant, coagulation persists, causing a **hypercoagulable state** and recurrent venous thromboses.\n\nAnswer: Mutation in factor V causing resistance to activated protein C\u2013mediated inactivation (Factor V Leiden), leading to a hypercoagulable state.", "correct": false, "gold": "Resistance to clotting factor degradation"}
{"qid": "med:334", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Initiate propranolol therapy to treat neonatal hyperthyroidism.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Methimazole and propranolol therapy"}
{"qid": "med:334", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The newborn's history, timing of symptom onset, and clinical features (poor weight gain despite high-calorie intake, frequent stools, irritability, diaphoresis, resting tachycardia, tachypnea, and palpable goiter) are diagnostic of neonatal Graves disease caused by transplacental passage of maternal TSH-receptor\u2013stimulating antibodies. Maternal propylthiouracil taken in the third trimester merely delays the clinical presentation by a few days; it does not change the underlying pathophysiology once the drug is cleared.\nBecause the diagnosis remains presumptive until confirmed biochemically, the immediate next management step is to obtain rapid thyroid-function studies (free T4, total T3, and TSH). These results both verify the diagnosis and serve as a baseline for guiding antithyroid-drug and \u03b2-blocker therapy. Empiric treatment without laboratory confirmation is not warranted in a stable outpatient neonate, while further imaging or other interventions are unnecessary.\nAnswer: measure serum free T4, T3 & TSH (confirm neonatal Graves)", "correct": false, "gold": "Methimazole and propranolol therapy"}
{"qid": "med:335", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has recurrent deep vein thromboses (DVT) at a young age, with a positive family history suggested by an inherited condition. The most common inherited thrombophilia is Factor V Leiden mutation, which causes activated protein C resistance. This prevents normal inactivation of Factor V, leading to a hypercoagulable state.\n\nAnswer: Activated protein C resistance (due to Factor V Leiden mutation)", "correct": false, "gold": "Resistance to clotting factor degradation"}
{"qid": "med:335", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Most likely diagnosis: Factor V Leiden mutation**\n\n**Pathophysiology:**\n\nThis patient's presentation\u2014recurrent, unprovoked venous thromboembolism (VTE) at a young age, alternating between legs, with no other risk factors\u2014is classic for an inherited thrombophilia, most commonly **Factor V Leiden**.\n\n**Mechanism:**\n\n1. **Normal physiology:** Factor Va is a key cofactor in the coagulation cascade that helps convert prothrombin to thrombin. Normally, activated Protein C (APC), with its cofactor Protein S, inactivates Factor Va by cleaving it at specific arginine residues (particularly at position 506), thereby limiting clot formation and maintaining hemostatic balance.\n\n2. **The mutation:** Factor V Leiden results from a single point mutation in the *F5* gene (G1691A), which causes a substitution of glutamine for arginine at position 506 (Arg506Gln). This mutation occurs at the exact site where APC normally cleaves Factor Va.\n\n3. **Consequence:** Because APC can no longer efficiently cleave and inactivate the mutant Factor Va, this is termed **\"APC resistance.\"** The mutant Factor Va remains active for a prolonged period, continuing to promote the conversion of prothrombin to thrombin unchecked.\n\n4. **Result:** This leads to a **hypercoagulable state** with increased thrombin generation, predisposing the patient to venous thromboembolism (DVT and PE), particularly in the deep veins of the legs, as seen in this patient with recurrent episodes affecting different legs.\n\nFactor V Leiden is the most common inherited thrombophilia in Caucasian populations, consistent with this patient's demographic and clinical history.\n\n---\n\nAnswer: The patient has Factor V Leiden mutation, in which a point mutation in the Factor V gene renders Factor Va resistant to cleavage/inactivation by activated Protein C, resulting in sustained procoagulant activity and a hypercoagulable state that predisposes to recurrent venous thromboembolism.", "correct": false, "gold": "Resistance to clotting factor degradation"}
{"qid": "med:335", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient is a young Caucasian woman with recurrent, unprovoked lower-extremity DVTs. In the absence of acquired risk factors, an inherited thrombophilia is the most probable etiology. Among inherited thrombophilias, the factor-V Leiden mutation is by far the most prevalent in individuals of European ancestry.  \n\nThe mutation substitutes arginine for glutamine at position 506 of the factor-V heavy chain. This amino-acid change abolishes one of the cleavage sites normally recognized by activated protein C, rendering factor Va resistant to proteolytic inactivation. Consequently, the prothrombinase complex remains active longer, thrombin generation is amplified, and the balance of coagulation tilts toward thrombosis\u2014particularly in the venous circulation.  \n\nThis single molecular defect accounts for the clinical picture of recurrent venous thromboembolism observed in the vignette.\n\nAnswer: FVL mut. \u2192 APC-resistant factor Va (hypercoagulable)", "correct": false, "gold": "Resistance to clotting factor degradation"}
{"qid": "med:335", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The patient\u2019s recurrent deep\u2011vein thrombosis at a young age, combined with a positive genetic test for an inherited thrombophilia, most commonly points to Factor\u202fV Leiden mutation. This mutation renders Factor\u202fV resistant to inactivation by activated protein\u202fC, so the coagulation cascade proceeds unchecked: activated Factor\u202fV (and Factor\u202fVIII) remain active longer, leading to excessive thrombin generation and a hypercoagulable state that predisposes to venous thrombosis.\n\n**Answer:** Factor\u202fV Leiden mutation causing activated protein\u202fC resistance, resulting in persistent activation of clotting factors and recurrent venous thromboembolism.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Resistance to clotting factor degradation"}
{"qid": "med:332", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient has advanced HIV (CD4 count 80\u202fcells/mm\u00b3) and clinical and laboratory findings consistent with cryptococcal meningitis: subacute headache, fever, oral thrush, somnolence, lymphocytic CSF pleocytosis, low CSF glucose, and India\u2011ink\u2013positive encapsulated yeasts. The standard induction regimen for cryptococcal meningitis in AIDS is amphotericin B (liposomal preferred) plus flucytosine for at least two weeks, followed by fluconazole consolidation. This combination provides the fastest fungal clearance and is the most appropriate initial therapy for his neurological symptoms.\n\nAnswer: Amphotericin B and flucytosine.", "correct": true, "gold": "Amphotericin B and flucytosine"}
{"qid": "med:336", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has an acute, painful, tender mass in the labia with fever, suggesting an infected Bartholin's gland abscess. The most appropriate next step is incision and drainage, as antibiotics alone are insufficient for an abscess.\n\nAnswer: Incision and drainage.", "correct": true, "gold": "Incision and drainage"}
{"qid": "med:336", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This presentation is classic for a **Bartholin gland abscess**: acute, painful unilateral labial swelling with erythema, edema, dyspareunia, and fever.\n\nManagement:\n- **Symptomatic Bartholin cyst** without infection can sometimes be managed conservatively.\n- But a **Bartholin abscess** requires **drainage**, and the preferred treatment is **incision and drainage with placement of a Word catheter** to allow continued drainage and prevent recurrence.\n- Antibiotics are not the primary treatment unless there is cellulitis, STI, pregnancy-related concern, or systemic infection.\n\nAnswer: Incision and drainage of the Bartholin gland abscess with placement of a Word catheter.", "correct": true, "gold": "Incision and drainage"}
{"qid": "med:336", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation (acute unilateral tender mass at the introitus with surrounding erythema/edema, fever, and dyspareunia) is diagnostic of an infected Bartholin-gland abscess. Source control is required before adjunctive measures; therefore the immediate next step is incision and drainage (typically followed by Word-catheter placement or marsupialization to prevent recurrence). Antibiotics or sitz baths alone are insufficient for an abscess with systemic signs.\nAnswer: incision & drainage (Bartholin abscess)", "correct": false, "gold": "Incision and drainage"}
{"qid": "med:336", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The presentation is classic for a Bartholin\u2019s gland abscess: acute painful labial swelling, surrounding erythema and edema, mild fever, and dyspareunia. For an abscess that is symptomatic, >2\u202fcm, and causing functional discomfort, the definitive management is incision and drainage (I&D). Warm compresses and analgesics can be tried for very small, early lesions, but this patient\u2019s size, pain, fever, and functional impairment warrant immediate I&D, often performed under local anesthesia with postoperative culture and appropriate antibiotics.\n\n**Answer:** Immediate incision and drainage of the Bartholin\u2019s gland abscess.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "Incision and drainage"}
{"qid": "med:336", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Most appropriate next step: Incision and drainage of the Bartholin gland abscess (with placement of a Word catheter)**\n\n**Rationale:**\nThis patient's presentation\u2014acute, painful, unilateral swelling of the labia with surrounding erythema and edema, dyspareunia, low-grade fever, and a tender mass at the posterior introitus (typical location of the Bartholin gland, at the 4 or 8 o'clock position of the labia majora)\u2014is classic for a **Bartholin gland abscess**.\n\n**Management approach:**\n- **Incision and drainage (I&D)** is the first-line treatment for a symptomatic, fluctuant Bartholin gland abscess.\n- A **Word catheter** should be placed after drainage and left in place for 4\u20136 weeks. This allows for continued drainage and epithelialization of a new tract, reducing the risk of recurrence.\n- Alternatively, **marsupialization** (suturing the cyst wall to the adjacent vaginal mucosa/skin to create a permanent open pouch) can be performed, especially for recurrent abscesses, though I&D with Word catheter placement is typically preferred as initial management due to its simplicity and effectiveness.\n- **Antibiotics** are not routinely required unless there is surrounding cellulitis, systemic signs of infection (e.g., high fever, sepsis), or the patient is immunocompromised. In this case, drainage is the priority; antibiotics could be considered as adjunct therapy given her low-grade fever, but the definitive step is I&D.\n- **Culture of the fluid** may be obtained to rule out gonorrhea or chlamydia, especially given her sexual activity and dyspareunia, but this is secondary to the immediate management of the abscess itself.\n\nAnswer: Incision and drainage of the Bartholin gland abscess with Word catheter placement.", "correct": true, "gold": "Incision and drainage"}
{"qid": "med:334", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Answer: Obtain serum TSH and free T4 levels.", "correct": false, "gold": "Methimazole and propranolol therapy"}
{"qid": "med:326", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "This patient has **primary dysmenorrhea**, which is characterized by painful menstrual cramps without underlying pelvic pathology. The diagnosis is supported by her age (typical onset 1\u20132 years after menarche when ovulatory cycles begin), regular cycles, normal physical examination (no abdominal tenderness, normal external genitalia), and classic symptoms of cramping pain localized to the lower abdomen and back occurring with menses. The family history of endometriosis is notable but does not change the initial management in an adolescent with a normal exam and regular cycles.\n\n**First-line treatment** for primary dysmenorrhea is **NSAIDs** (e.g., ibuprofen or naproxen), which inhibit prostaglandin production. They should be scheduled (not PRN) starting 1\u20132 days before menses or at the onset of cramping and continued for 2\u20133 days. Hormonal contraceptives (combined oral contraceptives or progestin-only methods) are also effective and are indicated if NSAIDs provide inadequate relief after 2\u20133 cycles, if the patient desires contraception, or if endometriosis is strongly suspected. Pelvic imaging or an internal pelvic exam is unnecessary at this time because there are no red flags for structural anomalies (she has been menstruating regularly for years) and she is not sexually active.\n\nSince she has only tried non-pharmacologic therapy (heating pads), the best next step is to initiate NSAIDs.\n\nAnswer: Start NSAIDs (nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs) such as ibuprofen or naproxen on a scheduled basis.", "correct": true, "gold": "NSAIDs"}
{"qid": "med:337", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient presents with thrombocytopenia (platelets 95,000), mucocutaneous bleeding (menorrhagia, gum bleeding, easy bruising, petechiae), and a normal PT/aPTT, suggesting a platelet disorder. The peripheral smear shows no platelet clumping or abnormal cells, making pseudothrombocytopenia or leukemia less likely. Given her age, sex, and sexual history, immune thrombocytopenia (ITP) is a strong possibility, but HIV and other viral infections can cause secondary thrombocytopenia. The next step is to test for HIV and other causes before proceeding to more invasive tests like bone marrow biopsy.\n\n**Answer:** HIV testing", "correct": false, "gold": "HIV and HCV testing"}
{"qid": "med:337", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient has **isolated thrombocytopenia with mucocutaneous bleeding** (menorrhagia, gingival bleeding, petechiae, ecchymoses), with **normal PT/aPTT** and otherwise normal CBC/smear, which is most consistent with **immune thrombocytopenia (ITP)**.\n\nIn adults, secondary causes of ITP should be evaluated, especially **HIV** and **hepatitis C**. Her history of **multiple sexual partners** is an important risk factor for HIV. HIV-associated thrombocytopenia can present as isolated thrombocytopenia.\n\nSo the **next diagnostic study** is **HIV testing** (typically an HIV-1/2 antigen/antibody assay; hepatitis C testing is also often part of the workup).\n\nAnswer: HIV testing (eg, HIV-1/2 antigen/antibody assay)", "correct": false, "gold": "HIV and HCV testing"}
{"qid": "med:337", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Answer: HIV testing (HIV antibody/antigen screening)**\n\n**Rationale:** This patient's presentation\u2014isolated thrombocytopenia with mucocutaneous bleeding (menorrhagia, gum bleeding, petechiae, ecchymoses), normal PT/aPTT, and normocytic RBCs on a normal-appearing smear\u2014is classic for immune thrombocytopenia (ITP). ITP is typically a diagnosis of exclusion after ruling out secondary causes.\n\nGiven her risk factor of being sexually active with multiple partners, **HIV infection** is an important and treatable secondary cause of thrombocytopenia that must be excluded before labeling this as primary ITP. HIV can cause immune-mediated platelet destruction similar to primary ITP, and identifying it changes management (antiretroviral therapy often improves the thrombocytopenia). Hepatitis C testing should also be considered for similar reasons, but HIV is the most directly suggested by her risk factors and is the most classically tested next step in this scenario.\n\n**Answer: HIV testing**", "correct": false, "gold": "HIV and HCV testing"}
{"qid": "med:335", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient is a young Caucasian with recurrent, unprovoked deep vein thrombosis. In this population, the most common inherited thrombophilia is the Factor\u202fV\u202fLeiden mutation. This mutation substitutes glutamine for arginine at position 506 of factor\u202fV, the cleavage site for activated protein\u202fC (APC). Consequently, factor\u202fVa is resistant to APC\u2011mediated inactivation, the prothrombinase complex remains active longer, thrombin generation is increased, and a hypercoagulable state results.\n\nAnswer: Activated protein C resistance due to the Factor V Leiden mutation (impaired cleavage/inactivation of factor Va by APC).", "correct": false, "gold": "Resistance to clotting factor degradation"}
{"qid": "med:337", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient's presentation (mucocutaneous bleeding, petechiae, ecchymoses) plus isolated mild thrombocytopenia with an otherwise normal smear and coagulation studies is consistent with immune thrombocytopenia (ITP). ITP is a diagnosis of exclusion; secondary causes must be ruled out before labeling a case idiopathic, especially in an adult. The history of multiple sexual partners supplies a clear risk factor for HIV, which is a well-documented etiology of secondary ITP via molecular mimicry and increased platelet clearance. Consequently, the immediate next diagnostic step is HIV serology (fourth-generation antigen/antibody assay). Other tests (bone-marrow biopsy, platelet-antibody assays, routine coagulation studies) are not indicated at this stage given the patient's age, normal hemoglobin/white-cell count, and non-invasive alternatives that can identify treatable secondary causes.\nAnswer: HIV serology (Ag/Ab assay)", "correct": false, "gold": "HIV and HCV testing"}
{"qid": "med:325", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient has marked hyperglycemia (HbA1c\u202f10.5\u202f%), severe hypernatremia, and a serum osmolality of 354\u202fmOsm/kg. In this setting, the urine osmolality of 400\u202fmOsm/kg is inappropriately low (the kidney should concentrate urine to >800\u202fmOsm/kg when serum osmolality is high). A urine osmolality that is isotonic or only mildly hypertonic to plasma indicates an osmotic diuresis rather than diabetes insipidus (which would produce very dilute urine, <200\u202fmOsm/kg). The uncontrolled hyperglycemia acts as an osmotic agent in the renal tubule, causing water loss and hypernatremia despite increased thirst. The metabolic alkalosis is explained by vomiting and thiazide use. These findings are classic for **hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS)** secondary to uncontrolled type\u202f2 diabetes mellitus.\n\nAnswer: Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS) due to uncontrolled diabetes mellitus (osmotic diuresis from severe hyperglycemia).", "correct": true, "gold": "Osmotic diuresis"}
{"qid": "med:339", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Emergent fasciotomy\n\nAnswer: Emergent surgical fasciotomy of the right leg", "correct": false, "gold": "Emergent fasciotomy"}
{"qid": "med:335", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "This patient presents with recurrent unprovoked deep vein thromboses (DVTs) at a young age, with no acquired risk factors. In a Caucasian individual, the most common inherited thrombophilia is **Factor V Leiden**.  \r\n\r\n**Pathophysiology:** Factor V Leiden is caused by a point mutation (Arg506Gln) in the factor V gene, which renders factor V resistant to cleavage by activated protein C (APC). This impairs the inactivation of factors Va and VIIIa, leading to prolonged thrombin generation and a hypercoagulable state, predisposing to venous thromboembolism.  \r\n\r\nAnswer: Resistance to activated protein C due to the Factor V Leiden mutation, resulting in impaired inactivation of factors Va and VIIIa and increased thrombin generation.", "correct": false, "gold": "Resistance to clotting factor degradation"}
{"qid": "med:339", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient has **acute compartment syndrome with limb ischemia** after a crush injury.\n\nKey findings:\n- Severe swelling after being pinned\n- Pain, sensory loss, paralysis\n- Pale, cold extremity\n- Absent distal pulses\n- Compartment pressure **35 mm Hg**, which is concerning and, with this clinical picture, is enough to act immediately\n\nCompartment syndrome is primarily a **clinical diagnosis**, and treatment should not be delayed for further testing when there are clear signs of neurovascular compromise. The definitive management is **emergent fasciotomy** to decompress all affected compartments and attempt limb salvage.\n\nAnswer: Immediate emergent fasciotomy of the right lower extremity.", "correct": true, "gold": "Emergent fasciotomy"}
