{"qid": "med:340", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The spirometry shows a reduced FEV1/FVC ratio (56%, normal >70%), indicating obstructive lung disease. The FEV1 is 48% of predicted, which is severely reduced. Given the history of heavy smoking, hyperinflation on chest X-ray, and clinical findings, this is consistent with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), specifically emphysema.\n\nAnswer: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) / emphysema", "correct": true, "gold": "Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)"}
{"qid": "med:340", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient has an **obstructive lung disease** pattern on spirometry:\n\n- **FEV1 = 48%** of predicted \u2192 reduced\n- **FVC = 85%** of predicted \u2192 relatively preserved\n- **FEV1/FVC = 56%** \u2192 decreased, confirming obstruction\n\nHis history and exam strongly suggest **COPD**, especially:\n- long smoking history\n- dyspnea and productive cough\n- hyperresonance and decreased breath sounds\n- chest x-ray with **hyperinflation**\n\nSeverity is classified by **FEV1** in COPD:\n- Mild: \u226580%\n- Moderate: 50\u201379%\n- **Severe: 30\u201349%**\n- Very severe: <30%\n\nSince his **FEV1 is 48%**, this is **severe COPD**. Given the hyperinflation and decreased breath sounds, the predominant subtype is **emphysema**.\n\nAnswer: **Severe COPD (emphysema-predominant obstructive lung disease)**", "correct": false, "gold": "Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)"}
{"qid": "med:337", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "The patient presents with isolated thrombocytopenia and mucocutaneous bleeding, raising concern for immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP). However, given her high-risk sexual history (multiple partners), secondary causes of ITP\u2014particularly **HIV** and **HCV** infections\u2014must be ruled out first. \r\n\r\n**Answer:** HIV antibody (fourth-generation) testing.", "correct": false, "gold": "HIV and HCV testing"}
{"qid": "med:340", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The patient\u2019s symptoms (dyspnea on exertion, productive cough), long smoking history, physical findings of hyperresonance and decreased breath sounds, radiographic evidence of hyperinflated lungs, and spirometric values showing a reduced FEV\u2081 (48\u202f% of predicted) with a preserved FVC (85\u202f%) and an FEV\u2081/FVC ratio below 70\u202f% (56\u202f%) are characteristic of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. The pattern of relatively normal FVC with low FEV\u2081 and marked hyperinflation points specifically toward emphysema, a phenotype of COPD.\n\n**Answer:** Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) \u2013 most likely emphysema.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)"}
{"qid": "med:339", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation is consistent with acute compartment syndrome in the setting of crush injury. Key diagnostic features include a tense, swollen compartment with pain on palpation, motor and sensory deficits, and an absolute intracompartmental pressure of 35 mm Hg (well above the 20\u201330 mm Hg threshold that produces ischemia). Although hard signs of ischemia are present, the history of prolonged compression plus the explicit provision of an elevated compartment pressure point to elevated pressure within a fixed fascial space as the primary, reversible driver of tissue loss.\nIn an otherwise stable trauma patient, the immediate next step is therefore definitive decompression rather than additional imaging or isolated vascular exploration. This is accomplished by a four-compartment fasciotomy of the affected leg, which restores perfusion, prevents further myoneural necrosis, and can be performed in the operating room at the same time any associated fractures are addressed.\nAnswer: four-compartment fasciotomy (right leg)", "correct": false, "gold": "Emergent fasciotomy"}
{"qid": "med:339", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has signs of acute compartment syndrome (pain, pallor, pulselessness, paresthesia, poikilothermia, and elevated compartment pressure >30 mm Hg). The next best step is emergent fasciotomy to decompress the compartments and restore perfusion.\n\nAnswer: Emergent fasciotomy", "correct": true, "gold": "Emergent fasciotomy"}
{"qid": "med:341", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "For a pregnant traveler to **Ethiopia**, the main concern is prophylaxis against **chloroquine-resistant *Plasmodium falciparum***.\n\nIn pregnancy:\n- **Doxycycline** is contraindicated\n- **Primaquine/tafenoquine** are contraindicated\n- **Atovaquone-proguanil** is generally not preferred in pregnancy due to limited safety data\n- **Mefloquine** is considered safe in pregnancy and is recommended for travel to chloroquine-resistant regions\n\nTherefore, the most suitable pre-exposure prophylaxis is:\n\n**Answer: Mefloquine**", "correct": true, "gold": "Mefloquine"}
{"qid": "med:341", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Chloroquine (or mefloquine, if chloroquine-resistant areas) \u2014 since Ethiopia has chloroquine-resistant P. falciparum, **mefloquine** is the most suitable choice for malaria prophylaxis in this pregnant patient.\n\nAnswer: Mefloquine", "correct": true, "gold": "Mefloquine"}
{"qid": "med:341", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient is pregnant (20 weeks gestation) and traveling to Ethiopia, a malaria-endemic area. For pregnant women, chloroquine is considered safe in all trimesters, but it is only effective in areas without chloroquine-resistant *Plasmodium falciparum*. In Ethiopia, there is significant chloroquine resistance, so chloroquine is not suitable. Mefloquine is the preferred drug for malaria prophylaxis in pregnant women traveling to areas with chloroquine-resistant malaria, as it is considered safe in the second and third trimesters (and can be used in the first trimester if needed). Doxycycline and atovaquone-proguanil are contraindicated in pregnancy. Therefore, mefloquine is the most suitable option.\n\nAnswer: Mefloquine", "correct": true, "gold": "Mefloquine"}
{"qid": "med:340", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient's long smoking history, exertional dyspnea, and mildly productive cough already suggest COPD. Physical findings of hyperresonance, globally reduced breath sounds, and radiographic hyperinflation further localize the pathology to alveolar septal destruction with air trapping. Spirometry supplies the physiologic confirmation: an FEV1/FVC ratio < 0.70 documents irreversible airflow obstruction, the FEV1 of 48 % predicted quantifies moderate-to-severe impairment, and the near-normal FVC excludes a significant restrictive component. These elements together are diagnostic of emphysema (the anatomic subset of COPD characterized by permanent enlargement of airspaces distal to the terminal bronchioles). Other obstructive entities (asthma, pure chronic bronchitis) are less likely given the patient\u2019s age, the chronicity of symptoms, and the clear radiographic and physical evidence of hyperinflation.\nAnswer: emphysema (COPD)", "correct": false, "gold": "Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)"}
{"qid": "med:340", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Based on the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings:\n\n**Key findings supporting the diagnosis:**\n1. **Risk factors**: 79-year-old with 25-year heavy smoking history\n2. **Symptoms**: Progressive dyspnea on exertion and productive cough\n3. **Physical exam**: Signs of air trapping (increased resonance to percussion, decreased breath sounds, pulmonary hyperinflation on imaging)\n4. **Spirometry**: FEV\u2081/FVC ratio of 56% (<70% confirms airflow obstruction); FEV\u2081 of 48% indicates severe obstruction; preserved FVC (85%) is consistent with obstructive (not restrictive) pattern\n\n**Analysis:**\nThe low FEV\u2081/FVC ratio (<0.70) establishes an **obstructive pattern**. The combination of chronic smoking history, progressive dyspnea, air trapping (hyperinflation), and irreversible airflow obstruction distinguishes COPD from asthma (which typically has variable/reversible obstruction and earlier onset). The physical findings of hyperresonance and decreased breath sounds suggest emphysematous changes, while the productive cough indicates chronic bronchitis components\u2014both fall under the COPD spectrum.\n\n**Severity**: FEV\u2081 of 48% indicates severe COPD (GOLD Stage III).\n\nAnswer: Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD)", "correct": true, "gold": "Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)"}
{"qid": "med:339", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\nThis patient presents with classic signs of acute compartment syndrome (ACS) following a crush injury: severe pain out of proportion, swelling, sensory deficits, motor weakness, absent distal pulses, pallor, and coolness. A measured compartment pressure of 35 mm Hg is highly concerning for ACS (typically, pressures >30 mm Hg or within 30 mm Hg of diastolic BP warrant intervention). The definitive treatment is emergent fasciotomy to relieve intracompartmental pressure and prevent irreversible muscle and nerve damage. Delaying this can lead to limb loss, rhabdomyolysis, and systemic complications. \r\n\r\nAnswer: Emergent fasciotomy", "correct": true, "gold": "Emergent fasciotomy"}
{"qid": "med:341", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient is a pregnant woman (second trimester) planning travel to Ethiopia, an area with endemic chloroquine-resistant Plasmodium falciparum (plus some P. vivax). Standard prophylactic agents such as doxycycline, atovaquone-proguanil, and primaquine are contraindicated or lack safety data in pregnancy. Chloroquine is safe but ineffective against resistant strains. Mefloquine remains the only CDC-recommended agent that is both effective against chloroquine-resistant falciparum and considered safe for use after the first trimester.\nAnswer: mefloquine\n(The final answer is placed after the step-by-step solution, as instructed.)", "correct": true, "gold": "Mefloquine"}
{"qid": "med:340", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\n\r\nThis patient's presentation is classic for **chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)**. Key findings supporting this diagnosis include:  \r\n\r\n- **Risk factor:** 25 years of heavy smoking, the most common cause of COPD.\r\n- **Symptoms:** Shortness of breath with moderate exertion and a slightly productive cough.\r\n- **Physical exam:** Increased resonance to percussion, decreased breath sounds, and crackles at the lung base, consistent with hyperinflation and airway disease.\r\n- **Imaging:** Chest radiography shows pulmonary hyperinflation.\r\n- **Spirometry:** Obstructive pattern with an FEV1/FVC ratio of 56% (<70%), FEV1 of 48% (moderate-to-severe obstruction), and a relatively preserved FVC (85%). \r\n\r\nThese findings are not consistent with restrictive lung diseases (which would show a reduced FVC) or asthma (which typically presents earlier and is more variable). \r\n\r\nAnswer: **Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)**", "correct": true, "gold": "Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)"}
