{"qid": "med:342", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has signs of a tension pneumothorax (hypotension, jugular venous distention, decreased breath sounds, hyperresonance, and a wound at the left chest). This is a life-threatening condition requiring immediate decompression.\n\nThe most appropriate next step is needle decompression of the left chest, followed by tube thoracostomy.\n\nAnswer: Needle decompression of the left chest (followed by tube thoracostomy).", "correct": true, "gold": "Needle decompression"}
{"qid": "med:342", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This trauma patient has **hypotension, hypoxemia, JVD, unilateral decreased breath sounds, and hyperresonance** after chest injury, which is classic for **tension pneumothorax** on the left.\n\nTension pneumothorax is a **clinical diagnosis** and requires **immediate decompression** without waiting for imaging.\n\nBecause there is also a chest wall wound, definitive immediate management is:\n- **Needle decompression** of the left chest, followed by\n- **Tube thoracostomy**\n\nAnswer: Immediate left-sided needle thoracostomy followed by chest tube placement.", "correct": false, "gold": "Needle decompression"}
{"qid": "med:339", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at this clinical scenario, the patient presents with classic signs of **acute compartment syndrome** in the right lower extremity following a crush injury:\n\n**Key findings supporting the diagnosis:**\n- **Mechanism**: Crush injury (pinned under motorcycle for 30 minutes)\n- **The \"6 P's\" present**: Pain, Pallor, Poikilothermia (cold), Paresthesia (decreased sensation), Paralysis (inability to move), and Pulselessness (absent DP/PT pulses)\n- **Objective measurement**: Compartment pressure of 35 mm Hg (normal <10 mm Hg; concerning >20-30 mm Hg)\n\n**Analysis:**\nWhile some guidelines use a delta P (diastolic BP minus compartment pressure) of <30 mm Hg or absolute pressure >40 mm Hg as surgical thresholds, **clinical findings trump absolute pressure numbers**. This patient has advanced compartment syndrome with neurovascular compromise (absent pulses, paralysis, and sensory deficits). \n\nThe measured pressure of 35 mm Hg is elevated and, combined with the clinical picture, confirms the diagnosis. This is a surgical emergency requiring immediate intervention to prevent irreversible muscle necrosis, nerve damage, and potential limb loss.\n\n**Why not other options:**\n- **Observation/monitoring**: Dangerous - \"time is muscle\" in compartment syndrome\n- **Elevation of the leg**: Contraindicated as it reduces perfusion\n- **Imaging/angiography**: Unnecessary delay; compartment syndrome is a clinical diagnosis\n- **Removing dressings**: Helpful but not definitive treatment\n\n**Definitive treatment:** Emergency surgical fasciotomy (decompression of all four compartments of the leg) is required immediately.\n\nAnswer: Emergent fasciotomy (immediate surgical decompression of the affected compartments).", "correct": true, "gold": "Emergent fasciotomy"}
{"qid": "med:341", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient is a pregnant traveler (20 weeks\u2019 gestation) going to Ethiopia, a region with chloroquine\u2011resistant *Plasmodium falciparum* malaria.  \n- **Doxycycline** and **primaquine** are contraindicated in pregnancy.  \n- **Atovaquone\u2011proguanil** has limited safety data and is generally not recommended for pregnant women.  \n- **Chloroquine** is safe in pregnancy but ineffective against the prevalent chloroquine\u2011resistant strains in Ethiopia.  \n- **Mefloquine** is the only agent with established safety in all trimesters and proven efficacy for prophylaxis against chloroquine\u2011resistant *P. falciparum*.\n\nAnswer: Mefloquine", "correct": true, "gold": "Mefloquine"}
{"qid": "med:342", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Most appropriate next step: Immediate needle decompression (needle thoracostomy) of the left chest**, performed at the **2nd intercostal space, midclavicular line** (or 4th/5th intercostal space, anterior axillary line), followed by chest tube placement.\n\n**Reasoning:**\nThis patient has classic signs of a **tension pneumothorax** secondary to penetrating chest trauma:\n- Hypotension (91/65) with tachypnea and hypoxia (SpO2 88%)\n- Jugular venous distention (impaired venous return due to increased intrathoracic pressure)\n- Decreased breath sounds and hyperresonance on the left (air trapped in pleural space)\n- Penetrating wound at the left 5th intercostal space, midclavicular line (likely violated the pleura)\n- Altered mental status (likely from hypoxia/hypotension)\n\nTension pneumothorax is a **immediately life-threatening emergency** that causes cardiovascular collapse due to mediastinal shift and impaired venous return to the heart. It must be treated based on clinical diagnosis alone\u2014**do not wait for imaging (chest X-ray or CT)**, as this delay could be fatal.\n\nThe immediate treatment is **needle decompression** to convert the tension pneumothorax to a simple pneumothorax, followed promptly by **tube thoracostomy (chest tube placement)** for definitive management.\n\nAnswer: Immediate needle decompression of the left chest (needle thoracostomy), followed by chest tube placement.", "correct": true, "gold": "Needle decompression"}
{"qid": "med:342", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Perform emergent needle thoracostomy (e.g., at the 2nd intercostal space midclavicular line) to decompress a suspected tension pneumothorax, followed by definitive chest tube placement.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Needle decompression"}
{"qid": "med:343", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Answer: Lumbar spinal stenosis", "correct": true, "gold": "Spinal stenosis"}
{"qid": "med:343", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient most likely has **lumbar spinal stenosis causing neurogenic claudication**.\n\nWhy:\n- **Leg numbness and weakness with walking** suggests claudication from nerve compression.\n- Pain is **relieved by flexion**: riding a bike or leaning forward on a shopping cart are classic for spinal stenosis because spinal flexion widens the spinal canal.\n- **Normal spine mobility and non-tender spine** make acute vertebral fracture less likely despite osteoporosis.\n- The worsening after moving furniture may be incidental or may have exacerbated chronic disease, but the key pattern is **posture-dependent leg symptoms**.\n\nThis is different from vascular claudication, which is relieved simply by rest and is not specifically improved by spinal flexion.\n\nAnswer: Lumbar spinal stenosis causing neurogenic claudication", "correct": true, "gold": "Spinal stenosis"}
{"qid": "med:343", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient's symptoms\u2014back pain worsened by standing/walking and relieved by sitting or leaning forward (like riding a bike or pushing a cart)\u2014are classic for lumbar spinal stenosis. The numbness and weakness in the legs during walking (neurogenic claudication) further support this. His age, osteoporosis, and degenerative changes make spinal stenosis likely. The non-tender spine and normal mobility argue against acute fracture or infection.\n\nAnswer: Lumbar spinal stenosis", "correct": true, "gold": "Spinal stenosis"}
{"qid": "med:343", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The patient's symptoms of worsening back pain with walking, leg numbness and weakness that improve with activities involving spinal flexion (riding a bike, pushing a cart), along with an older age and osteoporosis, point toward neurogenic claudication secondary to lumbar spinal stenosis rather than a fracture, infection, or vascular claudication. \n\nAnswer: Lumbar spinal stenosis.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "Spinal stenosis"}
{"qid": "med:343", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient's presentation centers on progressive back pain with neurogenic symptoms (leg numbness/weakness) provoked by walking/standing and relieved specifically by forward flexion (biking, leaning on a cart). These features constitute pseudoclaudication and are explained by dynamic narrowing of the lumbar canal that is worsened by extension and eased by flexion.  \nAge >60, degenerative risk factors, and a non-tender spine with preserved range of motion further support chronic lumbar stenosis rather than acute fracture, infection, or malignancy. Vascular claudication is excluded by the clear postural (not purely exertional) relief.  \nThus the unifying diagnosis is lumbar spinal stenosis.\nAnswer: lumbar spinal stenosis (neurogenic claudication)", "correct": true, "gold": "Spinal stenosis"}
{"qid": "med:342", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The vignette describes a patient with recent penetrating chest trauma and acute respiratory distress, hypoxia, hypotension, JVD, unilateral absence of breath sounds, and hyper-resonance to percussion. These findings establish the diagnosis of left-sided tension pneumothorax (air under pressure collapses the lung, impairs venous return, and produces obstructive shock). Airway patency is already present (patient follows commands and opens eyes spontaneously), so attention moves immediately to breathing.\nIn the presence of hemodynamic instability, the fastest way to convert a tension pneumothorax into a simple pneumothorax is needle thoracostomy. A large-bore needle is inserted into the second intercostal space, mid-clavicular line (or fifth intercostal space, anterior axillary line), allowing trapped air to escape. This maneuver restores venous return and oxygenation within seconds and is therefore the immediate next step. Tube thoracostomy is performed next to evacuate residual air and prevent recurrence, but it requires additional time and equipment and is therefore not the first action.\nAlternative considerations such as cardiac tamponade are excluded by the presence of hyper-resonance and absent breath sounds; an occlusive dressing would be appropriate only for a large open \u201csucking\u201d chest wound without tension physiology. Thus needle decompression is the single most appropriate immediate intervention.\nAnswer: needle decompression (2nd ICS, left chest)", "correct": true, "gold": "Needle decompression"}
{"qid": "med:345", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Hemophilia A is an X-linked recessive disorder.\n\n- The father is affected: genotype = X\u02b0Y\n- Since all three daughters are unaffected, each daughter must receive:\n  - the affected X\u02b0 from the father\n  - a normal X from the mother\n\nSo every unaffected daughter must be a carrier: X\u1d34X\u02b0.\n\nThus, the probability that the second daughter is a carrier is 100%.\n\nAnswer: 100%", "correct": true, "gold": "100%"}
{"qid": "med:342", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\nThis patient presents with classic signs of a **tension pneumothorax** following a penetrating chest injury:  \r\n- **Trauma:** Penetrating wound at the left 5th intercostal space, midclavicular line.\r\n- **Respiratory distress:** Decreased breath sounds, hyperresonance on the left, hypoxia (SpO2 88%), and tachypnea.\r\n- **Obstructive shock:** Hypotension (91/65 mm Hg), tachycardia, and jugular venous distention (due to impaired venous return from mediastinal shift).\r\n- **Altered mental status:** Likely secondary to hypoperfusion and hypoxia. \r\n\r\nTension pneumothorax is a life-threatening emergency that requires immediate decompression to relieve intrapleural pressure. Waiting for imaging or other interventions can lead to cardiac arrest. Needle decompression at the **2nd intercostal space, midclavicular line** on the affected side is the most appropriate next step, followed by definitive placement of a chest tube. \r\n\r\nAnswer: **Immediate needle decompression of the left chest at the 2nd intercostal space, midclavicular line.**", "correct": true, "gold": "Needle decompression"}
{"qid": "med:345", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "Hemophilia A is an X-linked recessive disorder.  \n- The father has hemophilia, so his genotype is X^h Y.  \n- The mother is unaffected, but since the father is affected and the daughters are unaffected, the mother must be a carrier (X^H X^h) to produce unaffected daughters (they get X^H from her).  \n- For each daughter: she inherits X^h from father and either X^H or X^h from mother.  \n  - If she gets X^H from mother \u2192 not a carrier (X^H X^h is actually carrier, wait\u2014 careful: X^H X^h is carrier, but she is unaffected because she has one normal allele).  \n  - Actually, all daughters get X^h from father. If mother gives X^H, daughter is X^H X^h (carrier). If mother gives X^h, daughter is X^h X^h (affected). But the problem says all three daughters are unaffected, so they must have received X^H from mother.  \n- Therefore, each daughter is a carrier with probability 1 (since they are unaffected and father is affected, they must have the normal allele from mother, making them carriers).  \n\nAnswer: 1", "correct": false, "gold": "100%"}
{"qid": "med:345", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Answer: 100% (probability = 1)**\n\n**Reasoning:**\n\nHemophilia A is an X-linked recessive disorder. \n\n- The father is affected, so his genotype is **X^h Y** (he has one mutant allele on his single X chromosome).\n- The mother is unaffected, with genotype **X^H X^H** (assuming no history of the disease in her family, i.e., not a carrier).\n\nFor any daughter:\n- She **must** inherit the father's X chromosome (since fathers pass their X chromosome to all daughters and their Y chromosome to all sons).\n- She will inherit an X^H from her mother.\n\nTherefore, **every daughter** of an affected father will have the genotype **X^H X^h** \u2014 making her an obligate carrier, regardless of birth order.\n\nSince the daughters are unaffected (they don't show hemophilia symptoms, consistent with being heterozygous carriers rather than affected, which would require two X^h alleles), this confirms they carry one normal and one mutant allele.\n\n**Answer: The probability that the second daughter is a carrier = 1 (100%)**", "correct": true, "gold": "100%"}
{"qid": "med:343", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\nThis patient's presentation is classic for **lumbar spinal stenosis**. Key findings include:  \r\n\r\n- **Age & risk factors:** 65-year-old with osteoporosis, diabetes, smoking, and alcohol use, all of which contribute to degenerative spine disease.\r\n- **Neurogenic claudication:** Numbness and weakness in the legs while walking, which is hallmark for spinal stenosis.\r\n- **Positional relief:** Pain improves with flexion-based activities (riding a bike or pushing a grocery cart), as these increase the diameter of the spinal canal and relieve nerve root compression. This distinguishes it from vascular claudication, which is not posture-dependent.\r\n- **History:** Gradual onset of back pain, worsened by a mechanical strain (moving furniture), with a non-tender spine on exam. \r\n\r\nAnswer: Lumbar spinal stenosis", "correct": true, "gold": "Spinal stenosis"}
{"qid": "med:343", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at this clinical presentation, the key diagnostic clues are:\n\n1. **Age and risk factors**: 65-year-old with vascular risk factors (smoking, diabetes, dyslipidemia) and osteoporosis\n2. **Classic symptom pattern**: Back pain with leg numbness/weakness during walking that **improves with forward flexion**\n3. **Pathognomonic \"shopping cart sign\"**: The patient specifically notes improvement when pushing a cart (forward flexed posture) and riding a bike (flexed position)\n4. **Physical exam**: Non-tender spine with normal mobility argues against fracture, infection, or inflammatory causes\n\n**Analysis of the differential:**\n- **Vertebral compression fracture**: Possible given osteoporosis and moving furniture, but would expect focal spinal tenderness and pain worsened by movement, not the specific positional relief pattern described\n- **Epidural abscess/osteomyelitis**: Low-grade fever and diabetes are risk factors, but typically presents with severe localized spinal tenderness and constant pain, not pain relieved by flexion\n- **Peripheral artery disease (vascular claudication)**: Risk factors present, but vascular claudication improves with standing still (not specifically sitting/flexion) and wouldn't explain the \"shopping cart sign\"\n- **Disc herniation**: Usually acute, pain worsened by flexion (not improved), and often has positive straight leg raise or focal neurologic deficits\n- **Lumbar spinal stenosis**: Fits perfectly - degenerative narrowing of the spinal canal causes neurogenic claudication (leg symptoms with walking/standing) that improves with sitting or forward flexion (which increases canal diameter)\n\nThe combination of neurogenic claudication symptoms with relief in flexed positions is diagnostic of lumbar spinal stenosis.\n\nAnswer: Lumbar spinal stenosis (neurogenic claudication)", "correct": true, "gold": "Spinal stenosis"}
{"qid": "med:341", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Mefloquine is the most suitable agent for pre-exposure malaria prophylaxis in this pregnant patient traveling to Ethiopia. \r\n\r\n**Reasoning:**\r\n- **Ethiopia has chloroquine-resistant *Plasmodium falciparum***, so chloroquine is not effective.\r\n- **Mefloquine is considered safe in the second and third trimesters** of pregnancy and provides reliable protection against chloroquine-resistant strains.\r\n- **Alternatives are contraindicated or not recommended:** doxycycline is contraindicated in pregnancy (risk to fetal teeth and bone), primaquine is contraindicated (risk of hemolysis in G6PD deficiency), and atovaquone-proguanil lacks sufficient safety data for use in pregnancy.\r\n\r\nAnswer: Mefloquine", "correct": true, "gold": "Mefloquine"}
{"qid": "med:347", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Fall on an outstretched hand with tenderness in the anatomic snuffbox strongly suggests a **scaphoid fracture**, even if initial swelling is mild. These fractures can be occult on initial x-ray and have risk of **avascular necrosis** due to tenuous blood supply.\n\nBest next step:\n- **Obtain wrist/hand radiographs**\n- **Immobilize immediately in a thumb spica splint/cast**, even if x-rays are negative, with repeat imaging or follow-up if needed\n\nAnswer: Immediate thumb spica immobilization with wrist radiographs for suspected scaphoid fracture.", "correct": false, "gold": "Radiograph of the wrist"}
{"qid": "med:347", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Best next step: Obtain wrist radiographs, including dedicated scaphoid views.**\n\n**Rationale:**\nThe mechanism of injury (fall on an outstretched hand), along with point tenderness in the **anatomic snuffbox** (the region bounded by the extensor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis tendons), is classic for a **scaphoid fracture**. This is the most commonly fractured carpal bone and is particularly prone to injury in this exact mechanism (axial load on a hyperextended, radially deviated wrist).\n\n**Key management points:**\n- **Imaging:** Plain radiographs of the wrist with scaphoid-specific views should be obtained first, as this is the initial, most accessible imaging modality.\n- **Occult fractures:** Scaphoid fractures are notorious for being radiographically occult in the first 1\u20132 weeks after injury. If initial X-rays are negative but clinical suspicion remains high (as in this case, given point tenderness and functional impairment), the patient should be treated presumptively with **thumb spica splinting/casting** and referred for repeat imaging (X-ray, or MRI/CT if available) in 1\u20132 weeks.\n- **Why this matters:** Missing a scaphoid fracture can lead to avascular necrosis (due to the tenuous, retrograde blood supply from the dorsal carpal branch of the radial artery) and subsequent nonunion, so a high index of suspicion and appropriate immobilization while awaiting definitive diagnosis is critical\u2014even if initial films are negative.\n\nAnswer: Obtain wrist radiographs with dedicated scaphoid views to evaluate for a scaphoid fracture, given the anatomic snuffbox tenderness.", "correct": false, "gold": "Radiograph of the wrist"}
{"qid": "med:337", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at this case, we have a 28-year-old woman with isolated thrombocytopenia (platelets 95,000/mL) presenting with mucocutaneous bleeding (menorrhagia, gum bleeding, petechiae, ecchymoses). The key features are:\n\n1. **Isolated thrombocytopenia** with normal hemoglobin, WBC, PT, and aPTT\n2. **Peripheral smear** confirms thrombocytopenia without schistocytes (ruling out TTP/HUS/DIC), without platelet clumping (ruling out pseudothrombocytopenia), and with normal RBC morphology\n3. **Clinical presentation** consistent with immune thrombocytopenia (ITP)\n4. **Risk factor**: Multiple sexual partners (HIV risk)\n\n**Analysis of the differential:**\n- This is classic for **ITP** (primary or secondary)\n- ITP is a diagnosis of exclusion\n- Before confirming ITP, secondary causes must be ruled out, particularly **viral infections** (HIV, HCV) given her risk factors\n- Bone marrow biopsy is NOT indicated in young patients with typical isolated thrombocytopenia (reserved for atypical features, other cytopenias, age >60, or treatment failure)\n- Antiplatelet antibody testing has poor sensitivity/specificity and is not routinely recommended\n- Pseudothrombocytopenia has been excluded by the peripheral smear showing individual platelets rather than clumping\n\n**Next step:** Since the peripheral smear confirms true thrombocytopenia and the presentation is otherwise typical for ITP, the priority is to test for secondary causes. Given her history of multiple sexual partners, **HIV testing** (HIV antibody/antigen test) is the most important next diagnostic study. HCV testing should also be performed.\n\nAnswer: HIV antibody testing (or HIV screening) should be performed next to evaluate for HIV-associated immune thrombocytopenia.", "correct": false, "gold": "HIV and HCV testing"}
{"qid": "med:345", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "Hemophilia A is an X-linked recessive disorder. The affected father therefore has the genotype \\(X^h Y\\) and must transmit his single X chromosome (bearing the disease-causing allele) to every daughter. The mother is phenotypically unaffected; consequently the only two biologically plausible maternal genotypes are \\(X^H X^H\\) (non-carrier) or \\(X^H X^h\\) (carrier).\n- If the mother is not a carrier she transmits \\(X^H\\) to every daughter, yielding the genotype \\(X^H X^h\\). Every daughter is therefore an obligate heterozygote (carrier) and is clinically unaffected.\n- If the mother is a carrier she transmits \\(X^H\\) or \\(X^h\\) with equal probability. A daughter who receives \\(X^h\\) from the mother becomes \\(X^h X^h\\) and is affected; a daughter who receives \\(X^H\\) becomes \\(X^H X^h\\) and is an unaffected carrier. Thus, conditional on a daughter being unaffected, she must again be an obligate heterozygote.\nIn both maternal genotypes the observation that a daughter is unaffected therefore implies that she received exactly one copy of the mutant allele\u2014from her father\u2014and is consequently a carrier. Because this conclusion holds for every daughter independently of the others, the probability that the second daughter is a carrier equals 1.\n(The existence of three unaffected daughters supplies no additional information that alters the transmission logic above.)\nAnswer: 1", "correct": false, "gold": "100%"}
{"qid": "med:334", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The infant\u2019s tachycardia, irritability, diaphoresis, diarrhea, weight loss, and midline neck swelling (goiter) in a neonate of a mother with Graves\u2019 disease are classic for neonatal Graves\u2019 disease caused by transplacental thyroid\u2011stimulating immunoglobulins. Maternal PTU is cleared after delivery, leaving unopposed antibody stimulation of the infant\u2019s thyroid. Management requires an antithyroid agent to block hormone synthesis (methimazole is preferred over PTU because of hepatotoxicity risk) and a \u03b2\u2011blocker (propranolol) to control the adrenergic symptoms (tachycardia, irritability).\n\nAnswer: Start methimazole and propranolol.", "correct": false, "gold": "Methimazole and propranolol therapy"}
{"qid": "med:347", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient's tenderness is in the anatomical snuffbox, which is concerning for a scaphoid fracture. Scaphoid fractures are common in falls on an outstretched hand and can be missed on initial X-rays. The best next step is to immobilize the wrist in a thumb spica splint and obtain follow-up imaging (or repeat X-rays in 10\u201314 days if initial X-rays are negative).  \n\nAnswer: Immobilize in a thumb spica splint and arrange follow-up.", "correct": false, "gold": "Radiograph of the wrist"}
