{"qid": "med:349", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient has **episodes of neurologic deficits separated in time and affecting different CNS locations**, with prior heat sensitivity (**Uhthoff phenomenon**), upper motor neuron signs (spasticity, hyperreflexia), sensory deficits, gait impairment, and urinary symptoms \u2014 all highly suggestive of **multiple sclerosis**.\n\nThe most appropriate next diagnostic step is **MRI of the brain and spinal cord with gadolinium** to look for demyelinating lesions disseminated in space and time.\n\nAnswer: **MRI of the brain and spinal cord with gadolinium contrast**", "correct": false, "gold": "MRI of the brain and spine"}
{"qid": "med:349", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient's symptoms (relapsing-remitting neurological deficits, upper motor neuron signs, sensory loss, urinary incontinence, and prior episode of weakness worsened by heat) strongly suggest multiple sclerosis. The most appropriate next step in diagnosis is MRI of the brain and spinal cord to look for demyelinating plaques.\n\nAnswer: MRI of the brain and spinal cord", "correct": false, "gold": "MRI of the brain and spine"}
{"qid": "med:347", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation of FOOSH trauma followed by persistent dorsal-radial wrist pain and focal tenderness precisely localized to the floor of the anatomic snuffbox is highly suggestive of scaphoid fracture. No imaging has yet been performed, so the immediate priority in the ED is to obtain plain radiographs (standard wrist series plus dedicated scaphoid views) to confirm the diagnosis, assess displacement, and exclude other carpal injuries.  \n\nIf radiographs are negative yet clinical suspicion remains high, empiric immobilization in a thumb-spica splint and outpatient follow-up radiographs (or advanced imaging) in 10\u201314 days would be appropriate; however, that step cannot be taken until radiographs have first been obtained.  \n\nThe additional historical details (prior mononucleosis, insulin-dependent diabetes, NSAID use) do not alter the immediate diagnostic sequence.  \n\nAnswer: wrist radiographs (scaphoid views)", "correct": false, "gold": "Radiograph of the wrist"}
{"qid": "med:342", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient presents with hypotension, jugular venous distention, unilateral decreased breath sounds, and hyperresonance\u2014classic findings of a tension pneumothorax. Although there is an open chest wound, the presence of JVD indicates that the pleural pressure is elevated and causing obstructive shock. In a trauma patient with suspected tension pneumothorax, the immediate life\u2011saving maneuver is rapid decompression; one should not wait for imaging or attempt to seal the wound first. The standard approach is needle thoracostomy (needle decompression) followed by formal chest tube placement.\n\nAnswer: Immediate needle decompression (needle thoracostomy) of the left hemithorax.", "correct": true, "gold": "Needle decompression"}
