{"qid": "med:350", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient has an acute anteroseptal STEMI with hypotension and signs of left ventricular dysfunction. In the first 24 hours after an MI, the **most dangerous and common cause of early death** is a **ventricular arrhythmia**, especially **ventricular fibrillation**.\n\nAlthough mechanical complications can occur after MI, they usually develop a bit later (typically days 1\u20137), whereas **VF can occur suddenly within the first 24 hours** and is the most concerning immediate development.\n\nAnswer: Ventricular fibrillation (a lethal ventricular arrhythmia)", "correct": false, "gold": "Hemoptysis"}
{"qid": "med:349", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation is consistent with multiple sclerosis (prior self-limited episode of hemiparesis with heat exacerbation, now a new myelopathic attack producing quadriparesis, hyperreflexia, sensory level, and bladder dysfunction). Acute or subacute myelopathy nevertheless requires urgent imaging to exclude compressive lesions (disc herniation, tumor, abscess) that constitute neurosurgical emergencies. Because the current deficits localize to the cervical cord, the single most appropriate immediate diagnostic study is MRI of the cervical spine (with and without gadolinium). Brain MRI and CSF analysis are obtained subsequently to satisfy dissemination-in-space criteria once a compressive cause has been ruled out.\nAnswer: MRI of cervical spine (r/o compression)", "correct": false, "gold": "MRI of the brain and spine"}
