{"qid": "med:347", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \nThis 14-year-old boy presents with a FOOSH injury, persistent pain, functional loss, and tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox (between the extensor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis). These findings are highly concerning for a **scaphoid fracture**, the most common carpal bone fracture in this age group. Initial radiographs can be negative in up to 30% of cases, but obtaining imaging is essential to confirm the diagnosis and guide management. A scaphoid series (PA, lateral, oblique, and dedicated scaphoid views) is the appropriate first diagnostic step.\n\nAnswer: Obtain a scaphoid series (anteroposterior, lateral, oblique, and dedicated scaphoid views) of the right wrist.", "correct": false, "gold": "Radiograph of the wrist"}
{"qid": "med:350", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Most concerning development: Ventricular septal rupture (acquired VSD) leading to acute biventricular failure and cardiogenic shock**\n\n**Reasoning:**\n\nThis patient has an **anteroseptal MI** (ST elevation in V1\u2013V2), which localizes to the **LAD territory**, specifically involving the septal perforator branches supplying the interventricular septum.\n\nKey findings already present suggest a large infarct with hemodynamic compromise:\n- Hypotension (90/56 mmHg)\n- Relative bradycardia (58/min) with tachypnea\n- Elevated JVP (2.5 cm above sternal angle)\n- Soft S1/S2 with S4 (impaired ventricular compliance/early failure)\n- Bilateral basal crackles (pulmonary venous congestion)\n\nThese findings indicate the patient is already trending toward **cardiogenic shock**, and large anteroseptal infarcts carry a high risk of **necrosis and rupture of the interventricular septum**, typically occurring within the first 24 hours to a week after infarction (with a peak incidence around day 1\u20135, but earliest and often most catastrophic ruptures can occur within the first 24 hours in large infarcts).\n\n**Why VSD is the most concerning complication here:**\n- Anteroseptal MI directly damages the interventricular septum.\n- Septal rupture produces a new, harsh **holosystolic murmur** at the left sternal border, often with a palpable thrill, and causes acute left-to-right shunting.\n- This leads to **sudden hemodynamic deterioration**, biventricular failure, and cardiogenic shock \u2014 a life-threatening surgical emergency.\n- It is distinguished from papillary muscle rupture (more typical of inferior/posterior MI causing acute mitral regurgitation).\n\nOther complications like free wall rupture (causing tamponade) and complete heart block are also possible with anterior MI, but **VSD is the classic and most feared mechanical complication specifically associated with anteroseptal infarction**, and this patient's presentation (impending pump failure) puts him at high risk for this feared complication in the next 24 hours.\n\n**Answer: Ventricular septal rupture (VSD) with resultant acute cardiogenic shock**", "correct": false, "gold": "Hemoptysis"}
{"qid": "med:350", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient presents with an acute anteroseptal ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) complicated by signs of right ventricular infarction (hypotension, elevated jugular venous pressure, clear lungs) and left heart failure (crackles). The most concerning development over the next 24 hours would be **cardiogenic shock** or **free wall rupture**, but given the specific presentation with right ventricular involvement and the use of thrombolytics, the most immediately life-threatening concern is **cardiac rupture** (free wall rupture or ventricular septal rupture), which is more common with anteroseptal infarctions and thrombolytic therapy, typically occurring within the first 24 hours.\n\nHowever, the most common cause of death in the first 24 hours is **pump failure/cardiogenic shock**, but the question asks for the \"most concerning development.\" Given the high risk of mechanical complications (especially rupture) with thrombolysis in an anteroseptal MI, that is the most feared.\n\n**Answer:** Cardiac rupture (free wall rupture or ventricular septal rupture)", "correct": false, "gold": "Hemoptysis"}
{"qid": "med:349", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient\u2019s history of two distinct episodes of neurological dysfunction (right lower\u2011extremity weakness last summer that resolved, and current upper\u2011extremity weakness with urinary incontinence) together with upper\u2011motor\u2011neuron signs (spasticity, hyperreflexia, absent abdominal reflex) and dorsal\u2011column sensory loss (decreased vibration and fine touch) is classic for a demyelinating disease, most likely **relapsing\u2011remitting multiple sclerosis** (dissemination in time and space). Heat sensitivity (worse when outside) further supports MS.  \n\nTo confirm the diagnosis and identify active or prior demyelinating lesions, the next step is neuroimaging. MRI of the brain and cervical spine (with gadolinium) is the most sensitive test for detecting white\u2011matter plaques and ruling out alternative structural causes (e.g., compressive myelopathy). CSF analysis or evoked potentials are reserved for cases where MRI is inconclusive.  \n\nAnswer: MRI of the brain and spinal cord (cervical spine) with gadolinium.", "correct": false, "gold": "MRI of the brain and spine"}
