{"qid": "med:351", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This trauma patient has **hemoperitoneum with peritonitis and hemodynamic instability despite fluid resuscitation**, most concerning for ongoing intra-abdominal hemorrhage (eg, splenic injury). An unstable trauma patient with a positive FAST/bedside ultrasound should go **immediately to surgery**, not CT.\n\nAnswer: Immediate exploratory laparotomy (emergent operative management)", "correct": true, "gold": "Exploratory laparotomy"}
{"qid": "med:351", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Emergent exploratory laparotomy (with splenectomy/splenic repair for suspected splenic injury) \u2014 the patient is hemodynamically unstable despite fluid resuscitation, so immediate surgical intervention is indicated rather than further imaging or observation.\n\nAnswer: Emergent exploratory laparotomy", "correct": true, "gold": "Exploratory laparotomy"}
{"qid": "med:351", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has unstable vital signs (hypotension, tachycardia) with evidence of hemoperitoneum after trauma, likely from splenic injury. Despite fluid resuscitation, he is deteriorating. The best next step is emergency laparotomy for hemorrhage control, not further imaging or non-operative management.\n\nAnswer: Emergency laparotomy.", "correct": false, "gold": "Exploratory laparotomy"}
