{"qid": "med:392", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient's symptoms\u2014slowly progressive vision loss, especially affecting night driving\u2014suggest possible retinal toxicity. Given his history of taking a low-potency typical antipsychotic (likely thioridazine or chlorpromazine), this could be drug-induced retinopathy. The best first step is to discontinue the offending antipsychotic and refer to an ophthalmologist for a full evaluation, including retinal examination.\n\nAnswer: Discontinue the antipsychotic and refer to ophthalmology.", "correct": false, "gold": "Slit-lamp examination"}
{"qid": "med:392", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient\u2019s slowly progressive visual impairment with worsening night driving in the setting of a **low-potency typical antipsychotic** suggests **thioridazine-induced retinopathy** (or another phenothiazine-related ocular toxicity). Thioridazine is classically associated with **pigmentary retinopathy**, causing gradual vision loss and impaired night vision.\n\nThe best first step is to **stop the offending medication and switch to another antipsychotic** in coordination with his psychiatrist. Ocular toxicity may progress and can become irreversible if the drug is continued.\n\nAnswer: Discontinue the low-potency typical antipsychotic (eg, thioridazine) and switch to an alternative antipsychotic.", "correct": false, "gold": "Slit-lamp examination"}
{"qid": "med:388", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient has a solitary colonic polyp detected on screening colonoscopy. Colonic adenomas are subclassified by the relative proportions of their glandular architecture:\n- tubular adenoma: <25 % villous component (i.e., >75 % tubular glands);\n- tubulovillous adenoma: 25\u201375 % villous component;\n- villous adenoma: >75 % villous component.\n\nThe biopsy description of exactly 75 % tubular architecture therefore implies a 25 % villous component, meeting the threshold definition of a tubulovillous adenoma. No invasive carcinoma is described, and the remainder of the colonoscopic examination is normal, excluding polyposis syndromes. The family history of colorectal cancer is a risk factor for adenoma formation but does not alter the histologic classification of the single lesion that was removed.\n\nAnswer: tubulovillous adenoma (adenomatous polyp)", "correct": true, "gold": "Adenomatous polyp"}
{"qid": "med:392", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Answer: Discontinue the low-potency typical antipsychotic (e.g., thioridazine/chlorpromazine) and switch to an alternative antipsychotic.**\n\n**Reasoning:**\nThis patient's chronic use of a low-potency typical antipsychotic (most classically **thioridazine** or **chlorpromazine**) is associated with **pigmentary retinopathy** and/or **lenticular/corneal deposits (cataracts)**, both of which cause progressive visual disturbance, particularly affecting night vision due to retinal pigment changes or lens opacities.\n\nGiven:\n- Chronic exposure to a low-potency typical antipsychotic\n- Progressive visual decline over months\n- Symptoms affecting night vision (suggestive of retinal toxicity, especially with thioridazine, which is well known for causing irreversible pigmentary retinopathy even at therapeutic doses)\n\nThe best initial step is to **identify the potential drug-induced etiology and discontinue/switch the offending medication** to prevent further irreversible retinal or lens damage, followed by ophthalmologic evaluation (dilated fundoscopic exam) to assess the extent of damage.\n\nAnswer: Discontinue the low-potency typical antipsychotic and switch to an alternative antipsychotic medication.", "correct": false, "gold": "Slit-lamp examination"}
{"qid": "med:386", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "This patient presents with classic features of **rheumatoid arthritis (RA)**, including symmetric polyarthritis (affecting proximal fingers, wrists, elbows, and knees), morning stiffness that improves with activity, fatigue, and unintentional weight loss. His symptoms have worsened despite NSAID therapy, indicating the need for disease-modifying treatment.  \n\n**First-line therapy:** Methotrexate is the standard first-line DMARD for RA due to its efficacy, safety profile, and long-term benefits. While his poorly controlled diabetes requires monitoring (especially for liver function and potential effects on glucose), it is not a contraindication to methotrexate.  \n\nAnswer: Methotrexate", "correct": true, "gold": "Methotrexate"}
{"qid": "med:388", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Answer: Tubular adenoma}}\n\\]", "correct": false, "gold": "Adenomatous polyp"}
{"qid": "med:388", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The polyp is an adenomatous lesion. Adenomas are classified by the percentage of villous architecture:  \n- Tubular adenoma: <25% villous (i.e., >75% tubular)  \n- Tubulovillous adenoma: 25\u201375% villous  \n- Villous adenoma: >75% villous  \n\nA biopsy showing 75% tubular architecture corresponds to 25% villous tissue, which meets the definition of a tubulovillous adenoma.\n\nAnswer: Tubulovillous adenoma.", "correct": false, "gold": "Adenomatous polyp"}
{"qid": "med:386", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient presents with symmetric polyarthritis involving small joints (proximal fingers, wrists) and larger joints (elbows, knees), morning stiffness lasting >30 minutes that improves with activity, systemic symptoms (fatigue, weight loss), and inadequate response to NSAIDs. This clinical picture is classic for **rheumatoid arthritis (RA)**.\n\nKey diagnostic features include:\n- Symmetric inflammatory polyarthritis (PIP, MCP, wrists)\n- Morning stiffness improving with activity\n- Systemic symptoms (fatigue, weight loss)\n- Progressive disease despite NSAID therapy\n\nAccording to current ACR/EULAR guidelines, the first-line treatment for RA is a disease-modifying antirheumatic drug (DMARD). **Methotrexate** is the preferred initial conventional synthetic DMARD for moderate to high disease activity RA due to its efficacy, long track record, and ability to slow disease progression. While the patient has poorly controlled type 2 diabetes, this is not a contraindication to methotrexate therapy (though liver function monitoring is important).\n\nOther options like hydroxychloroquine or sulfasalazine are less effective for moderate-severe disease. Biologics (TNF inhibitors) are typically reserved for patients who fail methotrexate. Corticosteroids may be used as bridge therapy for symptom control but are not first-line disease-modifying therapy.\n\nAnswer: Methotrexate", "correct": true, "gold": "Methotrexate"}
{"qid": "med:393", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Hepatitis A IgM antibody** (anti-HAV IgM serology)\n\nGiven the acute hepatitis picture (elevated AST/ALT, elevated bilirubin, dark urine, nausea/vomiting, fatigue) in a college student with likely fecal-oral exposure risk (foreign exchange student, communal living), acute Hepatitis A is high on the differential. However, given his risk factors (unprotected sex with multiple partners, IV drug use potential), a full hepatitis panel should be considered - but if choosing the single best initial test given the acute presentation, **Hepatitis A IgM** is most appropriate as it's the most common cause of acute viral hepatitis in this age group with this presentation.\n\nAnswer: Hepatitis A IgM antibody test", "correct": false, "gold": "Viral core antibody"}
{"qid": "med:393", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This patient has fatigue, nausea/vomiting, weight loss, mild fever, and elevated AST/ALT with elevated bilirubin, suggesting **acute viral hepatitis**. His major risk factors are **multiple unprotected sexual partners** and likely substance use. The refusal of genital exam hints at possible concern for an STI, but the systemic findings point most strongly to **hepatitis B infection**, which is sexually transmitted.\n\nThe best diagnostic test for **acute hepatitis B** is **serologic testing for hepatitis B surface antigen and IgM anti-HBc**. The marker most specific for acute infection is **IgM antibody to hepatitis B core antigen**.\n\nAnswer: **Serologic testing for acute hepatitis B infection \u2014 especially IgM anti-HBc (with HBsAg).**", "correct": false, "gold": "Viral core antibody"}
{"qid": "med:393", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient's symptoms (fatigue, nausea, vomiting, weight loss), elevated liver enzymes (AST, ALT), elevated bilirubin, and history of unprotected sex with multiple partners suggest a possible acute hepatitis, likely viral (hepatitis A, B, or C). The urine drug screen is positive for multiple substances, but the key concern here is the potential for a sexually transmitted infection causing hepatitis. Given he refuses genital exam, the best next diagnostic test is hepatitis serology.\n\n**Answer:** Hepatitis B surface antigen and hepatitis C antibody testing (or acute hepatitis panel).", "correct": false, "gold": "Viral core antibody"}
