{"qid": "med:394", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia mediated by immune activation of platelets via PF4\u2011heparin complexes, leading to platelet consumption and formation of venous thrombi (e.g., DVT) and cutaneous necrosis.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Autoantibody-mediated platelet activation"}
{"qid": "med:393", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The presentation includes constitutional symptoms (fatigue, low-grade fever, nausea, vomiting, anorexia/weight loss) together with modest transaminase elevation and hyperbilirubinemia that produces dark urine. Markedly elevated aminotransferases (>500 U/L) would be expected in acute viral hepatitis A or B; the modest laboratory abnormalities therefore make acute viral hepatitis unlikely.\n\nThe epidemiologic setting of multiple unprotected sexual partners supplies the key risk factor for syphilis. Secondary syphilis produces precisely this systemic picture and characteristically causes syphilitic hepatitis with only mild-to-moderate aminotransferase increases. Genital examination might have revealed a healed chancre or condyloma lata, but the patient declines examination; the diagnosis must therefore be established serologically.\n\nA non-treponemal screening test (VDRL or RPR) is consequently the single most appropriate next diagnostic study; a positive result is then confirmed with a treponemal-specific assay.\n\nAnswer: VDRL/RPR test", "correct": false, "gold": "Viral core antibody"}
{"qid": "med:393", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "The patient presents with fatigue, nausea, weight loss, and transaminitis (elevated AST/ALT and total bilirubin), along with high-risk sexual behavior. These findings are concerning for acute viral hepatitis. \r\n\r\nGiven his refusal of a genital exam, a noninvasive viral hepatitis panel is the best diagnostic approach. This should include testing for acute hepatitis A (anti-HAV IgM), hepatitis B (HBsAg and anti-HBc IgM), and hepatitis C (anti-HCV antibodies). \r\n\r\nAnswer: **Viral hepatitis panel (anti-HAV IgM, HBsAg, anti-HBc IgM, and anti-HCV antibodies).**", "correct": false, "gold": "Viral core antibody"}
{"qid": "med:397", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Night pain, point tenderness, and a history of treated non-small cell lung cancer make **vertebral metastasis** the most likely cause of this subacute back pain rather than a simple musculoskeletal strain from weight lifting. Lung cancer commonly metastasizes to bone, especially the spine.\n\nThe most likely imaging finding would be a **lytic vertebral body lesion**, often involving the pedicle, with possible vertebral collapse/compression fracture.\n\nAnswer: Osteolytic metastasis to the vertebral body (eg, a lytic spinal lesion/compression fracture from metastatic lung cancer)", "correct": false, "gold": "Lytic lesions of the lumbar spine"}
{"qid": "med:394", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient presents with thrombocytopenia (platelets 48,000/mm\u00b3) and new thrombosis (painful, swollen right calf) 5\u201310 days after exposure to unfractionated heparin. This classic combination\u2014falling platelet count with thrombotic complications following heparin therapy\u2014is diagnostic of heparin\u2011induced thrombocytopenia (HIT). HIT is an immune\u2011mediated prothrombotic disorder in which IgG antibodies form against platelet factor\u202f4 (PF4) bound to heparin. These antibodies activate platelets via Fc\u03b3RIIa receptors, causing platelet consumption (thrombocytopenia) and triggering widespread thrombin generation that leads to venous or arterial thrombosis.\n\nAnswer: Heparin\u2011induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) caused by anti\u2011platelet factor\u202f4 (PF4)/heparin antibodies leading to platelet activation, consumption, and thrombosis.", "correct": false, "gold": "Autoantibody-mediated platelet activation"}
{"qid": "med:397", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Most likely finding: A lytic (destructive) metastatic lesion in a lumbosacral vertebral body, often with involvement of the pedicle (the classic \"winking owl\" sign on plain film, or a destructive marrow-replacing lesion on MRI/CT).**\n\n**Reasoning:**\n- Night pain, subacute progressive back pain, and point tenderness in an older patient with a history of malignancy (NSCLC) should raise strong suspicion for spinal metastasis rather than a mechanical/musculoskeletal cause from weightlifting.\n- Even with a prior \"clean\" PET scan, metastatic disease can recur, and bone metastases\u2014especially from lung cancer\u2014are common and often lytic.\n- On imaging (MRI is most sensitive, though CT/X-ray may be used), the classic finding is a destructive lesion of the vertebral body, frequently extending into or destroying the pedicle. Pedicle involvement is a classic distinguishing feature of metastatic disease (as opposed to multiple myeloma, which tends to spare the pedicles).\n- MRI would typically show a marrow-replacing lesion with low T1 signal, variable T2 signal, and enhancement with contrast, often with cortical destruction and possible epidural extension.\n\nAnswer: A destructive (lytic) vertebral body lesion with pedicle involvement, consistent with metastatic bone disease from his prior lung cancer.", "correct": false, "gold": "Lytic lesions of the lumbar spine"}
{"qid": "med:397", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient has a history of non-small cell lung cancer, which commonly metastasizes to bone. The subacute back pain that is worse at night and point tenderness raise concern for metastatic disease, even with a prior negative PET scan. The most likely imaging finding would be a lytic bone lesion.\n\nAnswer: Lytic lesion in the spine.", "correct": false, "gold": "Lytic lesions of the lumbar spine"}
{"qid": "med:394", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Answer: **IgG antibody formation against heparin\u2013platelet factor 4 (PF4) complexes, leading to platelet activation and a prothrombotic state (Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia Type 2).**", "correct": false, "gold": "Autoantibody-mediated platelet activation"}
{"qid": "med:392", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Low\u2011potency typical antipsychotics (especially thioridazine) can cause a dose\u2011related retinal pigmentaryopathy that presents with nyctalopia and slowly progressive vision loss. The priority is to halt further toxic damage while maintaining psychiatric stability.\n\n**Reasoning:**  \n- Night\u2011driving difficulty (nyctalopia) and slowly deteriorating vision in a patient on a low\u2011potency typical antipsychotic strongly suggest drug\u2011induced retinopathy.  \n- Continued exposure can lead to irreversible visual loss.  \n- Although the schizophrenia is well\u2011controlled, the medication must be stopped promptly and replaced with an alternative (e.g., an atypical antipsychotic) to prevent further ocular toxicity.\n\n**Answer:** Discontinue the low\u2011potency typical antipsychotic and switch to an alternative antipsychotic (e.g., an atypical agent).", "correct": false, "gold": "Slit-lamp examination"}
{"qid": "med:397", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The patient\u2019s subacute night pain, point tenderness, and history of NSCLC make spinal metastasis the most likely diagnosis. Metastatic lesions in the spine typically appear as destructive (lytic) lesions in the vertebral body on imaging (often best seen on CT or MRI, where MRI also shows T1 hypointensity and enhancement).\n\nAnswer: A lytic lesion in the lumbar vertebral body consistent with spinal metastasis.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Lytic lesions of the lumbar spine"}
{"qid": "med:400", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "The outcome variables are continuous, approximately normally distributed, and the investigator wants to compare mean change from baseline to 12 months across 3 independent groups (pulmharkimab 75 mg, pulmharkimab 150 mg, placebo). The most appropriate method is a **one-way ANOVA** for comparing mean change between the three groups. If adjusting for sociodemographic variables and comorbidities, the corresponding method would be **ANCOVA / multivariable linear regression**.\n\nAnswer: One-way ANOVA to compare mean change across the 3 groups (or ANCOVA/linear regression if adjusting for covariates).", "correct": false, "gold": "Analysis of variance"}
{"qid": "med:397", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient's subacute, unremitting nocturnal back pain plus focal bony tenderness constitute classic \u201cred-flag\u201d features for spinal pathology. In an individual with a prior history of non-small-cell lung cancer, these symptoms raise immediate concern for osseous recurrence even though a PET scan one year earlier was negative. Lung-cancer metastases are typically osteolytic and preferentially involve the richly vascularized vertebral pedicles; consequently, the earliest plain-film manifestation is unilateral pedicle destruction (the \u201cwinking-owl\u201d sign on an AP radiograph). Mechanical strain from remote weight-lifting would produce activity-related rather than nocturnal pain and would lack focal bony tenderness, making metastatic disease far more probable. MRI is ultimately required for soft-tissue and cord detail, but the most characteristic initial imaging finding remains pedicle lysis on plain radiographs.\nAnswer: pedicle destruction (winking-owl sign) on AP XR", "correct": false, "gold": "Lytic lesions of the lumbar spine"}
{"qid": "med:400", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The study compares three independent groups (pulmharkimab 75 mg, pulmharkimab 150 mg, and placebo) on continuous, approximately normally distributed outcomes (changes in plaque volume, LDL-C, FEV1/FVC, ACQ scores). The appropriate method to test for differences among the three groups is one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA), as it compares means across multiple groups. The abstract's table already uses p-values from such an analysis.\n\nAnswer: One-way ANOVA", "correct": false, "gold": "Analysis of variance"}
{"qid": "med:392", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient's progressive visual impairment with prominent nyctalopia (night-driving difficulty) is characteristic of rod photoreceptor toxicity. Among commonly prescribed low-potency typical antipsychotics, thioridazine is the agent classically linked to a dose-dependent pigmentary retinopathy that produces exactly this clinical picture. Chlorpromazine more often causes only anterior-segment deposits and is far less likely to impair night vision.\nBecause the retinopathy is medication-induced and potentially progressive, the essential first management step is removal of the offending drug. This requires prompt liaison with the treating psychiatrist to substitute an alternative antipsychotic that maintains control of the patient\u2019s schizophrenia while eliminating ongoing retinal toxicity. Ancillary testing (fundus examination, visual-field testing, electroretinography) may be obtained concurrently, but it does not supersede cessation of the toxin.\nAnswer: discontinue thioridazine (switch antipsychotic)", "correct": false, "gold": "Slit-lamp examination"}
{"qid": "med:400", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>To determine whether the changes in plaque volume, LDL\u2011C, FEV\u2081/FVC ratio, and ACQ scores differ among the three treatment arms, the most appropriate approach is to compare the mean change scores across the three independent groups using a **one\u2011way analysis of variance (ANOVA)**.  \n\nBecause the outcome variables are approximately normally distributed, ANOVA provides an efficient test of the null hypothesis that all group means are equal. If the overall ANOVA yields a statistically significant result, pairwise differences can be explored with post\u2011hoc procedures (e.g., Tukey\u2019s HSD) to identify which specific groups differ.  \n\nIf normality assumptions were violated, a nonparametric alternative such as the **Kruskal\u2011Wallis test** would be appropriate, followed by Dunn\u2019s test for pairwise comparisons.  \n\n**Answer:** One\u2011way ANOVA (with Tukey\u2019s HSD for post\u2011hoc comparisons if needed).<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Analysis of variance"}
{"qid": "med:400", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Answer: One-way ANOVA (Analysis of Variance)**\n\n**Rationale:**\nThe study design involves comparing a continuous, normally distributed outcome (change in plaque volume, LDL-C, FEV1/FVC ratio, ACQ score) across **three independent groups** (Pulmharkimab 75 mg/day, Pulmharkimab 150 mg/day, and Placebo).\n\nKey features supporting ANOVA as the correct choice:\n\n1. **Number of groups**: There are three groups being compared simultaneously, not just two. A t-test would only be appropriate for pairwise comparisons between two groups, but using multiple t-tests to compare three groups would inflate the Type I error rate (increased risk of false positives due to multiple comparisons).\n\n2. **Type of outcome variable**: The outcomes (plaque volume, LDL-C, FEV1/FVC ratio, ACQ scores) are continuous variables that are stated to be **approximately normally distributed**, which satisfies the parametric assumption required for ANOVA.\n\n3. **Independent groups**: Patients were randomized into three distinct, non-overlapping treatment arms, satisfying the assumption of independent samples.\n\n4. **Comparing means across groups**: ANOVA tests whether there is a statistically significant difference between the means of three or more independent groups by comparing the variance *between* groups to the variance *within* groups (yielding an F-statistic and corresponding p-value), which is exactly what is reported in the table (single p-value for each three-group comparison).\n\nIf the overall ANOVA showed statistical significance, appropriate post-hoc testing (e.g., Tukey's HSD) would then be needed to identify which specific pairs of groups differ significantly from one another.\n\nAnswer: One-way ANOVA (Analysis of Variance)", "correct": true, "gold": "Analysis of variance"}
{"qid": "med:402", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This young man has recurrent, mild jaundice triggered by fasting and physiologic stress (post-op before, intermittent fasting now, possibly alcohol/stress contributing), with otherwise normal exam. That pattern is classic for **Gilbert syndrome**.\n\nGilbert syndrome is due to **reduced activity of UDP-glucuronosyltransferase (UGT1A1)**, causing **impaired conjugation of bilirubin** and intermittent **unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia**. Episodes are often precipitated by fasting, illness, stress, surgery, or exertion.\n\nAnswer: Gilbert syndrome due to decreased UGT1A1 activity causing impaired bilirubin conjugation and intermittent unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia.", "correct": false, "gold": "Decreased activity of UDP-glucuronosyltransferase"}
{"qid": "med:400", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The study involves a three-arm RCT with continuous outcome measures (changes from baseline). These outcomes are stated to be approximately normally distributed, and the goal is to test for differences in means across the three independent groups.\nA parametric test for more than two groups is therefore required. One-way ANOVA directly evaluates the null hypothesis that all three group means are equal and yields a single overall p-value per outcome (matching the table format). Post-hoc pairwise tests can be added only if the ANOVA p-value is significant.\nAlthough the published table notes covariate adjustment (suggesting ANCOVA or linear regression), the question itself does not mention covariates; the simplest and most direct method that still replicates the displayed table structure is therefore one-way ANOVA.\nAnswer: one-way ANOVA", "correct": false, "gold": "Analysis of variance"}
{"qid": "med:402", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The patient's symptoms (scleral icterus, history of similar episode during stress of surgery, recent fasting and binge drinking) point toward Gilbert syndrome. This is a benign condition caused by reduced activity of UDP-glucuronosyltransferase, leading to unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, which becomes apparent during periods of stress, fasting, or illness.\n\nAnswer: Gilbert syndrome", "correct": false, "gold": "Decreased activity of UDP-glucuronosyltransferase"}
{"qid": "med:402", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Answer: Gilbert syndrome \u2014 a benign, inherited partial deficiency of UDP-glucuronosyltransferase (UGT1A1), leading to decreased conjugation of bilirubin.**\n\nThis mild, unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia is typically asymptomatic at baseline but becomes clinically apparent (scleral icterus) during periods of physiologic stress such as fasting, illness/surgery, or alcohol use\u2014all of which this patient has experienced (intermittent fasting now, post-surgical stress previously, recent binge drinking). The otherwise unremarkable exam and lack of other risk factors (no IV drug use, though sexually active\u2014hepatitis serologies would be reasonable to rule out but the recurrent, self-limited pattern tied to fasting/stress is classic for Gilbert syndrome) support this benign diagnosis over hepatitis or hemolysis.\n\nAnswer: Gilbert syndrome (reduced UGT1A1 activity causing unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia exacerbated by fasting/stress).", "correct": false, "gold": "Decreased activity of UDP-glucuronosyltransferase"}
{"qid": "med:402", "domain": "medqa", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The patient presents with isolated scleral icterus and a history of a prior self-limited episode, without fever, abdominal pain, hepatomegaly, or other stigmata of liver disease. These features point to a mild, intermittent disturbance of bilirubin handling rather than acute hepatitis, biliary obstruction, or hemolysis.  \n\nIntermittent fasting, recent surgery, and alcohol binge are all well-documented physiologic stressors that reduce hepatic UDP-glucuronosyltransferase activity. In an individual with underlying Gilbert syndrome (partial deficiency of this enzyme), such stressors produce a transient rise in unconjugated bilirubin sufficient to cause visible jaundice. The sexual history is a distractor for viral hepatitis but is inconsistent with the recurrent, mild, and otherwise asymptomatic course.  \n\nThus the symptoms are explained by impaired bilirubin conjugation unmasked by fasting and alcohol.  \n\nAnswer: Gilbert syndrome (\u2193 UDP-glucuronosyltransferase) triggered by fasting/alcohol", "correct": false, "gold": "Decreased activity of UDP-glucuronosyltransferase"}
{"qid": "med:397", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient's presentation is highly concerning for **spinal metastasis**. Key red flags include:\n1. **History of malignancy** (non-small cell lung cancer) - even with prior successful treatment and negative PET scan 1 year ago, recurrence and bony metastasis can occur\n2. **Night pain** - classic red flag for malignancy or infection\n3. **Age** and **subacute course** (1 month)\n\nWhile the weight-lifting event might suggest musculoskeletal strain, the night pain and cancer history are dominant features. Lung cancer commonly metastasizes to bone (particularly the spine) and typically produces **osteolytic (lytic) lesions**.\n\nThe most likely imaging finding would be a **lytic lesion in the vertebral body** (or pedicle), which may appear as a focal area of bone destruction on X-ray, CT, or MRI. Other possible findings include pedicle erosion (\"winking owl\" sign on AP radiograph) or pathologic compression fracture, but the primary finding is the lytic metastatic lesion itself.\n\nAnswer: Lytic (osteolytic) lesion in the vertebral body (spinal metastasis).", "correct": false, "gold": "Lytic lesions of the lumbar spine"}
{"qid": "med:402", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The pattern of mild, episodic unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia triggered by fasting, alcohol intake, and stress (such as recent surgery) in a young adult is classic for Gilbert syndrome. This benign autosomal recessive condition results from reduced hepatic UDP\u2011glucuronosyltransferase activity, leading to impaired bilirubin conjugation. It typically presents with scleral icterus during precipitating events and resolves spontaneously.\n\nAnswer: Gilbert syndrome.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "Decreased activity of UDP-glucuronosyltransferase"}
{"qid": "med:397", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Answer: \\(\\boxed{\\text{Osteolytic lesion in the lumbosacral spine}}\\)", "correct": false, "gold": "Lytic lesions of the lumbar spine"}
{"qid": "med:387", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The patient\u2019s presentation of a prodromal flu\u2011like illness (fever, malaise, myalgia) followed by an acute, progressive rash with **targetoid lesions and central vesiculation** is classic for **Stevens\u2011Johnson syndrome (SJS)**, a severe mucocutaneous reaction most often triggered by medications.  \n\nAmong mood stabilizers used for bipolar disorder, **lamotrigine** is well\u2011known for causing rash, including SJS and erythema multiforme, especially within the first 2\u20138 weeks of therapy. The risk is heightened if the drug is titrated too quickly. Carbamazepine can also cause SJS, but lamotrigine is the agent most commonly associated with this specific rash presentation in the context of bipolar treatment and is frequently tested in this scenario. Other bipolar agents (valproate, atypical antipsychotics, lithium) do not typically produce targetoid vesicular lesions.\n\nAnswer: Lamotrigine", "correct": true, "gold": "Lamotrigine"}
{"qid": "med:402", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at this case, I need to identify the cause of recurrent jaundice in a young, otherwise healthy patient.\n\n**Key clinical features:**\n- 23-year-old with isolated scleral icterus (unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia pattern)\n- Recurrent episodes: one during post-surgical recovery (stress/fasting state) 4 years ago, and current episode\n- Current triggers: intermittent fasting and recent binge drinking\n- Otherwise healthy with unremarkable physical exam\n\n**Analysis of the pattern:**\nThe critical clue is the **recurrent nature** with specific triggers. Both episodes occurred during physiological stressors:\n1. Post-operative state (fasting, stress)\n2. Intermittent fasting + alcohol consumption\n\n**Differential diagnosis:**\n- **Viral hepatitis** (A, B, or C): Possible given sexual history, but unlikely to cause recurrent isolated episodes years apart with complete resolution, and usually associated with other symptoms (fatigue, nausea, hepatomegaly)\n- **Alcoholic hepatitis**: Requires chronic heavy use, not just binge drinking; doesn't explain the previous episode during appendectomy recovery\n- **Hemolysis**: Would typically cause anemia and other signs, not just isolated jaundice\n- **Gilbert syndrome**: Fits perfectly - a benign inherited condition (reduced UDP-glucuronosyltransferase activity) causing mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia that is exacerbated by fasting, stress, surgery, alcohol, and illness\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThis is classic **Gilbert syndrome**, the most common hereditary cause of unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia. It affects 3-7% of the population, typically presents in young men, and causes intermittent jaundice triggered precisely by the factors mentioned: fasting, surgery/recovery, and alcohol.\n\nAnswer: Gilbert syndrome (or unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia due to Gilbert syndrome)", "correct": false, "gold": "Decreased activity of UDP-glucuronosyltransferase"}
{"qid": "med:403", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The presentation is classic for a panic attack: acute onset of intense fear, palpitations, diaphoresis, shortness of breath, and a sense of impending doom, with spontaneous resolution and normal cardiac workup. The most likely diagnosis is panic disorder (or panic attack). To confirm the diagnosis, the patient must have recurrent, unexpected panic attacks and at least one month of worry about having another attack or a change in behavior related to the attacks. This is established by clinical history, as there is no specific lab or imaging test.\n\n**Answer:** Clinical history confirming recurrent attacks and persistent worry or behavioral change.", "correct": false, "gold": "1 month of associated symptoms "}
{"qid": "med:400", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Based on the study design described, the most appropriate statistical method is **one-way Analysis of Variance (ANOVA)**.\n\n**Reasoning:** The study involves comparing mean changes from baseline across **three independent groups** (two treatment doses and placebo) for **continuous, approximately normally distributed outcomes** (plaque volume, LDL-C, FEV1/FVC ratio, and ACQ scores). One-way ANOVA is the standard method for testing whether there are statistically significant differences between the means of three or more independent groups. While Analysis of Covariance (ANCOVA) could alternatively be used to adjust for baseline values directly, ANOVA applied to the change scores (as presented in the table) is the appropriate method given the data structure described.\n\nAnswer: One-way ANOVA (Analysis of Variance)", "correct": true, "gold": "Analysis of variance"}
{"qid": "med:403", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This presentation is most consistent with a **panic attack**: sudden onset chest pain, palpitations, sweating, shortness of breath, fear of dying, spontaneous resolution, normal ECG/cardiac enzymes, and normal exam.\n\nTo **confirm panic disorder** (the most likely diagnosis if these attacks recur), it is not enough to have a single panic attack. The diagnosis requires:\n\n- **Recurrent unexpected panic attacks**\n- Followed by **at least 1 month** of:\n  - persistent concern/worry about additional attacks or their consequences, and/or\n  - maladaptive behavioral change related to the attacks\n\nSo the necessary feature to confirm the diagnosis is **recurrent unexpected panic attacks with at least 1 month of persistent concern or behavior change**.\n\nAnswer: Recurrent unexpected panic attacks followed by \u22651 month of persistent worry about further attacks (or maladaptive behavior change).", "correct": true, "gold": "1 month of associated symptoms "}
{"qid": "med:400", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\nThe cardiologist is comparing three independent groups (pulmharkimab 75 mg/d, 150 mg/d, and placebo) on continuous outcome variables (changes in plaque volume, LDL-C, FEV1/FVC ratio, and ACQ scores) that are approximately normally distributed. The most appropriate omnibus test for this scenario is a **one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA)**. If the ANOVA reveals a statistically significant difference, post-hoc tests (e.g., Tukey's HSD or Bonferroni correction) should be performed to determine which specific groups differ. Additionally, since the original study adjusted for sociodemographic variables and medical comorbidities, an **analysis of covariance (ANCOVA)** could be even more appropriate if the cardiologist plans to make similar adjustments. \r\n\r\nAnswer: **One-way ANOVA (or ANCOVA if adjusting for covariates).**", "correct": false, "gold": "Analysis of variance"}
{"qid": "med:404", "domain": "medqa", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Answer: Adjust for lead-time bias by measuring survival from a fixed point (e.g., disease onset) rather than from time of diagnosis", "correct": false, "gold": "Using mortality rates in the analysis"}
{"qid": "med:404", "domain": "medqa", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "This observational comparison is vulnerable to **lead-time bias**: screening detects the cancer earlier, so measured \u201csurvival from diagnosis\u201d appears longer even if the actual time of death is unchanged.\n\nTo prevent this bias, outcomes should be compared using **disease-specific mortality (or overall mortality) from a common starting point**, ideally in a **randomized controlled trial**, rather than 5-year survival from time of diagnosis.\n\nAnswer: Compare screened vs unscreened groups by mortality (preferably in a randomized trial), not by survival time from diagnosis, to eliminate lead-time bias.", "correct": false, "gold": "Using mortality rates in the analysis"}
