{"qid": "med:404", "domain": "medqa", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "The key issue here is lead-time bias. The screening detects the cancer earlier, so survival time appears longer even if the actual time of death from the disease is unchanged. To prevent this bias, survival should be measured from the same point in the disease course (e.g., from the time clinical symptoms would have appeared) rather than from the time of diagnosis.\n\n**Answer:** Adjust survival time to account for lead-time bias, such as measuring survival from the onset of clinical symptoms rather than from diagnosis.", "correct": false, "gold": "Using mortality rates in the analysis"}
