{"qid": "mp:8670", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>To find \\(P(X<100{,}000 \\mid X>80{,}000)\\) for \\(X\\sim N(\\mu=75{,}000,\\;\\sigma=12{,}000)\\):\n\n1. Compute the standardized values  \n   \\[\n   z_{80} = \\frac{80{,}000-75{,}000}{12{,}000}\\approx 0.417,\\qquad \n   z_{100}= \\frac{100{,}000-75{,}000}{12{,}000}\\approx 2.083.\n   \\]\n\n2. Use the standard normal CDF \\(\\Phi(z)\\):  \n   \\[\n   \\Phi(z_{80})\\approx 0.662,\\qquad \n   \\Phi(z_{100})\\approx 0.981.\n   \\]\n\n3. Numerator: \\(P(80{,}000<X<100{,}000)=\\Phi(z_{100})-\\Phi(z_{80})\\approx 0.981-0.662=0.319.\\)\n\n4. Denominator: \\(P(X>80{,}000)=1-\\Phi(z_{80})\\approx 1-0.662=0.338.\\)\n\n5. Conditional probability:  \n   \\[\n   P(X<100{,}000 \\mid X>80{,}000)=\\frac{0.319}{0.338}\\approx 0.945.\n   \\]\n\nAnswer: Approximately 0.945 (or 94.5%).<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "0.94"}
{"qid": "med:405", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\nThis patient's presentation is classic for **psoriatic arthritis (PsA)**. Key supporting features include:  \r\n\r\n- **Inflammatory arthritis:** Swollen, warm, and painful DIP joints, along with knee stiffness and swelling, are common in PsA.\r\n- **Cutaneous psoriasis:** Rough, erythematous plaques over the elbows are characteristic of psoriasis.\r\n- **Nail changes:** Pitting and cracking of the nails are hallmark findings of psoriatic nail disease.\r\n- **Systemic symptoms:** Fatigue and difficulty moving reflect underlying inflammation.\r\n- **Demographics & family history:** A 31-year-old with a first-degree relative (father) affected is consistent with the genetic predisposition seen in PsA (HLA-B27 and others).\r\n\r\nThese features distinguish it from other conditions, such as rheumatoid arthritis (which typically spares DIP joints and lacks psoriatic skin/nail findings).\r\n\r\nAnswer: Psoriatic arthritis", "correct": true, "gold": "Psoriatic arthritis"}
{"qid": "mp:8437", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Both statements are true.\n\n- **Statement 1:** If an abelian group is free, it has a basis, so it is isomorphic to \\(\\mathbb{Z}^n\\) or more generally a direct sum of copies of \\(\\mathbb{Z}\\). In such a group, if \\(mx=0\\) for some nonzero integer \\(m\\), then each coordinate of \\(x\\) must be \\(0\\). So there is no nontrivial torsion.\n\n- **Statement 2:** This is a standard structure theorem for finitely generated abelian groups: every finitely generated abelian group is isomorphic to\n  \\[\n  \\mathbb{Z}^r \\oplus T\n  \\]\n  where \\(T\\) is a finite torsion subgroup. If the group is torsion-free, then \\(T=0\\), so the group is just \\(\\mathbb{Z}^r\\), hence free abelian.\n\nAnswer: Both Statement 1 and Statement 2 are true.", "correct": false, "gold": "True, True"}
{"qid": "med:403", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**  \r\nThis patient's presentation is classic for a **panic attack**. She experienced sudden-onset intense fear (\"going to die\"), palpitations, profuse sweating, shortness of breath, and chest pain that resolved spontaneously within minutes. Her normal ECG, cardiac enzymes, physical exam, and rapid return to baseline effectively rule out life-threatening organic causes (e.g., acute coronary syndrome, pulmonary embolism).  \r\n\r\nPanic attacks are a **clinical diagnosis**\u2014there is no specific laboratory or imaging test to confirm them. Instead, the diagnosis is made by meeting DSM-5 criteria after excluding medical conditions. \r\n\r\nAnswer: No specific confirmatory test is necessary; the diagnosis is clinical after ruling out organic causes.", "correct": false, "gold": "1 month of associated symptoms "}
{"qid": "mp:8437", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "Let\u2019s check each statement.\n\n**Statement 1:** A free abelian group has a basis, so any nonzero element can be expressed as a finite linear combination of basis elements with integer coefficients. If some element had finite order, multiplying it by a nonzero integer would give zero, which is impossible in a free abelian group because the basis representation would force all coefficients to be zero. So indeed, every free abelian group is torsion-free. This is true.\n\n**Statement 2:** A finitely generated torsion-free abelian group is isomorphic to \\(\\mathbb{Z}^n\\) for some \\(n\\), which is free abelian. This is a standard result (structure theorem for finitely generated abelian groups). So this is also true.\n\nBoth statements are true.\n\nAnswer: True, True", "correct": true, "gold": "True, True"}
{"qid": "mp:8670", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "This is a conditional probability for a normal random variable \\(X \\sim N(\\mu=75000, \\sigma=12000)\\):\n\\[\nP(X<100000 \\mid X>80000) = \\frac{P(80000<X<100000)}{P(X>80000)}.\n\\]\nStandardize to obtain the equivalent statement in terms of a standard normal variate \\(Z\\):\n\\[\nz_{80} = \\frac{80000-75000}{12000} \\approx 0.42, \\qquad z_{100} = \\frac{100000-75000}{12000} \\approx 2.08.\n\\]\nUsing a standard normal table,\n\\[\n\\Phi(0.42) \\approx 0.6628, \\qquad \\Phi(2.08) \\approx 0.9812,\n\\]\nwhich yields the tail probabilities\n\\[\nP(X>80000) = 1-\\Phi(0.42) \\approx 0.3372,\n\\]\n\\[\nP(X>100000) = 1-\\Phi(2.08) \\approx 0.0188.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nP(80000<X<100000) = \\Phi(2.08)-\\Phi(0.42) \\approx 0.3184,\n\\]\nand the desired conditional probability is\n\\[\n\\frac{0.3184}{0.3372} \\approx 0.944.\n\\]\n(Equivalently, subtract the normalized upper tail from 1:\n\\[\n1 - \\frac{0.0188}{0.3372} \\approx 0.944.)\n\\]\nAnswer: 0.944", "correct": true, "gold": "0.94"}
{"qid": "med:404", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The apparent survival advantage in the screened group is due to **lead\u2011time bias**: the test diagnoses the cancer 2\u202fyears earlier, so survival is measured from an earlier point in the natural history even though the actual time from biological onset to death is unchanged. To avoid this bias, survival must be calculated from a common biological milestone\u2014specifically, from the **onset of clinically significant symptoms** (or the lead time must be subtracted from the screened group\u2019s survival time) so that both groups are compared over the same phase of disease.\n\nAnswer: Adjust for lead\u2011time bias by measuring survival from the onset of clinical symptoms (or subtract the 2\u2011year lead time from the screened group\u2019s survival).", "correct": false, "gold": "Using mortality rates in the analysis"}
{"qid": "mp:7772", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "For a projectile launched and landing at the same height, the range is\n\n\\[\nR=\\frac{u^2\\sin 2\\theta}{g}\n\\]\n\nHere:\n- \\(R=90\\,\\text{m}\\)\n- \\(\\theta=45^\\circ\\)\n- \\(\\sin 2\\theta=\\sin 90^\\circ=1\\)\n\nSo,\n\n\\[\n90=\\frac{u^2}{g}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nu^2=90g\n\\]\n\nTaking \\(g\\approx 9.8\\,\\text{m/s}^2\\),\n\n\\[\nu^2=90\\times 9.8=882\n\\]\n\n\\[\nu=\\sqrt{882}\\approx 29.7\\,\\text{m/s}\n\\]\n\nSo the initial speed is about \\(30\\,\\text{m/s}\\).\n\nAnswer: \\(29.7\\ \\text{m/s}\\) (approximately \\(30\\ \\text{m/s}\\))", "correct": true, "gold": "30 $\\mathrm{m}/\\mathrm{s}$"}
{"qid": "mp:8437", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "Both statements are standard facts from the classification of finitely generated abelian groups (or, equivalently, from the structure theorem for modules over the PID \\(\\mathbb{Z}\\)).\n\n- A free abelian group is a direct sum of copies of \\(\\mathbb{Z}\\). In \\(\\mathbb{Z}\\) the only element of finite order is \\(0\\), so the same holds in any direct sum of copies of \\(\\mathbb{Z}\\). Hence every free abelian group is torsion-free, and Statement 1 is true.\n- Conversely, any finitely generated torsion-free abelian group \\(G\\) is isomorphic to \\(\\mathbb{Z}^r\\) for some finite rank \\(r\\). (One proves this by embedding \\(G\\) into a free abelian group of finite rank and then using the fact that subgroups of free abelian groups of finite rank are themselves free of rank at most that rank; the torsion-free hypothesis forces the quotient to be trivial.) Hence Statement 2 is also true.\n\n(The \u201cfinitely generated\u201d hypothesis in Statement 2 is essential: \\(\\mathbb{Q}\\) is torsion-free but not free abelian.)\n\nAnswer: both true", "correct": false, "gold": "True, True"}
{"qid": "mp:8670", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "We are given that taxicab miles per year follow a normal distribution with mean $\\mu = 75{,}000$ and standard deviation $\\sigma = 12{,}000$. We want the probability that a cab has been driven less than $100{,}000$ miles given that it has been driven over $80{,}000$ miles: \n\n$P(X < 100{,}000 \\mid X > 80{,}000) = \\frac{P(80{,}000 < X < 100{,}000)}{P(X > 80{,}000)}$. \n\nStandardizing to $Z \\sim N(0,1)$: \n\n- $z_1 = \\frac{80{,}000 - 75{,}000}{12{,}000} \\approx 0.417$\n- $z_2 = \\frac{100{,}000 - 75{,}000}{12{,}000} \\approx 2.083$\n\nUsing standard normal values: \n\n- $P(X > 80{,}000) = P(Z > 0.417) \\approx 1 - 0.6616 = 0.3384$\n- $P(80{,}000 < X < 100{,}000) = P(0.417 < Z < 2.083) \\approx 0.9814 - 0.6616 = 0.3198$\n\nThus: \n\n$P(X < 100{,}000 \\mid X > 80{,}000) \\approx \\frac{0.3198}{0.3384} \\approx 0.945$. \n\nAnswer: $\\approx 0.95$", "correct": true, "gold": "0.94"}
{"qid": "mp:7772", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We can use the range formula for projectile motion on level ground:\n\n\\[\nR = \\frac{v_0^2 \\sin(2\\theta)}{g}\n\\]\n\nGiven:\n- \\( R = 90 \\, \\text{m} \\)\n- \\( \\theta = 45^\\circ \\), so \\( \\sin(90^\\circ) = 1 \\)\n- \\( g = 9.8 \\, \\text{m/s}^2 \\)\n\nSubstitute:\n\n\\[\n90 = \\frac{v_0^2 \\cdot 1}{9.8}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nv_0^2 = 90 \\times 9.8 = 882\n\\]\n\n\\[\nv_0 = \\sqrt{882} \\approx 29.7 \\, \\text{m/s}\n\\]\n\nAnswer: \\( \\approx 29.7 \\, \\text{m/s} \\)  \nAnswer: 29.7 m/s", "correct": true, "gold": "30 $\\mathrm{m}/\\mathrm{s}$"}
{"qid": "mp:7772", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Setting up the equation:**\n\nFor projectile motion, the range formula is:\n$$R = \\frac{v_0^2 \\sin(2\\theta)}{g}$$\n\n**Given values:**\n- R = 90 m\n- \u03b8 = 45\u00b0\n- g = 9.8 m/s\u00b2\n\n**Solving for v\u2080:**\n\nSince \u03b8 = 45\u00b0, we have sin(2\u03b8) = sin(90\u00b0) = 1\n\n$$R = \\frac{v_0^2}{g}$$\n\n$$v_0^2 = R \\cdot g = 90 \\times 9.8 = 882$$\n\n$$v_0 = \\sqrt{882} \\approx 29.7 \\text{ m/s}$$\n\n**Answer:** v\u2080 \u2248 29.7 m/s", "correct": true, "gold": "30 $\\mathrm{m}/\\mathrm{s}$"}
{"qid": "mp:7772", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The range \\(R\\) of a projectile launched at speed \\(u\\) and angle \\(\\theta\\) is\n\\[\nR = \\frac{u^2 \\sin 2\\theta}{g}.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\theta = 45^\\circ\\) we have \\(\\sin 90^\\circ = 1\\), so the given range of 90 m simplifies to\n\\[\n90 = \\frac{u^2}{g} \\implies u = \\sqrt{90g}.\n\\]\nWith the conventional value \\(g = 10\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}\\) this yields the numerical result\n\\[\nu = 30\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}.\n\\]\nAnswer: 30 m/s (g=10)", "correct": true, "gold": "30 $\\mathrm{m}/\\mathrm{s}$"}
{"qid": "mp:8437", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Free abelian groups are direct sums of copies of\u202f\u2124, and \u2124 has no non\u2011zero elements of finite order. Consequently any element of a free abelian group that satisfies\u202fn\u00b7g\u202f=\u202f0 must be the zero element, so free abelian groups are torsion\u2011free. Hence Statement\u202f1 is true.\n\nFor finitely generated abelian groups, the fundamental classification theorem states that any such group decomposes as a direct sum of a free part (copies of\u202f\u2124) and a torsion part. If the group has no torsion, the torsion part is trivial, leaving only the free part. Therefore every finitely generated torsion\u2011free abelian group is free. Hence Statement\u202f2 is also true.\n\nAnswer: Both statements are true.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "True, True"}
{"qid": "med:404", "domain": "medqa", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "This scenario is a classic example of **lead-time bias**. \r\n\r\n### Reasoning:\r\n- **Earlier detection without delayed death:** The screening test detects the cancer 2 years before symptom onset. Since patients decline treatment, their time of death remains unchanged (average 3 years after symptom onset). \r\n- **Artificially inflated survival:** Screened patients are diagnosed at an earlier time point, so their survival measured from diagnosis becomes 5 years, while unscreened patients diagnosed at symptom onset have 3-year survival. This difference is due to the added lead time, not a true improvement in outcomes. \r\n- **Incorrect outcome measure:** Comparing survival from diagnosis in an observational study fails to account for this bias, leading to misleading conclusions. \r\n\r\n### Correct Step:\r\nTo prevent this bias, the study should compare **disease-specific mortality rates** (or overall mortality) between screened and unscreened groups, using a common starting point (e.g., time of enrollment, age, or date of screening invitation), rather than survival time from diagnosis. \r\n\r\nAnswer: Compare disease-specific mortality rates from a common baseline instead of survival from diagnosis.", "correct": false, "gold": "Using mortality rates in the analysis"}
{"qid": "mp:7772", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The range \\(R\\) of a projectile launched at the same height it lands, with speed \\(v_0\\) at angle \\(\\theta\\), is  \n\n\\[\nR=\\frac{v_0^{2}\\sin(2\\theta)}{g}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(\\theta = 45^\\circ\\), \\(\\sin(2\\theta)=\\sin 90^\\circ = 1\\), so  \n\n\\[\nR = \\frac{v_0^{2}}{g}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad v_0 = \\sqrt{R\\,g}.\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(R = 90\\ \\text{m}\\) and \\(g \\approx 9.8\\ \\text{m/s}^2\\),\n\n\\[\nv_0 = \\sqrt{90 \\times 9.8} \\approx \\sqrt{882} \\approx 29.7\\ \\text{m/s}.\n\\]\n\nAnswer: approximately \\(30\\ \\text{m/s}\\) (about \\(29.7\\ \\text{m/s}\\)).<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "30 $\\mathrm{m}/\\mathrm{s}$"}
{"qid": "med:403", "domain": "medqa", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The presentation is classic for a **panic attack** (acute onset of chest pain, palpitations, diaphoresis, dyspnea, and a sense of impending doom that peaks and resolves spontaneously, with normal vital signs and negative cardiac work\u2011up). Panic attack is a clinical diagnosis made by applying DSM\u20115 criteria after life\u2011threatening medical causes (ACS, arrhythmia, PE, pheochromocytoma, etc.) have been reasonably excluded. No laboratory or imaging test confirms panic; the diagnosis is established by a detailed **psychiatric history and mental status examination** to document the required symptom cluster and rule out substance\u2011induced or general medical causes.\n\nAnswer: Psychiatric evaluation (mental status examination and DSM\u20115 criteria assessment).", "correct": false, "gold": "1 month of associated symptoms "}
{"qid": "mp:8437", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "**Reasoning:**\n\n**Statement 1:** A free abelian group is isomorphic to a direct sum of copies of $\\mathbb{Z}$ (i.e., $\\bigoplus_{i\\in I} \\mathbb{Z}$). Since $\\mathbb{Z}$ has no torsion, any such direct sum is also torsion-free: if $n \\cdot (\\sum m_i e_i) = 0$ with $n>0$, then $nm_i=0$ for all coefficients, forcing each $m_i=0$. Thus Statement 1 is **true**.\n\n**Statement 2:** By the Structure Theorem for Finitely Generated Abelian Groups, any finitely generated abelian group $G$ decomposes as $G \\cong \\mathbb{Z}^r \\oplus T$, where $T$ is a finite torsion group. If $G$ is torsion-free, then $T=0$, so $G \\cong \\mathbb{Z}^r$, which is a free abelian group. Thus Statement 2 is **true**.\n\nAnswer: Both statements are true.", "correct": false, "gold": "True, True"}
{"qid": "mp:7891", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Statement 1: False. By Lagrange\u2019s theorem, the order of a subgroup must divide the order of the group. Since \\(10\\nmid 45\\), no group of order \\(45\\) can have a subgroup of order \\(10\\).\n\nStatement 2: False. For any subgroup \\(H\\le G\\), the number of left cosets of \\(H\\) always equals the number of right cosets of \\(H\\); this does not characterize normality. A subgroup is normal iff the *sets* of left and right cosets are the same, not merely their numbers.\n\nAnswer: Statement 1 is false; Statement 2 is false.", "correct": false, "gold": "False, False"}
{"qid": "mp:7891", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Statement 1: False \u2014 10 does not divide 45, so no subgroup of order 10 can exist (Lagrange's theorem).\n\nStatement 2: False \u2014 the number of left cosets always equals the number of right cosets (both equal [G:H]) regardless of whether H is normal, so this cannot be a valid criterion for normality.\n\nAnswer: False, False", "correct": true, "gold": "False, False"}
{"qid": "mp:7891", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "Let\u2019s check each statement.\n\n**Statement 1:**  \nOrder 45 = 3\u00b2 \u00d7 5.  \nAny group of order 45 is abelian (since 45 = p\u00b2q with p=3, q=5, and 3 does not divide 5\u22121=4, so it's a direct product of cyclic groups).  \nA subgroup of order 10 would require a divisor 10 of 45, but 10 does not divide 45. By Lagrange\u2019s theorem, no subgroup of order 10 can exist. So Statement 1 is false.\n\n**Statement 2:**  \nThe number of left cosets always equals the number of right cosets for any subgroup (both equal the index). This equality is not a condition for normality; normality requires that left cosets equal right cosets as sets, not just the count. So the statement is false.\n\n**Answer:** False, False  \n\nAnswer: False, False", "correct": true, "gold": "False, False"}
{"qid": "mp:8224", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "These two statements express the same standard result from the First Isomorphism Theorem.\n\n- If \\(\\varphi: G \\to H\\) is a homomorphism, then\n  \\[\n  G/\\ker(\\varphi) \\cong \\operatorname{Im}(\\varphi).\n  \\]\n  So every homomorphic image of \\(G\\) is isomorphic to a factor group of \\(G\\). This proves Statement 1.\n\n- Conversely, every factor group \\(G/N\\) (with \\(N \\trianglelefteq G\\)) is itself a homomorphic image of \\(G\\), via the natural projection\n  \\[\n  \\pi: G \\to G/N.\n  \\]\n  So the homomorphic images of \\(G\\) and the factor groups of \\(G\\) are exactly the same up to isomorphism. This proves Statement 2.\n\nHence both statements are true, and Statement 2 is essentially a stronger restatement of Statement 1 together with the obvious converse.\n\nAnswer: Both Statement 1 and Statement 2 are true.", "correct": false, "gold": "True, True"}
{"qid": "mp:8437", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Statement 1:** True. A free abelian group is isomorphic to a direct sum $\\bigoplus_{i \\in I} \\mathbb{Z}$, and $\\mathbb{Z}$ is torsion-free; direct sums of torsion-free groups are torsion-free. Thus, no nonzero element has finite order.\n\n**Statement 2:** True. By the structure theorem for finitely generated abelian groups, every finitely generated torsion-free abelian group is isomorphic to $\\mathbb{Z}^n$ for some $n \\ge 0$, which is a free abelian group.\n\nAnswer: Both statements are true.", "correct": false, "gold": "True, True"}
{"qid": "mp:8224", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Both statements express the First Isomorphism Theorem for groups: if \u03c6: G \u2192 H is a homomorphism, then the image \u03c6(G) is isomorphic to G/ker(\u03c6), a factor group of G. Conversely, every factor group G/N is itself a homomorphic image of G (via the canonical projection). So these two statements are just two ways of stating the same correspondence, and both are true.\n\nAnswer: True, True", "correct": true, "gold": "True, True"}
{"qid": "mp:7772", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "For projectile motion where a ball is launched from and lands at the same height, the horizontal range $R$ is given by:\n\n$$R = \\frac{v_0^2 \\sin(2\\theta)}{g}$$\n\nwhere $v_0$ is the initial speed, $\\theta$ is the launch angle, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nGiven $\\theta = 45^{\\circ}$ and $R = 90\\text{ m}$:\n$$\\sin(2 \\times 45^{\\circ}) = \\sin(90^{\\circ}) = 1$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula:\n$$90 = \\frac{v_0^2}{g}$$\n\nSolving for $v_0$:\n$$v_0 = \\sqrt{90g}$$\n\nUsing $g \\approx 9.8\\text{ m/s}^2$:\n$$v_0 = \\sqrt{90 \\times 9.8} = \\sqrt{882} \\approx 29.7\\text{ m/s}$$\n\n(If using $g \\approx 10\\text{ m/s}^2$, then $v_0 = \\sqrt{900} = 30\\text{ m/s}$.)\n\nAnswer: approximately $30\\text{ m/s}$ (or $29.7\\text{ m/s}$ using $g = 9.8\\text{ m/s}^2$)", "correct": true, "gold": "30 $\\mathrm{m}/\\mathrm{s}$"}
{"qid": "mp:8224", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "Let\u2019s analyze both statements.\n\n**Statement 1:**  \n\"Every homomorphic image of a group G is isomorphic to a factor group of G.\"  \nThis is true by the First Isomorphism Theorem: if \u03c6: G \u2192 H is a homomorphism, then Im(\u03c6) \u2245 G / ker(\u03c6), which is a factor group of G.\n\n**Statement 2:**  \n\"The homomorphic images of a group G are the same (up to isomorphism) as the factor groups of G.\"  \nThis is also true, because every factor group G/N is itself a homomorphic image via the canonical projection \u03c0: G \u2192 G/N, and conversely every homomorphic image is isomorphic to some G/N by the First Isomorphism Theorem.\n\nBoth statements are true.\n\nAnswer: True, True", "correct": true, "gold": "True, True"}
{"qid": "mp:7891", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>**Reasoning**\n\n- **Statement\u202f1:** By Lagrange\u2019s theorem, the order of any subgroup must divide the order of the group. Here \\(10 \\nmid 45\\), so no group of order\u202f45 (abelian or otherwise) can have a subgroup of order\u202f10. Hence Statement\u202f1 is false.\n\n- **Statement\u202f2:** For any subgroup \\(H\\) of a group \\(G\\), the number of left cosets equals the number of right cosets; both equal the index \\([G\\!:\\!H]\\). This equality holds for every subgroup, not just normal ones. Normality requires that each left coset equals a right coset as sets, not merely that their counts match. Therefore the given condition does not characterize normality, making Statement\u202f2 false.\n\n**Answer:** Both statements are false.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "False, False"}
{"qid": "mp:7772", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Answer: }v_0 \\approx 3.0 \\times 10^1\\,\\mathrm{m/s}}\n\\]", "correct": false, "gold": "30 $\\mathrm{m}/\\mathrm{s}$"}
{"qid": "mp:7891", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "By Lagrange's theorem, the order of any subgroup of a finite group \\(G\\) must divide \\(|G|\\). Here \\(|G| = 45 = 3^2 \\cdot 5\\) while \\(10 = 2 \\cdot 5\\), so 10 does not divide 45. Consequently no group of order 45 (abelian or otherwise) can contain a subgroup of order 10, and Statement 1 is false.\n\nThe collection of all left cosets of any subgroup \\(H \\leq G\\) and the collection of all right cosets of \\(H\\) always have the same cardinality, namely the index \\([G:H] = |G|/|H|\\). Statement 2 therefore asserts that \\(H\\) is normal if and only if a property that holds for every subgroup. The asserted equivalence is false, so Statement 2 is false.\n\nBoth statements are therefore false.\n\nAnswer: both false", "correct": false, "gold": "False, False"}
{"qid": "mp:8192", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Use partial fractions:\n\\[\n\\frac{5z-2}{z(z-1)}=\\frac{A}{z}+\\frac{B}{z-1}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n5z-2=A(z-1)+Bz=(A+B)z-A.\n\\]\nSo\n\\[\nA+B=5,\\qquad -A=-2 \\Rightarrow A=2,\\quad B=3.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n\\frac{5z-2}{z(z-1)}=\\frac{2}{z}+\\frac{3}{z-1}.\n\\]\n\nBoth poles \\(z=0\\) and \\(z=1\\) lie inside \\(|z|=2\\), so\n\\[\n\\int_{|z|=2}\\frac{5z-2}{z(z-1)}\\,dz\n=2\\int_{|z|=2}\\frac{dz}{z}+3\\int_{|z|=2}\\frac{dz}{z-1}.\n\\]\nBy the standard contour integral formula,\n\\[\n\\int \\frac{dz}{z-a}=2\\pi i\n\\]\nwhen \\(a\\) is inside the contour. Therefore\n\\[\n2(2\\pi i)+3(2\\pi i)=5(2\\pi i)=10\\pi i.\n\\]\n\nSo \\(A=10\\pi\\).\n\nAnswer: 10\u03c0", "correct": false, "gold": "31.4"}
{"qid": "mp:8224", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: Both statements are true.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "True, True"}
{"qid": "mp:8192", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Setting up partial fractions:**\n\n$$\\frac{5z-2}{z(z-1)} = \\frac{2}{z} + \\frac{3}{z-1}$$\n\n**Checking poles:** Both $z=0$ and $z=1$ lie inside $|z|=2$.\n\n**Applying the Residue Theorem:**\n\n$$\\oint_{|z|=2} \\frac{5z-2}{z(z-1)}\\,dz = 2\\pi i \\left(\\text{Res}_{z=0} + \\text{Res}_{z=1}\\right) = 2\\pi i(2+3) = 10\\pi i$$\n\nAnswer: 10\u03c0", "correct": false, "gold": "31.4"}
{"qid": "mp:7891", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "**Statement 1:** By Lagrange's theorem, if $H$ is a subgroup of a finite group $G$, then $|H|$ must divide $|G|$. Here $|G| = 45 = 3^2 \\cdot 5$ and the proposed subgroup has order $10 = 2 \\cdot 5$. Since $10 \\nmid 45$ (as $2 \\nmid 45$), no group of order 45 can have a subgroup of order 10, abelian or otherwise. **False.**\n\n**Statement 2:** For **any** subgroup $H$ of $G$ (normal or not), the number of left cosets always equals the number of right cosets; both equal the index $[G:H] = |G|/|H|$. However, $H$ is normal if and only if $gH = Hg$ as **sets** for every $g \\in G$ (i.e., each left coset coincides with the corresponding right coset), not merely that the counts are equal. For example, in $S_3$, the subgroup $H = \\langle(12)\\rangle$ has 3 left cosets and 3 right cosets, but $H$ is not normal since $(13)H \\neq H(13)$. **False.**\n\nAnswer: Both statements are false.", "correct": false, "gold": "False, False"}
{"qid": "mp:8192", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We need to compute  \n\\[\n\\int_{|z| = 2} \\frac{5z - 2}{z(z-1)} \\, dz.\n\\]\n\nThe integrand has singularities at \\(z = 0\\) and \\(z = 1\\), both inside the contour \\(|z| = 2\\).  \nWe can use the residue theorem.\n\nFirst, find the residues.\n\nAt \\(z = 0\\):\n\\[\n\\text{Res}_{z=0} = \\lim_{z \\to 0} z \\cdot \\frac{5z - 2}{z(z-1)} = \\lim_{z \\to 0} \\frac{5z - 2}{z-1} = \\frac{-2}{-1} = 2.\n\\]\n\nAt \\(z = 1\\):\n\\[\n\\text{Res}_{z=1} = \\lim_{z \\to 1} (z-1) \\cdot \\frac{5z - 2}{z(z-1)} = \\lim_{z \\to 1} \\frac{5z - 2}{z} = \\frac{5 - 2}{1} = 3.\n\\]\n\nSum of residues = \\(2 + 3 = 5\\).\n\nBy the residue theorem,\n\\[\n\\int_{|z| = 2} \\frac{5z - 2}{z(z-1)} \\, dz = 2\\pi i \\times 5 = 10\\pi i.\n\\]\n\nThe problem says the answer is \\(A i\\), so \\(A = 10\\pi\\).\n\nAnswer: \\(10\\pi\\)", "correct": false, "gold": "31.4"}
{"qid": "mp:8224", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The First Isomorphism Theorem states that if \\(\\phi:G\\to H\\) is any group homomorphism, then\n\\[\nG/\\ker\\phi\\cong\\operatorname{im}\\phi.\n\\]\nThus every homomorphic image of \\(G\\) arises (up to isomorphism) as a quotient of \\(G\\) by the kernel of the homomorphism, which proves Statement 1.\n\nConversely, for every normal subgroup \\(N\\trianglelefteq G\\) the canonical projection\n\\[\n\\pi:G\\to G/N,\\qquad g\\mapsto gN\n\\]\nis a surjective homomorphism whose image is exactly the quotient group \\(G/N\\). Consequently every quotient of \\(G\\) is realized as a homomorphic image of \\(G\\). Combining the two directions shows that the collection of all homomorphic images of \\(G\\) coincides, up to isomorphism, with the collection of all factor groups of \\(G\\), which proves Statement 2.\n\nBoth statements are therefore true.\n\nAnswer: both true", "correct": false, "gold": "True, True"}
{"qid": "mp:8224", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Both statements are true by the First Isomorphism Theorem for groups. \r\n\r\n- **Statement 1**: If $\\phi: G \\to H$ is a group homomorphism, then $\\operatorname{Im}(\\phi) \\cong G / \\ker(\\phi)$, so every homomorphic image is isomorphic to some factor group of $G$.\r\n- **Statement 2**: Conversely, for any normal subgroup $N \\trianglelefteq G$, the canonical projection $\\pi: G \\to G/N$ is a homomorphism with image $G/N$. Thus, up to isomorphism, the homomorphic images and factor groups of $G$ are exactly the same. \r\n\r\nAnswer: Both Statement 1 and Statement 2 are true.", "correct": false, "gold": "True, True"}
{"qid": "mp:8192", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The integrand has simple poles at \\(z=0\\) and \\(z=1\\), both of which lie inside the contour \\(|z|=2\\) (assumed positively oriented). Partial-fraction decomposition yields\n\\[\n\\frac{5z-2}{z(z-1)}=\\frac{2}{z}+\\frac{3}{z-1}.\n\\]\nThe residue at each pole is therefore the corresponding coefficient, and the sum of the residues inside the contour is \\(2+3=5\\).\n\nBy the residue theorem the integral equals\n\\[\n2\\pi i\\cdot5=10\\pi i.\n\\]\nHence the integral is of the form \\(A i\\) with \\(A=10\\pi\\).\n\nAnswer: 10\\pi", "correct": false, "gold": "31.4"}
{"qid": "mp:8192", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The integrand has simple poles at \\(z=0\\) and \\(z=1\\), both inside \\(|z|=2\\).\n\nWrite it in partial fractions:\n\\[\n\\frac{5z-2}{z(z-1)}=\\frac{A}{z}+\\frac{B}{z-1}.\n\\]\nSolving gives \\(A=2,\\;B=3\\).\n\nResidues:\n- At \\(z=0\\): \\(\\operatorname{Res}=A=2\\).\n- At \\(z=1\\): \\(\\operatorname{Res}=B=3\\).\n\nSum of residues \\(=2+3=5\\).\n\nBy the residue theorem,\n\\[\n\\int_{|z|=2}\\frac{5z-2}{z(z-1)}\\,dz = 2\\pi i \\times 5 = 10\\pi i.\n\\]\n\nThus the integral equals \\(Ai\\) with \\(A=10\\pi\\).\n\nAnswer: 10\u03c0<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "31.4"}
{"qid": "mp:8280", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Use each of \\(+\\), \\(\\times\\), and \\(-\\) exactly once in\n\\[\n2\\ \\_\\ 4\\ \\_\\ 6\\ \\_\\ 8\n\\]\nwith normal order of operations, so multiplication is done before addition/subtraction.\n\nCheck the 6 possible arrangements:\n\n1. \\(2+4\\times 6-8=2+24-8=18\\)\n2. \\(2+4-6\\times 8=6-48=-42\\)\n3. \\(2\\times 4+6-8=8+6-8=6\\)\n4. \\(2\\times 4-6+8=8-6+8=10\\)\n5. \\(2-4+6\\times 8=-2+48=46\\)\n6. \\(2-4\\times 6+8=2-24+8=-14\\)\n\nThe largest value is \\(46\\).\n\nAnswer: 46", "correct": true, "gold": "46"}
{"qid": "mp:8192", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The integrand has simple poles at \\(z=0\\) and \\(z=1\\), both inside \\(|z|=2\\).  \nUsing partial fractions:\n\\[\n\\frac{5z-2}{z(z-1)}=\\frac{2}{z}+\\frac{3}{z-1}.\n\\]\nThe residue at \\(z=0\\) is \\(2\\) and at \\(z=1\\) is \\(3\\).  \nBy the residue theorem,\n\\[\n\\int_{|z|=2}\\frac{5z-2}{z(z-1)}\\,dz = 2\\pi i\\,(2+3)=10\\pi i.\n\\]\nThus the coefficient \\(A\\) is \\(10\\pi\\).\n\nAnswer: \\(10\\pi\\)", "correct": false, "gold": "31.4"}
{"qid": "mp:8280", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Answer: 46**\n\nTesting all 6 arrangements of +, \u00d7, \u2212 in the blanks:\n\n- 2 + 4 \u00d7 6 \u2212 8 = 2 + 24 \u2212 8 = 18\n- 2 + 4 \u2212 6 \u00d7 8 = 2 + 4 \u2212 48 = \u221242\n- 2 \u00d7 4 + 6 \u2212 8 = 8 + 6 \u2212 8 = 6\n- 2 \u00d7 4 \u2212 6 + 8 = 8 \u2212 6 + 8 = 10\n- 2 \u2212 4 + 6 \u00d7 8 = 2 \u2212 4 + 48 = **46**\n- 2 \u2212 4 \u00d7 6 + 8 = 2 \u2212 24 + 8 = \u221214\n\nThe maximum value comes from **2 \u2212 4 + 6 \u00d7 8 = 46**.\n\nAnswer: 46", "correct": true, "gold": "46"}
{"qid": "mp:8280", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We have the numbers 2, 4, 6, 8 in that order, and we must insert each of the three operations \\(+\\), \\(\\times\\), and \\(-\\) exactly once, with no parentheses.\n\nWithout parentheses, the order of operations (PEMDAS/BODMAS) applies: multiplication before addition and subtraction.\n\nWe need to try all possible arrangements of the three operations and compute the result.\n\nLet\u2019s denote the expression as:\n\n\\[\n2 \\ \\text{op}_1 \\ 4 \\ \\text{op}_2 \\ 6 \\ \\text{op}_3 \\ 8\n\\]\n\nPossible sequences of operations (permutations of +, \u00d7, -):\n\n1. \\(+\\), \\(\\times\\), \\(-\\)  \n   Expression: \\(2 + 4 \\times 6 - 8\\)  \n   Multiplication first: \\(4 \\times 6 = 24\\)  \n   Then: \\(2 + 24 - 8 = 18\\)\n\n2. \\(+\\), \\(-\\), \\(\\times\\)  \n   Expression: \\(2 + 4 - 6 \\times 8\\)  \n   Multiplication first: \\(6 \\times 8 = 48\\)  \n   Then: \\(2 + 4 - 48 = -42\\)\n\n3. \\(\\times\\), \\(+\\), \\(-\\)  \n   Expression: \\(2 \\times 4 + 6 - 8\\)  \n   Multiplication first: \\(2 \\times 4 = 8\\)  \n   Then: \\(8 + 6 - 8 = 6\\)\n\n4. \\(\\times\\), \\(-\\), \\(+\\)  \n   Expression: \\(2 \\times 4 - 6 + 8\\)  \n   Multiplication first: \\(2 \\times 4 = 8\\)  \n   Then: \\(8 - 6 + 8 = 10\\)\n\n5. \\(-\\), \\(+\\), \\(\\times\\)  \n   Expression: \\(2 - 4 + 6 \\times 8\\)  \n   Multiplication first: \\(6 \\times 8 = 48\\)  \n   Then: \\(2 - 4 + 48 = 46\\)\n\n6. \\(-\\), \\(\\times\\), \\(+\\)  \n   Expression: \\(2 - 4 \\times 6 + 8\\)  \n   Multiplication first: \\(4 \\times 6 = 24\\)  \n   Then: \\(2 - 24 + 8 = -14\\)\n\nThe highest result is 46.\n\nAnswer: 46", "correct": true, "gold": "46"}
{"qid": "mp:7995", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Setup (Chebyshev's Inequality):**\n\nFor $Y \\sim b(n, 0.25)$, we have $E(Y/n) = 0.25$ and \n$$\\text{Var}(Y/n) = \\frac{p(1-p)}{n} = \\frac{(0.25)(0.75)}{n} = \\frac{0.1875}{n}$$\n\nBy Chebyshev's inequality:\n$$P\\left(\\left|\\frac{Y}{n}-0.25\\right| < 0.05\\right) \\geq 1 - \\frac{\\text{Var}(Y/n)}{(0.05)^2} = 1 - \\frac{0.1875/n}{0.0025}$$\n\n**For n = 500:**\n\n$$P\\left(\\left|\\frac{Y}{500}-0.25\\right| < 0.05\\right) \\geq 1 - \\frac{0.1875}{500(0.0025)} = 1 - \\frac{0.1875}{1.25} = 1 - 0.15 = 0.85$$\n\n**Answer: 0.85**", "correct": true, "gold": "$0.85$"}
{"qid": "mp:7995", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Let \\(Y \\sim b(n,0.25)\\). Then\n\n\\[\n\\frac{Y}{n}\n\\]\n\nis the sample proportion, with\n\n\\[\nE\\!\\left(\\frac Yn\\right)=0.25,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}\\!\\left(\\frac Yn\\right)=\\frac{0.25(0.75)}{n}=\\frac{0.1875}{n}.\n\\]\n\nWe want a lower bound for\n\n\\[\nP\\left(\\left|\\frac Yn-0.25\\right|<0.05\\right).\n\\]\n\nUse Chebyshev\u2019s inequality:\n\n\\[\nP\\left(\\left|\\frac Yn-0.25\\right|<0.05\\right)\\ge 1-\\frac{\\operatorname{Var}(Y/n)}{(0.05)^2}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=500\\),\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Var}\\!\\left(\\frac Yn\\right)=\\frac{0.1875}{500}=0.000375.\n\\]\n\nAlso,\n\n\\[\n(0.05)^2=0.0025.\n\\]\n\nSo\n\n\\[\n1-\\frac{0.000375}{0.0025}=1-0.15=0.85.\n\\]\n\nAnswer: \\(\\boxed{0.85}\\)", "correct": true, "gold": "$0.85$"}
{"qid": "mp:8280", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The problem requires inserting the operators \\(+\\), \\(\\times\\), and \\(-\\) (each used once) into the expression \\(2 \\underline{\\hphantom{8}} 4 \\underline{\\hphantom{8}} 6 \\underline{\\hphantom{8}} 8\\) to maximize the result. Standard operator precedence applies (\\(\\times\\) is always performed before \\(+\\) or \\(-\\)), and parentheses are disallowed.\n\nThere are exactly six possible expressions, which can be evaluated as follows (performing multiplication first where applicable):\n\n- \\(2 + 4 \\times 6 - 8 = 2 + 24 - 8 = 18\\)\n- \\(2 + 4 - 6 \\times 8 = 6 - 48 = -42\\)\n- \\(2 \\times 4 + 6 - 8 = 8 + 6 - 8 = 6\\)\n- \\(2 \\times 4 - 6 + 8 = 8 - 6 + 8 = 10\\)\n- \\(2 - 4 + 6 \\times 8 = -2 + 48 = 46\\)\n- \\(2 - 4 \\times 6 + 8 = 2 - 24 + 8 = -14\\)\n\nAmong these results, the maximum value is 46 (from the expression \\(2 - 4 + 6 \\times 8\\)).\n\nNo other arrangement of the operators is possible, and all other results are strictly smaller than 46.\n\nAnswer: 46", "correct": true, "gold": "46"}
{"qid": "mp:8192", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "\\[\n\\boxed{A=10\\pi}\n\\]", "correct": false, "gold": "31.4"}
{"qid": "mp:7995", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We have \\( Y \\sim b(n, 0.25) \\), so \\( E[Y/n] = 0.25 \\) and \\( \\text{Var}(Y/n) = \\frac{0.25 \\cdot 0.75}{n} = \\frac{0.1875}{n} \\).\n\nWe want a lower bound for  \n\\[\nP\\left( \\left| \\frac{Y}{n} - 0.25 \\right| < 0.05 \\right)\n\\]  \nwhen \\( n = 500 \\).\n\nUsing Chebyshev's inequality:  \n\\[\nP\\left( \\left| \\frac{Y}{n} - 0.25 \\right| \\geq \\epsilon \\right) \\leq \\frac{\\text{Var}(Y/n)}{\\epsilon^2}\n\\]  \nHere \\(\\epsilon = 0.05\\), \\(\\text{Var} = 0.1875 / 500 = 0.000375\\).\n\nSo  \n\\[\nP\\left( \\left| \\frac{Y}{n} - 0.25 \\right| \\geq 0.05 \\right) \\leq \\frac{0.000375}{0.0025} = 0.15\n\\]  \nThus  \n\\[\nP\\left( \\left| \\frac{Y}{n} - 0.25 \\right| < 0.05 \\right) \\geq 1 - 0.15 = 0.85\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 0.85", "correct": true, "gold": "$0.85$"}
{"qid": "mp:7995", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The random variable \\(Y\\) follows a binomial distribution with parameters \\(n\\) and success probability \\(p = 0.25\\). The goal is to bound from below the probability\n\\[\nP\\bigl(|Y/n - 0.25| < 0.05\\bigr)\n\\]\nwhen \\(n = 500\\).  \n\nLet \\(\\hat p = Y/n\\). Then\n\\[\n\\mathbb{E}(\\hat p) = 0.25, \\qquad\n\\operatorname{Var}(\\hat p) = \\frac{0.25\\cdot 0.75}{n} = \\frac{0.1875}{n}.\n\\]\nTchebysheff\u2019s inequality applied to \\(\\hat p\\) states that, for any \\(k > 0\\),\n\\[\nP\\bigl(|\\hat p - 0.25| \\ge k\\bigr) \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{Var}(\\hat p)}{k^2},\n\\]\nand therefore\n\\[\nP\\bigl(|\\hat p - 0.25| < k\\bigr) \\ge 1 - \\frac{\\operatorname{Var}(\\hat p)}{k^2}.\n\\]\nSubstituting the concrete values \\(n = 500\\) and \\(k = 0.05\\) yields\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Var}(\\hat p) = \\frac{0.1875}{500} = 0.000375,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{0.000375}{(0.05)^2} = \\frac{0.000375}{0.0025} = 0.15.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nP\\bigl(|Y/n - 0.25| < 0.05\\bigr) \\ge 1 - 0.15 = 0.85.\n\\]\n(The same numerical bound is obtained by writing the variance as the exact fraction \\(3/8000\\) and simplifying the resulting ratio.)\n\nAnswer: 0.85", "correct": true, "gold": "$0.85$"}
{"qid": "mp:8280", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>To maximize the expression \\(2\\;\\_\\,4\\;\\_\\,6\\;\\_\\,8\\) using each of \\(+\\), \\(-\\), and \\(\\times\\) exactly once, evaluate all six possible operator orders, respecting the standard precedence (multiplication before addition/subtraction).\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2+4-6\\times8 &= -42 \\\\\n2+4\\times6-8 &= 18 \\\\\n2-4+6\\times8 &= 46 \\\\\n2-4\\times6+8 &= -14 \\\\\n2\\times4+6-8 &= 6 \\\\\n2\\times4-6+8 &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe largest result is obtained with the operators \\(-,\\;+\\;,\\times\\) in that order: \\(2-4+6\\times8 = 46\\).\n\nAnswer: 46<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "46"}
{"qid": "mp:8715", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Reduce modulo 23:\n\n- \\(25 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{23}\\), so\n  \\[\n  25^{1059} \\equiv 2^{1059} \\pmod{23}.\n  \\]\n\nSince 23 is prime, by Fermat\u2019s little theorem:\n\\[\n2^{22} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{23}.\n\\]\n\nNow reduce the exponent:\n\\[\n1059 \\mod 22.\n\\]\nSince \\(22 \\cdot 48 = 1056\\), we get\n\\[\n1059 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{22}.\n\\]\n\nSo\n\\[\n2^{1059} \\equiv 2^3 = 8 \\pmod{23}.\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 8", "correct": true, "gold": "8"}
{"qid": "mp:8715", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Answer: 8", "correct": true, "gold": "8"}
{"qid": "mp:8715", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "25 \u2261 2 (mod 23), so the problem reduces to computing \\(2^{1059} \\mod 23\\).\nSince 23 is prime and does not divide 2, Fermat's Little Theorem gives \\(2^{22} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{23}\\).\nReduce the exponent: \\(1059 = 22 \\cdot 48 + 3\\), so \\(1059 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{22}\\).\nThus \\(2^{1059} \\equiv 2^3 \\equiv 8 \\pmod{23}\\).\n(The same congruence holds after replacing the original base 25 by 2.)\nAnswer: 8", "correct": true, "gold": "8"}
{"qid": "mp:8715", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We want \\( 25^{1059} \\bmod 23 \\).\n\nFirst, reduce the base modulo 23:  \n\\( 25 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{23} \\).  \nSo the problem becomes \\( 2^{1059} \\bmod 23 \\).\n\nBy Fermat\u2019s little theorem, since 23 is prime and 2 is not divisible by 23:  \n\\( 2^{22} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{23} \\).\n\nNow reduce the exponent modulo 22:  \n\\( 1059 \\div 22 \\):  \n\\( 22 \\times 48 = 1056 \\), remainder \\( 1059 - 1056 = 3 \\).  \nSo \\( 2^{1059} \\equiv 2^{3} \\pmod{23} \\).\n\n\\( 2^3 = 8 \\).\n\nThus the remainder is 8.\n\nAnswer: 8", "correct": true, "gold": "8"}
{"qid": "mp:8224", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Both statements are true.\n\n**Reasoning:** By the First Isomorphism Theorem, if $\\phi: G \\to H$ is a surjective homomorphism, then $H \\cong G/\\ker(\\phi)$. This proves Statement 1: every homomorphic image is isomorphic to a factor group. \n\nConversely, for any normal subgroup $N \\trianglelefteq G$, the natural projection $\\pi: G \\to G/N$ is a surjective homomorphism, so every factor group is itself a homomorphic image. Therefore, the two collections contain exactly the same isomorphism classes of groups, proving Statement 2.\n\nAnswer: Both statements are true.", "correct": false, "gold": "True, True"}
{"qid": "mp:8715", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>To find \\(25^{1059} \\mod 23\\), first reduce the base modulo 23:\n\n\\[\n25 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{23}\n\\]\n\nThus we need \\(2^{1059} \\mod 23\\).\n\nSince 23 is prime, \\(\\phi(23) = 22\\). By Euler\u2019s theorem, for any integer coprime to 23,\n\n\\[\n2^{22} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{23}\n\\]\n\nHence we can reduce the exponent modulo 22:\n\n\\[\n1059 \\equiv 1059 - 22 \\times 48 = 1059 - 1056 = 3 \\pmod{22}\n\\]\n\nSo\n\n\\[\n2^{1059} \\equiv 2^{3} \\pmod{23}\n\\]\n\nFinally,\n\n\\[\n2^{3} = 8\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the remainder when \\(25^{1059}\\) is divided by 23 is 8.\n\n**Answer:** 8<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "8"}
{"qid": "mp:7995", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "We use Chebyshev's inequality for the sample proportion $\\hat{p}=Y/n$. Since $Y \\sim b(n,0.25)$, we have $E[\\hat{p}]=0.25$ and $\\operatorname{Var}(\\hat{p})=\\frac{0.25\\cdot 0.75}{n}=\\frac{0.1875}{n}$. \n\nFor $n=500$ and $\\epsilon=0.05$,\n$$\nP\\!\\left(\\left|\\frac{Y}{n}-0.25\\right|<0.05\\right)=P\\!\\left(|\\hat{p}-0.25|<\\epsilon\\right)\\ge 1-\\frac{\\operatorname{Var}(\\hat{p})}{\\epsilon^2}=1-\\frac{0.1875/500}{(0.05)^2}=1-\\frac{0.000375}{0.0025}=1-0.15=0.85.\n$$\n\nAnswer: $0.85$", "correct": true, "gold": "$0.85$"}
{"qid": "mp:8715", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "First, reduce the base modulo 23:\n$$25 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{23}$$\n\nSo we need to find the remainder of $2^{1059}$ when divided by 23.\n\nSince 23 is prime, by Fermat's Little Theorem, $a^{22} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{23}$ for any $a$ not divisible by 23. Thus:\n$$2^{22} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{23}$$\n\nNow, reduce the exponent 1059 modulo 22:\n$$1059 = 22 \\times 48 + 3$$\nSo $1059 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{22}$.\n\nTherefore:\n$$2^{1059} = 2^{22 \\times 48 + 3} = (2^{22})^{48} \\cdot 2^3 \\equiv 1^{48} \\cdot 8 \\equiv 8 \\pmod{23}$$\n\n(Note: The order of 2 modulo 23 is actually 11, and $1059 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{11}$ as well, yielding the same result.)\n\nAnswer: 8", "correct": true, "gold": "8"}
{"qid": "mp:7990", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Setup:** Q(t) = Q\u2080e^(\u2212t/100), where Q\u2080 = 200 g (since flow rate/volume = 2/200 = 1/100 min\u207b\u00b9).\n\n**Condition:** Concentration reaches 1% of original means Q(t)/Q\u2080 = 0.01:\n\ne^(\u2212t/100) = 0.01\n\nt = 100 ln(100) \u2248 460.5 minutes\n\nAnswer: t = 100 ln(100) \u2248 460.5 minutes", "correct": true, "gold": "460.5 min"}
{"qid": "mp:7990", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Let \\(y(t)\\) be the amount of dye (in grams) in the tank at time \\(t\\) minutes.\n\nInitial amount of dye:\n\\[\ny(0)=200\\text{ L}\\cdot 1\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}=200\\text{ g}\n\\]\n\nSince fresh water flows in, dye enters at rate \\(0\\).  \nThe tank volume stays \\(200\\) L, and the outflow is \\(2\\) L/min.\n\nSo the dye leaves at rate\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{y}{200}\\right)(2)=\\frac{y}{100}\\quad \\text{g/min}\n\\]\n\nThus\n\\[\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-\\frac{y}{100}\n\\]\n\nSolve:\n\\[\ny=200e^{-t/100}\n\\]\n\nThe concentration is \\(1\\%\\) of the original when the amount of dye is \\(1\\%\\) of \\(200\\) g, i.e.\n\\[\ny=2\\text{ g}\n\\]\n\nSo\n\\[\n200e^{-t/100}=2\n\\]\n\\[\ne^{-t/100}=0.01\n\\]\n\\[\n-\\frac{t}{100}=\\ln(0.01)=-\\ln 100\n\\]\n\\[\nt=100\\ln 100\\approx 460.5\n\\]\n\nAnswer: \\(\\boxed{100\\ln 100 \\text{ min} \\approx 461 \\text{ min}}\\)", "correct": true, "gold": "460.5 min"}
{"qid": "mp:8280", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Answer: 46", "correct": true, "gold": "46"}
{"qid": "mp:7995", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Using Chebyshev\u2019s inequality for the sample proportion \\(\\bar{Y}=Y/n\\):\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar{Y}) = \\frac{p(1-p)}{n}= \\frac{0.25\\cdot0.75}{500}=0.000375 .\n\\]\n\nChebyshev gives  \n\n\\[\nP(|\\bar{Y}-0.25|\\ge 0.05)\\le\\frac{\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar{Y})}{0.05^{2}}\n      =\\frac{0.000375}{0.0025}=0.15 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nP(|\\bar{Y}-0.25|<0.05)=1-P(|\\bar{Y}-0.25|\\ge 0.05)\\ge 1-0.15=0.85 .\n\\]\n\nSo a guaranteed lower bound for the required probability when \\(n=500\\) is \\(0.85\\).\n\nAnswer: 0.85<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "$0.85$"}
{"qid": "mp:7990", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We have an initial volume \\( V = 200 \\, \\text{L} \\), initial concentration \\( C_0 = 1 \\, \\text{g/L} \\), so initial mass \\( m_0 = 200 \\, \\text{g} \\).  \nFresh water flows in at \\( 2 \\, \\text{L/min} \\), and the well-stirred solution flows out at the same rate, so volume stays constant.\n\nThe differential equation for mass \\( m(t) \\) of dye:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{dm}{dt} = -\\frac{2}{200} m = -\\frac{m}{100}\n\\]\n\nSolving:\n\n\\[\nm(t) = m_0 e^{-t/100}\n\\]\n\nConcentration \\( C(t) = m(t)/200 \\), so:\n\n\\[\nC(t) = C_0 e^{-t/100}\n\\]\n\nWe want \\( C(t) = 0.01 C_0 \\):\n\n\\[\ne^{-t/100} = 0.01\n\\]\n\n\\[\n-\\frac{t}{100} = \\ln(0.01) = -\\ln(100)\n\\]\n\n\\[\nt = 100 \\ln(100) = 100 \\cdot 2 \\ln(10) \\approx 100 \\cdot 4.60517 = 460.517 \\, \\text{min}\n\\]\n\nAnswer: \\( 100 \\ln 100 \\) minutes, or approximately \\( 460.5 \\) minutes.\n\nAnswer: \\( t = 100 \\ln 100 \\) minutes (\u2248 460.5 min)", "correct": true, "gold": "460.5 min"}
{"qid": "mp:7995", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "For the sample proportion \\(\\hat p = Y/n\\) we have  \n\\(E[\\hat p]=0.25\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Var}(\\hat p)=\\frac{0.25(0.75)}{n}=\\frac{0.1875}{n}\\).  \nChebyshev\u2019s inequality gives  \n\\[\nP\\bigl(|\\hat p-0.25|\\ge 0.05\\bigr)\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{Var}(\\hat p)}{(0.05)^2}\n= \\frac{0.1875/n}{0.0025}= \\frac{75}{n}.\n\\]  \nHence  \n\\[\nP\\bigl(|\\hat p-0.25|<0.05\\bigr)\\ge 1-\\frac{75}{n}.\n\\]  \nWith \\(n=500\\),  \n\\[\n1-\\frac{75}{500}=1-0.15=0.85.\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 0.85", "correct": true, "gold": "$0.85$"}
{"qid": "mp:8715", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "We want the remainder when $25^{1059}$ is divided by $23$. \r\n\r\n1. **Reduce the base modulo 23:**  \r\n   $25 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{23}$, so $25^{1059} \\equiv 2^{1059} \\pmod{23}$.\r\n\r\n2. **Apply Fermat's Little Theorem:**  \r\n   Since $23$ is prime and $\\gcd(2,23)=1$, we have $2^{22} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{23}$.\r\n\r\n3. **Reduce the exponent modulo 22:**  \r\n   $22 \\times 48 = 1056$, so $1059 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{22}$.  \r\n   Thus, $2^{1059} \\equiv 2^3 \\pmod{23} \\equiv 8 \\pmod{23}$.\r\n\r\nAnswer: 8", "correct": true, "gold": "8"}
{"qid": "mp:8646", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Let \\(P\\) be the probability a patient visits a physical therapist, and \\(C\\) the probability a patient visits a chiropractor.\n\nGiven:\n- \\(P(P \\cap C) = 0.28\\)\n- \\(P(\\text{neither}) = 0.08\\), so \\(P(P \\cup C) = 1 - 0.08 = 0.92\\)\n- \\(P = C + 0.16\\)\n\nUsing inclusion-exclusion:\n\\[\nP(P \\cup C) = P + C - P(P \\cap C)\n\\]\n\\[\n0.92 = P + C - 0.28\n\\]\n\\[\nP + C = 1.20\n\\]\n\nAlso:\n\\[\nP - C = 0.16\n\\]\n\nNow solve the system:\n\\[\nP + C = 1.20\n\\]\n\\[\nP - C = 0.16\n\\]\n\nAdd:\n\\[\n2P = 1.36\n\\]\n\\[\nP = 0.68\n\\]\n\nAnswer: \\(\\boxed{68\\%}\\)", "correct": false, "gold": "0.68"}
{"qid": "mp:7990", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The mixing process is governed by the balance law for the amount of dye \\(Q(t)\\) (in grams) at time \\(t\\) (in minutes). Because the inflow equals the outflow, the volume of fluid in the tank remains constant at \\(200\\) L. Consequently the only contribution to the rate of change of \\(Q\\) is the dye leaving the tank:\n\\[\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{200}Q=-\\frac{Q}{100}.\n\\]\nThis is a separable first-order equation. Rearrangement and integration yield\n\\[\n\\int\\frac{dQ}{Q}=-\\int\\frac{dt}{100}\\implies\\ln|Q|=-t/100+C,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\nQ(t)=Ae^{-t/100}.\n\\]\nThe initial condition \\(Q(0)=200\\) fixes the constant: \\(A=200\\). Hence the explicit solution is\n\\[\nQ(t)=200e^{-t/100}.\n\\]\nThe target concentration is \\(1\\%\\) of the original value \\(1\\) g/L, i.e., \\(0.01\\) g/L. With the fixed volume of \\(200\\) L this corresponds to\n\\[\nQ=2\\text{ g}.\n\\]\nSubstitution into the solution formula produces the algebraic equation\n\\[\n200e^{-t/100}=2\\implies e^{-t/100}=1/100.\n\\]\nTaking the natural logarithm of both sides immediately gives the exact elapsed time\n\\[\nt=100\\ln100\\text{ minutes}.\n\\]\n(If a decimal approximation is desired, \\(\\ln100\\approx4.60517\\), so \\(t\\approx460.5\\) min.)\nAnswer: 100 ln(100) min", "correct": false, "gold": "460.5 min"}
{"qid": "mp:8646", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Setting up the equations:**\n\nLet P = event of visiting a physical therapist, C = event of visiting a chiropractor.\n\nGiven information:\n- P(P \u2229 C) = 0.28\n- P(neither) = 0.08, so P(P \u222a C) = 1 \u2212 0.08 = 0.92\n- P(P) \u2212 P(C) = 0.16\n\n**Using the union formula:**\n\n$$P(P \\cup C) = P(P) + P(C) - P(P \\cap C)$$\n\n$$0.92 = P(P) + P(C) - 0.28$$\n\n$$P(P) + P(C) = 1.20$$\n\n**Solving the system:**\n\n$$P(P) + P(C) = 1.20$$\n$$P(P) - P(C) = 0.16$$\n\nAdding these equations:\n\n$$2P(P) = 1.36$$\n\n$$P(P) = 0.68$$\n\nAnswer: 0.68", "correct": true, "gold": "0.68"}
{"qid": "mp:7990", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The tank volume stays constant at \\(V = 200\\ \\text{L}\\) because the inflow and outflow rates are equal (\\(Q = 2\\ \\text{L/min}\\)).  \nLet \\(M(t)\\) be the mass of dye (grams) in the tank at time \\(t\\).  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{dM}{dt}= \\text{inflow of dye} - \\text{outflow of dye}\n               = 0 - Q\\,C(t)\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C(t)=\\dfrac{M(t)}{V}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{dM}{dt}= -\\frac{Q}{V}\\,M(t)\n\\]\n\nwhich gives the exponential decay solution\n\n\\[\nM(t)=M_0 e^{-(Q/V)t},\n\\qquad M_0 = 200\\ \\text{g}.\n\\]\n\nHere \\(\\dfrac{Q}{V}= \\dfrac{2}{200}=0.01\\ \\text{min}^{-1}\\).\n\nThe original dye concentration was \\(1\\ \\text{g/L}\\).  \nWe want the concentration to fall to \\(1\\%\\) of this value:\n\n\\[\nC_{\\text{target}} = 0.01 \\times 1\\ \\text{g/L}=0.01\\ \\text{g/L}.\n\\]\n\nWith the volume unchanged, the required dye mass is\n\n\\[\nM_{\\text{target}} = C_{\\text{target}} \\times V = 0.01\\ \\text{g/L} \\times 200\\ \\text{L}=2\\ \\text{g}.\n\\]\n\nSet \\(M(t)=2\\)\u202fg:\n\n\\[\n200\\,e^{-0.01t}=2\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\ne^{-0.01t}=0.01\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n-0.01t=\\ln(0.01)=-4.605170\\ldots\n\\]\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{4.605170\\ldots}{0.01}\\approx 460.5\\ \\text{minutes}.\n\\]\n\nConverting to hours:\n\n\\[\n460.5\\ \\text{min}\\approx \\frac{460.5}{60}=7.68\\ \\text{hours}.\n\\]\n\n**Answer:** Approximately \\(4.6\\times10^{2}\\)\u202fminutes, i.e., about **7.7\u202fhours**.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "460.5 min"}
{"qid": "mp:8280", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Since multiplication is done before addition or subtraction, the position of the \u00d7 matters.  \nThere are three possible places for the \u00d7:\n\n1. **Between 2 and 4:**\u2003\\(2\\times4\\pm6\\pm8 = 8\\pm6\\pm8\\).  \n   The best we can get is \\(8-6+8=10\\).\n\n2. **Between 4 and 6:**\u2003\\(2\\pm4\\times6\\pm8 = 2\\pm24\\pm8\\).  \n   The best is \\(2+24-8=18\\).\n\n3. **Between 6 and 8:**\u2003\\(2\\pm4\\pm6\\times8 = 2\\pm4\\pm48\\).  \n   Using the remaining symbols \\(+\\) and \\(-\\), the largest value is obtained by adding 48 and subtracting the smallest amount:  \n   \\(2-4+48 = 46\\).\n\nThe other arrangements give smaller results, so the maximum possible value is 46.\n\nAnswer: 46", "correct": true, "gold": "46"}
{"qid": "mp:8646", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We are given:\n\n- \\( P(\\text{Both}) = 0.28 \\)\n- \\( P(\\text{Neither}) = 0.08 \\)\n- \\( P(\\text{PT}) = P(\\text{Chiro}) + 0.16 \\)\n\nLet:\n- \\( P(\\text{PT}) = p \\)\n- \\( P(\\text{Chiro}) = c \\)\n\nWe know:\n\\[\np = c + 0.16\n\\]\n\nAlso, from inclusion-exclusion:\n\\[\nP(\\text{PT or Chiro}) = p + c - 0.28\n\\]\nBut \\( P(\\text{PT or Chiro}) = 1 - P(\\text{Neither}) = 1 - 0.08 = 0.92 \\).\n\nSo:\n\\[\np + c - 0.28 = 0.92\n\\]\n\\[\np + c = 1.20\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\( p = c + 0.16 \\):\n\\[\n(c + 0.16) + c = 1.20\n\\]\n\\[\n2c + 0.16 = 1.20\n\\]\n\\[\n2c = 1.04\n\\]\n\\[\nc = 0.52\n\\]\nThen:\n\\[\np = 0.52 + 0.16 = 0.68\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 0.68", "correct": true, "gold": "0.68"}
{"qid": "mp:8646", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Let \\(P\\) be the event that a patient visits a physical therapist and \\(C\\) the event of visiting a chiropractor.\n\nGiven:\n- \\(P(P\\cap C)=0.28\\)\n- \\(P(\\text{neither})=0.08\\Rightarrow P(P\\cup C)=1-0.08=0.92\\)\n- \\(P(P)=P(C)+0.16\\)\n\nBy inclusion\u2013exclusion:\n\\[\nP(P\\cup C)=P(P)+P(C)-P(P\\cap C)\n\\]\n\\[\n0.92 = P(P)+P(C)-0.28 \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; P(P)+P(C)=1.20.\n\\]\n\nWe also have \\(P(P)-P(C)=0.16\\). Adding the two equations:\n\\[\n2P(P)=1.20+0.16=1.36 \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; P(P)=0.68.\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 0.68", "correct": true, "gold": "0.68"}
{"qid": "mp:8049", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Find the prime factorizations:\n\n- \\(252 = 2^2 \\cdot 3^2 \\cdot 7\\)\n- \\(96 = 2^5 \\cdot 3\\)\n\nTake the common prime factors with the smallest exponents:\n\n- \\(2^2\\)\n- \\(3^1\\)\n\nSo the greatest common factor is:\n\n\\[\n2^2 \\cdot 3 = 4 \\cdot 3 = 12\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 12", "correct": true, "gold": "12"}
{"qid": "mp:8049", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We need the greatest common factor (GCF) of 252 and 96.\n\nFirst, find the prime factorization:\n\n- 252 = 2 \u00d7 126 = 2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 63 = 2\u00b2 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 21 = 2\u00b2 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 7 = 2\u00b2 \u00d7 3\u00b2 \u00d7 7  \n- 96 = 2 \u00d7 48 = 2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 24 = 2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 12 = 2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 6 = 2\u2075 \u00d7 3\n\nCommon prime factors: 2 and 3.  \nSmallest exponent for 2: 2\u00b2, for 3: 3\u00b9.  \nGCF = 2\u00b2 \u00d7 3 = 4 \u00d7 3 = 12.\n\nAnswer: 12", "correct": true, "gold": "12"}
{"qid": "mp:8049", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Finding the GCF of 252 and 96**\n\nFirst, find the prime factorization of each number:\n- 252 = 2\u00b2 \u00d7 3\u00b2 \u00d7 7\n- 96 = 2\u2075 \u00d7 3\n\nTake the lowest power of each common prime factor:\n- Common factor 2: lowest power is 2\u00b2 = 4\n- Common factor 3: lowest power is 3\u00b9 = 3\n\nGCF = 4 \u00d7 3 = 12\n\nAnswer: 12", "correct": true, "gold": "12"}
{"qid": "mp:8646", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "Let \\(PT\\) denote the event of visiting a physical therapist and \\(C\\) the event of visiting a chiropractor. Then\n\\[\nP(PT \\cap C) = 0.28, \\qquad P(\\text{neither}) = 0.08.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\nP(PT \\cup C) = 1 - 0.08 = 0.92.\n\\]\nThe inclusion-exclusion principle supplies the linear relation\n\\[\n0.92 = P(PT) + P(C) - 0.28,\n\\]\nwhich rearranges to\n\\[\nP(PT) + P(C) = 1.20.\n\\]\nThe remaining datum is the absolute difference of the marginal probabilities:\n\\[\nP(PT) = P(C) + 0.16.\n\\]\nSubstitution yields the elementary equation\n\\[\n(P(C) + 0.16) + P(C) = 1.20 \\implies 2P(C) = 1.04 \\implies P(C) = 0.52.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nP(PT) = 0.52 + 0.16 = 0.68.\n\\]\n(The solution is readily verified by recovering the four disjoint regions of the Venn diagram and confirming that they sum to 1.)\nAnswer: 0.68", "correct": true, "gold": "0.68"}
{"qid": "mp:8049", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "To find the greatest common factor (GCF) of 252 and 96, I can use the Euclidean algorithm:\n\n- $252 = 2 \\times 96 + 60$\n- $96 = 1 \\times 60 + 36$\n- $60 = 1 \\times 36 + 24$\n- $36 = 1 \\times 24 + 12$\n- $24 = 2 \\times 12 + 0$\n\nThe last non-zero remainder is 12.\n\nAlternatively, using prime factorization:\n- $252 = 2^2 \\times 3^2 \\times 7$\n- $96 = 2^5 \\times 3$\n- GCF = $2^2 \\times 3 = 4 \\times 3 = 12$\n\nVerification: $252 \\div 12 = 21$ and $96 \\div 12 = 8$.\n\nAnswer: 12", "correct": true, "gold": "12"}
{"qid": "mp:8854", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Using the binomial theorem,\n\\[\n(x+2y)^7=\\sum_{k=0}^7 \\binom{7}{k}x^{7-k}(2y)^k.\n\\]\n\nWe want the term with \\(x^2y^5\\).  \nSo we need \\(7-k=2\\), which gives \\(k=5\\).\n\nThen the coefficient is\n\\[\n\\binom{7}{5}2^5 = 21\\cdot 32 = 672.\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 672", "correct": true, "gold": "672"}
{"qid": "mp:7990", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Let $Q(t)$ be the amount of dye (in grams) in the tank at time $t$ (in minutes). The tank volume remains constant at $V = 200$ L since the inflow and outflow rates are equal (2 L/min).\n\nThe rate of change of dye is governed by the differential equation:\n$$\\frac{dQ}{dt} = \\text{rate in} - \\text{rate out}$$\n\nSince fresh water enters, the rate in is $0$. The concentration of dye in the tank at time $t$ is $\\frac{Q(t)}{200}$ g/L, so the rate out is:\n$$\\text{rate out} = \\left(\\frac{Q}{200}\\right) \\times 2 = \\frac{Q}{100}$$\n\nThus:\n$$\\frac{dQ}{dt} = -\\frac{Q}{100}$$\n\nSolving this separable equation with initial condition $Q(0) = 200 \\times 1 = 200$ g:\n$$Q(t) = 200e^{-t/100}$$\n\nThe concentration is $c(t) = \\frac{Q(t)}{200} = e^{-t/100}$ g/L. We want the time when the concentration reaches $1\\%$ of its original value ($1$ g/L), i.e., $0.01$ g/L:\n\n$$e^{-t/100} = 0.01 = \\frac{1}{100}$$\n\nTaking the natural logarithm:\n$$-\\frac{t}{100} = \\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{100}\\right) = -\\ln(100)$$\n\n$$t = 100\\ln(100) \\text{ minutes}$$\n\nSince $\\ln(100) = 2\\ln(10) \\approx 4.6052$, we have $t \\approx 460.5$ minutes (or about 7.", "correct": true, "gold": "460.5 min"}
{"qid": "mp:8646", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Let  \n\n- \\(P\\) = probability of visiting a physical therapist,  \n- \\(C\\) = probability of visiting a chiropractor.\n\nGiven:  \n\n- \\(P(C\\cap P)=0.28\\) (both),  \n- \\(P(\\text{neither})=0.08\\) \u2192 \\(P(P\\cup C)=1-0.08=0.92\\),  \n- \\(P > C\\) by \\(16\\%\\) \u2192 \\(P = C + 0.16\\).\n\nUsing inclusion\u2013exclusion:\n\\[\nP(P\\cup C)=P+P(C)-P(C\\cap P) \\\\\n0.92 = P + C - 0.28 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad P + C = 1.20.\n\\]\n\nNow solve the system:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nP + C = 1.20,\\\\\nP - C = 0.16,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nadding gives \\(2P = 1.36\\), so \\(P = 0.68\\).\n\nThus the probability that a randomly selected person visits a physical therapist is **68\u202f%**.\n\nAnswer: 68%<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "0.68"}
{"qid": "mp:8049", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The greatest common factor (GCF) of two numbers is the largest positive integer that divides both without a remainder. It is equivalent to the greatest common divisor (GCD).\nTo compute it, apply the Euclidean algorithm, which repeatedly replaces the larger number by the remainder of their division:\n- \\(252 = 2 \\times 96 + 60\\)\n- \\(96 = 1 \\times 60 + 36\\)\n- \\(60 = 1 \\times 36 + 24\\)\n- \\(36 = 1 \\times 24 + 12\\)\n- \\(24 = 2 \\times 12 + 0\\)\nWhen the remainder reaches 0, the divisor at that step (12) is the GCD.\nVerification via prime factorization yields the same result:\n- \\(252 = 2^2 \\times 3^2 \\times 7\\)\n- \\(96 = 2^5 \\times 3\\)\nThe product of the lowest powers of all shared primes is \\(2^2 \\times 3 = 12\\).\nAnswer: 12", "correct": true, "gold": "12"}
{"qid": "mp:8854", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Using the binomial theorem: term with x\u00b2y\u2075 comes from k=5:\n\nC(7,5)\u00b7x\u00b2\u00b7(2y)\u2075 = 21 \u00b7 32 \u00b7 x\u00b2y\u2075 = 672x\u00b2y\u2075\n\nAnswer: 672", "correct": true, "gold": "672"}
{"qid": "mp:8049", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>To find the greatest common factor (GCF) of 252 and 96, we can use the Euclidean algorithm:\n\n1. Divide 252 by 96:  \n   \\(252 = 2 \\times 96 + 60\\) \u2192 remainder 60.  \n2. Divide 96 by 60:  \n   \\(96 = 1 \\times 60 + 36\\) \u2192 remainder 36.  \n3. Divide 60 by 36:  \n   \\(60 = 1 \\times 36 + 24\\) \u2192 remainder 24.  \n4. Divide 36 by 24:  \n   \\(36 = 1 \\times 24 + 12\\) \u2192 remainder 12.  \n5. Divide 24 by 12:  \n   \\(24 = 2 \\times 12 + 0\\) \u2192 remainder 0.\n\nWhen the remainder becomes 0, the last non\u2011zero remainder is the GCF. Here, that value is 12.\n\nAnswer: 12<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "12"}
{"qid": "mp:8854", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We want the coefficient of \\(x^2 y^5\\) in \\((x + 2y)^7\\).\n\nUsing the binomial theorem:\n\n\\[\n(x + 2y)^7 = \\sum_{k=0}^{7} \\binom{7}{k} x^{7-k} (2y)^k\n\\]\n\nFor the term \\(x^2 y^5\\), we need \\(7 - k = 2\\) so \\(k = 5\\).\n\nThe term is:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{7}{5} x^{2} (2y)^5 = \\binom{7}{5} \\cdot 2^5 \\cdot x^2 y^5\n\\]\n\nCompute:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{7}{5} = \\binom{7}{2} = 21\n\\]\n\\[\n2^5 = 32\n\\]\n\\[\n21 \\times 32 = 672\n\\]\n\nThus the coefficient is \\(672\\).\n\nAnswer: 672", "correct": true, "gold": "672"}
{"qid": "mp:8854", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: 672<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "672"}
{"qid": "mp:8854", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "To find the coefficient of $x^2y^5$ in $(x + 2y)^7$, I use the binomial theorem. The general term is:\n\n$$\\binom{7}{k} x^{7-k}(2y)^k$$\n\nFor the term $x^2y^5$, I need:\n- $x^{7-k} = x^2$, so $7-k = 2$, giving $k = 5$\n- This gives $(2y)^5 = 2^5 y^5 = 32y^5$\n\nThe coefficient is:\n$$\\binom{7}{5} \\cdot 2^5$$\n\nCalculating:\n- $\\binom{7}{5} = \\binom{7}{2} = \\frac{7 \\cdot 6}{2} = 21$\n- $2^5 = 32$\n- $21 \\times 32 = 672$\n\nAnswer: 672", "correct": true, "gold": "672"}
