{"qid": "mp:8789", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We need to follow the order of operations (PEMDAS/BODMAS).\n\nFirst, solve inside the parentheses:  \n8 \u2212 5 = 3\n\nNow the expression is:  \n6 \u2022 24 \u00f7 3\n\nMultiplication and division have the same precedence, so we work left to right:  \n6 \u2022 24 = 144  \n144 \u00f7 3 = 48\n\nAnswer: 48", "correct": true, "gold": "48"}
{"qid": "mp:8766", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Solve the ODE:\n\\[\n9y''+12y'+4y=0.\n\\]\nIts characteristic equation is\n\\[\n9r^2+12r+4=0=(3r+2)^2,\n\\]\nso there is a repeated root\n\\[\nr=-\\frac23.\n\\]\nHence the general solution is\n\\[\ny(t)=(C_1+C_2 t)e^{-2t/3}.\n\\]\n\nUse the initial conditions.\n\nFrom \\(y(0)=a\\), we get\n\\[\nC_1=a.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiate:\n\\[\ny'(t)=\\left(C_2-\\frac23(C_1+C_2 t)\\right)e^{-2t/3}.\n\\]\nSo\n\\[\ny'(0)=C_2-\\frac23 C_1=-1.\n\\]\nSubstitute \\(C_1=a\\):\n\\[\nC_2-\\frac23 a=-1\n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\nC_2=-1+\\frac23 a.\n\\]\n\nThus\n\\[\ny(t)=\\left(a+\\left(-1+\\frac23 a\\right)t\\right)e^{-2t/3}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(e^{-2t/3}>0\\) for all \\(t\\), the sign of \\(y(t)\\) is determined by\n\\[\na+\\left(-1+\\frac23 a\\right)t.\n\\]\n\nFor the solution to stay positive for all \\(t\\ge 0\\), this linear factor must never become negative. Since it starts at \\(a>0\\), this happens exactly when its slope is nonnegative:\n\\[\n-1+\\frac23 a \\ge 0.\n\\]\nSo the critical value is\n\\[\n\\frac23 a = 1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a=\\frac32.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(a<\\frac32\\), the slope is negative and the solution eventually becomes negative.\nIf \\(a\\ge \\frac32\\), the solution stays positive for all \\(t\\ge 0\\) (with \\(a=\\frac32\\) giving \\(y(t)=\\frac32 e^{-2t/3}>0\\)).\n\nAnswer: \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}\\)", "correct": false, "gold": "1.5"}
{"qid": "mp:8646", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "\\[\n\\boxed{P(\\text{visit physical therapist})=0.68}\n\\]", "correct": true, "gold": "0.68"}
{"qid": "mp:8766", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Critical value:** a = 3/2\n\n**Reasoning:**\n\nThe characteristic equation is \n$$9r^2+12r+4=0 \\implies (3r+2)^2=0,$$ \ngiving a repeated root $r=-\\tfrac{2}{3}$.\n\nSo the general solution is\n$$y(t) = (C_1 + C_2 t)e^{-2t/3}.$$\n\nApplying initial conditions:\n- $y(0)=C_1=a$\n- $y'(t) = C_2 e^{-2t/3} - \\tfrac{2}{3}(C_1+C_2 t)e^{-2t/3}$, so $y'(0)=C_2-\\tfrac{2}{3}a=-1 \\Rightarrow C_2 = \\tfrac{2}{3}a - 1$.\n\nThus\n$$y(t) = \\left[a + \\left(\\tfrac{2}{3}a - 1\\right)t\\right]e^{-2t/3}.$$\n\nSince $e^{-2t/3}>0$ always, the sign of $y(t)$ depends on the linear factor $a + (\\tfrac{2}{3}a-1)t$.\n\n- If $\\tfrac{2}{3}a - 1 < 0$ (i.e., $a < \\tfrac{3}{2}$), the linear term eventually decreases and crosses zero for some $t>0$, making $y$ negative.\n- If $\\tfrac{2}{3}a - 1 \\ge 0$ (i.e., $a \\ge \\tfrac{3}{2}$), the linear factor stays positive for all $t>0$, so $y(t)>0$ for all $t$.\n\nTherefore, the critical value separating solutions that become negative from those that remain positive is:\n\nAnswer: a = 3/2", "correct": false, "gold": "1.5"}
{"qid": "mp:7941", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "For a strictly convex function, the graph lies **below** every chord connecting two points on the graph.\n\nWe know:\n- \\(f(-2)=5\\)\n- \\(f(2)=9\\)\n\nFirst, use the chord from \\((-2,5)\\) to \\((2,9)\\). Its slope is\n\n\\[\n\\frac{9-5}{2-(-2)}=\\frac{4}{4}=1\n\\]\n\nSo the line is\n\n\\[\ny=x+7\n\\]\n\nAt \\(x=1\\), this line has value\n\n\\[\n1+7=8\n\\]\n\nSince the function is strictly convex, the point at \\(x=1\\) must lie strictly below this chord:\n\n\\[\nf(1)<8\n\\]\n\nNow find a lower bound. Since \\(1\\) lies between \\(-2\\) and \\(2\\), strict convexity also implies the slopes increase as \\(x\\) increases. Thus,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{f(1)-f(-2)}{1-(-2)} < \\frac{f(2)-f(-2)}{2-(-2)}\n\\]\n\nThat is,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{f(1)-5}{3} < 1\n\\]\n\nso\n\n\\[\nf(1)<8\n\\]\n\nFor the other side, compare the slope from \\(-2\\) to \\(2\\) with the slope from \\(1\\) to \\(2\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{f(2)-f(-2)}{2-(-2)} < \\frac{f(2)-f(1)}{2-1}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n1 < 9-f(1)\n\\]\n\n\\[\nf(1)<8\n\\]\n\nTo get the lower bound, compare slopes from \\(-2\\) to \\(1\\) and from \\(-2\\) to \\(2\\) in the other direction of convexity: equivalently, a strictly convex function can approach any value arbitrarily low at an interior point while still staying below the chord and remaining strictly convex. So there is **no finite lower bound**.\n\nHence the possible values are all real numbers less than \\(8\\):\n\nAnswer: \\((-\\infty,8)\\)", "correct": true, "gold": "(-inf, 8)"}
{"qid": "mp:8766", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We start with the differential equation:\n\n\\[\n9 y'' + 12 y' + 4 y = 0\n\\]\n\nThe characteristic equation is:\n\n\\[\n9r^2 + 12r + 4 = 0\n\\]\n\nThe discriminant is:\n\n\\[\n12^2 - 4 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 4 = 144 - 144 = 0\n\\]\n\nSo there is a repeated root:\n\n\\[\nr = \\frac{-12}{18} = -\\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nThe general solution is:\n\n\\[\ny(t) = (C_1 + C_2 t) e^{-\\frac{2}{3}t}\n\\]\n\nUsing initial conditions:\n\n\\[\ny(0) = C_1 = a\n\\]\n\\[\ny'(t) = C_2 e^{-\\frac{2}{3}t} - \\frac{2}{3}(C_1 + C_2 t) e^{-\\frac{2}{3}t}\n\\]\n\\[\ny'(0) = C_2 - \\frac{2}{3}C_1 = -1\n\\]\nSo:\n\n\\[\nC_2 - \\frac{2}{3}a = -1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad C_2 = \\frac{2}{3}a - 1\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\n\\[\ny(t) = \\left( a + \\left( \\frac{2}{3}a - 1 \\right) t \\right) e^{-\\frac{2}{3}t}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( e^{-\\frac{2}{3}t} > 0 \\) for all \\(t\\), the sign of \\(y(t)\\) is determined by:\n\n\\[\nf(t) = a + \\left( \\frac{2}{3}a - 1 \\right) t\n\\]\n\nThis is a linear function in \\(t\\). For \\(t \\ge 0\\), if the slope \\(\\frac{2}{3}a - 1\\) is positive, then \\(f(t)\\) increases from \\(a>0\\) and stays positive.  \nIf the slope is negative, eventually \\(f(t)\\) will cross zero and become negative.\n\nThe critical case is when the slope is zero:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2}{3}a - 1 = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad a = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(a > \\frac{3}{2}\\), slope positive \u2192 always positive.  \nFor \\(a < \\frac{3}{2}\\), slope negative \u2192 eventually negative.\n\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{3}{2}\\)  \n\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{3}{2}\\)", "correct": false, "gold": "1.5"}
{"qid": "mp:7941", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "## Setting Up the Constraint\n\nSince $f$ is strictly convex, for any $a < b < c$, the point $(b, f(b))$ must lie **below** the line segment connecting $(a, f(a))$ and $(c, f(c))$.\n\nHere, I'll use $a = -2$, $b = 1$, $c = 2$, since $-2 < 1 < 2$.\n\n## Finding the Line Segment\n\nThe line segment connects $(-2, 5)$ and $(2, 9)$.\n\n**Slope:**\n$$m = \\frac{9-5}{2-(-2)} = \\frac{4}{4} = 1$$\n\n**Equation of the line:**\n$$y - 5 = 1(x-(-2)) \\implies y = x + 7$$\n\n**Value at $x=1$:**\n$$y = 1 + 7 = 8$$\n\n## Applying the Convexity Condition\n\nBy strict convexity, the point $(1, f(1))$ must lie **strictly below** this line segment at $x=1$:\n$$f(1) < 8$$\n\n## Checking for a Lower Bound\n\nIs there any restriction preventing $f(1)$ from being very negative? Since only two points of $f$ are fixed $(-2,5)$ and $(2,9)$, and no other points are specified, we can construct convex functions passing through these points with $f(1)$ arbitrarily low.\n\n**Example:** Consider a steep parabola like $f(x) = k(x-c)^2 + d$ for large $k$, adjusted to pass through $(-2,5)$ and $(2,9)$ \u2014 such curves exist for any target value of $f(1)$ less than 8, while remaining strictly convex. Since convexity only imposes an **upper bound** at each interior point (not a lower bound), $f(1)$ can be made arbitrarily negative.\n\nThus, there is no lower bound on $f(1)$.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe range of possible values for $f(1)$ is:\n\n**Answer:** $(-\\infty, 8)$", "correct": true, "gold": "(-inf, 8)"}
{"qid": "mp:8766", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The given initial-value problem is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. The associated characteristic equation is\n\\[\n9r^2 + 12r + 4 = 0.\n\\]\nIts discriminant is zero, so there is a repeated real root \\(r = -2/3\\). Consequently the general solution takes the form\n\\[\ny(t) = (A + Bt)e^{-(2/3)t}.\n\\]\nThe initial condition \\(y(0) = a\\) immediately yields \\(A = a\\), and the initial condition \\(y'(0) = -1\\) then yields\n\\[\nB = \\frac{2}{3}a - 1.\n\\]\nThus the unique solution of the initial-value problem is\n\\[\ny(t) = \\Bigl[a + \\bigl(\\tfrac{2}{3}a - 1\\bigr)t\\Bigr]e^{-(2/3)t}.\n\\]\nBecause the exponential factor is strictly positive for all \\(t\\), the sign of \\(y(t)\\) is completely determined by the linear factor\n\\[\np(t) := a + \\bigl(\\tfrac{2}{3}a - 1\\bigr)t.\n\\]\n- If the slope of \\(p\\) satisfies \\(\\frac{2}{3}a - 1 \\ge 0\\), i.e., if \\(a \\ge 3/2\\), then \\(p(t)\\) is non-decreasing and remains at least as large as its positive initial value \\(a\\). Hence \\(y(t) > 0\\) for every \\(t \\ge 0\\).\n- If the slope is negative, i.e., if \\(a < 3/2\\), then \\(p(t)\\) has a unique positive zero\n  \\[\n  t^* = \\frac{a}{1 - \\frac{2}{3}a} > 0.\n  \\]\n  Consequently \\(y(t)\\) changes from positive to negative at \\(t^*\\) and remains negative for all larger \\(t\\).\n\nThe value \\(a = 3/2\\) is therefore the threshold that separates initial data producing solutions that eventually become negative from initial data producing solutions that stay positive for all future time.\n\nAnswer: 3/2", "correct": false, "gold": "1.5"}
{"qid": "mp:8766", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The differential equation has a repeated root \\(r=-\\frac23\\), giving  \n\n\\[\ny(t)=\\bigl(a+C_2 t\\bigr)e^{-2t/3},\n\\qquad \nC_2=-1+\\frac{2}{3}a .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\ny(t)=\\Bigl(a+\\Bigl(\\frac{2a}{3}-1\\Bigr)t\\Bigr)e^{-2t/3}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(e^{-2t/3}>0\\) for all \\(t\\), the sign of \\(y(t)\\) is determined by the linear factor  \n\n\\[\nL(t)=a+\\Bigl(\\frac{2a}{3}-1\\Bigr)t .\n\\]\n\n- If \\(\\frac{2a}{3}-1\\ge 0\\) (i.e. \\(a\\ge\\frac32\\)), then \\(L(t)\\ge a>0\\) for all \\(t\\ge0\\); the solution stays positive.\n- If \\(\\frac{2a}{3}-1<0\\) (i.e. \\(a<\\frac32\\)), then \\(L(t)\\) decreases, reaches zero at  \n  \\[\n  t_0=\\frac{a}{1-\\frac{2a}{3}}>0,\n  \\]\n  and becomes negative thereafter, so the solution eventually becomes negative.\n\nThus the critical value separating the two behaviours is \\(a=\\frac32\\).\n\nAnswer: 3/2<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "1.5"}
