{"qid": "mp:8438", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Statement 1 is **false**: in a group, distinct elements can have the same square.  \nExample: in the multiplicative group \\(\\{1,-1,i,-i\\}\\), we have \\(i \\neq -i\\) but\n\\[\ni^2 = (-i)^2 = -1.\n\\]\n\nStatement 2 is **true**: in a dihedral group, the rotations form a cyclic subgroup generated by a single rotation \\(r\\), typically\n\\[\n\\langle r\\rangle = \\{e, r, r^2, \\dots, r^{n-1}\\}.\n\\]\n\nAnswer: Statement 1 is false; Statement 2 is true.", "correct": false, "gold": "False, True"}
{"qid": "mp:8438", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "Let\u2019s check each statement.\n\n**Statement 1:**  \nIn a group, it is possible for two distinct elements to have the same square. For example, in the group of integers under addition, \\( a = 1 \\) and \\( b = -1 \\) are distinct, but \\( a^2 = 1 \\) and \\( b^2 = 1 \\) (using additive notation, this means \\( 1+1 = 2 \\) and \\( -1 + -1 = -2 \\), which are not equal\u2014so that example fails).  \nBetter: In the Klein four-group, every non-identity element squared is the identity, so distinct non-identity elements have the same square. So Statement 1 is false.\n\n**Statement 2:**  \nIn a dihedral group \\( D_n \\), the rotations are \\( \\{ e, r, r^2, \\dots, r^{n-1} \\} \\), which form a cyclic subgroup of order \\( n \\). This is true.\n\n**Answer:** False, True\n\nAnswer: False, True", "correct": true, "gold": "False, True"}
{"qid": "mp:7941", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We are told that \\( f \\) is strictly convex, meaning for any \\( a < b \\), the graph lies below the chord connecting \\((a, f(a))\\) and \\((b, f(b))\\).\n\nGiven points:  \n\\((-2, 5)\\) and \\((2, 9)\\).  \nThe chord between them has slope  \n\\[\nm = \\frac{9 - 5}{2 - (-2)} = \\frac{4}{4} = 1.\n\\]  \nEquation of chord:  \n\\( y - 5 = 1(x + 2) \\Rightarrow y = x + 7 \\).\n\nFor convexity, at \\( x = 1 \\), the function must lie below the chord:  \n\\[\nf(1) < 1 + 7 = 8.\n\\]\n\nNow, we also need a lower bound. Strict convexity also implies that for any point between \\(-2\\) and \\(1\\), the chord from \\((-2,5)\\) to \\((1, f(1))\\) must lie above the function at \\(x=2\\)? Actually, we can use the chord from \\((1, f(1))\\) to \\((2,9)\\): the function at \\(x=-2\\) must lie below the line through \\((1, f(1))\\) and \\((2,9)\\).\n\nSlope from \\((1, f(1))\\) to \\((2,9)\\) is \\(9 - f(1)\\).  \nEquation: \\( y - f(1) = (9 - f(1))(x - 1) \\).  \nAt \\(x = -2\\):  \n\\[\ny = f(1) + (9 - f(1))(-3) = f(1) - 27 + 3f(1) = 4f(1) - 27.\n\\]  \nSince \\( f(-2) = 5 \\) must be below this line (strict convexity),  \n\\[\n5 < 4f(1) - 27.\n\\]  \nSo  \n\\[\n32 < 4f(1) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad f(1) > 8.\n\\]\n\nWait \u2014 this gives \\( f(1) > 8 \\) and earlier we had \\( f(1) < 8 \\). That\u2019s impossible.  \nThis suggests I used the wrong chord direction. For convexity, the point \\((-2,5)\\) must lie below the chord from \\((1, f(1))\\) to \\((2,9)\\)? Let\u2019s check: For convex function, the graph lies below the chord between any two points. So if we take \\(a=1, b=2\\), the chord is above the graph for all \\(x\\) between 1 and 2, but \\(x=-2\\) is not between them, so that inequality doesn\u2019t apply.\n\nWe need to use points that bracket the unknown. Use \\(a=-2, b=1\\) and \\(a=1, b=2\\) to get bounds.\n\n**Using chord from \\((-2,5)\\) to \\((1, f(1))\\):**  \nSlope = \\(\\frac{f(1)-5}{3}\\).  \nAt \\(x=2\\), the chord\u2019s y-value is  \n\\[\ny = 5 + \\frac{f(1)-5}{3}(4) = 5 + \\frac{4(f(1)-5)}{3}.\n\\]  \nSince \\(f(2)=9\\) must be above this chord (convex: graph below chord between -2 and 1, so at x=2, which is outside, the function is above the chord extension), we have  \n\\[\n9 > 5 + \\frac{4(f(1)-5)}{3}.\n\\]  \nSimplify:  \n\\(4 > \\frac{4(f(1)-5)}{3} \\Rightarrow 12 > 4(f(1)-5) \\Rightarrow 3 > f(1)-5 \\Rightarrow f(1) < 8\\).\n\n**Using chord from \\((1, f(1))\\) to \\((2,9)\\):**  \nSlope = \\(9 - f(1)\\).  \nAt \\(x=-2\\), chord value:  \n\\[\ny = f(1) + (9 - f(1))(-3) = f(1) -27 + 3f(1) = 4f(1) - 27.\n\\]  \nSince \\(f(-2)=5\\) must be above this chord (because -2 is outside the interval [1,2], convex function lies above the extension), we have  \n\\[\n5 > 4f(1) - 27.\n\\]  \nSo  \n\\[\n32 > 4f(1) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad f(1) < 8.\n\\]\n\nBoth give \\(f(1) < 8\\). For a lower bound, use chord from \\((-2,5)\\) to \\((2,9)\\): at \\(x=1\\), chord value is \\(1+7=8\\), and convexity says \\(f(1) < 8\\). That\u2019s only an upper bound.\n\nBut convexity also gives a lower bound from the fact that the function lies above the chord connecting points on either side? Actually, for convex functions, the graph lies below chords, so the chord from (-2,5) to (2,9) gives an upper bound. There is no lower bound from just two points \u2014 \\(f(1)\\) can be arbitrarily low? But convexity and given points might restrict it: consider chord from (-2,5) to (1, f(1)) must lie above f at x=2? Already used. Similarly chord from (1, f(1)) to (2,9) must lie above f at x=-2? Already used. Both gave \\(f(1) < 8\\).  \n\nTo get a lower bound, consider the chord from (-2,5) to (2,9) and the fact that f is convex: For any \\(t\\) between, \\(f(t) < \\text{chord}\\). That\u2019s only upper bound. For a lower bound, we need a third point? Actually, convexity doesn\u2019t prevent f(1) from being very negative; the chords would just become steeper. So likely the range is \\((-\\infty, 8)\\).\n\nBut check: If \\(f(1)\\) is very negative, chord from (-2,5) to (1, f(1)) has negative slope; at x=2, chord value might be less than 9? Let's test \\(f(1) = -100\\): slope = (-100-5)/3 = -35. At x=2, chord value = 5 + (-35)(4) = 5 - 140 = -135, and f(2)=9 > -135, condition holds. Similarly other side: chord from (1,-100) to (2,9): slope = 109, at x=-2: -100 + 109(-3) = -100 -327 = -427, and f(-2)=5 > -427, holds. So indeed no lower bound.\n\nThus possible values: \\(f(1) < 8\\).\n\nAnswer: \\((-\\infty, 8)\\)\n\nAnswer: \\((-\\infty, 8)\\)", "correct": true, "gold": "(-inf, 8)"}
{"qid": "mp:8438", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Statement 1: False.**\nCounterexample: In the group \u2124\u2084 (integers mod 4, under addition, where \"a\u00b2\" means a+a), let a = 1 and b = 3. Then a \u2260 b, but a\u00b2 = 1+1 = 2 and b\u00b2 = 3+3 = 6 \u2261 2 (mod 4). So a\u00b2 = b\u00b2 even though a \u2260 b.\n\n**Statement 2: True.**\nIn a dihedral group D\u2099, the rotations form a subgroup generated by a single rotation of minimal angle (360\u00b0/n), so this subgroup is cyclic of order n.\n\nAnswer: False, True", "correct": true, "gold": "False, True"}
{"qid": "mp:8438", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "Statement 1 is false: a counterexample is any group with more than one element of order dividing 2 (e.g., the Klein four-group, or \\(\\mathbb{Z}/4\\mathbb{Z}\\), or \\(S_3\\)). In each case distinct elements \\(a,b\\) satisfy \\(a^2=b^2\\).\n\nStatement 2 is true: the rotations of a regular \\(n\\)-gon form the cyclic subgroup of order \\(n\\) generated by a single rotation through \\(2\\pi/n\\); this is the unique index-2 subgroup of any dihedral group.\n\nAnswer: only statement 2 true", "correct": false, "gold": "False, True"}
{"qid": "mp:8438", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>**Statement\u202f1:**  \nIf \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are elements of a group and \\(a \\neq b\\), it does **not** necessarily follow that \\(a^{2} \\neq b^{2}\\).  \n*Counterexample:* In the group \\(\\mathbb{Z}_{2}\\) (with multiplication written additively), the elements are \\(\\{e,\\,x\\}\\) where \\(x^{2}=e\\). Take \\(a=x\\) and \\(b=e\\). Then \\(a \\neq b\\) but \\(a^{2}=e=b^{2}\\). Hence the statement is **false**.\n\n**Statement\u202f2:**  \nIn a dihedral group \\(D_{n}\\) (the symmetry group of a regular \\(n\\)-gon), the set of all rotations forms a subgroup. This subgroup is generated by a single rotation of order \\(n\\); therefore it is cyclic (isomorphic to \\(\\mathbb{Z}_{n}\\)). Consequently, the statement is **true**.\n\n**Answer:** Statement\u202f1 is false; Statement\u202f2 is true.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "False, True"}
