{"qid": "mp:8438", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reasoning:**\n\n- **Statement 1:** False. In a group, $a^2 = b^2$ does not necessarily imply $a = b$. A simple counterexample is in the symmetric group $S_3$: take the transpositions $a = (1\\ 2)$ and $b = (1\\ 3)$. Then $a \\neq b$, but $a^2 = e$ and $b^2 = e$, so $a^2 = b^2$.\n\n- **Statement 2:** True. The set of all rotations in a dihedral group $D_n$ forms a subgroup of order $n$. This subgroup is cyclic, generated by a single rotation by $2\\pi/n$ radians (the smallest nontrivial rotation).\n\nAnswer: Statement 1 is false; Statement 2 is true.", "correct": false, "gold": "False, True"}
{"qid": "mp:8420", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "We use the fixed-point iteration\n\\[\nx_{k+1}=\\phi(x_k)=\\frac12+\\sin x_k,\\qquad x_0=1,\n\\]\nwith root\n\\[\n\\alpha \\approx 1.497300.\n\\]\n\nFor linear fixed-point iteration, the asymptotic error satisfies\n\\[\ne_{k+1}\\approx \\phi'(\\alpha)e_k,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n\\phi'(x)=\\cos x.\n\\]\nSo the convergence factor is\n\\[\n\\lambda=|\\phi'(\\alpha)|=|\\cos(1.497300)|.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(1.497300\\approx \\frac{\\pi}{2}-0.073496\\),\n\\[\n\\cos(1.497300)\\approx \\sin(0.073496)\\approx 0.07343.\n\\]\nThus the iteration converges linearly with ratio about \\(0.07343\\).\n\nAlso,\n\\[\ne_0=|x_0-\\alpha|=|1-1.497300|=0.497300.\n\\]\n\nTo get 6 decimal places, require\n\\[\n|e_k|<0.5\\times 10^{-6}.\n\\]\nUsing\n\\[\n|e_k|\\approx \\lambda^k |e_0|,\n\\]\nwe need\n\\[\n0.4973(0.07343)^k < 0.5\\times 10^{-6}.\n\\]\nSo\n\\[\n(0.07343)^k < 1.005\\times 10^{-6}.\n\\]\nTaking logs gives\n\\[\nk>\\frac{\\log(1.005\\times 10^{-6})}{\\log(0.07343)}\\approx 5.19.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nk=6.\n\\]\n\nSo ordinary fixed-point iteration needs **6 steps**.\n\n---\n\n### With Aitken \\(\\Delta^2\\)-acceleration\nOnce three iterates are available, Aitken gives\n\\[\n\\hat x_n = x_n-\\frac{(x_{n+1}-x_n)^2}{x_{n+2}-2x_{n+1}+x_n}.\n\\]\nFor linearly convergent sequences, Aitken \\(\\Delta^2\\) greatly accelerates convergence, effectively eliminating the dominant linear error term.\n\nCompute the first few iterates:\n\\[\nx_0=1,\n\\]\n\\[\nx_1=\\tfrac12+\\sin 1 \\approx 1.341470985,\n\\]\n\\[\nx_2=\\tfrac12+\\sin 1.341470985 \\approx 1.473055826,\n\\]\n\\[\nx_3=\\tfrac12+\\sin 1.473055826 \\approx 1.495245271.\n\\]\n\nNow apply Aitken to \\(x_1,x_2,x_3\\):\n\\[\n\\hat x_1\n= x_1-\\frac{(x_2-x_1)^2}{x_3-2x_2+x_1}\n\\approx 1.497320.\n\\]\nThis is not yet correct to 6 decimals.\n\nNext compute\n\\[\nx_4=\\tfrac12+\\sin x_3 \\approx 1.497139750.\n\\]\nApply Aitken to \\(x_2,x_3,x_4\\):\n\\[\n\\hat x_2\n= x_2-\\frac{(x_3-x_2)^2}{x_4-2x_3+x_2}\n\\approx 1.497300388,\n\\]\nwhich is correct to 6 decimals:\n\\[\n1.497300.\n\\]\n\nThus with Aitken, we need iterates up to \\(x_4\\), i.e. **4 iteration steps**.\n\nAnswer: Ordinary linear iteration requires 6 steps; with Aitken\u2019s \\(\\Delta^2\\)-process, 4 steps are required.", "correct": false, "gold": "3"}
