{"qid": "mp:8531", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The temperature field is \\(u(x,y,z)=2y^{2}+2z^{2}\\).  \nFourier\u2019s law gives the heat\u2011flux vector  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{q}= -K\\nabla u = -6.5\\,(0,\\,4y,\\,4z)=(0,\\,-26y,\\,-26z).\n\\]\n\nOn the cylindrical surface \\(y^{2}+z^{2}=6\\) the outward unit normal is  \n\n\\[\n\\hat{\\mathbf n}= \\frac{(0,2y,2z)}{\\sqrt{(2y)^{2}+(2z)^{2}}}\n               =\\left(0,\\frac{y}{\\sqrt6},\\frac{z}{\\sqrt6}\\right).\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{q}\\cdot\\hat{\\mathbf n}= \n(-26y)\\frac{y}{\\sqrt6}+(-26z)\\frac{z}{\\sqrt6}\n= -\\frac{26(y^{2}+z^{2})}{\\sqrt6}\n= -\\frac{26\\cdot6}{\\sqrt6}= -\\frac{156}{\\sqrt6}.\n\\]\n\nFor a cylinder of radius \\(\\sqrt6\\) the surface element on the curved side is  \n\n\\[\ndS = (\\text{radius})\\,dx\\,d\\theta = \\sqrt6\\;dx\\,d\\theta,\n\\qquad 0\\le x\\le4,\\;0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi .\n\\]\n\nThe total outward heat flow through the curved surface is  \n\n\\[\n\\dot Q_{\\text{out}}=\n\\int_{0}^{4}\\!\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\n(\\mathbf{q}\\cdot\\hat{\\mathbf n})\\,dS\\,d\\theta\n= \\left(-\\frac{156}{\\sqrt6}\\right)\n   \\bigl(\\sqrt6\\bigr)(4)(2\\pi)\n= -1248\\pi .\n\\]\n\nThe negative sign indicates that heat is actually entering the cylinder; therefore the inward rate of heat flow is  \n\n\\[\n\\dot Q_{\\text{in}} = 1248\\pi .\n\\]\n\nAnswer: \\(1248\\pi\\) (units of heat flow, e.g., watts).<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "$1248\\pi$"}
{"qid": "mp:8939", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Yes. For a Galton\u2013Watson branching process with offspring pgf \\(G\\), we have\n\\[\nG_n = G^{\\circ n},\n\\]\nand if \\(H_n = G_n^{-1}\\) on \\([0,1]\\), then\n\\[\nG(H_{n+1}(s)) = H_n(s),\n\\]\nsince \\(G_{n+1}=G_n\\circ G\\).\n\nNow check the martingale property for\n\\[\nM_n = \\bigl(H_n(s)\\bigr)^{Z_n}.\n\\]\nUsing the branching property,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\!\\left[t^{Z_{n+1}} \\mid Z_n\\right] = \\bigl(G(t)\\bigr)^{Z_n}.\n\\]\nSet \\(t=H_{n+1}(s)\\). Then\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\!\\left[M_{n+1}\\mid Z_n\\right]\n= \\mathbb E\\!\\left[\\bigl(H_{n+1}(s)\\bigr)^{Z_{n+1}} \\mid Z_n\\right]\n= \\bigl(G(H_{n+1}(s))\\bigr)^{Z_n}\n= \\bigl(H_n(s)\\bigr)^{Z_n}\n= M_n.\n\\]\nSo \\(M_n\\) is indeed a martingale.\n\nAnswer: 1", "correct": true, "gold": "1.0"}
{"qid": "mp:8104", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "To find the maximum order of an element in $S_6$, I need to consider all possible cycle types (partitions of 6) and compute the LCM of the cycle lengths for each type.\n\nThe partitions of 6 and their corresponding orders are:\n\n- $6$: LCM(6) = **6**\n- $5+1$: LCM(5,1) = 5\n- $4+2$: LCM(4,2) = 4\n- $4+1+1$: LCM(4,1,1) = 4\n- $3+3$: LCM(3,3) = 3\n- $3+2+1$: LCM(3,2,1) = **6**\n- $3+1+1+1$: LCM(3,1,1,1) = 3\n- $2+2+2$: LCM(2,2,2) = 2\n- $2+2+1+1$: LCM(2,2,1,1) = 2\n- $2+1+1+1+1$: LCM(2,1,1,1,1) = 2\n- $1+1+1+1+1+1$: LCM(1,1,1,1,1,1) = 1\n\nThe maximum value is 6, achieved by:\n- A 6-cycle (e.g., $(1\\ 2", "correct": true, "gold": "6"}
{"qid": "mp:8939", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "A branching process satisfies \\(Z_0=1\\) and the recursion \\(Z_{n+1}=\\sum_{i=1}^{Z_n}X_{n,i}\\) with i.i.d. offspring random variables whose common PGF is denoted \\(G_1\\). The iterates of this PGF are exactly the generation PGFs:\n\\[\nG_{n}(t)=G_1^{\\circ n}(t)=G_{n-1}(G_1(t)).\n\\]\nBecause \\(\\mathrm{Var}(Z_1)>0\\), each \\(G_n\\) is strictly increasing on \\([0,1]\\) and therefore admits a well-defined inverse \\(H_n\\) on that interval. Fix an arbitrary parameter \\(s\\in[0,1]\\) and write\n\\[\nh_n:=H_n(s),\\qquad\\text{i.e.}\\qquad G_n(h_n)=s.\n\\]\nThe candidate process is then\n\\[\nM_n=h_n^{Z_n}.\n\\]\nTo verify the martingale property it is enough to check the one-step conditional expectation with respect to the natural filtration \\(\\mathcal{F}_n=\\sigma(Z_0,\\dots,Z_n)\\). The functional equation for the iterates immediately yields\n\\[\nG_n\\bigl(G_1(h_{n+1})\\bigr)=s=G_n(h_n).\n\\]\nInjectivity of \\(G_n\\) forces the identity\n\\[\nG_1(h_{n+1})=h_n.\n\\]\nConditioned on \\(\\mathcal{F}_n\\), the random variable \\(Z_{n+1}\\) is the sum of exactly \\(Z_n\\) independent copies of the offspring distribution; consequently its conditional PGF is\n\\[\n\\mathbb{E}[t^{Z_{n+1}}\\,|\\,\\mathcal{F}_n]=[G_1(t)]^{Z_n}.\n\\]\nSubstituting the particular value \\(t=h_{n+1}\\) therefore produces\n\\[\n\\mathbb{E}[M_{n+1}\\,|\\,\\mathcal{F}_n]=\\mathbb{E}[h_{n+1}^{Z_{n+1}}\\,|\\,\\mathcal{F}_n]=[G_1(h_{n+1})]^{Z_n}=h_n^{Z_n}=M_n.\n\\]\nHence \\(\\{M_n\\}_{n\\ge0}\\) is a martingale. (The same argument also shows that the process is well-defined for the deterministic initial condition \\(Z_0=1\\).)\n\nAnswer: 1", "correct": true, "gold": "1.0"}
{"qid": "mp:8104", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "The maximum order of an element in $S_6$ is determined by the least common multiple (lcm) of the lengths of disjoint cycles whose sum is 6. The possible cycle type partitions and their lcms are: $(6)=6$, $(3,2)=\\operatorname{lcm}(3,2)=6$, $(5,1)=5$, $(4,2)=4$, $(3,3)=3$, etc. The largest among these is $6$.\n\nAnswer: $6$", "correct": true, "gold": "6"}
