{"qid": "mp:8703", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "In a complete graph with \\(n\\) vertices, every pair of distinct vertices is connected by exactly one edge. So the number of edges is\n\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{2} = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=10\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{10 \\cdot 9}{2} = 45\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 45", "correct": true, "gold": "45"}
{"qid": "mp:8703", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "A complete graph with \\(n\\) vertices has an edge between every pair of distinct vertices.  \nThe number of edges is the number of ways to choose 2 vertices from \\(n\\), which is \\(\\binom{n}{2}\\).\n\nFor \\(n = 10\\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{2} = \\frac{10 \\times 9}{2} = 45\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 45", "correct": true, "gold": "45"}
{"qid": "mp:8939", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We need to check whether  \n\\( M_n = [H_n(s)]^{Z_n} \\) defines a martingale.\n\nRecall that for a branching process with offspring pgf \\( f(s) \\), we have  \n\\( G_n(s) = f(G_{n-1}(s)) \\), and \\( H_n \\) is the inverse of \\( G_n \\) on \\([0,1]\\).\n\nA standard result: For a supercritical branching process (or even generally when variance > 0), the process  \n\\( W_n = \\frac{Z_n}{\\mu^n} \\) is a martingale.  \nBut here we have a different form: \\( [H_n(s)]^{Z_n} \\).\n\nConsider the conditional expectation:\n\n\\[\nE[M_{n+1} \\mid \\mathcal{F}_n] = E\\left[ (H_{n+1}(s))^{Z_{n+1}} \\mid Z_n \\right].\n\\]\n\nGiven \\( Z_n = k \\), \\( Z_{n+1} \\) is the sum of \\( k \\) i.i.d. offspring with pgf \\( f \\). So:\n\n\\[\nE\\left[ (H_{n+1}(s))^{Z_{n+1}} \\mid Z_n = k \\right] = \\left[ f(H_{n+1}(s)) \\right]^k.\n\\]\n\nNow, since \\( G_{n+1}(s) = f(G_n(s)) \\), taking inverses gives  \n\\( H_{n+1}(f(s)) = H_n(s) \\).  \nSet \\( t = f(s) \\), then \\( H_{n+1}(t) = H_n(f^{-1}(t)) \\) \u2014 but more directly,  \nif \\( G_{n+1}(x) = f(G_n(x)) \\), then applying inverse:  \n\\( H_{n+1}(s) = G_n^{-1}(f^{-1}(s)) \\) is not the simple relation we need.\n\nBetter: Since \\( G_{n+1}(s) = f(G_n(s)) \\), then  \n\\( H_{n+1}(s) = G_n^{-1}(f^{-1}(s)) \\).  \nBut we want \\( f(H_{n+1}(s)) \\).  \nLet \\( y = H_{n+1}(s) \\), so \\( G_{n+1}(y) = s \\). But \\( G_{n+1}(y) = f(G_n(y)) \\), so  \n\\( f(G_n(y)) = s \\Rightarrow G_n(y) = f^{-1}(s) \\Rightarrow y = H_n(f^{-1}(s)) \\).  \nThus \\( H_{n+1}(s) = H_n(f^{-1}(s)) \\).\n\nThen  \n\\( f(H_{n+1}(s)) = f(H_n(f^{-1}(s))) \\).  \nBut \\( f(H_n(u)) = ? \\)  \nSince \\( G_n(H_n(u)) = u \\), and \\( G_n = f(G_{n-1}) \\), we have \\( f(G_{n-1}(H_n(u))) = u \\), so  \n\\( G_{n-1}(H_n(u)) = f^{-1}(u) \\), hence \\( H_n(u) = G_{n-1}^{-1}(f^{-1}(u)) \\).  \nThis is messy, but the key known result:  \n\\( f(H_{n+1}(s)) = H_n(s) \\). Let's verify:\n\nWe have \\( H_{n+1}(s) = H_n(f^{-1}(s)) \\). Then  \n\\( f(H_{n+1}(s)) = f(H_n(f^{-1}(s))) \\).  \nBut note: \\( G_n(H_n(t)) = t \\), and \\( G_n = f(G_{n-1}) \\), so  \n\\( f(G_{n-1}(H_n(t))) = t \\Rightarrow G_{n-1}(H_n(t)) = f^{-1}(t) \\).  \nThus \\( H_n(t) = G_{n-1}^{-1}(f^{-1}(t)) \\).  \nThen \\( f(H_n(f^{-1}(s))) = f(G_{n-1}^{-1}(f^{-1}(f^{-1}(s)))) \\) \u2014 not obviously \\( H_n(s) \\).\n\nLet's try a simpler approach:  \nWe know \\( G_{n+1}(s) = f(G_n(s)) \\).  \nTake \\( s = H_{n+1}(t) \\):  \n\\( t = G_{n+1}(H_{n+1}(t)) = f(G_n(H_{n+1}(t))) \\).  \nThus \\( f^{-1}(t) = G_n(H_{n+1}(t)) \\).  \nApply \\( H_n \\) to both sides:  \n\\( H_n(f^{-1}(t)) = H_{n+1}(t) \\).  \nSo indeed \\( H_{n+1}(t) = H_n(f^{-1}(t)) \\).  \n\nNow compute \\( f(H_{n+1}(t)) = f(H_n(f^{-1}(t))) \\).  \nBut from \\( G_n(H_n(u)) = u \\), we have \\( f(G_{n-1}(H_n(u))) = u \\), so  \n\\( G_{n-1}(H_n(u)) = f^{-1}(u) \\).  \nThus \\( H_n(u) = G_{n-1}^{-1}(f^{-1}(u)) \\).  \nThen \\( f(H_n(u)) = f(G_{n-1}^{-1}(f^{-1}(u))) \\).  \nThis is not simply \\( H_{n-1}(u) \\) unless \\( f \\) is invertible in a nice way.\n\nActually, the standard martingale in branching processes using generating functions is:  \n\\( M_n = \\frac{f^n(s)^{Z_n}}{s} \\) type? No.\n\nLet\u2019s test with a simple case:  \nOffspring: Poisson(1), so \\( f(s)=e^{s-1} \\).  \nThen \\( G_n(s) = \\exp^{(n)}(s-1) \\) (n-fold exponential). Inverse \\( H_n \\) is messy.  \nBut known fact: For a branching process, \\( M_n = [H_n(s)]^{Z_n} \\) is indeed a martingale (it\u2019s the likelihood ratio martingale associated with the change of measure for branching processes). This is a known result: For \\( s \\in [0,1] \\),  \n\\( E[s^{Z_{n+1}} \\mid \\mathcal{F}_n] = [f(s)]^{Z_n} \\), and by the property of the inverse,  \n\\( E[ H_{n+1}(s)^{Z_{n+1}} \\mid \\mathcal{F}_n] = [f(H_{n+1}(s))]^{Z_n} = [H_n(s)]^{Z_n} \\).  \nSo yes, it is a martingale.\n\nThus answer is 1.\n\nAnswer: 1", "correct": true, "gold": "1.0"}
{"qid": "mp:8703", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "A complete graph \\(K_n\\) has an edge between every pair of distinct vertices. The number of such edges is the number of ways to choose 2 vertices out of \\(n\\), given by the binomial coefficient\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{2} = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(n=10\\) yields\n\\[\n\\frac{10 \\times 9}{2} = 45.\n\\]\nEquivalently, the handshaking lemma counts each vertex's degree as \\(n-1=9\\), so the sum of degrees is \\(10 \\times 9=90\\); dividing by 2 (each edge contributes to two degrees) again gives 45 edges.\nAnswer: 45", "correct": true, "gold": "45"}
{"qid": "mp:8703", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "In a complete graph with $n$ vertices, each pair of distinct vertices is connected by exactly one edge. The number of edges equals the number of ways to choose 2 vertices from $n$, which is $\\binom{n}{2} = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}$.\n\nFor $n = 10$:\n$$\\binom{10}{2} = \\frac{10 \\times 9}{2} = \\frac{90}{2} = 45$$\n\nAlternatively, counting by connections: the first vertex connects to 9 others, the second to 8 new ones, the third to 7 new ones, etc.: $9 + 8 + 7 + \\cdots + 1 = 45$.\n\nAnswer: 45", "correct": true, "gold": "45"}
{"qid": "mp:8703", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>In a complete graph, every pair of distinct vertices is joined by an edge. The number of such unordered pairs for \\(n\\) vertices is given by the combination \\(\\binom{n}{2} = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\). For \\(n = 10\\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{2} = \\frac{10 \\times 9}{2} = 45.\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 45<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "45"}
{"qid": "mp:8052", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Complete the square:\n\n\\[\n6j^2 - 4j + 12 = 6\\left(j^2 - \\frac{2}{3}j\\right) + 12\n\\]\n\nInside the parentheses:\n\n\\[\nj^2 - \\frac{2}{3}j = \\left(j - \\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 - \\frac{1}{9}\n\\]\n\nSo,\n\n\\[\n6\\left[\\left(j - \\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 - \\frac{1}{9}\\right] + 12\n= 6\\left(j - \\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 - \\frac{6}{9} + 12\n= 6\\left(j - \\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 + \\frac{34}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus in the form \\(c(j+p)^2+q\\),\n\n\\[\n6\\left(j + \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\right)^2 + \\frac{34}{3}\n\\]\n\nso\n\n\\[\np=-\\frac{1}{3}, \\quad q=\\frac{34}{3}\n\\]\n\nThen\n\n\\[\n\\frac{q}{p}=\\frac{34/3}{-1/3}=-34\n\\]\n\nAnswer: \\(-34\\)", "correct": true, "gold": "-34"}
{"qid": "mp:8703", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "In a complete graph, every pair of vertices is connected by exactly one edge. For a graph with $n$ vertices, the number of pairs is given by the binomial coefficient $\\binom{n}{2} = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}$. \r\n\r\nFor $n=10$: $\\frac{10 \\times 9}{2} = 45$. \r\n\r\nAnswer: 45", "correct": true, "gold": "45"}
{"qid": "mp:8052", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Answer: -34**\n\nWork: 6j\u00b2 \u2212 4j + 12 = 6(j\u00b2 \u2212 (2/3)j) + 12 = 6[(j \u2212 1/3)\u00b2 \u2212 1/9] + 12 = 6(j \u2212 1/3)\u00b2 + 34/3\n\nSo c = 6, p = \u22121/3, q = 34/3, giving q/p = (34/3)/(\u22121/3) = **\u221234**", "correct": true, "gold": "-34"}
{"qid": "mp:8052", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We start with  \n\\[\n6j^2 - 4j + 12.\n\\]  \nFactor out the coefficient of \\(j^2\\) from the first two terms:  \n\\[\n6\\left(j^2 - \\frac{2}{3}j\\right) + 12.\n\\]  \nComplete the square inside the parentheses. Half of \\(-\\frac{2}{3}\\) is \\(-\\frac{1}{3}\\), and squaring gives \\(\\frac{1}{9}\\). So:  \n\\[\n6\\left(j^2 - \\frac{2}{3}j + \\frac{1}{9} - \\frac{1}{9}\\right) + 12\n= 6\\left((j - \\frac{1}{3})^2 - \\frac{1}{9}\\right) + 12.\n\\]  \nDistribute the 6:  \n\\[\n6\\left(j - \\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 - \\frac{6}{9} + 12\n= 6\\left(j - \\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 - \\frac{2}{3} + 12.\n\\]  \nCombine constants:  \n\\[\n6\\left(j - \\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 + \\frac{34}{3}.\n\\]  \nIn the form \\(c(j + p)^2 + q\\), we have  \n\\(c = 6\\), \\(p = -\\frac{1}{3}\\), \\(q = \\frac{34}{3}\\).  \n\nThen  \n\\[\n\\frac{q}{p} = \\frac{34/3}{-1/3} = -34.\n\\]  \n\nAnswer: -34", "correct": true, "gold": "-34"}
