{"qid": "mp:8052", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "To rewrite $6j^2 - 4j + 12$ in the form $c(j + p)^2 + q$, complete the square:\n\nFactor out 6 from the first two terms:\n$$6j^2 - 4j + 12 = 6\\left(j^2 - \\frac{2}{3}j\\right) + 12$$\n\nComplete the square inside the parentheses. Take half of $-\\frac{2}{3}$, which is $-\\frac{1}{3}$, and square it to get $\\frac{1}{9}$:\n$$j^2 - \\frac{2}{3}j = \\left(j - \\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 - \\frac{1}{9}$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n$$6\\left(\\left(j - \\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 - \\frac{1}{9}\\right) + 12 = 6\\left(j - \\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 - \\frac{6}{9} + 12$$\n\nSimplify:\n$$= 6\\left(j - \\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 - \\frac{2}{3} + 12 = 6\\left(j - \\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 + \\frac{34}{3}$$\n\nMatching with $c(j + p)^2 + q$:\n- $c = 6$\n- $j + p = j - \\frac{1}{3}$, so $p = -\\frac{1}{3}$\n- $q = \\frac{34}{3}$\n\nCalculate $\\frac{q}{p}$:\n$$\\frac{q}{p} = \\frac{\\frac{34}{3}}{-\\frac{1}{3}} = \\frac{34}{3} \\times (-3) = -34$$\n\nAnswer: -34", "correct": true, "gold": "-34"}
{"qid": "mp:8052", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The given quadratic expression is \\(6j^2 - 4j + 12\\).\nFactor the leading coefficient of 6 out of the \\(j^2\\) and \\(j\\) terms:\n\\[\n6\\left(j^2 - \\frac{2}{3}j\\right) + 12.\n\\]\nComplete the square inside the parentheses. Half the coefficient of \\(j\\) is \\(-\\frac{1}{3}\\), and squaring this value yields \\(\\frac{1}{9}\\). Add and subtract this quantity inside the parentheses:\n\\[\n6\\left(j^2 - \\frac{2}{3}j + \\frac{1}{9} - \\frac{1}{9}\\right) + 12 = 6\\left[\\left(j - \\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 - \\frac{1}{9}\\right] + 12.\n\\]\nDistribute the factor of 6 and simplify the constant terms:\n\\[\n6\\left(j - \\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 - \\frac{2}{3} + 12 = 6\\left(j - \\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 + \\frac{34}{3}.\n\\]\nRewrite the squared binomial to match the required form \\(c(j + p)^2 + q\\):\n\\[\n6\\left(j + \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\right)^2 + \\frac{34}{3}.\n\\]\nThus the constants are \\(c = 6\\), \\(p = -\\frac{1}{3}\\), and \\(q = \\frac{34}{3}\\). Their ratio is\n\\[\n\\frac{q}{p} = \\frac{34/3}{-1/3} = -34.\n\\]\nTo confirm, expand the completed-square form and verify that the original coefficients are recovered:\n\\[\n6\\left(j - \\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 + \\frac{34}{3} = 6j^2 - 4j + 12.\n\\]\nAnswer: -34", "correct": true, "gold": "-34"}
{"qid": "mp:8402", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "For 10 distinct people seated around 1 identical round table, rotations are considered the same.\n\nThe number of circular arrangements of \\(n\\) distinct people is:\n\n\\[\n(n-1)!\n\\]\n\nSo for \\(n=10\\):\n\n\\[\n(10-1)! = 9! = 362880\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 362880", "correct": true, "gold": "362880"}
{"qid": "mp:8402", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "For 10 people seated at one round table, the number of distinct circular arrangements is (n-1)! since rotations are considered identical.\n\n(10-1)! = 9! = 362880\n\nAnswer: 362880", "correct": true, "gold": "362880"}
{"qid": "mp:8402", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We have 10 people and 1 identical round table.  \nFor a round table, arrangements that differ only by rotation are considered the same.  \nThe number of distinct seating arrangements for \\(n\\) people around a round table is \\((n-1)!\\).  \n\nHere, \\(n = 10\\), so the number is:\n\n\\[\n(10-1)! = 9! = 362880\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 362880", "correct": true, "gold": "362880"}
{"qid": "mp:8052", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>To rewrite \\(6j^2 - 4j + 12\\) in the form \\(c(j + p)^2 + q\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n6j^2 - 4j + 12 &= 6\\bigl(j^2 - \\tfrac{2}{3}j\\bigr) + 12 \\\\\n&= 6\\Bigl[\\bigl(j - \\tfrac13\\bigr)^2 - \\tfrac19\\Bigr] + 12 \\qquad\\text{(complete the square)}\\\\\n&= 6\\bigl(j - \\tfrac13\\bigr)^2 - \\frac{6}{9} + 12 \\\\\n&= 6\\bigl(j - \\tfrac13\\bigr)^2 - \\frac23 + 12 \\\\\n&= 6\\bigl(j - \\tfrac13\\bigr)^2 + \\frac{34}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(c = 6\\), \\(p = -\\tfrac13\\) (since \\((j - \\tfrac13) = (j + (-\\tfrac13))\\)), and \\(q = \\tfrac{34}{3}\\).\n\nNow compute \\(\\frac{q}{p}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{q}{p} = \\frac{\\tfrac{34}{3}}{-\\tfrac13} = \\frac{34}{3} \\cdot \\left(-3\\right) = -34.\n\\]\n\nAnswer: -34<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "-34"}
{"qid": "mp:8052", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "First, complete the square for $6j^2 - 4j + 12$:\n\n\\[\n6j^2 - 4j + 12 = 6\\left(j^2 - \\frac{2}{3}j\\right) + 12 = 6\\left(\\left(j - \\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 - \\frac{1}{9}\\right) + 12 = 6\\left(j - \\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 - \\frac{2}{3} + 12 = 6\\left(j - \\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 + \\frac{34}{3}.\n\\]\n\nThis matches the form $c(j + p)^2 + q$ with $c=6$, $p=-\\frac{1}{3}$, and $q=\\frac{34}{3}$.\n\nThus, $\\frac{q}{p} = \\frac{34/3}{-1/3} = -34$.\n\nAnswer: $-34$", "correct": true, "gold": "-34"}
{"qid": "mp:8402", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "For seating \\(n\\) distinct people at a single round table (with rotations considered identical), the standard count is given by the circular permutation formula \\((n-1)!\\). This is also the value of the unsigned Stirling number of the first kind \\(c(n,1)\\), which enumerates the distinct ways to arrange \\(n\\) people into exactly one directed cycle.\nHere \\(n=10\\), so the expression simplifies directly to\n\\[\n(10-1)! = 9!.\n\\]\n(The single table is automatically non-empty, satisfying the problem constraint, and the fact that the table is identical introduces no further symmetry factor when there is only one table.)\nReflections are treated as distinct, which is the usual convention for seating arrangements (left and right neighbors differ). Thus the numerical value is \\(362880\\).\nAnswer: 9!", "correct": false, "gold": "362880"}
{"qid": "mp:8402", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "In circular arrangements, rotations of the same seating order are considered identical. Since we have 10 distinct people and 1 identical round table, the number of ways to seat them is $(10 - 1)! = 9!$. \r\n\r\nAnswer: 362880", "correct": true, "gold": "362880"}
