{"qid": "mp:7839", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Rewrite the equation in standard circle form.\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n2x^2=-2y^2+12x-4y+20\n\\]\n\nMove everything to one side:\n\\[\n2x^2+2y^2-12x+4y-20=0\n\\]\n\nDivide by 2:\n\\[\nx^2+y^2-6x+2y-10=0\n\\]\n\nComplete the square:\n\\[\n(x^2-6x)+(y^2+2y)=10\n\\]\n\\[\n(x-3)^2-9+(y+1)^2-1=10\n\\]\n\\[\n(x-3)^2+(y+1)^2=20\n\\]\n\nSo the circle has radius\n\\[\nr=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt5\n\\]\n\nA circle inscribed in a square has the square\u2019s side length equal to the circle\u2019s diameter:\n\\[\ns=2r=4\\sqrt5\n\\]\n\nArea of the square:\n\\[\ns^2=(4\\sqrt5)^2=16\\cdot 5=80\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 80", "correct": true, "gold": "80"}
{"qid": "mp:8402", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "To seat $n$ distinct people at a single round table (where rotations are considered the same arrangement), the number of distinct arrangements is $(n-1)!$.\n\nAssuming \"1 identical round tables\" refers to **one** round table (with \"identical\" indicating that rotational symmetry applies, and \"tables\" being a typo for \"table\"), we calculate:\n\n$$(10-1)! = 9! = 362{,}880$$\n\nThe condition that each table must have at least one person is automatically satisfied since there is only one table and 10 people to seat.\n\n*(Note: If the question intended to ask about seating 10 people at any number of identical round tables\u2014i.e., partitioning them into any number of non-empty groups\u2014the answer would be $10! = 3{,}628{,}800$. However, given the explicit \"1\", the answer for a single table is appropriate.)*\n\nAnswer: 362880 (or $9!$)", "correct": true, "gold": "362880"}
{"qid": "mp:7839", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We start with the equation:\n\n\\[\n2x^2 = -2y^2 + 12x - 4y + 20\n\\]\n\nBring all terms to one side:\n\n\\[\n2x^2 + 2y^2 - 12x + 4y - 20 = 0\n\\]\n\nDivide through by 2:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 - 6x + 2y - 10 = 0\n\\]\n\nComplete the square for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 6x = (x-3)^2 - 9\n\\]\n\nComplete the square for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\ny^2 + 2y = (y+1)^2 - 1\n\\]\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n\\[\n(x-3)^2 - 9 + (y+1)^2 - 1 - 10 = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n(x-3)^2 + (y+1)^2 - 20 = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n(x-3)^2 + (y+1)^2 = 20\n\\]\n\nSo the circle has center \\((3, -1)\\) and radius \\(r = \\sqrt{20} = 2\\sqrt{5}\\).\n\nThe square has sides parallel to the axes, so its side length equals the diameter of the circle:\n\n\\[\n\\text{side} = 2r = 4\\sqrt{5}\n\\]\n\nArea of the square:\n\n\\[\n(4\\sqrt{5})^2 = 16 \\cdot 5 = 80\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 80", "correct": true, "gold": "80"}
{"qid": "mp:7839", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The given equation can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\n2x^2+2y^2-12x+4y-20=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x^2+y^2-6x+2y-10=0.\n\\]\n\nCompleting the square:\n\n\\[\n(x^2-6x+9)+(y^2+2y+1)=10+9+1=20,\n\\]\n\nso  \n\n\\[\n(x-3)^2+(y+1)^2=20.\n\\]\n\nThus the circle has center \\((3,-1)\\) and radius \\(r=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt5\\).\n\nSince the square has a pair of sides parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, it is axis\u2011aligned.  \nFor a circle inscribed in such a square, the side length of the square equals the diameter \\(2r\\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{side}=2r=4\\sqrt5.\n\\]\n\nHence the area of the square is  \n\n\\[\n(4\\sqrt5)^2=16\\cdot5=80.\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 80", "correct": true, "gold": "80"}
{"qid": "mp:8939", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The conditional expectation can be computed using the branching property. Given \\(Z_n=k\\),\n\n\\[\nZ_{n+1}= \\sum_{i=1}^{k} X_i,\n\\]\n\nwhere the \\(X_i\\) are i.i.d. copies of \\(Z_1\\). Then\n\n\\[\n\\mathbb{E}\\big[ [H_{n+1}(s)]^{Z_{n+1}} \\mid Z_n=k \\big]\n= \\mathbb{E}\\bigg[\\prod_{i=1}^{k} [H_{n+1}(s)]^{X_i}\\bigg]\n= \\big(\\mathbb{E}[ [H_{n+1}(s)]^{X_1}]\\big)^{k}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(G_1(t)=\\mathbb{E}[t^{X_1}]\\) is the pgf of \\(Z_1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\mathbb{E}[ [H_{n+1}(s)]^{X_1}]\n= G_1\\!\\big(H_{n+1}(s)\\big)\n= H_n(s),\n\\]\n\nusing the relation \\(G_1(H_{n+1}(s))=H_n(s)\\) that follows from \\(G_{n+1}=G_n\\circ G_1\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\mathbb{E}\\big[ [H_{n+1}(s)]^{Z_{n+1}} \\mid Z_n\\big]\n= \\big(H_n(s)\\big)^{Z_n}\n= [H_n(s)]^{Z_n}=M_n(s).\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\{M_n(s)\\}_{n\\ge0}\\) satisfies \\(\\mathbb{E}[M_{n+1}\\mid Z_n]=M_n\\) for every fixed \\(s\\in[0,1]\\), making it a martingale (bounded by 1, hence integrable).  \n\nAnswer: 1<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "1.0"}
