{"qid": "mp:8511", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The integral to be evaluated is\n\\[\nI = \\int_2^4 \\frac{\\sqrt{\\log(9-x)}}{\\sqrt{\\log(9-x)}+\\sqrt{\\log(x+3)}}\\,dx.\n\\]\nApply the substitution \\(x=6-t\\). Then \\(dx=-dt\\), and the limits change from \\(x=2\\) to \\(x=4\\) into \\(t=4\\) to \\(t=2\\). This produces the equivalent representation\n\\[\nI = \\int_2^4 \\frac{\\sqrt{\\log(x+3)}}{\\sqrt{\\log(x+3)}+\\sqrt{\\log(9-x)}}\\,dx.\n\\]\nAdding the two expressions for \\(I\\) yields\n\\[\n2I = \\int_2^4 1\\,dx = 2,\n\\]\nso \\(I=1\\).\n\n(The integrand is well-defined on \\([2,4]\\) because both \\(9-x\\) and \\(x+3\\) lie in \\([5,7]\\), hence both logarithms are positive.)\n\nAnswer: 1", "correct": true, "gold": "1.0"}
{"qid": "mp:8511", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We are asked to evaluate\n\n\\[\nI = \\int_2^4 \\frac{\\sqrt{\\log(9-x)}}{\\sqrt{\\log(9-x)}+\\sqrt{\\log(x+3)}} \\, dx.\n\\]\n\nA common trick for integrals of this symmetric form is to use the substitution \\( x \\mapsto a+b-x \\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are the limits. Here \\(a=2\\), \\(b=4\\), so let \\( x = 6 - t \\). Then when \\(x=2\\), \\(t=4\\); when \\(x=4\\), \\(t=2\\), and \\(dx = -dt\\). So\n\n\\[\nI = \\int_{4}^{2} \\frac{\\sqrt{\\log(9-(6-t))}}{\\sqrt{\\log(9-(6-t))}+\\sqrt{\\log((6-t)+3)}} (-dt).\n\\]\n\nSimplify inside:\n\n- \\(9 - (6-t) = 3 + t\\)\n- \\((6-t)+3 = 9 - t\\)\n\nThus\n\n\\[\nI = \\int_{2}^{4} \\frac{\\sqrt{\\log(3+t)}}{\\sqrt{\\log(3+t)}+\\sqrt{\\log(9-t)}} \\, dt.\n\\]\n\nBut note that the integrand is now the original integrand with numerator and denominator swapped in a sense: originally numerator had \\(\\sqrt{\\log(9-x)}\\), now it has \\(\\sqrt{\\log(3+t)}\\). Since the variable is dummy, rename \\(t\\) to \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nI = \\int_{2}^{4} \\frac{\\sqrt{\\log(x+3)}}{\\sqrt{\\log(x+3)}+\\sqrt{\\log(9-x)}} \\, dx.\n\\]\n\nNow add the original expression for \\(I\\):\n\n\\[\nI + I = \\int_{2}^{4} \\frac{\\sqrt{\\log(9-x)} + \\sqrt{\\log(x+3)}}{\\sqrt{\\log(9-x)}+\\sqrt{\\log(x+3)}} \\, dx = \\int_{2}^{4} 1 \\, dx = (4-2) = 2.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(2I = 2\\), so \\(I = 1\\).\n\nAnswer: 1", "correct": true, "gold": "1.0"}
