{"qid": "mp:8604", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The upward (vertical) component of the satellite\u2019s velocity is given by the derivative of \\(y(t)\\):\n\n\\[\ny(t)=4\\sin t+\\sin(12t)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \n\\frac{dy}{dt}=4\\cos t+12\\cos(12t).\n\\]\n\nEvaluating at \\(t=1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\left.\\frac{dy}{dt}\\right|_{t=1}=4\\cos(1)+12\\cos(12).\n\\]\n\nNumerically,\n\\[\n\\cos(1)\\approx0.540302,\\qquad \\cos(12)\\approx0.843854,\n\\]\nso\n\n\\[\n4\\cos(1)+12\\cos(12)\\approx4(0.540302)+12(0.843854)\\approx2.161+\\;10.126\\approx12.29.\n\\]\n\nAnswer: \\(4\\cos(1)+12\\cos(12)\\approx12.29\\).<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "12.287"}
{"qid": "mp:8511", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Let  \n\\[\nI=\\int_2^4 \\frac{\\sqrt{\\log(9-x)}}{\\sqrt{\\log(9-x)}+\\sqrt{\\log(x+3)}}\\,dx.\n\\]\n\nUse the symmetry substitution\n\\[\nx \\mapsto 6-x.\n\\]\nThen as \\(x\\) goes from \\(2\\) to \\(4\\), \\(6-x\\) also goes from \\(4\\) to \\(2\\), so over the same interval we get\n\\[\nI=\\int_2^4 \\frac{\\sqrt{\\log(x+3)}}{\\sqrt{\\log(x+3)}+\\sqrt{\\log(9-x)}}\\,dx.\n\\]\n\nNow add the two expressions for \\(I\\):\n\\[\n2I=\\int_2^4 \\left(\n\\frac{\\sqrt{\\log(9-x)}}{\\sqrt{\\log(9-x)}+\\sqrt{\\log(x+3)}}\n+\n\\frac{\\sqrt{\\log(x+3)}}{\\sqrt{\\log(x+3)}+\\sqrt{\\log(9-x)}}\n\\right)dx.\n\\]\nThe terms inside sum to \\(1\\), so\n\\[\n2I=\\int_2^4 1\\,dx=4-2=2.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nI=1.\n\\]\n\nAnswer: \\(1\\)", "correct": true, "gold": "1.0"}
{"qid": "mp:8511", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Let  \n\n\\[\nI=\\int_{2}^{4}\\frac{\\sqrt{\\log(9-x)}}{\\sqrt{\\log(9-x)}+\\sqrt{\\log(x+3)}}\\,dx .\n\\]\n\nUse the substitution \\(x\\mapsto 6-x\\) (since the interval \\([2,4]\\) is symmetric about \\(3\\) and \\(2+4=6\\)).  \nThen\n\n\\[\nI=\\int_{2}^{4}\\frac{\\sqrt{\\log(9-(6-x))}}{\\sqrt{\\log(9-(6-x))}+\\sqrt{\\log((6-x)+3)}}\\,dx\n   =\\int_{2}^{4}\\frac{\\sqrt{\\log(x+3)}}{\\sqrt{\\log(x+3)}+\\sqrt{\\log(9-x)}}\\,dx .\n\\]\n\nAdding the two expressions for \\(I\\) gives\n\n\\[\n2I=\\int_{2}^{4}\\left[\\frac{\\sqrt{\\log(9-x)}}{\\sqrt{\\log(9-x)}+\\sqrt{\\log(x+3)}}\n      +\\frac{\\sqrt{\\log(x+3)}}{\\sqrt{\\log(x+3)}+\\sqrt{\\log(9-x)}}\\right]dx\n    =\\int_{2}^{4}1\\,dx = 2 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(I=1\\).\n\nAnswer: 1", "correct": true, "gold": "1.0"}
{"qid": "mp:8853", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Using the identity x - 9 = (\u221ax - 3)(\u221ax + 3):\n\nlim_{x\u21929} (x-9)/(\u221ax-3) = lim_{x\u21929} (\u221ax + 3) = \u221a9 + 3 = 6\n\nAnswer: 6", "correct": true, "gold": "6"}
{"qid": "mp:8853", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "As \\(x \\to 9\\), the expression\n\\[\n\\frac{x-9}{\\sqrt{x}-3}\n\\]\nis indeterminate in the form \\(0/0\\). Factor the numerator:\n\\[\nx-9 = (\\sqrt{x}-3)(\\sqrt{x}+3).\n\\]\nSo for \\(x \\ne 9\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{x-9}{\\sqrt{x}-3} = \\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-3)(\\sqrt{x}+3)}{\\sqrt{x}-3} = \\sqrt{x}+3.\n\\]\nNow take the limit:\n\\[\n\\lim_{x\\to 9} (\\sqrt{x}+3) = 3+3 = 6.\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 6", "correct": true, "gold": "6"}
{"qid": "mp:8853", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We want to find  \n\\[\n\\lim_{x \\to 9} \\frac{x - 9}{\\sqrt{x} - 3}.\n\\]\n\nNotice that direct substitution gives \\(0/0\\), so we can simplify.  \nFactor \\(x - 9\\) as \\((\\sqrt{x} - 3)(\\sqrt{x} + 3)\\). Then:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x - 9}{\\sqrt{x} - 3} = \\frac{(\\sqrt{x} - 3)(\\sqrt{x} + 3)}{\\sqrt{x} - 3} = \\sqrt{x} + 3,\n\\]\nfor \\(x \\neq 9\\).\n\nNow take the limit as \\(x \\to 9\\):\n\n\\[\n\\lim_{x \\to 9} (\\sqrt{x} + 3) = \\sqrt{9} + 3 = 3 + 3 = 6.\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 6", "correct": true, "gold": "6"}
{"qid": "mp:8853", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at $\\lim_{x \\to 9} \\frac{x - 9}{\\sqrt{x} - 3}$, direct substitution gives $\\frac{0}{0}$ (indeterminate).\n\n**Method 1: Factoring**\nNotice that $x - 9 = (\\sqrt{x})^2 - 3^2 = (\\sqrt{x} - 3)(\\sqrt{x} + 3)$.\n\nFor $x \\neq 9$:\n$$\\frac{x - 9}{\\sqrt{x} - 3} = \\frac{(\\sqrt{x} - 3)(\\sqrt{x} + 3)}{\\sqrt{x} - 3} = \\sqrt{x} + 3$$\n\nTherefore:\n$$\\lim_{x \\to 9} \\frac{x - 9}{\\sqrt{x} - 3} = \\lim_{x \\to 9} (\\sqrt{x} + 3) = \\sqrt{9} + 3 = 3 + 3 = 6$$\n\n**Method 2: L'H\u00f4pital's Rule**\nSince we have $\\frac{0}{0}$:\n$$\\lim_{x \\to 9} \\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}} = \\lim_{x \\to 9} 2\\sqrt{x} = 2 \\cdot 3 = 6$$\n\nAnswer: 6", "correct": true, "gold": "6"}
{"qid": "mp:8853", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The given limit evaluates to the indeterminate form \\(0/0\\) upon direct substitution of \\(x = 9\\). To resolve it, multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\\[\n\\frac{x-9}{\\sqrt{x}-3} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x}+3}{\\sqrt{x}+3} = \\frac{(x-9)(\\sqrt{x}+3)}{x-9}.\n\\]\nFor all \\(x \\neq 9\\), this simplifies exactly to \\(\\sqrt{x} + 3\\). The resulting expression is continuous at \\(x = 9\\), so the original limit equals \\(\\sqrt{9} + 3 = 6\\). (The original function is defined on both sides of \\(x = 9\\) in a deleted neighborhood, so the two-sided limit exists.)\nAnswer: 6", "correct": true, "gold": "6"}
{"qid": "mp:8853", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>To evaluate the limit \\(\\lim_{x \\to 9} \\frac{x - 9}{\\sqrt{x} - 3}\\), note that both numerator and denominator approach zero, giving an indeterminate form \\(0/0\\).\n\nRationalize the denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x - 9}{\\sqrt{x} - 3} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x} + 3}{\\sqrt{x} + 3}\n= \\frac{(x - 9)(\\sqrt{x} + 3)}{(\\sqrt{x})^2 - 3^2}\n= \\frac{(x - 9)(\\sqrt{x} + 3)}{x - 9}\n= \\sqrt{x} + 3 \\quad (x \\neq 9)\n\\]\n\nNow take the limit as \\(x\\) approaches 9:\n\n\\[\n\\lim_{x \\to 9} (\\sqrt{x} + 3) = \\sqrt{9} + 3 = 3 + 3 = 6\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 6<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "6"}
{"qid": "mp:9001", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Answer: 192 miles**\n\nHere's the reasoning:\n\n1. **Find humpback whale rate:** 2,240 miles \u00f7 28 days = 80 miles/day\n\n2. **Calculate humpback distance for 32 days:** 80 miles/day \u00d7 32 days = 2,560 miles\n\n3. **Compare to gray whale distance:** 2,560 miles \u2212 2,368 miles = 192 miles\n\nAnswer: 192", "correct": true, "gold": "192"}
{"qid": "mp:8604", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Answer: $\\boxed{4\\cos(1) + 12\\cos(12)}$", "correct": false, "gold": "12.287"}
{"qid": "mp:9001", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Humpback whales\u2019 rate:\n2,240 \u00f7 28 = 80 miles per day\n\nAt that rate for 32 days:\n80 \u00d7 32 = 2,560 miles\n\nGray whales traveled:\n2,368 miles\n\nDifference:\n2,560 \u2212 2,368 = 192\n\nAnswer: 192 miles", "correct": true, "gold": "192"}
{"qid": "mp:8511", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: 1<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "1.0"}
{"qid": "mp:8853", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "We have the limit\n$$\nL = \\lim_{x \\to 9} \\frac{x - 9}{\\sqrt{x} - 3}.\n$$\nDirect substitution gives $\\frac{0}{0}$, which is indeterminate. We can simplify by factoring the numerator:\n$$\nx - 9 = (\\sqrt{x} - 3)(\\sqrt{x} + 3).\n$$\nFor $x \\neq 9$, this gives\n$$\n\\frac{x - 9}{\\sqrt{x} - 3} = \\sqrt{x} + 3.\n$$\nTaking the limit as $x \\to 9$:\n$$\nL = \\sqrt{9} + 3 = 3 + 3 = 6.\n$$\n\nAnswer: 6", "correct": true, "gold": "6"}
{"qid": "mp:9001", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "Humpback whales' daily rate: \\(2240 \\div 28 = 80\\) miles/day.\nDistance in 32 days: \\(80 \\times 32 = 2560\\) miles.\nGray whales traveled 2368 miles in the same period.\nDifference: \\(2560 - 2368 = 192\\) miles.\nAnswer: 192 miles", "correct": true, "gold": "192"}
{"qid": "mp:9001", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The humpback whales traveled 2,240 miles in 28 days, which is a rate of  \n\n\\( \\frac{2240\\text{ miles}}{28\\text{ days}} = 80\\text{ miles per day} \\).\n\nIf they maintained this rate for 32 days, they would cover  \n\n\\( 80\\text{ miles/day} \\times 32\\text{ days} = 2560\\text{ miles} \\).\n\nThe gray whales actually traveled 2,368 miles in 32 days.  \n\nThe difference between the hypothetical humpback distance and the gray whale distance is  \n\n\\( 2560\\text{ miles} - 2368\\text{ miles} = 192\\text{ miles} \\).\n\nAnswer: 192 miles<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "192"}
{"qid": "mp:9001", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We first find the daily rate for the humpback whales:  \n2,240 miles \u00f7 28 days = 80 miles per day.\n\nAt that rate for 32 days:  \n80 miles/day \u00d7 32 days = 2,560 miles.\n\nThe gray whales traveled 2,368 miles in 32 days.  \nDifference: 2,560 \u2212 2,368 = 192 miles.\n\nAnswer: 192", "correct": true, "gold": "192"}
{"qid": "mp:9001", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "First, find the rate of the humpback whales:  \r\n\r\n`2,240 miles \u00f7 28 days = 80 miles per day`\r\n\r\nNext, calculate how far they would travel in 32 days at that rate:  \r\n\r\n`80 miles/day \u00d7 32 days = 2,560 miles`\r\n\r\nThe gray whales traveled 2,368 miles in 32 days. Now find the difference:  \r\n\r\n`2,560 miles - 2,368 miles = 192 miles`\r\n\r\nAnswer: 192", "correct": true, "gold": "192"}
{"qid": "mp:7692", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Use Sylow\u2019s theorems.\n\n**Statement 1:** Every group of order \\(159\\) is cyclic.\n\nFactor:\n\\[\n159=3\\cdot 53\n\\]\nLet \\(G\\) be a group of order \\(159\\).\n\n- Number of Sylow \\(53\\)-subgroups, \\(n_{53}\\), satisfies\n  \\[\n  n_{53}\\equiv 1 \\pmod{53}, \\qquad n_{53}\\mid 3\n  \\]\n  The divisors of \\(3\\) are \\(1,3\\), and only \\(1\\equiv 1\\pmod{53}\\). So \\(n_{53}=1\\). Hence the Sylow \\(53\\)-subgroup is normal.\n\n- Number of Sylow \\(3\\)-subgroups, \\(n_3\\), satisfies\n  \\[\n  n_3\\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}, \\qquad n_3\\mid 53\n  \\]\n  The divisors of \\(53\\) are \\(1,53\\), and both satisfy the congruence condition? Check:\n  \\[\n  53\\equiv 2 \\pmod 3\n  \\]\n  so \\(53\\not\\equiv 1\\pmod 3\\). Thus \\(n_3=1\\). So the Sylow \\(3\\)-subgroup is also normal.\n\nTherefore \\(G\\) is the direct product of its normal Sylow subgroups:\n\\[\nG\\cong P_{53}\\times P_3\n\\]\nBoth are cyclic, of orders \\(53\\) and \\(3\\). Since \\(\\gcd(53,3)=1\\), their direct product is cyclic of order \\(159\\).\n\nSo **Statement 1 is true**.\n\n---\n\n**Statement 2:** Every group of order \\(102\\) has a nontrivial proper normal subgroup.\n\nFactor:\n\\[\n102=2\\cdot 3\\cdot 17\n\\]\nLet \\(G\\) be a group of order \\(102\\).\n\nConsider the Sylow \\(17\\)-subgroups. Their number \\(n_{17}\\) satisfies\n\\[\nn_{17}\\equiv 1 \\pmod{17}, \\qquad n_{17}\\mid 6\n\\]\nThe divisors of \\(6\\) are \\(1,2,3,6\\). Only \\(1\\) is congruent to \\(1\\pmod{17}\\). Hence\n\\[\nn_{17}=1\n\\]\nSo the Sylow \\(17\\)-subgroup is unique, hence normal. It is nontrivial and proper.\n\nSo **Statement 2 is true**.\n\nAnswer: Both Statement 1 and Statement 2 are true.", "correct": false, "gold": "True, True"}
{"qid": "mp:7692", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We need to check each statement.\n\n**Statement 1:** Order 159 = 3 \u00d7 53.  \nBoth 3 and 53 are prime, and 53 \u2261 2 mod 3, so 3 does not divide 53\u22121 = 52.  \nBy the classification of groups of order \\(pq\\) with primes \\(p<q\\) and \\(p \\nmid q-1\\), the group is cyclic. So Statement 1 is true.\n\n**Statement 2:** Order 102 = 2 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 17.  \nBy Sylow theorems:  \n- Number of Sylow 17-subgroups \\(n_{17}\\) divides 6 and \u2261 1 mod 17 \u2192 \\(n_{17}=1\\).  \nThus the Sylow 17-subgroup is normal. That is a nontrivial proper normal subgroup. So Statement 2 is true.\n\nBoth statements are true.\n\nAnswer: True, True", "correct": true, "gold": "True, True"}
{"qid": "mp:7692", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "Both statements concern finite groups whose orders factor into a small number of distinct primes, so Sylow theory supplies the necessary counting arguments.\n\nFor Statement 1, let \\(G\\) be a group of order \\(159=3\\cdot53\\). The number \\(n_{53}\\) of Sylow \\(53\\)-subgroups satisfies \\(n_{53}\\equiv1\\pmod{53}\\) and \\(n_{53}|3\\). The only possibility is \\(n_{53}=1\\), so the (unique) Sylow \\(53\\)-subgroup \\(P\\) is normal. Likewise \\(n_3\\equiv1\\pmod{3}\\) and \\(n_3|53\\). Because \\(53\\) is prime the candidates are \\(1\\) and \\(53\\); the latter is incongruent to \\(1\\) modulo \\(3\\), hence \\(n_3=1\\) as well and the Sylow \\(3\\)-subgroup \\(Q\\) is also normal. Consequently \\(G\\) is the semidirect product \\(P\\rtimes Q\\). The automorphism group \\(\\operatorname{Aut}(P)\\cong C_{52}\\) has order \\(52=2^2\\cdot13\\), which is not divisible by \\(3\\). The only homomorphism \\(Q\\to\\operatorname{Aut}(P)\\) is therefore the trivial one, the semidirect product is direct, and\n\\[\nG\\cong C_{53}\\times C_3\\cong C_{159}.\n\\]\nThus every group of order \\(159\\) is cyclic.\n\nFor Statement 2, let \\(G\\) be a group of order \\(102=2\\cdot3\\cdot17\\). The number \\(n_{17}\\) of Sylow \\(17\\)-subgroups satisfies \\(n_{17}\\equiv1\\pmod{17}\\) and \\(n_{17}|6\\). The only integer satisfying both conditions is \\(n_{17}=1\\). Hence the (unique) Sylow \\(17\\)-subgroup is normal in \\(G\\). Its order is \\(17\\), which is strictly between \\(1\\) and \\(102\\), so it is a nontrivial proper normal subgroup. Consequently every group of order \\(102\\) possesses such a subgroup.\n\nBoth statements are therefore true.\n\nAnswer: both true", "correct": false, "gold": "True, True"}
{"qid": "mp:7692", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Looking at this problem, I need to verify both statements using Sylow's theorems.\n\n## Statement 1: Every group of order 159 is cyclic\n\nFactor 159 = 3 \u00d7 53.\n\n**Sylow 53-subgroups:** Let $n_{53}$ = number of Sylow 53-subgroups.\n- $n_{53} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{53}$ and $n_{53} \\mid 3$\n- Divisors of 3 are {1, 3}. Only 1 satisfies $n_{53} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{53}$\n- So $n_{53} = 1$: the Sylow 53-subgroup is **normal**\n\n**Sylow 3-subgroups:** Let $n_3$ = number of Sylow 3-subgroups.\n- $n_3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}$ and $n_3 \\mid 53$\n- Divisors of 53 are {1, 53}. Since $53 = 51+2 \\equiv 2 \\pmod 3$, only 1 satisfies the condition\n- So $n_3 = 1$: the Sylow 3-subgroup is **normal**\n\nSince both Sylow subgroups are normal, of coprime order, and their product has order 159 = |G|, we get:\n$$G \\cong \\mathbb{Z}_3 \\times \\mathbb{Z}_{53} \\cong \\mathbb{Z}_{159}$$\n(using CRT since gcd(3,53)=1). So **every group of order 159 is cyclic** \u2014 Statement 1 is **True**.\n\n## Statement 2: Every group of order 102 has a nontrivial proper normal subgroup\n\nFactor 102 = 2 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 17.\n\n**Sylow 17-subgroups:** Let $n_{17}$ = number of Sylow 17-subgroups.\n- $n_{17} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{17}$ and $n_{17} \\mid 6$\n- Divisors of 6 are {1, 2, 3, 6}. Checking each: only 1 satisfies $n_{17} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{17}$\n- So $n_{17} = 1$: the Sylow 17-subgroup is **normal**\n\nThis Sylow 17-subgroup has order 17, which is nontrivial (\u2260 {e}) and proper (\u2260 G, since |G|=102). \n\nThus **every group of order 102 has a nontrivial proper normal subgroup** \u2014 Statement 2 is **True**.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nBoth statements are true.\n\nAnswer: True, True", "correct": true, "gold": "True, True"}
{"qid": "mp:7692", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "**Reasoning:**\n\n- **Order 159:** \\(159 = 3 \\cdot 53\\). By Sylow\u2019s theorems, the number of Sylow\u201153 subgroups \\(n_{53}\\) divides 3 and is \\(\\equiv 1 \\pmod{53}\\), forcing \\(n_{53}=1\\). Similarly, \\(n_3\\) divides 53 and is \\(\\equiv 1 \\pmod 3\\), so \\(n_3=1\\). Hence the group is a direct product of its unique Sylow subgroups, \\(C_{53}\\times C_3\\cong C_{159}\\). Thus every group of order 159 is cyclic.\n\n- **Order 102:** \\(102 = 2\\cdot 3\\cdot 17\\). The number of Sylow\u201117 subgroups \\(n_{17}\\) divides \\(102/17=6\\) and satisfies \\(n_{17}\\equiv 1\\pmod{17}\\). The only divisor of 6 congruent to 1 mod 17 is 1, so the Sylow\u201117 subgroup is unique and therefore normal. It has order 17, which is nontrivial and proper. Hence every group of order 102 possesses a nontrivial proper normal subgroup.\n\n**Answer:** Both statements are true.", "correct": false, "gold": "True, True"}
{"qid": "mp:7692", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>For Statement\u202f1:  \nThe order 159 factors as \\(3 \\times 53\\). Since the smaller prime 3 does **not** divide \\(53-1 = 52\\), a classic result on groups of order \\(pq\\) (with \\(p<q\\) primes) tells us that any group of order 159 must be cyclic. Hence Statement\u202f1 is true.\n\nFor Statement\u202f2:  \nThe order 102 factors as \\(2 \\times 3 \\times 17\\). By Sylow\u2019s theorems, the number \\(n_{17}\\) of Sylow\u201117 subgroups satisfies \\(n_{17} \\mid 6\\) and \\(n_{17} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{17}\\). The only divisor of 6 congruent to 1 modulo 17 is 1, so the Sylow\u201117 subgroup is unique and therefore normal. This subgroup has order 17, which is nontrivial and proper in a group of order 102. Consequently every group of order 102 possesses a nontrivial proper normal subgroup, making Statement\u202f2 true.\n\nAnswer: Both statements are true.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "True, True"}
{"qid": "mp:8957", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Solve the ODE first:\n\\[\n2r^2+3r-2=0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad (2r-1)(r+2)=0\n\\]\nso the roots are\n\\[\nr=\\frac12,\\,-2.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\ny(x)=C_1e^{x/2}+C_2e^{-2x}.\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(y(0)=1\\):\n\\[\nC_1+C_2=1.\n\\]\n\nAlso,\n\\[\ny'(x)=\\frac12 C_1e^{x/2}-2C_2e^{-2x},\n\\]\nand \\(y'(0)=-\\beta\\) gives\n\\[\n\\frac12 C_1-2C_2=-\\beta.\n\\]\n\nSolve the system:\n\\[\nC_2=\\frac{1+2\\beta}{5}, \\qquad C_1=\\frac{4-2\\beta}{5}.\n\\]\n\nSo\n\\[\ny'(x)=\\frac{2-\\beta}{5}e^{x/2}-\\frac{2(1+2\\beta)}{5}e^{-2x}.\n\\]\n\nA minimum point occurs where \\(y'(x)=0\\) and the derivative changes from negative to positive. Since\n\\[\ny''(x)=\\frac{2-\\beta}{10}e^{x/2}+\\frac{4(1+2\\beta)}{5}e^{-2x},\n\\]\nat any critical point \\(y'(x)=0\\), the second term dominates so such a critical point is indeed a minimum.\n\nThus, to have **no minimum point**, we need \\(y'(x)\\neq 0\\) for all \\(x\\ge 0\\). Since \\(e^{-2x}>0\\), set \\(y'(x)=0\\):\n\\[\n(2-\\beta)e^{x/2}=2(1+2\\beta)e^{-2x}\n\\]\n\\[\ne^{5x/2}=\\frac{2(1+2\\beta)}{2-\\beta}.\n\\]\n\nA real \\(x\\ge 0\\) exists iff the right-hand side is \\(\\ge 1\\), and also \\(2-\\beta>0\\). So for no minimum, we need either:\n\n- \\(\\beta\\ge 2\\), in which case \\(2-\\beta\\le 0\\) and \\(y'(x)<0\\) for all \\(x\\), or\n- if \\(\\beta<2\\), then require\n\\[\n\\frac{2(1+2\\beta)}{2-\\beta}<1.\n\\]\n\nSolve:\n\\[\n2+4\\beta<2-\\beta\n\\Rightarrow 5\\beta<0,\n\\]\nimpossible for \\(\\beta>0\\).\n\nSo the smallest \\(\\beta\\) for which there is no minimum point is\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}.\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 2", "correct": true, "gold": "2"}
