{"qid": "mp:8957", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "## Solution\n\n**Setting up the characteristic equation:**\n\nFor $2y'' + 3y' - 2y = 0$, the characteristic equation is:\n$$2r^2 + 3r - 2 = 0 \\implies r = \\frac{-3 \\pm 5}{4}$$\n\nThis gives roots $r_1 = \\frac{1}{2}$ and $r_2 = -2$, so the general solution is:\n$$y(t) = Ae^{t/2} + Be^{-2t}$$\n\n**Applying initial conditions:**\n\nFrom $y(0) = 1$:\n$$A + B = 1$$\n\nFrom $y'(0) = -\\beta$:\n$$\\frac{A}{2} - 2B = -\\beta$$\n\nSubstituting $B = 1 - A$ into the second equation:\n$$\\frac{A}{2} - 2(1-A) = -\\beta \\implies \\frac{5A}{2} - 2 = -\\beta \\implies A = \\frac{4 - 2\\beta}{5}$$\n\nThen:\n$$B = 1 - A = \\frac{1 + 2\\beta}{5}$$\n\nSince $\\beta > 0$, we always have $B > 0$.\n\n**Analyzing critical points:**\n\nCompute the derivative:\n$$y'(t) = \\frac{A}{2}e^{t/2} - 2Be^{-2t}$$\n\nSetting $y'(t) = 0$ (assuming $A \\neq 0$):\n$$\\frac{A}{2}e^{t/2} = 2Be^{-2t} \\implies e^{(5/2)t} = \\frac{4B}{A}$$\n\n**Case 1: $A > 0$ (i.e., $\\beta < 2$).**  \nSince $B > 0$, we get $\\frac{4B}{A} > 0$, so a real solution $t^*$ exists. Since $y(0) = 1$ and $y'(0) = -\\beta < 0$ (initially decreasing), but $y(t) \\to +\\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty$ (because $A > 0$ dominates), the solution must turn around \u2014 this critical point is a **minimum**.\n\n**Case 2: $A < 0$ (i.e., $\\beta > 2$).**  \nSince $B > 0$, we get $\\frac{4B}{A} < 0$. But $e^{(5/2)t} > 0$ always, so **no real critical point exists**. Since $y'(0) = -\\beta < 0$ and $y'(t)$ never changes sign, $y(t)$ is **monotonically decreasing** for all $t$ (with $y(t) \\to -\\infty$). There is no minimum point.\n\n**Case 3: $A = 0$ (i.e., $\\beta = 2$).**  \nThen $y(t) = Be^{-2t}$ with $B > 0$, so:\n$$y'(t) = -2Be^{-2t} < 0 \\text{ for all } t$$\n\nThe solution is monotonically decreasing to $0$, with **no minimum point** (the infimum $0$ is never attained).\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\n- For $\\beta < 2$: solution has a minimum point.\n- For $\\beta \\geq 2$: solution has **no** minimum point.\n\nThe smallest value of $\\beta$ for which the solution has no minimum point is:\n\n**Answer: \u03b2 = 2**", "correct": true, "gold": "2"}
{"qid": "mp:8957", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We have the differential equation:\n\n\\[\n2 y'' + 3 y' - 2 y = 0\n\\]\n\nThe characteristic equation is:\n\n\\[\n2r^2 + 3r - 2 = 0\n\\]\n\nSolve:\n\n\\[\nr = \\frac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 + 16}}{4} = \\frac{-3 \\pm 5}{4}\n\\]\n\nSo:\n\n\\[\nr_1 = \\frac{2}{4} = \\frac12, \\quad r_2 = \\frac{-8}{4} = -2\n\\]\n\nGeneral solution:\n\n\\[\ny(t) = C_1 e^{t/2} + C_2 e^{-2t}\n\\]\n\nApply initial conditions:\n\n\\( y(0) = C_1 + C_2 = 1 \\)  \n\\( y'(t) = \\frac12 C_1 e^{t/2} - 2 C_2 e^{-2t} \\)  \n\\( y'(0) = \\frac12 C_1 - 2 C_2 = -\\beta \\)\n\nFrom \\( C_1 + C_2 = 1 \\), we have \\( C_2 = 1 - C_1 \\).  \nPlug into derivative condition:\n\n\\[\n\\frac12 C_1 - 2(1 - C_1) = -\\beta\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac12 C_1 - 2 + 2C_1 = -\\beta\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac52 C_1 - 2 = -\\beta\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac52 C_1 = 2 - \\beta\n\\]\n\\[\nC_1 = \\frac{2(2 - \\beta)}{5}\n\\]\nThen:\n\\[\nC_2 = 1 - \\frac{2(2 - \\beta)}{5} = \\frac{5 - 4 + 2\\beta}{5} = \\frac{1 + 2\\beta}{5}\n\\]\n\nSo the solution is:\n\n\\[\ny(t) = \\frac{2(2 - \\beta)}{5} e^{t/2} + \\frac{1 + 2\\beta}{5} e^{-2t}\n\\]\n\nA minimum point occurs if \\( y'(t) = 0 \\) for some \\( t \\) and the second derivative is positive.  \nDerivative:\n\n\\[\ny'(t) = \\frac{2(2 - \\beta)}{5} \\cdot \\frac12 e^{t/2} + \\frac{1 + 2\\beta}{5} \\cdot (-2) e^{-2t}\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\frac{2 - \\beta}{5} e^{t/2} - \\frac{2(1 + 2\\beta)}{5} e^{-2t}\n\\]\n\nSet \\( y'(t) = 0 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2 - \\beta}{5} e^{t/2} = \\frac{2(1 + 2\\beta)}{5} e^{-2t}\n\\]\n\\[\n(2 - \\beta) e^{t/2} = 2(1 + 2\\beta) e^{-2t}\n\\]\n\\[\ne^{(t/2 + 2t)} = e^{(5t/2)} = \\frac{2(1 + 2\\beta)}{2 - \\beta}\n\\]\n\nSo:\n\n\\[\ne^{5t/2} = \\frac{2(1 + 2\\beta)}{2 - \\beta}\n\\]\n\nFor a real \\( t \\) to exist, the right-hand side must be positive.  \nSince \\(\\beta > 0\\), \\(1 + 2\\beta > 0\\), so positivity requires \\(2 - \\beta > 0\\), i.e., \\(\\beta < 2\\).\n\nIf \\(\\beta = 2\\), the right-hand side is undefined (division by zero), and the equation becomes \\(0 \\cdot e^{t/2} = 2(1+4)e^{-2t} = 10 e^{-2t} > 0\\), impossible. So no critical point.\n\nIf \\(\\beta > 2\\), then \\(2 - \\beta < 0\\), making RHS negative, so no solution for \\(t\\).\n\nThus, for \\(\\beta \\ge 2\\), there is no critical point, hence no minimum point. The smallest such \\(\\beta\\) is:\n\nAnswer: 2", "correct": true, "gold": "2"}
{"qid": "mp:8433", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "For \\(X \\sim \\text{Unif}(0,1)\\),\n\n- \\(\\mu = E[X] = \\tfrac12\\)\n- \\(\\sigma^2 = \\operatorname{Var}(X) = \\tfrac{1}{12}\\)\n\nFor a sample of size \\(n=12\\), the sample mean \\(\\bar X\\) has\n\n\\[\nE[\\bar X]=\\frac12,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X)=\\frac{1/12}{12}=\\frac{1}{144},\n\\]\n\nso\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{\\bar X}=\\frac{1}{12}.\n\\]\n\nBy the Central Limit Theorem,\n\n\\[\n\\bar X \\approx N\\!\\left(\\frac12,\\frac{1}{144}\\right).\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\nP\\left(\\frac12 \\le \\bar X \\le \\frac23\\right)\n= P\\left(\\frac{\\frac12-\\frac12}{1/12} \\le Z \\le \\frac{\\frac23-\\frac12}{1/12}\\right)\n= P(0 \\le Z \\le 2),\n\\]\n\nsince\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2/3-1/2}{1/12}=\\frac{1/6}{1/12}=2.\n\\]\n\nFrom the standard normal table,\n\n\\[\nP(0 \\le Z \\le 2)=\\Phi(2)-\\Phi(0)=0.97725-0.5=0.47725.\n\\]\n\nAnswer: \\( \\boxed{0.4772 \\text{ (approximately)}} \\)", "correct": true, "gold": "$0.4772$"}
{"qid": "mp:8433", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Setup:** For a Uniform(0,1) distribution, mean = 1/2 and variance = 1/12.\n\nFor the sample mean $\\bar{X}$ with $n=12$:\n- $E(\\bar{X}) = 1/2$\n- $\\text{Var}(\\bar{X}) = \\frac{1/12}{12} = \\frac{1}{144}$, so $\\text{SD}(\\bar{X}) = \\frac{1}{12}$\n\n**Apply Central Limit Theorem:** By CLT, $\\bar{X}$ is approximately normal with mean $1/2$ and standard deviation $1/12$.\n\n**Standardize the bounds:**\n\n$$Z_1 = \\frac{1/2 - 1/2}{1/12} = 0$$\n\n$$Z_2 = \\frac{2/3 - 1/2}{1/12} = \\frac{1/6}{1/12} = 2$$\n\n**Compute probability:**\n\n$$P\\left(\\frac{1}{2} \\le \\bar{X} \\le \\frac{2}{3}\\right) \\approx P(0 \\le Z \\le 2) = \\Phi(2) - \\Phi(0)$$\n\nUsing standard normal table: $\\Phi(2) = 0.9772$, $\\Phi(0) = 0.5$\n\n$$P(0 \\le Z \\le 2) = 0.9772 - 0.5 = 0.4772$$\n\nAnswer: 0.4772", "correct": true, "gold": "$0.4772$"}
