{"qid": "mp:8433", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We have a sample of size \\( n = 12 \\) from a uniform distribution on \\((0,1)\\).  \nFor a single observation, the mean is \\( \\mu = \\frac{1}{2} \\) and variance is \\( \\sigma^2 = \\frac{1}{12} \\).\n\nFor the sample mean \\(\\bar{X}\\):\n\\[\nE[\\bar{X}] = \\frac12, \\quad \\text{Var}(\\bar{X}) = \\frac{1/12}{12} = \\frac{1}{144}.\n\\]\nSo the standard deviation is \\(\\sigma_{\\bar{X}} = \\frac{1}{12}\\).\n\nWe approximate using the normal distribution (CLT):\n\\[\nP\\left( \\frac12 \\le \\bar{X} \\le \\frac23 \\right)\n= P\\left( \\frac{0}{1/12} \\le Z \\le \\frac{2/3 - 1/2}{1/12} \\right)\n= P\\left( 0 \\le Z \\le \\frac{1/6}{1/12} \\right)\n= P(0 \\le Z \\le 2).\n\\]\n\nFrom standard normal tables, \\(P(0 \\le Z \\le 2) \\approx 0.4772\\).\n\nAnswer: 0.4772", "correct": true, "gold": "$0.4772$"}
{"qid": "mp:8433", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The uniform distribution on \\((0,1)\\) has mean \\(\\mu=1/2\\) and variance \\(\\sigma^2=1/12\\). For a random sample of size \\(n=12\\), the sample mean \\(\\bar{X}\\) therefore has mean \\(1/2\\) and variance \\(1/144\\) (standard deviation \\(1/12\\)).\nBy the central limit theorem,\n\\[\n\\bar{X}\\approx N(1/2,1/12).\n\\]\nThe desired probability is standardized as follows:\n\\[\nP(1/2\\le\\bar{X}\\le2/3)=P\\Bigl(0\\le\\frac{\\bar{X}-1/2}{1/12}\\le2\\Bigr).\n\\]\nFor a standard normal random variable \\(Z\\), this equals\n\\[\n\\Phi(2)-\\Phi(0)\\approx0.9772-0.5=0.4772.\n\\]\n(The underlying distribution is continuous, so no continuity correction is required.)\nAnswer: 0.4772", "correct": true, "gold": "$0.4772$"}
