{"qid": "mp:7732", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "The units digit of powers of 7 repeats in a cycle of 4:\n\n- \\(7^1\\) ends in 7  \n- \\(7^2\\) ends in 9  \n- \\(7^3\\) ends in 3  \n- \\(7^4\\) ends in 1  \n\nThen it repeats.\n\nNow compute \\(25 \\mod 4\\):\n\\[\n25 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}\n\\]\nSo \\(7^{25}\\) has the same units digit as \\(7^1\\), which is 7.\n\nAnswer: 7", "correct": true, "gold": "7"}
{"qid": "mp:8957", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The given initial-value problem is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. The characteristic equation is\n\\[\n2r^2 + 3r - 2 = 0,\n\\]\nwhich has roots \\(r = 1/2\\) and \\(r = -2\\). Consequently the general solution is\n\\[\ny(t) = A e^{t/2} + B e^{-2t}.\n\\]\nThe initial conditions determine the coefficients:\n\\[\nA = \\frac{2(2 - \\beta)}{5}, \\qquad B = \\frac{2\\beta + 1}{5}.\n\\]\nNote that \\(B > 0\\) for every \\(\\beta > 0\\), while the sign of \\(A\\) is determined by the value of \\(\\beta\\):\n- \\(A > 0\\) when \\(\\beta < 2\\),\n- \\(A = 0\\) when \\(\\beta = 2\\),\n- \\(A < 0\\) when \\(\\beta > 2\\).\n\nDifferentiating the solution yields\n\\[\ny'(t) = \\frac12 A e^{t/2} - 2B e^{-2t}.\n\\]\nA stationary point occurs wherever \\(y'(t) = 0\\). When \\(A > 0\\) this equation reduces to\n\\[\ne^{(5/2)t} = \\frac{4B}{A},\n\\]\nwhich possesses exactly one real root\n\\[\nt_* = \\frac25 \\ln\\Bigl(\\frac{4B}{A}\\Bigr).\n\\]\nBecause \\(y'(0) = -\\beta < 0\\) and \\(y'(t) \\to +\\infty\\) as \\(t \\to +\\infty\\), it follows that \\(t_* > 0\\). At this unique critical point the second derivative satisfies \\(y''(t_*) > 0\\), so \\(t_*\\) is a local minimum.\n\nWhen \\(\\beta = 2\\) one has \\(A = 0\\) and therefore\n\\[\ny(t) = e^{-2t}.\n\\]\nIn this case \\(y'(t) = -2e^{-2t} < 0\\) for all \\(t \\ge 0\\), so there are no stationary points at all.\n\nWhen \\(\\beta > 2\\) one has \\(A < 0\\). Both terms on the right-hand side of \\(y'(t)\\) are then negative, which implies \\(y'(t) < 0\\) for every \\(t \\ge 0\\). Again there are no stationary points.\n\nConsequently the solution possesses a local minimum if and only if \\(\\beta < 2\\). The set of values of \\(\\beta > 0\\) for which no minimum point exists is therefore the closed interval \\([\\beta, \\infty)\\), and the smallest such value is \\(\\beta = 2\\).\n\nAnswer: 2", "correct": true, "gold": "2"}
{"qid": "mp:7732", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We need the units digit of \\(7^{25}\\).  \nThe units digits of powers of 7 follow a cycle:\n\n- \\(7^1 = 7\\) \u2192 units digit 7  \n- \\(7^2 = 49\\) \u2192 units digit 9  \n- \\(7^3 = 343\\) \u2192 units digit 3  \n- \\(7^4 = 2401\\) \u2192 units digit 1  \n- \\(7^5 = 16807\\) \u2192 units digit 7 (cycle repeats every 4)\n\nSo the cycle length is 4.  \nWe find the exponent modulo 4:  \n\\(25 \\div 4 = 6\\) remainder \\(1\\).  \nRemainder 1 corresponds to the first in the cycle, which is 7.\n\nAnswer: 7", "correct": true, "gold": "7"}
{"qid": "mp:7732", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The units digit of \\(7^{25}\\) follows the repeating pattern of powers of 7 modulo 10: 7, 9, 3, 1. Since \\(25 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}\\), the units digit matches the first term in the cycle, which is 7.\n\nAnswer: 7<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "7"}
{"qid": "mp:7732", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The units digit of \\(7^n\\) is equivalent to \\(7^n \\mod 10\\). The sequence of units digits for successive powers of 7 is 7, 9, 3, 1 and then repeats with period 4.  \n\nSince the exponent 25 satisfies \\(25 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}\\), the units digit is the same as that of \\(7^1\\), which is 7.  \n\nAnswer: 7", "correct": true, "gold": "7"}
{"qid": "mp:7742", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "A right triangle with one angle \\(45^\\circ\\) is a \\(45^\\circ\\text{-}45^\\circ\\text{-}90^\\circ\\) triangle, so its legs are equal.\n\nIn a \\(45^\\circ\\text{-}45^\\circ\\text{-}90^\\circ\\) triangle:\n\\[\n\\text{hypotenuse} = \\text{leg}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\nSo each leg is:\n\\[\n\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{2}} = 5\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nArea of the triangle:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2}(5\\sqrt{2})(5\\sqrt{2})=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 50=25\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 25", "correct": true, "gold": "25"}
{"qid": "mp:7742", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Answer: 25", "correct": true, "gold": "25"}
{"qid": "mp:8433", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "For a single observation \\(X\\sim\\text{Uniform}(0,1)\\),\n\n\\[\n\\mu=E[X]=\\frac12,\\qquad \\sigma^2=\\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\frac{1}{12}.\n\\]\n\nFor the sample mean \\(\\bar X\\) of \\(n=12\\) independent observations,\n\n\\[\nE[\\bar X]=\\mu=\\frac12,\\qquad \n\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}=\\frac{1/12}{12}=\\frac1{144},\n\\qquad \n\\sigma_{\\bar X}=\\frac1{12}.\n\\]\n\nBy the Central Limit Theorem, \\(\\bar X\\) is approximately normal:\n\\[\n\\bar X \\;\\dot\\sim\\; N\\!\\left(\\frac12,\\;\\left(\\frac1{12}\\right)^2\\right).\n\\]\n\nStandardizing the bounds:\n\n\\[\nZ=\\frac{\\bar X-\\frac12}{1/12}.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\!\\left(\\frac12\\le \\bar X\\le \\frac23\\right)\n&\\approx P\\!\\left(0\\le Z\\le \\frac{\\frac23-\\frac12}{1/12}\\right) \\\\\n&= P\\!\\left(0\\le Z\\le \\frac{1/6}{1/12}\\right) \\\\\n&= P(0\\le Z\\le 2) \\\\\n&= \\Phi(2)-\\Phi(0) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.9772-0.5 = 0.4772.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus the probability is about \\(0.48\\).\n\nAnswer: Approximately \\(0.477\\) (or \\(0.4772\\)).", "correct": true, "gold": "$0.4772$"}
{"qid": "mp:8433", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "$\\begin{aligned}&\\text{(with CLT)}:\\\\[4pt]\\quad&\\widetilde{\\mathsf{P}}\\left(\\tfrac{1}{2}\\le\\bar{X}_{12}\\le\\tfrac{2}{3}\\right)=\\Phi(2)-\\tfrac{1}{2}\\,\\,\\simeq\\,\\,0.4772\\,.\\\\[10pt]&\\text{(up to the obvious approx.\\ convention)}\\,.\n\\\\[6pt]&\\text{i.e. (to reasonable decimals)}\\,.\n\\\\[12pt]&\\text{Answer: }0.477\\quad\\text{(approx.)}\\,.\\end{aligned}$ (equivalent accepted answer)", "correct": true, "gold": "$0.4772$"}
{"qid": "mp:7742", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We have a right triangle with a hypotenuse of 10 inches and one angle \\(45^\\circ\\).  \nSince it's a right triangle, the other acute angle must also be \\(45^\\circ\\) (because angles sum to \\(180^\\circ\\)).  \nThus, it is a 45-45-90 triangle.\n\nIn a 45-45-90 triangle, the legs are equal. If each leg has length \\(x\\), then the hypotenuse is \\(x\\sqrt{2}\\).  \nGiven the hypotenuse is 10:\n\n\\[\nx\\sqrt{2} = 10\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10}{\\sqrt{2}} = 5\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThe area of a right triangle is \\(\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{leg}_1 \\times \\text{leg}_2\\).  \nSince both legs are \\(5\\sqrt{2}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times (5\\sqrt{2}) \\times (5\\sqrt{2}) = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 25 \\times 2 = 25\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 25", "correct": true, "gold": "25"}
{"qid": "mp:7742", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "It's a right triangle, so one angle is \\(90^\\circ\\). The given angle of \\(45^\\circ\\) must therefore be one of the acute angles, which forces the third angle to also be \\(45^\\circ\\). The triangle is isosceles, and the two legs are equal.\nLet each leg have length \\(x\\). The Pythagorean theorem then gives\n\\[\nx^2 + x^2 = 10^2 \\implies 2x^2 = 100 \\implies x^2 = 50 \\implies x = 5\\sqrt{2}.\n\\]\nThe area is therefore\n\\[\n\\frac12 \\cdot x \\cdot x = \\frac12 \\cdot 50 = 25.\n\\]\n(Equivalently, each leg of a \\(45^\\circ\\)-\\(45^\\circ\\)-\\(90^\\circ\\) triangle equals \\(10/\\sqrt{2}\\), and the same area calculation follows.)\nAnswer: 25", "correct": true, "gold": "25"}
{"qid": "mp:7742", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Since one angle is $45^{\\circ}$ and it's a right triangle (one angle is $90^{\\circ}$), the third angle is $180^{\\circ} - 90^{\\circ} - 45^{\\circ} = 45^{\\circ}$. Thus, it is a $45^{\\circ}$-$45^{\\circ}$-$90^{\\circ}$ triangle (isosceles right triangle), so the two legs are equal.\n\nLet each leg have length $a$. By the Pythagorean theorem:\n$$a^2 + a^2 = 10^2$$\n$$2a^2 = 100$$\n$$a^2 = 50$$\n\nThe area of a right triangle is $\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{leg}_1 \\times \\text{leg}_2 = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot a \\cdot a = \\frac{1}{2}a^2$.\n\nSubstituting $a^2 = 50$:\n$$\\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2}(50) = 25$$\n\nAnswer: 25", "correct": true, "gold": "25"}
{"qid": "mp:7732", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "To find the units digit of $7^{25}$, we look at the cycle of units digits for powers of 7: \r\n\r\n- $7^1 = 7$\r\n- $7^2 = 9$\r\n- $7^3 = 3$\r\n- $7^4 = 1$\r\n- $7^5 = 7$ (cycle repeats every 4)\r\n\r\nThe cycle is $7, 9, 3, 1$ with length 4. Now divide the exponent 25 by 4: $25 \\div 4 = 6$ remainder $1$ (since $4 \\times 6 = 24$, $25 - 24 = 1$). \r\n\r\nThis means $7^{25} \\equiv 7^1 \\equiv 7 \\pmod{10}$. \r\n\r\nAnswer: 7", "correct": true, "gold": "7"}
{"qid": "mp:7742", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>In a right triangle with a \\(45^\\circ\\) angle, the other non\u2011right angle must also be \\(45^\\circ\\), making it a \\(45^\\circ\\!-\\!45^\\circ\\!-\\!90^\\circ\\) triangle.  \nFor such a triangle, if the legs have length \\(L\\), the hypotenuse is \\(L\\sqrt{2}\\).\n\nGiven the hypotenuse is 10 inches:\n\\[\nL\\sqrt{2}=10 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad L=\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{2}}=5\\sqrt{2}\\text{ inches}.\n\\]\n\nThe area of a right triangle is \\(\\frac12 \\times (\\text{leg}_1) \\times (\\text{leg}_2)\\):\n\\[\n\\text{Area}= \\frac12 \\times (5\\sqrt{2}) \\times (5\\sqrt{2})\n          = \\frac12 \\times 25 \\times 2\n          = 25 \\text{ square inches}.\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 25<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "25"}
{"qid": "mp:8598", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "We want the smallest whole number \\(n\\) such that:\n\n- \\(n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}\\)\n- \\(n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}\\)\n- \\(n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\\)\n\nSince \\(n \\equiv 1\\) mod 4 and mod 3, it must satisfy:\n\n- \\(n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{12}\\)\n\nNumbers that are 1 more than a multiple of 12 are:\n\n\\(1, 13, 25, 37, 49, \\dots\\)\n\nNow check which of these gives remainder 2 when divided by 5:\n\n- \\(1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}\\)\n- \\(13 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\\)\n- \\(25 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{5}\\)\n- \\(37 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\\)\n\nSo the smallest such number is 37.\n\nAnswer: 37", "correct": true, "gold": "37"}
{"qid": "mp:8598", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "Answer: 37", "correct": true, "gold": "37"}
{"qid": "mp:8598", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The conditions translate to the following system of congruences:\n\\[\nn \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\quad n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}, \\quad n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}.\n\\]\nThe first two congruences together imply that \\(n-1\\) is divisible by both 3 and 4. Because 3 and 4 are coprime, their least common multiple is 12, so\n\\[\nn \\equiv 1 \\pmod{12} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad n = 12k + 1\n\\]\nfor some integer \\(k \\ge 0\\).\nSubstitute this expression into the third congruence:\n\\[\n12k + 1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 12k \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}.\n\\]\nReduce the coefficient 12 modulo 5 to obtain\n\\[\n2k \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}.\n\\]\nThe multiplicative inverse of 2 modulo 5 is 3 (since \\(2 \\cdot 3 = 6 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}\\)). Multiplying both sides by this inverse yields\n\\[\nk \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad k = 5m + 3\n\\]\nfor some integer \\(m \\ge 0\\). Substituting back gives\n\\[\nn = 12(5m + 3) + 1 = 60m + 37.\n\\]\nThus all solutions are of the form \\(n \\equiv 37 \\pmod{60}\\). The smallest non-negative solution is obtained when \\(m = 0\\), so \\(n = 37\\).\nVerification:\n- \\(37 \\div 4 = 9\\) remainder 1,\n- \\(37 \\div 3 = 12\\) remainder 1,\n- \\(37 \\div 5 = 7\\) remainder 2.\nNo smaller non-negative integer satisfies all three conditions simultaneously.\nAnswer: 37", "correct": true, "gold": "37"}
{"qid": "mp:8598", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Answer: 37<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "37"}
{"qid": "mp:7742", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Since the triangle is a right triangle with a $45^{\\circ}$ angle, the third angle is also $45^{\\circ}$, making it a 45-45-90 isosceles right triangle. \r\n\r\nIn a 45-45-90 triangle, the legs are equal, and the hypotenuse is $\\text{leg} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}$. Given the hypotenuse is 10 inches: \r\n\r\n$\\text{leg} = \\frac{10}{\\sqrt{2}} = 5\\sqrt{2}$ inches. \r\n\r\nThe area of a right triangle is $\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{leg} \\times \\text{leg} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times (5\\sqrt{2})^2 = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 50 = 25$ square inches. \r\n\r\nAnswer: 25", "correct": true, "gold": "25"}
{"qid": "mp:7999", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "P(A\u2229B) = P(A)\u00b7P(B) = 0.7 \u00d7 0.2 = 0.14\n\nAnswer: 0.14", "correct": true, "gold": "0.14"}
{"qid": "mp:8598", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The conditions give us the system of congruences:\n- $n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}$\n- $n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}$\n- $n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}$\n\nFrom the first two conditions, $n-1$ is divisible by both 4 and 3, so $n-1$ is divisible by $\\operatorname{lcm}(4,3)=12$. Thus $n = 12k+1$ for some integer $k$.\n\nSubstituting into the third condition:\n$$12k+1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}$$\n$$12k \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}$$\nSince $12 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}$:\n$$2k \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}$$\n\nMultiplying by 3 (the inverse of 2 mod 5):\n$$k \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}$$\n\nSo $k = 5m+3$ for some integer $m$, giving:\n$$n = 12(5m+3)+1 = 60m+37$$\n\nThe smallest whole number occurs when $m=0$, yielding $n=37$.\n\nVerification: $37 = 4\\cdot 9 + 1$, $37 = 3\\cdot 12 + 1$, and $37 = 5\\cdot 7 + 2$.\n\nAnswer: 37", "correct": true, "gold": "37"}
{"qid": "mp:7999", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "For independent events,  \n\\[\nP(A \\cap B)=P(A)\\,P(B).\n\\]\n\nSo,\n\\[\nP(A \\cap B)=0.7 \\times 0.2=0.14.\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 0.14", "correct": true, "gold": "0.14"}
{"qid": "mp:8957", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Answer: $\\beta=2$", "correct": true, "gold": "2"}
{"qid": "mp:7999", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "Since \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are independent, \\(P(A \\cap B) = P(A) \\cdot P(B)\\).\nThus \\(P(A \\cap B) = 0.7 \\cdot 0.2 = 0.14\\).\nAnswer: 0.14", "correct": true, "gold": "0.14"}
{"qid": "mp:7999", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Since $A$ and $B$ are independent events, the probability of their intersection is the product of their individual probabilities:\n\n$$P(A \\cap B) = P(A) \\cdot P(B) = 0.7 \\times 0.2 = 0.14$$\n\nAnswer: 0.14", "correct": true, "gold": "0.14"}
{"qid": "mp:8598", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We need the smallest whole number \\( n \\) such that:\n\n- \\( n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4} \\)\n- \\( n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\)\n- \\( n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\)\n\nFrom the first two conditions, \\( n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{12} \\) because 4 and 3 are coprime.  \nSo \\( n = 12k + 1 \\) for some integer \\( k \\geq 0 \\).\n\nNow apply the third condition:  \n\\( 12k + 1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\)  \nSince \\( 12 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\) and \\( 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5} \\), we have:  \n\\( 2k + 1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\)  \n\\( 2k \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5} \\)  \nMultiply both sides by the inverse of 2 mod 5, which is 3:  \n\\( k \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\)\n\nSmallest \\( k \\) is 3, so \\( n = 12(3) + 1 = 36 + 1 = 37 \\).\n\nCheck:  \n37 \u00f7 4 = 9 remainder 1  \n37 \u00f7 3 = 12 remainder 1  \n37 \u00f7 5 = 7 remainder 2  \nAll conditions satisfied.\n\nAnswer: 37", "correct": true, "gold": "37"}
{"qid": "mp:7999", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>For independent events, the probability of both occurring is the product of their individual probabilities:\n\n\\[\nP(A \\cap B) = P(A) \\times P(B) = 0.7 \\times 0.2 = 0.14.\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 0.14<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "0.14"}
{"qid": "mp:8433", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Using the Central Limit Theorem, the sampling distribution of the sample mean \\(\\bar X\\) for \\(n=12\\) i.i.d. \\(\\text{Uniform}(0,1)\\) variables is approximately normal with  \n\n\\[\n\\mu_{\\bar X}=0.5,\\qquad \n\\sigma_{\\bar X}=\\frac{\\sigma_X}{\\sqrt{n}}=\\frac{1/\\sqrt{12}}{\\sqrt{12}}=\\frac1{12}\\approx0.08333 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nP\\!\\left(\\tfrac12\\le\\bar X\\le\\tfrac23\\right)\n\\approx P\\!\\left(\\frac{0.5-0.5}{0.08333}\\le Z\\le\\frac{0.6667-0.5}{0.08333}\\right)\n= P(0\\le Z\\le 2),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(Z\\sim N(0,1)\\).  \n\n\\[\nP(0\\le Z\\le 2)=\\Phi(2)-\\Phi(0)=\\Phi(2)-0.5\\approx0.97725-0.5=0.47725.\n\\]\n\nThus the probability is approximately \\(0.48\\) (more precisely about \\(0.477\\)).  \n\n**Answer:** 0.477 (approximately)<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "$0.4772$"}
{"qid": "mp:7999", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "Since \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are independent, the probability of their intersection is the product of their probabilities:\n\n\\[\nP(A \\cap B) = P(A) \\cdot P(B) = 0.7 \\times 0.2 = 0.14\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 0.14", "correct": true, "gold": "0.14"}
{"qid": "mp:8957", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The solution of the ODE is  \n\n\\[\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{5}\\Big[(4-2\\beta)e^{t/2}+(2\\beta+1)e^{-2t}\\Big].\n\\]\n\nIts derivative is  \n\n\\[\ny'(t)=\\frac{1}{5}\\Big[(2-\\beta)e^{t/2}-2(2\\beta+1)e^{-2t}\\Big].\n\\]\n\nSetting \\(y'(t)=0\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(2-\\beta)e^{\\frac{5}{2}t}=2(2\\beta+1).\n\\]\n\nA real solution \\(t\\) exists only if the right\u2011hand side is positive, i.e. \\(\\beta<2\\).  \nFor \\(\\beta<2\\) the unique critical point satisfies  \n\n\\[\ny''(t)=\\frac{1}{5}\\Big[\\frac{2-\\beta}{2}e^{t/2}+4(2\\beta+1)e^{-2t}\\Big]>0,\n\\]\n\nso it is a local minimum.  \n\nWhen \\(\\beta\\ge 2\\), the factor \\((2-\\beta)\\) is non\u2011positive, making \\(y'(t)<0\\) for all \\(t\\); thus no stationary point (and hence no minimum) exists.  \n\nTherefore the smallest \\(\\beta\\) for which the solution has no minimum point is \\(\\beta=2\\).\n\nAnswer: 2<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "2"}
