{"qid": "mp:8419", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Since the 2 teams together contain all 6 people, and the members **within each team are ordered**, each team is really an ordered list.\n\nInterpret \u201cdivided into 2 teams\u201d as splitting the 6 people into two nonempty groups, where:\n\n- the two teams themselves are **not** distinguished, but\n- the order of members **inside** each team matters.\n\nCount by choosing the size of one team.\n\nIf one team has size \\(k\\), the other has size \\(6-k\\), where \\(k=1,2,3,4,5\\).\n\nFor a fixed split \\(k\\) and \\(6-k\\):\n\n- choose which \\(k\\) people are in the first team: \\(\\binom{6}{k}\\)\n- order that team: \\(k!\\)\n- order the other team: \\((6-k)!\\)\n\nSo for each \\(k\\), the count is\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{k}k!(6-k)! = 6!\n\\]\n\nThus summing over \\(k=1\\) to \\(5\\) gives\n\n\\[\n5\\cdot 6! = 5\\cdot 720 = 3600\n\\]\n\nBut this counts each division twice, because swapping the two teams does not create a new division.\n\nSo divide by 2:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{3600}{2}=1800\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 1800", "correct": false, "gold": "720"}
{"qid": "mp:8419", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We need to divide 6 people into 2 teams where the members in each team are ordered.  \nThat means each team is essentially a sequence (ordered list) of its members, not just a set.\n\nFirst, choose an ordering of all 6 people: there are \\(6!\\) ways.  \nThen, we need to split this ordered list into two ordered teams.  \nA natural way: choose a cut point between positions.  \nIf we cut after the \\(k\\)-th person, the first \\(k\\) form team 1 (in that order), and the remaining \\(6-k\\) form team 2 (in that order).  \nHere \\(k\\) can be 1, 2, 3, 4, or 5 (since each team must have at least 1 person).  \n\nSo number of ways = \\(6! \\times 5\\).\n\nBut check: Does the order of the teams themselves matter? The problem says \"divided into 2 teams\" without labeling them (like Team A and Team B). If teams are unlabeled, then each division into two ordered teams would be counted twice (swapping the two teams).  \nHowever, since members are ordered within each team, swapping teams gives a different assignment of which ordered list is first vs second. Usually, if teams are unlabeled, we divide by 2.  \n\nLet\u2019s see: If teams are unlabeled, then for each cut \\(k\\) and each permutation, swapping the two halves gives the same division but with teams reversed. So total = \\(6! \\times 5 / 2\\).\n\nBut the phrase \"members in each team are ordered\" might mean that within a team, order matters, but teams themselves are not ordered. So we should divide by 2.\n\nThus:  \n\\(6! = 720\\)  \n\\(720 \\times 5 = 3600\\)  \n\\(3600 / 2 = 1800\\)\n\nAnswer: 1800", "correct": false, "gold": "720"}
{"qid": "mp:7732", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "To find the units digit of $7^{25}$, I look at the pattern of units digits in powers of 7:\n\n- $7^1 = 7$ \u2192 units digit **7**\n- $7^2 = 49$ \u2192 units digit **9**\n- $7^3 = 343$ \u2192 units digit **3**\n- $7^4 = 2401$ \u2192 units digit **1**\n- $7^5 = 16807$ \u2192 units digit **7**\n\nThe pattern repeats every 4 powers: **7, 9, 3, 1**.\n\nTo find where $7^{25}$ falls in this cycle, I compute $25 \\bmod 4$:\n$25 = 4 \\times 6 + 1$, so the remainder is 1.\n\nThis means $7^{25}$ has the same units digit as $7^1$, which is **7**.\n\nAnswer: 7", "correct": true, "gold": "7"}
