{"qid": "mp:8299", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Let\n\\[\nI_r=\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r\\sin x\\,dx,\\qquad\nJ_r=\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r\\cos x\\,dx.\n\\]\nWe want\n\\[\n\\lim_{r\\to\\infty}\\frac{I_r}{rJ_r}.\n\\]\n\nAs \\(r\\to\\infty\\), the factor \\(x^r\\) makes both integrals concentrate near the endpoint \\(x=\\pi/2\\). So we compare the endpoint values of \\(\\sin x\\) and \\(\\cos x\\) there:\n\n- \\(\\sin(\\pi/2)=1\\), so \\(I_r\\) behaves like \\(\\int x^r\\,dx\\), i.e. on the order of \\((\\pi/2)^{r+1}/r\\).\n- \\(\\cos(\\pi/2)=0\\), so we need the first-order expansion near \\(\\pi/2\\):\n  \\[\n  \\cos x \\sim \\frac{\\pi}{2}-x.\n  \\]\n  Thus \\(J_r\\) is smaller, on the order of \\((\\pi/2)^{r+2}/r^2\\).\n\nSo \\(rJ_r\\) is of the same order as \\(I_r\\), and the limit should be a finite constant.\n\nA clean way is integration by parts on \\(J_r\\):\n\\[\nJ_r=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}x^r\\cos x\\,dx.\n\\]\nTake \\(u=\\cos x,\\; dv=x^r dx\\). Then\n\\[\nJ_r=\\left[\\frac{x^{r+1}}{r+1}\\cos x\\right]_0^{\\pi/2}\n+\\frac1{r+1}\\int_0^{\\pi/2}x^{r+1}\\sin x\\,dx.\n\\]\nThe boundary term is 0, since \\(\\cos(\\pi/2)=0\\), so\n\\[\nJ_r=\\frac1{r+1}\\int_0^{\\pi/2}x^{r+1}\\sin x\\,dx.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nrJ_r=\\frac{r}{r+1}\\int_0^{\\pi/2}x^{r+1}\\sin x\\,dx.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\frac{I_r}{rJ_r}\n=\\frac{\\int_0^{\\pi/2}x^r\\sin x\\,dx}\n{\\frac{r}{r+1}\\int_0^{\\pi/2}x^{r+1}\\sin x\\,dx}\n=\\frac{r+1}{r}\\cdot\n\\frac{\\int_0^{\\pi/2}x^r\\sin x\\,dx}\n{\\int_0^{\\pi/2}x^{r+1}\\sin x\\,dx}.\n\\]\n\nNow since these integrals are dominated near \\(x=\\pi/2\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{\\int x^{r+1}\\sin x\\,dx}{\\int x^r\\sin x\\,dx}\\to \\frac{\\pi}{2},\n\\]\nso\n\\[\n\\frac{\\int x^r\\sin x\\,dx}{\\int x^{r+1}\\sin x\\,dx}\\to \\frac{2}{\\pi}.\n\\]\nAlso \\((r+1)/r\\to 1\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\lim_{r\\to\\infty}\\frac{I_r}{rJ_r}=\\frac{2}{\\pi}.\n\\]\n\nAnswer: \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{\\pi}\\)", "correct": false, "gold": "0.63662"}
{"qid": "mp:8299", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We want to evaluate  \n\\[\n\\lim_{r \\to \\infty} \\frac{\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\sin x \\, dx}{r \\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos x \\, dx}.\n\\]\n\nFor large \\(r\\), the dominant contribution to integrals of the form \\(\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r f(x) dx\\) comes from near \\(x = \\pi/2\\), because \\(x^r\\) is largest there.  \n\nLet\u2019s set \\(x = \\pi/2 - t\\), so \\(t \\to 0^+\\). Then  \n\\[\nx^r = \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} - t \\right)^r = \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)^r \\left(1 - \\frac{2t}{\\pi}\\right)^r \\approx \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)^r e^{- (2r/\\pi) t}.\n\\]  \nAlso, \\(\\sin x = \\sin(\\pi/2 - t) = \\cos t \\approx 1 - t^2/2\\), and \\(\\cos x = \\cos(\\pi/2 - t) = \\sin t \\approx t\\).\n\nThus, for large \\(r\\):\n\nNumerator:  \n\\[\n\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\sin x \\, dx \\approx \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)^r \\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-(2r/\\pi) t} \\cdot 1 \\, dt\n= \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)^r \\cdot \\frac{\\pi}{2r}.\n\\]\n\nDenominator part:  \n\\[\n\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos x \\, dx \\approx \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)^r \\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-(2r/\\pi) t} \\cdot t \\, dt\n= \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)^r \\cdot \\frac{1}{(2r/\\pi)^2}\n= \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)^r \\cdot \\frac{\\pi^2}{4 r^2}.\n\\]\n\nSo the ratio becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)^r \\cdot \\frac{\\pi}{2r}}{r \\cdot \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)^r \\cdot \\frac{\\pi^2}{4 r^2}}\n= \\frac{\\frac{\\pi}{2r}}{r \\cdot \\frac{\\pi^2}{4 r^2}}\n= \\frac{\\frac{\\pi}{2r}}{\\frac{\\pi^2}{4 r}}\n= \\frac{\\pi/2}{\\pi^2/4} = \\frac{2}{\\pi}.\n\\]\n\nThus the limit is \\(\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\).\n\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\)", "correct": false, "gold": "0.63662"}
{"qid": "mp:8957", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "The characteristic equation \\(2r^2+3r-2=0\\) gives roots \\(r=\\frac12\\) and \\(r=-2\\), so  \n\\[\ny(t)=C_1e^{t/2}+C_2e^{-2t}.\n\\]\nFrom \\(y(0)=1\\) and \\(y'(0)=-\\beta\\) we obtain  \n\\[\nC_1=\\frac{4-2\\beta}{5},\\qquad C_2=\\frac{1+2\\beta}{5}.\n\\]\nHence  \n\\[\ny'(t)=\\frac15\\Big[(2-\\beta)e^{t/2}-2(1+2\\beta)e^{-2t}\\Big].\n\\]\nA critical point requires \\(y'(t)=0\\), i.e.  \n\\[\n(2-\\beta)e^{5t/2}=2(1+2\\beta).\n\\]\nSince \\(2(1+2\\beta)>0\\), a solution exists only when \\(2-\\beta>0\\) (i.e. \\(\\beta<2\\)).  \nFor \\(0<\\beta<2\\) there is a unique \\(t^*>0\\) where \\(y'=0\\); there \\(y''>0\\), so \\(t^*\\) is a local (and global) minimum.\n\nIf \\(\\beta=2\\), then \\(C_1=0\\) and \\(y(t)=e^{-2t}\\), which is strictly decreasing (\\(y'<0\\)) and has no critical point.  \nIf \\(\\beta>2\\), then \\(2-\\beta<0\\) and both terms in \\(y'(t)\\) are negative, so \\(y'(t)<0\\) for all \\(t\\); again no critical point exists.\n\nThus the solution has no minimum point precisely when \\(\\beta\\ge 2\\). The smallest such value is  \n\nAnswer: 2", "correct": true, "gold": "2"}
{"qid": "mp:8419", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "## Setting Up the Problem\n\nI need to divide 6 people into 2 teams where:\n- Both teams must be non-empty\n- Members within each team are **ordered** (i.e., team members form a sequence, not just a set)\n- The two teams themselves are **unlabeled** (Team A/Team B distinction doesn't matter \u2014 just \"2 teams\")\n\n## Counting with Labeled Teams First\n\nSuppose temporarily that the teams *are* labeled (say Team A and Team B), and let team sizes be $k$ and $6-k$, where $k = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5$.\n\nFor a fixed $k$:\n- Choose which $k$ people go to Team A: $\\binom{6}{k}$ ways\n- Arrange those $k$ people in order: $k!$ ways  \n- Arrange the remaining $6-k$ people in Team B: $(6-k)!$ ways\n\nTotal for fixed $k$:\n$$\n\\binom{6}{k} \\cdot k! \\cdot (6-k)! = \\frac{6!}{k!(6-k)!} \\cdot k! \\cdot (6-k)! = 6! = 720\n$$\n\n**Key insight:** This equals $6! = 720$ for *every* valid $k$! This makes sense: arranging all 6 people in a sequence and splitting after position $k$ gives exactly one (ordered Team A, ordered Team B) pair, and this correspondence is a bijection.\n\n## Summing Over All Team Sizes\n\nSince $k$ ranges over $1, 2, 3, 4, 5$ (both teams non-empty):\n\n$$\n\\text{Total (labeled teams)} = 5 \\times 720 = 3600\n$$\n\n## Adjusting for Unlabeled Teams\n\nSince the two teams are **not** distinguished (just \"2 teams,\" not \"Team A and Team B\"), each actual division has been counted twice \u2014 once as $(A,B)$ and once as $(B,A)$. This holds even when $k=3$ (equal sizes), because swapping which ordered sequence is \"first\" still produces a different labeled pair but the same actual grouping.\n\nSo divide by 2:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3600}{2} = 1800\n$$\n\n## Answer\n\n$$\n\\boxed{1800}\n$$\n\nAnswer: 1800", "correct": false, "gold": "720"}
