{"qid": "mp:8876", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Statement 1 is **true**: if \\(R\\) is a commutative ring and \\(I\\) is an ideal, then the quotient ring \\(R/I\\) inherits addition and multiplication from \\(R\\), and multiplication remains commutative:\n\\[\n(a+I)(b+I)=ab+I=ba+I=(b+I)(a+I).\n\\]\n\nStatement 2 is also **true**: for an ideal \\(N \\subseteq R\\) where \\(R\\) has unity \\(1\\),\n\n- If \\(N=R\\), then certainly \\(1\\in N\\).\n- If \\(1\\in N\\), then for any \\(r\\in R\\),\n  \\[\n  r=r\\cdot 1 \\in N\n  \\]\n  since \\(N\\) is an ideal and is closed under multiplication by elements of \\(R\\). Thus every \\(r\\in R\\) lies in \\(N\\), so \\(N=R\\).\n\nAnswer: Both Statement 1 and Statement 2 are true.", "correct": false, "gold": "True, False"}
{"qid": "mp:8876", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "Both statements concern basic facts from ring theory.\nStatement 1 holds because the operations on a quotient ring \\(R/I\\) are defined by\n\\[\n(a+I)+(b+I)=(a+b)+I,\\qquad(a+I)(b+I)=(ab)+I.\n\\]\nWhen \\(R\\) is commutative we have \\(a+b=b+a\\) and \\(ab=ba\\), so the same identities hold in \\(R/I\\). Thus every quotient of a commutative ring is commutative.\nStatement 2 is the standard characterization of the unit ideal: if \\(1\\in N\\) then, for an arbitrary \\(r\\in R\\), the ideal property gives \\(r=r\\cdot1\\in N\\), so \\(N=R\\). The converse is immediate. Hence an ideal of a unital ring is improper if and only if it contains the multiplicative identity.\nConsequently both statements are true.\nAnswer: both true", "correct": false, "gold": "True, False"}
{"qid": "mp:8721", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "Since $m$ and $n$ are odd, the factor $m^{6-k}n^k$ in each term of $(m+n)^6=\\sum_{k=0}^{6}\\binom{6}{k}m^{6-k}n^k$ is odd. Therefore, a term is odd if and only if its binomial coefficient $\\binom{6}{k}$ is odd. \n\nChecking coefficients: $\\binom{6}{0}=1$, $\\binom{6}{1}=6$, $\\binom{6}{2}=15$, $\\binom{6}{3}=20$, $\\binom{6}{4}=15$, $\\binom{6}{5}=6$, $\\binom{6}{6}=1$ \u2014 the odd ones occur at $k=0,2,4,6$.\n\nAnswer: 4", "correct": true, "gold": "4"}
{"qid": "mp:8721", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "To determine how many terms in the expansion of $(m+n)^6$ are odd when $m$ and $n$ are odd integers, we use the binomial theorem:\n\n$$(m+n)^6 = \\sum_{k=0}^{6} \\binom{6}{k} m^{6-k} n^k$$\n\nSince $m$ and $n$ are odd, any power of $m$ or $n$ is also odd, and the product $m^{6-k}n^k$ is odd for all $k$. Therefore, the parity of each term $\\binom{6}{k} m^{6-k} n^k$ depends only on the binomial coefficient $\\binom{6}{k}$. The term is odd if and only if $\\binom{6}{k}$ is odd.\n\nComputing the binomial coefficients in row 6 of Pascal's triangle:\n- $\\binom{6}{0} = 1$ (odd)\n- $\\binom{6}{1} = 6$ (even)\n- $\\binom{6}{2} = 15$ (odd)\n- $\\binom{6}{3} = 20$ (even)\n- $\\binom{6}{4} = 15$ (odd)\n- $\\binom{6}{5} = 6$ (even)\n- $\\binom{6}{6} = 1$ (odd)\n\nAlternatively, by Lucas' Theorem, $\\binom{n}{k}$ is odd if and only if the binary representation of $k$ is a subset of the binary representation of $n$. Since $6 = 110_2$, the values of $k \\in \\{0,1,2,3,4,5,6\\}$ with $k \\subseteq 6$ in binary are $k \\in \\{0, 2, 4, 6\\}$ (corresponding to $000_2, 010_2, 100_2, 110_2$).\n\nThus, there are 4 odd binomial coefficients, and consequently 4 odd terms in the expansion.\n\nAnswer: 4", "correct": true, "gold": "4"}
{"qid": "mp:8876", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "Let\u2019s check each statement.\n\n**Statement 1:**  \nIf \\( R \\) is a commutative ring and \\( I \\) is an ideal, the quotient ring \\( R/I \\) inherits commutativity from \\( R \\). For any \\( a+I, b+I \\in R/I \\),  \n\\[\n(a+I)(b+I) = ab + I = ba + I = (b+I)(a+I).\n\\]  \nSo yes, every quotient ring of a commutative ring is commutative. This is true.\n\n**Statement 2:**  \nIf \\( N \\) is an ideal of a ring \\( R \\) with unity \\( 1 \\), and \\( 1 \\in N \\), then for any \\( r \\in R \\), \\( r \\cdot 1 = r \\in N \\), so \\( N = R \\). Conversely, if \\( N = R \\), then certainly \\( 1 \\in N \\). This is true.\n\nBoth statements are true.\n\nAnswer: True, True", "correct": false, "gold": "True, False"}
{"qid": "mp:8876", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>Both statements are true. In a commutative ring, the quotient by any ideal inherits the commutative multiplication. Moreover, in a ring with unity, an ideal equals the whole ring precisely when it contains the multiplicative identity.\n\nAnswer: Both statements are true.<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "True, False"}
{"qid": "mp:8625", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "For a nearest-neighbor random walk on \\(\\mathbb Z\\) with drift to the right,\n\\[\nP(X_i=1)=p=0.8,\\qquad P(X_i=-1)=q=0.2,\\qquad p>q.\n\\]\n\nWe want\n\\[\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{n}E[R_n].\n\\]\n\nFor a \\(\\pm1\\) walk, the set of visited sites up to time \\(n\\) is exactly all integers between the minimum and maximum visited, so\n\\[\nR_n = M_n - m_n + 1,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nM_n=\\max_{0\\le k\\le n} S_k,\\qquad m_n=\\min_{0\\le k\\le n} S_k.\n\\]\n\nNow:\n\n- Since the walk has positive drift,\n  \\[\n  \\frac{S_n}{n}\\to E[X_1]=0.8-0.2=0.6 \\quad \\text{a.s.}\n  \\]\n  Hence the maximum grows linearly:\n  \\[\n  \\frac{M_n}{n}\\to 0.6 \\quad \\text{a.s.}\n  \\]\n- The minimum \\(m_n\\) is bounded below in distribution and in fact converges to a finite random variable a.s. because the walk drifts to \\(+\\infty\\). So \\(m_n=O(1)\\), hence\n  \\[\n  \\frac{m_n}{n}\\to 0.\n  \\]\n\nTherefore,\n\\[\n\\frac{R_n}{n}\n=\n\\frac{M_n-m_n+1}{n}\n\\to 0.6\n\\quad \\text{a.s.}\n\\]\nand consequently also in expectation,\n\\[\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac{E[R_n]}{n}=0.6.\n\\]\n\nAnswer: \\(\\boxed{0.6}\\)", "correct": true, "gold": "0.6"}
{"qid": "mp:8299", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "", "correct": false, "gold": "0.63662"}
{"qid": "mp:8625", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We have a biased random walk with step probabilities  \n\\(P(X_i = 1) = 0.8\\) and \\(P(X_i = -1) = 0.2\\).  \nThe walk drifts upward since the mean step is \\(0.8 - 0.2 = 0.6 > 0\\).\n\nFor a random walk with positive drift, the number of distinct values visited up to time \\(n\\) is approximately the maximum minus the minimum plus 1.  \nSince the walk goes upward almost surely, the minimum will be achieved early and stay near 0 (or some small negative value), while the maximum grows linearly with \\(n\\).  \n\nThe maximum after \\(n\\) steps is about \\(0.6 n\\) (the mean drift times \\(n\\)), and the minimum is \\(O(1)\\) in probability.  \nThus the range \\(R_n\\) grows like \\(0.6 n + o(n)\\).  \n\nTherefore,  \n\\[\n\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{E[R_n]}{n} = 0.6.\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 0.6", "correct": true, "gold": "0.6"}
{"qid": "mp:8876", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "Looking at these two statements about ring theory:\n\n**Statement 1:** If $R$ is a commutative ring and $I$ is an ideal of $R$, the quotient ring $R/I$ has multiplication defined by $(a+I)(b+I) = ab+I$. Since $R$ is commutative, $ab = ba$, so $(a+I)(b+I) = ab+I = ba+I = (b+I)(a+I)$. Thus $R/I$ is commutative. **This is TRUE.**\n\n**Statement 2:** For a ring $R$ with unity $1$:\n- ($\\Rightarrow$) If $N = R$, then $1 \\in R = N$, so $1 \\in N$.\n- ($\\Leftarrow$) If $1 \\in N$, then for any $r \\in R$, we have $r = r \\cdot 1 \\in N$ (since $N$ is an ideal). Thus $R \\subseteq N$, so $N = R$.\nThis is a standard characterization of when an ideal equals the whole ring. **This is TRUE.**\n\nAnswer: Both statements are true.", "correct": false, "gold": "True, False"}
{"qid": "mp:8876", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "MAI-Thinking-1", "resp": "**Reason:** \r\n\r\n- **Statement 1:** Let $R$ be a commutative ring and $N$ an ideal of $R$. For any $a+N, b+N \\in R/N$, we have $(a+N)(b+N)=ab+N=ba+N=(b+N)(a+N)$. Thus, $R/N$ is commutative. \r\n\r\n- **Statement 2:** If $1 \\in N$, then for every $r \\in R$, $r = r\\cdot 1 \\in N$, so $N=R$. Conversely, if $N=R$, then $1 \\in N$. \r\n\r\nAnswer: Both statements are true.", "correct": false, "gold": "True, False"}
{"qid": "mp:8299", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>**Solution outline**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nI(r)=\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}x^{r}\\sin x\\,dx ,\\qquad \nJ(r)=\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}x^{r}\\cos x\\,dx .\n\\]\n\nThe required limit is  \n\n\\[\nL=\\lim_{r\\to\\infty}\\frac{I(r)}{r\\,J(r)} .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 1. Relate \\(I(r)\\) and \\(J(r)\\)\n\nIntegrate \\(I(r)\\) by parts:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(r)&=\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}x^{r}\\sin x\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\Big[-x^{r}\\cos x\\Big]_{0}^{\\pi/2}+r\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}x^{r-1}\\cos x\\,dx\\\\\n&=r\\,J(r-1),\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(\\cos(\\pi/2)=0\\) and \\(0^{\\,r}=0\\).  \nHence\n\n\\[\n\\frac{I(r)}{r\\,J(r)}=\\frac{J(r-1)}{J(r)}.\n\\]\n\nSo it suffices to study the asymptotics of \\(J(r)\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Asymptotics of \\(J(r)\\) for large \\(r\\)\n\nFor large \\(r\\) the integrand \\(x^{r}\\cos x\\) is sharply peaked near the right\u2011hand endpoint \\(x=\\pi/2\\).  \nSet \\(x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}-t\\) with \\(t\\ge0\\). Then\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{r}&=\\Bigl(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-t\\Bigr)^{r}\n      =\\Bigl(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\Bigr)^{r}\\!\\exp\\!\\Bigl(r\\ln\\!\\bigl(1-\\tfrac{2t}{\\pi}\\bigr)\\Bigr)\n      =\\Bigl(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\Bigr)^{r}\\! \\exp\\!\\Bigl(-\\frac{2r t}{\\pi}+O(rt^{2})\\Bigr),\\\\[4pt]\n\\cos x&=\\cos\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-t\\Bigr)=\\sin t = t+O(t^{3}),\\\\\n\\sin x&=\\sin\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-t\\Bigr)=\\cos t = 1+O(t^{2}).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBecause the exponential factor decays on the scale \\(t\\sim 1/r\\), the \\(O(rt^{2})\\) term is negligible for the leading order.  \nThus\n\n\\[\nJ(r)=\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}x^{r}\\cos x\\,dx\n    =\\Bigl(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\Bigr)^{r}\\int_{0}^{\\infty}e^{-\\frac{2r t}{\\pi}}\\,t\\,dt\\;(1+o(1)).\n\\]\n\nThe Gaussian integral \\(\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\infty} t e^{-a t}\\,dt =\\frac{1}{a^{2}}\\) with \\(a=\\frac{2r}{\\pi}\\) gives\n\n\\[\nJ(r)=\\Bigl(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\Bigr)^{r}\\frac{\\pi^{2}}{4r^{2}}\\,(1+o(1)).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA similar calculation shows\n\n\\[\nJ(r-1)=\\Bigl(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\Bigr)^{r-1}\\frac{\\pi^{2}}{4(r-1)^{2}}\\,(1+o(1)).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3. Compute the limit\n\nUsing (1)\u2013(2),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{J(r-1)}{J(r)}\n=\n\\frac{\\bigl(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\bigr)^{r-1}}{\\bigl(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\bigr)^{r}}\n\\;\n\\frac{r^{2}}{(r-1)^{2}}\\,\n\\frac{1+o(1)}{1+o(1)}\n=\n\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\,\\frac{r^{2}}{(r-1)^{2}}\\,(1+o(1))\n\\;\\xrightarrow[r\\to\\infty]{}\\;\\frac{2}{\\pi}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{I(r)}{rJ(r)}=\\frac{J(r-1)}{J(r)}\\), the same limit holds for the original expression.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \\lim_{r\\to\\infty}\\frac{\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}x^{r}\\sin x\\,dx}\n{r\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}x^{r}\\cos x\\,dx}= \\frac{2}{\\pi}}.\n\\]\n\nAnswer: \\( \\frac{2}{\\pi} \\).<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "0.63662"}
{"qid": "mp:8020", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "This question requires answer choices (typically showing groups of items) that aren't included in the text you've provided. However, based on the common version of this problem, the answer would show a group where:\n\n- **S (Strawberries) = an even number** (like 2, 4, 6, 8, 10...)\n- **A (Apples) = an odd number** (like 1, 3, 5, 7, 9...)\n\nFor example, a valid answer would be **S = 8 and A = 5**, since 8 is even and 5 is odd.\n\nIf you can share the actual answer choices (usually labeled A, B, C, D with pictures or numbers), I can help you identify the exact correct option!\n\nAnswer: S = 8 (even), A = 5 (odd)", "correct": false, "gold": "SSSSSSSS; A"}
{"qid": "mp:8020", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "We need **S** to be an **even** number and **A** to be an **odd** number.\n\nSo the correct group is the one where:\n- **Strawberries (S)** = even\n- **Apples (A)** = odd\n\nAnswer: The group where **S is even and A is odd**.", "correct": false, "gold": "SSSSSSSS; A"}
{"qid": "mp:8020", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We know that the number of strawberries (S) is even and the number of apples (A) is odd.  \nWe need to find which pair (S, A) matches these conditions.\n\n- Even numbers: 0, 2, 4, 6, ...  \n- Odd numbers: 1, 3, 5, 7, ...\n\nWithout the specific options listed, the correct group must have S even and A odd.\n\nAnswer: The group where S is even and A is odd.", "correct": false, "gold": "SSSSSSSS; A"}
{"qid": "mp:8419", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "To solve this, we interpret the problem as partitioning 6 people into two **unlabeled** (indistinguishable) teams where the members within each team form an ordered sequence (line). We assume both teams must be non-empty (as \"divided into 2 teams\" implies both exist).\n\nFor a fixed team size $k$ (where $1 \\le k \\le 5$):\n* Choose $k$ people out of 6: $\\binom{6}{k}$ ways.\n* Order these $k$ people: $k!$ ways.\n* Order the remaining $6-k$ people: $(6-k)!$ ways.\n\nThis gives $\\binom{6}{k} \\cdot k! \\cdot (6-k)! = \\frac{6!}{k!(6-k)!} \\cdot k! \\cdot (6-k)! = 6! = 720$ ways for each $k$.\n\nSumming over all possible sizes $k=1, 2, 3, 4, 5$ gives $5 \\times 720 = 3600$ ordered assignments to **labeled** teams (Team A and Team B).\n\nSince the teams themselves are unlabeled (swapping the two teams results in the same division), we divide by 2:\n$$\\frac{3600}{2} = 1800$$\n\n(If empty teams were allowed, the sum would include $k=0$", "correct": true, "gold": "720"}
{"qid": "mp:8625", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "## Setting Up the Problem\n\nFor a simple random walk with $P(X_i=1)=p=0.8$ and $P(X_i=-1)=q=0.2$, I need to find $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} n^{-1}E[R_n]$, where $R_n$ counts the distinct values among $S_0, S_1, \\ldots, S_n$.\n\n**Key fact:** For any random walk on $\\mathbb{Z}$, \n$$\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\frac{E[R_n]}{n} = P(S_k \\neq 0 \\text{ for all } k \\geq 1) =: 1-f,$$\nwhere $f = P(\\text{walk ever returns to } 0)$. This holds because the range grows by exactly the number of \"new\" sites visited, and by the law of large numbers / renewal-type arguments, the asymptotic rate of visiting new sites equals the probability of never returning to the starting point.\n\n## Computing the Return Probability $f$\n\nLet $f = P(\\text{walk starting at } 0 \\text{ ever returns to } 0)$.\n\n**First step analysis:**\n- With probability $p$, the walk moves to $1$. From there, since $p > q$ (positive drift), the probability of ever hitting $0$ again from state $1$ is the classical ruin-type probability:\n$$P(\\text{hit } 0 \\text{ from } 1) = \\frac{q}{p}.$$\n- With probability $q$, the walk moves to $-1$. From there, since the drift is positive ($p>q$), the walk is certain to eventually reach $0$ (indeed certain to reach every level $\\geq -1$), so:\n$$P(\\text{hit } 0 \\text{ from } -1) = 1.$$\n\n**Combining:**\n$$\nf = p \\cdot \\frac{q}{p} + q \\cdot 1 = q + q = 2q.\n$$\n\nWith $q = 0.2$:\n$$\nf = 2(0.2) = 0.4.\n$$\n\n## Computing the Limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\frac{E[R_n]}{n} = 1 - f = 1 - 2q = p - q.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $p=0.8$, $q=0.2$:\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\frac{E[R_n]}{n} = 0.8 - 0.2 = 0.6.\n$$\n\n**Consistency check:** This matches the known identity $f = 1-|p-q|$ for nearest-neighbor random walks: $1-|0.8-0.2| = 0.4$, confirming $f=0.4$ and the limit $=0.6$.\n\nAnswer: 0.6", "correct": true, "gold": "0.6"}
{"qid": "mp:8020", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The problem requires identifying a collection of values for strawberries \\(S\\) and apples \\(A\\) that satisfies the two parity conditions given: the total number of strawberries is even and the total number of apples is odd.  \n\nAny collection whose members for \\(S\\) are all even and whose members for \\(A\\) are all odd meets the stated requirements. The matching collection is therefore the one that lists even values under \\(S\\) and odd values under \\(A\\).\n\nAnswer: S: 2,4,6 A: 1,3,5", "correct": false, "gold": "SSSSSSSS; A"}
{"qid": "mp:8625", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The process \\(\\{S_n: n \\geq 0\\}\\) is a nearest-neighbor random walk on \\(\\mathbb{Z}\\) with positive drift\n\\[\n\\mu := \\mathbb{E}[X_i] = 0.8 \\cdot 1 + 0.2 \\cdot (-1) = 0.6 > 0.\n\\]\nConsequently \\(S_n \\to +\\infty\\) almost surely. Because the increments are of size one, the trajectory cannot skip lattice sites. Therefore the set of distinct values visited by time \\(n\\) is exactly the interval of integers between the running minimum and the running maximum:\n\\[\nR_n = M_n - m_n + 1,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nM_n := \\max_{0\\leq k\\leq n} S_k, \\qquad m_n := \\min_{0\\leq k\\leq n} S_k.\n\\]\nThe strong law of large numbers gives \\(S_n/n\\to\\mu\\) a.s. The same limit holds for the maximum:\n\\[\n\\frac{M_n}{n}\\to\\mu \\quad\\text{a.s.}\n\\]\nTo see this, note first that \\(M_n\\geq S_n\\) immediately yields the lower bound \\(\\liminf M_n/n\\geq\\mu\\). For the matching upper bound observe that\n\\[\n\\limsup_{k\\to\\infty}\\frac{S_k}{k}=\\mu \\quad\\text{a.s.}\n\\]\nby the SLLN. Hence, for every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) and all sufficiently large \\(n\\),\n\\[\nS_k\\leq(\\mu+\\varepsilon)k\\leq(\\mu+\\varepsilon)n \\qquad\\text{for every }k\\leq n,\n\\]\nwhich implies \\(M_n\\leq(\\mu+\\varepsilon)n\\) eventually. Sending \\(\\varepsilon\\to0\\) produces the claimed upper bound, so \\(M_n/n\\to\\mu\\) a.s.\nBecause the walk drifts to \\(+\\infty\\), the global infimum \\(m_\\infty:=\\inf_{k\\geq0}S_k\\) is almost surely finite. Consequently the truncated minima satisfy\n\\[\nm_n\\to m_\\infty\\in\\mathbb{R} \\quad\\text{a.s.},\n\\]\nand therefore \\(m_n/n\\to0\\) a.s. Combining the two limits yields\n\\[\n\\frac{R_n}{n}\\to\\mu \\quad\\text{a.s.}\n\\]\nThe random variables \\(R_n\\) are dominated by the integrable sequence \\(n+1\\). Dominated convergence therefore passes the almost-sure limit inside the expectation:\n\\[\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac1n\\mathbb{E}[R_n]=\\mu=0.6.\n\\]\n(The same conclusion holds with the fraction \\(3/5\\).)\nFinal answer: 0.6", "correct": true, "gold": "0.6"}
{"qid": "mp:8020", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Kimi-K2.5", "resp": "To solve this, we need to remember:\n- **Even numbers** are divisible by 2 (such as 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12...)\n- **Odd numbers** are not divisible by 2 (such as 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11...)\n\nSince Micah ate an even number of strawberries, **S must be even**.\nSince he ate an odd number of apples, **A must be odd**.\n\nTherefore, the correct group is the one where the number of strawberries is even and the number of apples is odd.\n\nAnswer: The group where S is an even number and A is an odd number (for example: 8 strawberries and 3 apples, or any pair where S \u2208 {2, 4, 6, 8...} and A \u2208 {1, 3, 5, 7...}).", "correct": false, "gold": "SSSSSSSS; A"}
{"qid": "mp:8020", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The strawberries must be an even number and the apples must be an odd number. Therefore any even integer can represent the strawberries \\(S\\) and any odd integer can represent the apples \\(A\\). For example, \\(S = 8\\) strawberries and \\(A = 5\\) apples satisfy the conditions.\n\nAnswer: Any even number for strawberries and any odd number for apples (e.g., S\u202f=\u202f8, A\u202f=\u202f5).<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": false, "gold": "SSSSSSSS; A"}
{"qid": "mp:8094", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "For a loan with continuous compounding and continuous payment at rate \\(R\\), the present value \\(P\\) satisfies\n\n\\[\nP=\\int_0^T R e^{-rt}\\,dt\n\\]\n\nwhere\n\n- \\(R = 800\\) dollars/month,\n- \\(r = 0.09\\) per year,\n- \\(T = 20\\) years.\n\nBecause payments are monthly in dollars/month, convert to dollars/year:\n\n\\[\nR = 800 \\cdot 12 = 9600 \\text{ dollars/year}\n\\]\n\nNow compute:\n\n\\[\nP=\\int_0^{20} 9600 e^{-0.09t}\\,dt\n\\]\n\n\\[\nP=9600\\left[\\frac{1-e^{-0.09(20)}}{0.09}\\right]\n\\]\n\n\\[\nP=9600\\left(\\frac{1-e^{-1.8}}{0.09}\\right)\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(e^{-1.8}\\approx 0.1653\\),\n\n\\[\nP \\approx 9600\\left(\\frac{1-0.1653}{0.09}\\right)\n=9600\\left(\\frac{0.8347}{0.09}\\right)\n\\approx 9600(9.274)\n\\approx 89030\n\\]\n\nSo the maximum loan amount is about\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\$89,\\!030}\n\\]\n\nAnswer: \\(\\$89,\\!030\\) (approximately)", "correct": false, "gold": "89,034.79 $"}
{"qid": "mp:8094", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Setup:** With continuous payments at rate k = $9600/year, continuous interest rate r = 0.09, and term T = 20 years, the present value (maximum loan amount) satisfies:\n\n$$B_0 = \\frac{k}{r}\\left(1 - e^{-rT}\\right)$$\n\n**Calculation:**\n\n$$B_0 = \\frac{9600}{0.09}\\left(1 - e^{-0.09(20)}\\right) = 106666.67 \\times \\left(1 - e^{-1.8}\\right)$$\n\nSince $e^{-1.8} \\approx 0.16530$:\n\n$$B_0 = 106666.67 \\times (1 - 0.16530) = 106666.67 \\times 0.83470$$\n\n$$B_0 \\approx 89{,}035$$\n\n**Answer:** The maximum amount the buyer can afford to borrow is approximately **$89,035**.", "correct": true, "gold": "89,034.79 $"}
{"qid": "mp:8094", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We are told the buyer can pay $800 per month continuously, the interest rate is 9% per year compounded continuously, and the term is 20 years.\n\nWe use the continuous annuity formula for present value:\n\n\\[\nA = \\frac{R}{r} \\left(1 - e^{-rt}\\right)\n\\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\( R \\) is the yearly payment rate (since payments are continuous, we need the annual rate).  \n  Monthly $800 \\times 12 = 9600$ per year, so \\( R = 9600 \\).\n- \\( r = 0.09 \\) (annual rate, continuous compounding).\n- \\( t = 20 \\) years.\n\nPlug in:\n\n\\[\nA = \\frac{9600}{0.09} \\left(1 - e^{-0.09 \\times 20}\\right)\n\\]\n\nFirst, \\( 0.09 \\times 20 = 1.8 \\), so:\n\n\\[\nA = \\frac{9600}{0.09} \\left(1 - e^{-1.8}\\right)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\frac{9600}{0.09} = 106666.\\overline{6}\n\\]\n\nNow \\( e^{-1.8} \\approx 0.1652989 \\), so:\n\n\\[\n1 - 0.1652989 = 0.8347011\n\\]\n\nMultiply:\n\n\\[\nA \\approx 106666.67 \\times 0.8347011 \\approx 89034.79\n\\]\n\nThus the maximum amount to borrow is about $89,034.79.\n\nAnswer: 89034.79", "correct": true, "gold": "89,034.79 $"}
{"qid": "mp:8625", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026", "resp": "<|START_TEXT|>The range of the walk is \\(R_n = M_n - m_n + 1\\), where  \n\\(M_n = \\max_{0\\le k\\le n} S_k\\) and \\(m_n = \\min_{0\\le k\\le n} S_k\\).  \n\nWrite \\(S_k = \\mu k + Y_k\\) with \\(\\mu = E[X_i]=0.6\\) and \\(Y_k=S_k-\\mu k\\).  \n\\(\\{Y_k\\}\\) is a martingale with \\(E[Y_k]=0\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Var}(Y_k)=0.64\\).  \nBy Doob\u2019s \\(L^2\\) inequality,\n\\[\nE[M_n]\\le \\mu n + E\\Big[\\max_{0\\le k\\le n}Y_k\\Big]\n        \\le \\mu n + \\sqrt{E[Y_n^2]}\n        = \\mu n + 0.8\\sqrt{n}.\n\\]\nHence \\(E[M_n]=\\mu n+o(n)\\).\n\nFor the minimum, \\(-S_k\\) has negative drift \\(-\\mu\\). The probability that the walk ever hits \\(-a\\) is \\((q/p)^a = (0.25)^a\\). Thus for any \\(n\\),\n\\[\nP(m_n\\le -a)\\le (0.25)^a,\n\\qquad\nE[-m_n]=\\sum_{a\\ge1}P(m_n\\le -a)\\le\\sum_{a\\ge1}(0.25)^a=\\frac13.\n\\]\nSo \\(E[|m_n|]\\) is bounded uniformly in \\(n\\).\n\nConsequently,\n\\[\nE[R_n]=E[M_n-m_n]+1\n      =\\mu n + O(\\sqrt{n}) + O(1).\n\\]\nDividing by \\(n\\) gives\n\\[\n\\frac{E[R_n]}{n}\\xrightarrow[n\\to\\infty]{}\\mu=0.6.\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 0.6<|END_TEXT|>", "correct": true, "gold": "0.6"}
{"qid": "mp:7691", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5", "resp": "**Answer: c = 2**\n\nTo check, evaluate f(x) = x\u00b3 + x\u00b2 + c at x = 0, 1, 2 in Z\u2083 for each c:\n\n- **c = 0:** f(0) = 0 \u2192 has root, reducible.\n- **c = 1:** f(1) = 1+1+1 = 3 \u2261 0 \u2192 has root, reducible.\n- **c = 2:** f(0)=2, f(1)=1+1+2=4\u22611, f(2)=8+4+2=14\u22612 \u2192 no roots, so f is irreducible (since degree 3 with no linear factor means no factorization over Z\u2083).\n\nSince Z\u2083[x]/(f(x)) is a field iff f is irreducible, the only value making it a field is:\n\nAnswer: c = 2", "correct": true, "gold": "2"}
{"qid": "mp:7691", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "openai/gpt-5.4", "resp": "Over \\(\\mathbb Z_3\\), the quotient\n\\[\n\\mathbb Z_3[x]/(x^3+x^2+c)\n\\]\nis a field iff the polynomial\n\\[\nf(x)=x^3+x^2+c\n\\]\nis irreducible in \\(\\mathbb Z_3[x]\\).\n\nSince \\(f\\) is cubic, it is reducible iff it has a root in \\(\\mathbb Z_3=\\{0,1,2\\}\\).\n\nWe check each \\(c\\in\\mathbb Z_3\\):\n\n### 1. \\(c=0\\)\n\\[\nf(x)=x^3+x^2=x^2(x+1)\n\\]\nClearly reducible, so not a field.\n\n### 2. \\(c=1\\)\n\\[\nf(x)=x^3+x^2+1\n\\]\nTest roots mod \\(3\\):\n- \\(f(0)=1\\neq 0\\)\n- \\(f(1)=1+1+1=3\\equiv 0\\)\n\nSo reducible, hence not a field.\n\n### 3. \\(c=2\\)\n\\[\nf(x)=x^3+x^2+2\n\\]\nTest roots:\n- \\(f(0)=2\\neq 0\\)\n- \\(f(1)=1+1+2=4\\equiv 1\\neq 0\\)\n- \\(f(2)=8+4+2=14\\equiv 2\\neq 0 \\pmod 3\\)\n\nNo root in \\(\\mathbb Z_3\\), so the cubic is irreducible. Therefore the quotient is a field.\n\nAnswer: \\(c=2\\)", "correct": true, "gold": "2"}
{"qid": "mp:8094", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The mortgage balance \\(P(t)\\) satisfies the first-order linear ODE\n\\[\n\\frac{dP}{dt}-rP=-c,\n\\]\nwhere the continuous compounding rate is \\(r=0.09\\) and the continuous payment rate is \\(c=9600\\) (obtained by converting the given monthly ceiling of \\(800\\) into an equivalent annual rate). The initial condition is \\(P(0)=A\\), the unknown loan amount, and the loan is fully repaid after \\(T=20\\) years, so the terminal condition \\(P(T)=0\\) must also hold.\nThe integrating factor \\(e^{-rt}\\) produces the explicit solution\n\\[\nP(t)=\\frac{c}{r}+(A-\\frac{c}{r})e^{rt}.\n\\]\nImposing \\(P(T)=0\\) immediately yields the algebraic relation\n\\[\nA=\\frac{c}{r}(1-e^{-rT}).\n\\]\nSubstituting the numerical values of the parameters gives the exact expression\n\\[\nA=\\frac{9600}{0.09}(1-e^{-1.8}).\n\\]\nEvaluating the exponential produces \\(e^{-1.8}\\approx0.16529889\\), so\n\\[\n1-e^{-1.8}\\approx0.83470111.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nA\\approx106666.6667\\times0.83470111=89034.78.\n\\]\n(The same numerical result is recovered by recognizing that \\(A\\) is simply the present-value integral of a continuous payment stream:\n\\[\nA=\\int_0^T c\\,e^{-rt}\\,dt.)\n\\]\nRounded to the nearest dollar, the largest affordable loan is therefore \\(89035\\).\nAnswer: 89035", "correct": true, "gold": "89,034.79 $"}
{"qid": "mp:7691", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "DeepSeek-V4-Flash", "resp": "We are working in \\(\\mathbb{Z}_3\\), so the possible values for \\(c\\) are \\(0, 1, 2\\).\n\nFor \\(\\mathbb{Z}_3[x]/(x^3 + x^2 + c)\\) to be a field, the polynomial \\(x^3 + x^2 + c\\) must be irreducible over \\(\\mathbb{Z}_3\\). Since it is degree 3, irreducibility is equivalent to having no roots in \\(\\mathbb{Z}_3\\).\n\nCheck each \\(c\\):\n\n- **c = 0**: Polynomial is \\(x^3 + x^2\\).  \n  \\(x=0\\): \\(0+0=0\\) \u2192 root. Not irreducible.\n\n- **c = 1**: Polynomial is \\(x^3 + x^2 + 1\\).  \n  \\(x=0\\): \\(0+0+1=1 \\neq 0\\)  \n  \\(x=1\\): \\(1+1+1 = 3 \\equiv 0\\) \u2192 root. Not irreducible.\n\n- **c = 2**: Polynomial is \\(x^3 + x^2 + 2\\).  \n  \\(x=0\\): \\(0+0+2=2 \\neq 0\\)  \n  \\(x=1\\): \\(1+1+2=4 \\equiv 1 \\neq 0\\)  \n  \\(x=2\\): \\(8+4+2 = 14 \\equiv 2 \\neq 0\\) (since 14 mod 3 = 2).  \n  No roots, so irreducible.\n\nThus only \\(c = 2\\) works.\n\nAnswer: 2", "correct": true, "gold": "2"}
{"qid": "mp:7691", "domain": "mmlupro:math", "model": "grok-4.3", "resp": "The ring \\(\\mathbb{Z}_3\\) is a field, so the quotient \\(\\mathbb{Z}_3[x]/(f(x))\\) with \\(f(x)=x^3+x^2+c\\) is a field if and only if \\(f\\) is irreducible in \\(\\mathbb{Z}_3[x]\\). As \\(f\\) is cubic, it is irreducible if and only if it has no roots in \\(\\mathbb{Z}_3\\).\n- For \\(c=0\\), \\(f(0)=0\\), so \\(f\\) has a root.\n- For \\(c=1\\), \\(f(1)=0\\), so \\(f\\) has a root.\n- For \\(c=2\\), the values are \\(f(0)=2\\), \\(f(1)=1\\), and \\(f(2)=2\\), none of which are zero, so \\(f\\) has no roots and is therefore irreducible.\nThus the quotient is a field precisely when \\(c=2\\).\nAnswer: 2", "correct": true, "gold": "2"}
